RmdsOGpOSEROY1VMVjg4UUNNN3hVdm9RV1Z1WXJaajMtRjVPT0ZzeU5WMktrNTZ3cjd6dGNUVkYxOUNjNkRsdUdjbTdhVHh1WlRWUm4zS0VvNXZjblNfYlBsam5vMVQx?oc=5", "source": "topic_geopolitics", "published_at": "2026-10-17T12:12:07+00:00", "text": "Zelenskyy expects US to ease more Russia sanctions after Graham Act waiver - Türkiye Today\nZelenskyy expects US to ease more Russia sanctions after Graham Act waiver Türkiye Today", "truncated": false}, {"id": "1a74d53d71b8e5fd2f322696", "title": "Labyrinth Canyon on Lake Powell, where the lake ends and the ground of the side canyon begins, as seen on Oct. 7, 2026. This area was once under about 100 feet of water when the lake was full. - The Lufkin Daily News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihwJBVV95cUxOdFBuVDFUQ0RkMTdlYTBoa1ZJVDN4d2VqZGxYaWFibGNyRE1HcEVadlNYelBQNFpqUE5hMVpjZkQ3eS10WEZvbFJ5ZkNhTDQ1dWlwdFdHdjBIQTZiRER2cHJEVmRXR1FJWmVDN2g0MVpxcVV6TUo0MmxDYkRXUk5xNDhIczBiOWQ4X3BSeEZwSHBQY1VRbXdZeVhlVC1XZWdqYjZNMTRHZ0dBd1BHeUZwcXpuWXQ0QVNodnV5NGdaVlJ", "source": "topic_fed", "published_at": "2026-10-17T12:12:01+00:00", "text": "Labyrinth Canyon on Lake Powell, where the lake ends and the ground of the side canyon begins, as seen on Oct. 7, 2026. This area was once under about 100 feet of water when the lake was full. - The Lufkin Daily News\nLabyrinth Canyon on Lake Powell, where the lake ends and the ground of the side canyon begins, as seen on Oct. 7, 2026. This area was once under about 100 feet of water when the lake was full. The Lufkin Daily News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "20938e43864dab7dcc546b08", "title": "Nursery owner in China jailed for life after hundreds of children poisoned", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqe9lx54g7x9o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-17T12:11:58+00:00", "text": "Nursery owner in China jailed for life after hundreds of children poisoned\nKitchen staff was instructed to use inedible paint to enhance the look of meals to attract more students, the court found\nA nursery owner and five members of her staff have been jailed after they were found to have poisoned more than 200 children with lead-tainted food.\nLi Huifang, who operated the Heshi Peixin Kindergarten in Tianshui, in the Gansu province, was sentenced to life in prison for producing and selling toxic and harmful food, according to Chinese state media.\nIn July 2025, 247 pupils and staff at the nursery were found to have\nA report into the incident found that chefs at Heshi Peixin had used inedible paint to \"enhance the look\" of its meals to attract more students - despite the paint being being clearly marked as such.\nIn addition to the sentence, the Lanzhou Intermediate People's Court also ordered the confiscation of 500,000 yuan (Â£56,200; $74,700) of Li's personal assets, state broadcaster CCTV reported.\nNursery executive principal Zhi Jinglin was sentenced to 15 years in prison and fined 300,00 yuan.\nFour other members of staff who worked in the nursery's kitchen received sentences of between six to eight years, according to state media.\nThe court found the four had been instructed by Li and Zhi to add toxic and harmful ingredients to food served to children and staff.\nFood samples were later found to contain lead 2,000 times in excess of China's national safety limit.\nThe investigation into the poisoning began after 235 children from Heshi Peixin nursery had to be treated for lead poisoning after eating steamed red date cakes and sausage corn buns.\nInvestigators found that the red date cake and the corn sausage rolls had lead levels of 1052mg/kg and 1340mg/kg respectively, both of which far exceed the national food safety standard limit of 0.5mg/kg.\nLead poisoning is \"particularly harmful to young children\" and can \"severely damage the brain and central nervous system\", according to the World Health Organization.\nOfficials later found that dozens of provincial officials and hospital staff\nby tampering with blood tests and accepting bribes from a nursery investor.\nChina has since published a set of national guidelines for the provision of meals on campuses. Among the measures is the requirement for every new batch of food to be tested, and for flour, rice and cooking oil to be purchased from centralised procurement points only.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "b0d87c2daacf29c226f6619e", "title": "Health Workers’ Strike Disrupts Services At Turai Yar’adua Hospital #trusttvnews - Instagram", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiVkFVX3lxTFAtNjNUWkh4djNaUjl4TzRndnc3Z01aa1cyV2dZTzRHd2lHeVFSVTYzbl96N1ZCdW5Ta3RmemgyT0YyM25PMDNqUElWTWFNYkNTb3RXazZn?oc=5", "source": "lane_labor_stop", "published_at": "2026-10-17T12:11:13+00:00", "text": "Health Workers’ Strike Disrupts Services At Turai Yar’adua Hospital #trusttvnews - Instagram\nHealth Workers’ Strike Disrupts Services At Turai Yar’adua Hospital #trusttvnews Instagram", "truncated": false}, {"id": "d85e52835b4722e0e5f6dbde", "title": "US and Iran both claim control of Strait of Hormuz amid attacks on vessels", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/17/us-and-iran-both-claim-control-of-strait-of-hormuz-amid-attacks-on-vessels?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-17T12:10:56+00:00", "text": "US and Iran both claim control of Strait of Hormuz amid attacks on vessels\nShare US and Iran both claim control of Strait of Hormuz amid attacks on vessels on social media\nA vessel transits the Gulf of Oman off the coast of Khor Fakkan in the emirate of Sharjah, eastern UAE, on October 13, 2026 [AFP]\nDozens of ships were attacked in or near the Strait of Hormuz in October as Iran and the United States compete to assert their authority over the waterway.\nAt least 12 ships were attacked in the week ending on October 16, the United Kingdom Maritime Trade Operations (UKMTO) reported late Friday, with one important caveat.\nFighting in Yemen intensifies: What’s the latest on the battlefield?\nIraq devalues dinar to ease cash crunch, leaving traders in limbo\nContainers and debris captured floating around the Strait of Hormuz\n“UKMTO is struggling to accurately report true number of attacks with majority of vessels attacked refusing to report to UKMTO,” the maritime security organisation stated. It said it was aware of more incidents in the waterway via other sources but could not verify them due to a lack of information.\nThe same pattern has been true for the first half of October. The month has seen the\nsince the start of the US-Israel war on Iran, which began on February 28.\nOf the 12 vessels that were confirmed attacked, the organisation said half continued to their destination or reached port. One sank, two were disabled, and three were either damaged or had an unconfirmed outcome.\nThe UKMTO also reported a new type of attack on Saturday. The master of a tanker said their vessel was hit by a rocket-propelled grenade (RPG) fired from a small boat 78 nautical miles (equivalent to about 144km) northeast of Saudi Arabia’s city of Jubail, Eastern province, resulting in damage to the aft port quarter.\nthrough the Strait of Hormuz and surrounding waterways through a\nshuttle system coordinated with Arab powers\n. But volumes have oscillated weekly due to rising Iranian attacks.\nOil flows through Hormuz were at 11.6 million barrels per day (bpd) over the seven days to October 13. This was about 68 percent of the 17.1 million bpd pre-war baseline, according to Kpler figures.\nOn Friday, Brent crude also touched $110 a barrel for the first time since May in light of ongoing attacks. The situation has been exacerbated by the\nIran-aligned Houthis’ attacks on Saudi energy infrastructure\nand their capture of the Bab al-Mandeb strait. Expectations that the conflict between the US and Iran was escalating have also impacted prices.\nThe US military has provided a starkly different outlook on the situation in and around the Strait of Hormuz, which accounted for roughly a fifth of global oil and natural gas flows before the war began.\nUS Central Command (CENTCOM) chief Brad Cooper claimed in a video message late Friday that despite the Iranian attacks, 400 ships and 100 million barrels of oil moved through the Strait of Hormuz over the past week.\n“Let me be clear: The Strait of Hormuz is controlled by the United States and our regional partners, not Iran,” he said, attributing the greatly increased ship attacks to “desperation” by the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC).\nThe US military also claimed that it continues to prevent Iranian exports and imports via its southern ports through an “ironclad”\n. It claims the blockade has cost Iran $25bn in oil export income, and that the US has recently seized $350m in Iranian cryptocurrency financing.\nThe UKMTO report said total outbound transit over the last seven days was 125 ships, with total inbound transits standing at 129. Over the week, most transits by the Western security agency were for dry bulk cargo, followed by tanker products and general cargo.\nTehran has not provided exact figures on how many vessels have been attacked, or how many are passing through the Hormuz, although it has said Washington is exaggerating numbers to project success of its naval campaign.\nIt comes as two vessels were reportedly hit by naval mines in the Strait of Hormuz this week, with the US and Iran offering contradictory claims about the situation.\nThe IRGC has increasingly reported that vessels are hitting mines across the strategic waterway, which it says remains unsafe for vessels which do not coordinate with Iran’s Persian Gulf Strait Authority, an entity set up during the war to manage passage through the Hormuz.\nThe latest claim came on Saturday, when the IRGC said a supertanker carrying more than two million barrels of crude oil caught fire after striking a mine while moving through an “unauthorised” route.\nThe Panama-flagged container shipping vessel Ten Neptune transits the Gulf of Oman off the coast of Khor Fakkan in the emirate of Sharjah, eastern UAE, on October 13, 2026 [AFP]\nIran’s state-run Press TV shared footage it says showed the tanker on fire. But CENTCOM said it had cleared the strait of naval mines, and that vessels are being struck by projectiles.\nThe latest incident “fact-checked” by the US military was on Thursday, when it said a civilian container ship, the M/V GFS Pride, was hit by an IRGC projectile, not by a mine as the Iranians had claimed. The vessel sank as it was being towed to shore.\nThe US has refrained from responding to any of the recent vessel attacks, but there are concerns that Washington could escalate the war after the November 3 midterm elections.\nDiplomacy has not fully halted, with messages continuing to be relayed between the two parties by regional mediators like Pakistan, Qatar and others. But no breakthrough in talks is expected any time soon.\nPresident Donald Trump has reportedly ordered the US military to remain prepared for launching bigger attacks after the elections. Those attacks could potentially include using the GBU-43/B Massive Ordnance Air Blast bomb, nicknamed the\nBut Tehran has signalled that it intends to continue the attacks, with acting Defence Minister Majid Ibn Reza saying on Saturday that it will be Iranian missiles, not US bombs, that determine the future of the war.\n“Your bombs will explode elsewhere; in your global reputation, the US economy, and even your electoral calculations,” he was quoted as saying by state media.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "4a22fc5c735ec5eec6f499be", "title": "Racist comments spark walkout at Albany-area high school - NewYorkUpstate.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiqAFBVV95cUxQcF9MdnJDLU1hOHpsdV9pYWRMUTByRGJXV1cySS1JUUM3Q0ZqNkZ4NjFTbGlXRGhmel84N2ZZd1o1Uk1XcDZNWVJVOGswelRTT3E3cmhZQnpLbkRPTnc2N0tVYkxNOWZndDJEWmdFRlZ1UzlnNVZFTkg2SGN0ak9DbzNXM2hselAtZlNSOXI5SEl0OVFHazRaUXJsZ3ZOc3