under, Execs over Accounting Fraud nippon.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "6d6cd4a2e5b7ce1a73e401e4", "title": "Rarely Seen ‘Happy Days’ Spinoff Is Back on TV 45 Years Later - Parade Magazine", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMilwFBVV95cUxQbXpoYXFsY3lNaTJkOGU1UFVWLUVBbXlETnB2d2pFSEtDTDR6bmgzT2NUekxDcUQyYnVDM1dEVHB5dzBPTU5aalpobXBRUUNKbUtHS3FzeEhnTXdXbms0QVNKR2FPWk51NGVTR3pBakRxYVFmQUlJdGpJQ1lZT1drZWlyVHZCd0tsU2ZwQndwY2lnM3VvUmww?oc=5", "source": "lane_corporate_action_spinoff", "published_at": "2026-10-17T10:46:43+00:00", "text": "Rarely Seen ‘Happy Days’ Spinoff Is Back on TV 45 Years Later - Parade Magazine\nRarely Seen ‘Happy Days’ Spinoff Is Back on TV 45 Years Later Parade Magazine", "truncated": false}, {"id": "77b386c30370c768bd4ab17b", "title": "Rare pink diamond goes on display in France 100 years after amateur heist", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c61m4zkx47vlo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-17T10:46:41+00:00", "text": "Rare pink diamond goes on display in France 100 years after amateur heist\nLâÃcole des Arts Joailliers, Benjamin Chelly\nThe nine-carat, pear-shaped gemstone was one of few royal jewels to survive the French Revolution\nA rare pink diamond is going on display in France for the first time since it was stolen almost 100 years ago to the day - the last time it was shown to the public.\nThe nine-carat, pear-shaped gemstone known as the \"Grand CondÃ©\" was pilfered in an overnight raid on the ChÃ¢teau de Chantilly in October 1926 and concealed in a hollowed-out apple.\nThe diamond - one of few royal jewels to survive the French Revolution - was recovered a few weeks later when a hungry maid at the hotel in which the thieves were staying took a bite of the apple.\nIt will once again be on display in the CondÃ© Museum at the ChÃ¢teau de Chantilly until 3 January.\nThe museum's director Mathieu Deldicque said that while the apple story may sounds far-fetched, it was widely covered by newspapers at the time.\nDeldicque has faced questions about whether the exhibition may be tempting fate, especially given the string of heists on European cultural institutions in the past 12 months, including the daring daylight robbery of jewellery from the Louvre museum in Paris.\nWhile he declined to reveal to CNN how much the diamond was insured for, he noted that extensive academic study into the properties of the diamond had taken place to make it uniquely identifiable.\nThe results of this analysis, as well as previously unseen archive material about the gem's history and theft, form part of the exhibition, according to the museum.\nNaturally occurring pink diamonds are exceptionally rare and often sell for tens of millions of dollars. The highest price ever fetched for one at auction was $71.2m (Â£53.6m) in 2017, according to Sotheby's.\nThe exact origins of the Grand CondÃ© remain a mystery.\nIt was first mentioned in 1740 in an inventory of Louis Henri de Bourbon, Prince of CondÃ©, drawn up following his death, the ChÃ¢teau de Chantilly said.\nIt then became part of the collection of Queen Marie-AmÃ©lie of Bourbon-Sicily, the wife of French King Louis Philippe I, and then her son, Prince Henri of OrlÃ©ans, the Duke of Aumale.\nHe bequeathed it to the Institut de France, which now owns the ChÃ¢teau de Chantilly, 50km (31 miles) north of Paris, along with thousands of artworks and manuscripts which became the MusÃ©e CondÃ© collection.\nPurple-pink diamond sells for $26.6m (Â£20.1m)", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "050b0db6b8ad86a0bdb787fe", "title": "Weekly Review: Brent Holds Above $105 as Hormuz Attacks Cut Gulf Flows 68%; WTI Jumps 5.81% on Record US Inventory Draw - HDFC Sky", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi1gFBVV95cUxQZTBkS2ZMVV9Ub0djdndiQW9EQzZ1NXFKejJ0Yzd6QnVzV2Z4T1QtbUtGZGhTd3Bfb1Z0RnhUZG5kMUhESDY4ckdxZ1lORjVRSVRGdXcxbEl3TmgzaURjeXp3ei13UXc0TUxSVzdWRUxwU01fZHFYZXBqbW50bFRfSS05MUE4TmtDTmw3T0V