ogy In Bangladesh Is Transforming Flood Zones - The Borgen Project", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMibEFVX3lxTE42OEhNU0t1bVNYQjVpWFFMczF2N3BZRkQwekR3YmRyZjkzY24yQ1Z4THFGdFRUcUJ2N1hXNF9xRmFfbV94dnV1X1dGeUpZN09NZjJtTm1RdDZqSjBiYTdDVFhzVlU0VzU3cU1FYQ?oc=5", "source": "topic_technology", "published_at": "2026-10-17T08:30:50+00:00", "text": "How Floating Technology In Bangladesh Is Transforming Flood Zones - The Borgen Project\nHow Floating Technology In Bangladesh Is Transforming Flood Zones The Borgen Project", "truncated": false}, {"id": "32e4e45c7349aeded0525205", "title": "ECB officials see December rate hike as uncertainty clouds 2027 outlook - Business Standard", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi0gFBVV95cUxPTlhUMTdKajM1a2Jlek83djVyU3FUbjJ1VDBqREpGSko3VWFONTI0TmlRMmZhQTMtM1p6R2Z3czM1c1Zfc0FCUXd3WUhHd3p5bjh6eXJqWDVjNUJYMk5YWF9IYVk0bUMwN1RkMzg1THBodTRsS01nT0VfT0ktbGNrMGtCSHVZdG9kNlZVVWJfenVSaE9xLUJOcXFMdVRGN29JSjE2dG42dUhKT1NSWEt0UTFNZWdlNFgzNDVsSTRySHF", "source": "topic_central_banks", "published_at": "2026-10-17T08:30:08+00:00", "text": "ECB officials see December rate hike as uncertainty clouds 2027 outlook - Business Standard\nECB officials see December rate hike as uncertainty clouds 2027 outlook Business Standard", "truncated": false}, {"id": "0e299fac6ee9adb8722d0155", "title": "Rubio’s Uzbekistan trip underlines Central Asia’s hunt for diverse partners", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news-analysis/2026/10/17/rubios-uzbekistan-trip-underlines-central-asias-hunt-for-diverse-partners?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-17T08:27:28+00:00", "text": "Rubio’s Uzbekistan trip underlines Central Asia’s hunt for diverse partners\nShare Rubio’s Uzbekistan trip underlines Central Asia’s hunt for diverse partners on social media\nRussian President Vladimir Putin, left, and Uzbekistan's President Shavkat Mirziyoyev greet each other on the sidelines of the Commonwealth of Independent States (CIS) summit in Turkmenistan, on Friday, October 9, 2026, but relations with US Trump administration may also be warming up [Gavriil Grigorov, Sputnik, Kremlin Pool Photo via AP]\nUnited States Secretary of State Marco Rubio is arriving in Samarkand, the fabled Silk Road city in what is now central Uzbekistan, for talks with key regional ministers on Saturday.\nThe C5+1 – Central Asia plus the US – summit to be held from Saturday to Tuesday heralds Washington’s new approach to the ex-Soviet region – pragmatic, purely transactional and devoid of speeches about democratisation and human rights.\nAt the dawn of Central Asia’s independence, Americans used to show up in the mostly Muslim region strategically stretched between Russia, China and Iran, bearing gifts – and giving lectures.\nAfter the 1991 Soviet collapse, amid the region’s economic degradation, joblessness and poverty, US and European diplomats and businessmen were seen as ambassadors of the brave new world of capitalist prosperity.\nThey wanted reforms – and not just the economic ones.\nWestern diplomats made sure that former Communist apparatchiks-turned-new regional leaders understood that they’d get investments, technologies and international recognition only in exchange for abandoning the Soviet-era totalitarian ways of governing and building the economy.\nThe West held forums and workshops on human rights, democracy and decentralisation of governance, and sent thousands of students and officials to Western schools.\nAnd the West accused regional leaders of losing their moral compass and threatened sanctions when they began to amend their nation’s constitutions to extend their presidential terms, appoint their family members and friends to key government jobs and help them create business empires that often competed with Western investors.\n“When the West began moralising, teaching them about democracy, human rights, they immediately turned towards China or Russia,” Galiya Ibragimova, Uzbekistan-born, Moldova-based regional expert, told Al Jazeera.\nUzbekistan, the region’s most populous nation of 37 million, offers a perfect example.\nIts iron-fisted President Islam Karimov was the first ex-Soviet leader to allow a US military base on its soil in 2001.\nBut four years later, he kicked it out after Washington lambasted his\nof a popular revolt in the eastern city of Andijan that killed hundreds of unarmed civilians.\nIn 2005, a leaked US diplomatic cable called Karimov’s daughter,\n, a “robber baron” who forcibly took over lucrative businesses and was seen as her father’s successor.\nAs to moralising and lecturing, the tables have turned since Russia’s full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022, observers say.\nMoscow still has plenty of soft power in Central Asia – from movies and pop music to theatre festivals and propagandist TV shows that constantly lie about the war.\nThe Kremlin also uses its economic clout to promote the imperialist concept of the “Russian World”, where the region is seen as Moscow’s “near abroad”, and its pre-Russian heritage is snubbed as backwards and insignificant.\nIn 2014, Russian President Vladimir Putin claimed that Kazakhstan had “never had statehood”, and his ideological allies repeatedly threatened to annex its Russian-speaking northern part.\nThis year, Kazakhstan began extraditing Kremlin critics and supplying gas to Russia amid Ukrainian drone attacks on its oil refineries.\nIn January, Kremlin propagandist Vladimir Solovyov said Moscow should launch a “special military operation” – a term reserved for the war in Ukraine – in Central Asia and Armenia.\nCritical remarks from minor political figures followed – but no rebukes from the halls of real power.\nMeanwhile, Central Asian labour migrants have been duped or\nto fight in Ukraine in droves, while Moscow uses Central Asian companies to import microchips, communications equipment and machine tools to build drones and missiles.\n“These days, the US and Russia have switched roles. Today, it’s Russia that began building ties through moralising,” Ibragimova, the regional expert, said.\nMeanwhile, US President Donald Trump’s administration has abandoned the pro-democracy stance and now advocates a utilitarian, transactional approach to foreign policy.\n“Under Trump, the American policy in Central Asia is all about ‘just business and nothing more’, without being nosy about human rights, reforms. They are content with everything,” Ibragimova said.\nEarlier this month, Trump received Saida Mirziyoyeva, the daughter of Uzbek President Shavkat Mirziyoyev.\nA year earlier, Mirziyoyev pledged to invest up to $100bn in the US economy, buy Boeing aircraft and even US-grown cotton, even though Uzbekistan is one of the world’s largest cotton growers.\nWashington did not say a word about Mirziyoyev’s crackdown on critics or his daughter’s growing domestic clout.\nAfter largely ignoring Central Asia during Trump’s first term, the White House wants to resume its presence.\n“The interest is mostly geopolitical and military-strategic, considering the region’s location in the zone of Russia’s and China’s interests,” Alisher Ilkhamov, head of the London-based Central Asia Due Diligence think tank, told Al Jazeera.\n“In the future, Washington apparently wants to have a military base here as a deterrent measure against China,” he said.\nThe economic interests seem to be focused on rare-earth minerals – crucial for manufacturing a wide array of technology – and other critical elements, especially since China uses them as a political cudgel in its ties with Washington.\nThe deposits are abundant, but need development, technologies and investment.\nMeanwhile, the White House shows little interest in human rights violations in Central Asia, such as the jailing of political opponents and independent journalists.\n“An interest in these issues, unfortunately, is absent from Rubio’s agenda, because these problems went missing from US foreign policy priorities since Trump returned to the White House,” Ilkhamov said.\nA Norwegian human rights advocate echoes his concerns.\n“There are uncomfortable similarities between Trump’s worldview and established authoritarian regimes like Tajikistan and Turkmenistan,” Ivar Dale, a senior policy adviser with the Norwegian Helsinki Committee, a rights watchdog, told Al Jazeera.\n“However, Marco Rubio probably has a better understanding of Central Asia and the challenges there than does [Vice President] JD Vance and [Defense Secretary] Pete Hegseth,” he said.\nThe US State Department “should bring back the focus [on human rights] if the C5+1 format is to be taken seriously going forward”, he said.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "c991e85888e36d1d70e2b32f", "title": "Trump Blocks Lindsey Graham’s Russia Sanctions Just Weeks After Signing Them Into Law - UNITED24 Media", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiywFBVV95cUxQTFY5SUN3R1hMdVBtN04ta280bEpRNkgyM1p6VW15azFNa216ZWx2WGR5NHFWc1ItZFlielM0WVJqWnZ3UC1nMDU3Mm9oUUJacTFyZ01fcE1kQXJ0YVpVWXh0Tnp6NHV0OHZHSGh6VjNpS0paam1COTlIdUJvY1hMTV9TMHU4QmI4MmlHVVN0VjZSTUZiOGJvNVNpblc1a2dlVVUxcy1iYzExdEl2cGcySXBJeGNUbmhORW1GQUZnQmI", "source": "topic_geopolitics", "published_at": "2026-10-17T08:27:19+00:00", "text": "Trump Blocks Lindsey Graham’s Russia Sanctions Just Weeks After Signing Them Into Law - UNITED24 Media\nTrump Blocks Lindsey Graham’s Russia Sanctions Just Weeks After Signing Them Into Law UNITED24 Media", "truncated": false}, {"id": "2301b07082108725a82d6eb9", "title": "Vanta Closes Second Tranche of Private Placement Financing and Welcomes Launch of First U.S.-Listed Peptide ETF, Highlighting Growing Recognition of Peptide Industry - Lelezard", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi3AFBVV95cUxPVWlnbnJwMi1nQnM3Q0xaLWlKSXpRRTlOeG4wUE50d1JhRk5CYnlVWXl6bmdWckgtSDZ2RVdqM2V0LTh3NjZndThPeDMteG91NkpEeUZfcEsyaFM4MXg5QVU1WkdwTG5kOHZKa2dxUEJYamZlYVdRQUV4S2taTmNFNHQ2MlBrVjRjaGJHUVR4LWZoZ2lzak45QVRVemxiZ2xiT2htblotaU9rcTJtSjlmdjB