ine", "truncated": false}, {"id": "55aa8b4a2da246ab0e32181a", "title": "Donald Trump waives sanctions, tariffs on purchase of Russian oil and gas - The Hindu", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi0gFBVV95cUxQNEJCSWVBNk5fUVR0NVJDTVNSOG1TYUVWWW9WWTVUVC1OUDBUbGdoZGR0amxSUXozZHQtZHNVZzVmSDJDT3ZoZXB2OEFKbHVxbUl4TmlLQXVVQzhabmptSHk0d1BIcW05SHJtY0pvTmU2V3FUM29WbVlYQ05TTkR4ajVQa2NKXy1ycWhBMlpVdjY2bTZMeFRrVGFaUGgzSDhRQWRxQzFXQmJTSXlSTHE3MDhVLUdXX0dvZTF6TzB4UGg", "source": "topic_geopolitics", "published_at": "2026-10-17T07:47:26+00:00", "text": "Donald Trump waives sanctions, tariffs on purchase of Russian oil and gas - The Hindu\nDonald Trump waives sanctions, tariffs on purchase of Russian oil and gas The Hindu", "truncated": false}, {"id": "372eed4dcaeeae033fb54b6e", "title": "Retail price impacts from the Iran war are uneven. But they add up for US households before midterms. - The Boston Globe", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimwFBVV95cUxNMmlMZ05aSF96X09wQkJiMmZzZ2JzbUtHV3BLcjdoOU1CTTZ3RlJjbFVCenJ2am5TMHVHQ00zVUdoS09kWU9hT3g5V0VOUVIzSmVTWjNWajI2cllnWjlEMXZmNkRVX3ZIQTk2b053dXJzbDBoUEN3T3VjZkdjQ25uTDV1dmhLRGxJMHFPc213SHFXZ3FHTkU4VXpfNA?oc=5", "source": "lane_regime_state", "published_at": "2026-10-17T07:46:50+00:00", "text": "Retail price impacts from the Iran war are uneven. But they add up for US households before midterms. - The Boston Globe\nRetail price impacts from the Iran war are uneven. But they add up for US households before midterms. The Boston Globe", "truncated": false}, {"id": "4096413841642c490079327b", "title": "Five Months’ Pay After Resigning: Alexandria Police Auditor Leaves With Questions Unanswered - The Zebra", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMikgFBVV95cUxNR2hUUXdMVy13TWxOSVZTcElQcGlLWlA2OVlzLTBrLXp3QXVfd0lKNGpjcjQ4cUYycUpmaVpVSUtQdHZFeUIzX2ZGbWNpMVlVV3YxZ0VZNklHU00xYVA1WmFLYjVDelU4Y1ZSdUVoRWlUQm51R3MyQklWV0xSQzJfNkoyNDBTVUlNVUgzcUlDNXZkZw?oc=5", "source": "lane_integrity", "published_at": "2026-10-17T07:46:17+00:00", "text": "Five Months’ Pay After Resigning: Alexandria Police Auditor Leaves With Questions Unanswered - The Zebra\nFive Months’ Pay After Resigning: Alexandria Police Auditor Leaves With Questions Unanswered The Zebra", "truncated": false}, {"id": "3ed8e2e280b62572ae139aa2", "title": "Arrests made for pro-Nazi chants at Hajduk Split-Ajax match in Croatia", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/sports/2026/10/17/pro-nazi-arrests-made-for-chants-at-hajduk-split-ajax-in-uefa-europa-league?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-17T07:45:31+00:00", "text": "Arrests made for pro-Nazi chants at Hajduk Split-Ajax match in Croatia\nShare Arrests made for pro-Nazi chants at Hajduk Split-Ajax match in Croatia on social media\nThousands of members of Hajduk's Torcida fan club march towards the club's stadium before the Ajax match [Ante Cizmic/AP]\nPolice in the Croatian city of Split have arrested at least a dozen fans of the Hajduk Split football team, a day after unruly supporters held an anti-Semitic rally, clashed with officers and interrupted a match with the visiting Dutch team Ajax.\nThe arrests were carried out on Friday after thousands of fans shouted insults against Jewish people and chanted in praise of Nazi leader Adolf Hitler before the game on Thursday night. They marched behind a car on top of which was a mock-up of a coffin emblazoned with the name of the Ajax team, long been associated with Amsterdam’s Jewish community.\nOld rivalry has new twist as Man United head to Leeds – all to know\nFIFA set for rule change after Balogun red card row at the World Cup: reports\nManchester Utd have ‘moved on’ from Martinez outburst, says Carrick\nManchester City’s charges appeal to be heard by January, regulator says\nin Croatia. President Zoran Milanovic and the conservative government harshly criticised the fans and demanded punishment. Police said on Friday that they had arrested 12 fans on charges of inciting violence and hatred and indicated they may arrest more suspects.\nThe Hajduk fans also clashed with police and forced a 12-minute interruption of the match when they threw flares and other objects onto the pitch.\nHajduk eventually beat Ajax 2-1 in the third-tier UEFA Conference League in what was the home team’s first major European match for 16 years.\nThe Croatian Football Association said in a statement that the incident damaged the country’s football image. It called the behaviour completely opposed to “the basic values of sports: mutual respect, tolerance and togetherness”.\nMilanovic urged “measures and sanctions that will show that there is no place for glorification of Nazism in Croatia”.\nThe Croatian government long has been accused of turning a blind eye to rising nationalist and far-right sentiments related to the country’s World War II regime that collaborated with the Nazis. The government has denied this.\nMilorad Pupovac, the political leader of ethnic Serbs in Croatia, who were the victims of the pro-Nazi regime in Croatia, said “it was just a question of time before we will reach this point that happened in Split.”\nTens of thousands of ethnic Serbs, Jews, Roma and antifascist Croats perished in World War II concentration camps in Croatia. Nationalist Croats have used a World War II pro-Nazi chant as a symbol of Croatia’s struggle for independence from the Serb-led former Yugoslavia.\nThe Split incident also was condemned by the Israeli embassy in Zagreb, which said it was “deeply shocked” and urged a government response.\nThe Hajduk Split team, which could face disciplinary action from UEFA over the game’s disruption, apologised for the fans’ behaviour but said it reflected only some of the club’s fans.\n“Despite all efforts, things happened that we are not proud of, but we are ready to take the consequences as a club,” the team said in a statement.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "479502b89e82d2279142158b", "title": "Katie Zacharia resigns after just five days as White House press secretary - The Business Standard", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMivwFBVV95cUxOQ040N01qOG05NEZHUldBUl90Q183bWVNQlg3Zmpnb2g1QU1pMXlvb3JKS185RHFVbm9pLUxna3BoLXc3NjBVZE5teEpFc0FUVnVSYnBGNlNzZVhNaTZrVEpxMWZxdXpWWF83SFRVSE5vbzhDRVJtU2E5bGh0R1hEek5SRlhqZFlYcHFjMFd1aUVKZXdTbEVCVFR1bGNYR1RQMm80NVdieTdSOG15cGlmU3dKMUhVa2w5NGRuZ0hTRQ?", "source": "lane_key_person", "published_at": "2026-10-17T07:45:00+00:00", "text": "Katie Zacharia resigns after just five days as White House press secretary - The Business Standard\nKatie Zacharia resigns after just five days as White House press secretary The Business Standard", "truncated": false}, {"id": "1588c6b4ce4c8b1b2d3b7db4", "title": "CN Hotels acquires Tennessee hotels - Asian Hospitality", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMieEFVX3lxTE1vVmFMelhPZzBmZXNTQU8ybHlSWjdfY2VFVlhoUnlsbVVTR0FYaHVfNW9UUDFBYXFsLU9vakJ3RTdmOEFJdXhxWHB1bDd0cjY3N1hXTkRteE1jTkhJU2tVYWdnZE1NQXBoVm01NFZDS192U1RWRGJNaA?oc=5", "source": "lane_corporate_action_mna", "published_at": "2026-10-17T07:44:54+00:00", "text": "CN Hotels acquires Tennessee hotels - Asian Hospitality\nCN Hotels acquires Tennessee hotels Asian Hospitality", "truncated": false}, {"id": "e9359c3fc52e173b6e70c651", "title": "Senegal-sized Antarctic glacier melting faster than thought: Why it matters", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/17/senegal-sized-antarctic-glacier-melting-faster-than-thought-why-it-matters?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-17T07:43:19+00:00", "text": "Senegal-sized Antarctic glacier melting faster than thought: Why it matters\nShare Senegal-sized Antarctic glacier melting faster than thought: Why it matters on social media\nA NASA image of the Thwaites glacier in Antarctica. Massive glaciers in West Antarctica seem to be locked in an irreversible thaw linked to global warming that may raise sea levels for centuries, scientists say [Reuters]\nThe world’s largest glacier is melting much faster and could add twice as much to global sea levels as previously expected over the next century.\nNew research published this week by a team of glaciologists has found that the Thwaites glacier in West Antarctica is losing mass rapidly because warm seawater is pushing underneath it and melting its core. This could add 1.2 inches (30mm) to water levels even if global warming does not accelerate as predicted.\nThe Nepal floods question: When the Himalayas collapse, who gets warned?\nIndia to supply Nepal electricity after floods wrecked hydropower projects\nEl Nino reaches super status: Where will it bring rain, drought and heat?\n‘The drought has left its mark’: Slovenia’s potato lovers lament low yields\nadd to a growing body of evidence that shows that the glacier, which has been receding for decades, is now losing mass even faster than scientists thought.\nResearchers at the University of California in the United States collaborated with NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory for the study. Here’s what we know about the findings:\nThis 2022 satellite image from the European Space Agency, annotated by marine geophysicist Robert Larter, shows the positions of research vessels RVIB Nathaniel B Palmer and the RV Araon on ice shelf areas extending from Thwaites glacier in Antarctica. ‘EIS’ indicates the Eastern Ice Shelf, where research was conducted in 2020, and which recently published studies have suggested is likely to break up sometime within the next two decades [File: Robert Larter/British Antarctic Survey, ESA via AP]\nWhat is Thwaites and why is it important?\nNicknamed the “Doomsday Glacier”, Thwaites is the largest glacier on Earth, spanning an area of around 192,000sq km (74,130sq miles) or roughly the size of Senegal – and larger than the US state of Florida.\nIt is located on the Walgreen Coast of Marie Byrd Land in remote western Antarctica and forms part of the great West Antarctic ice sheet, which includes other glaciers and is 14 times the size of Thwaites.\nThe glacier was first mapped in 1947 and is named after the late American glaciologist Fredrik T Thwaites, although he never visited it.\nIn the 1980s, scientists discovered that Thwaites is melting rapidly and have closely monitored its melting process as concerns have grown ever since.\nBecause it is so big, the glacier’s collapse would sharply raise global sea levels by a catastrophic amount – although researchers don’t project that will happen for many decades, or even a few centuries.\nTogether, Thwaites and other glaciers such as the Pine Island glacier – an ice stream in West Antarctica which flows into Pine Island Bay in the Amundsen Sea – act as a barrier that holds back the vast and vulnerable West Antarctic ice sheet. If melted, the ice sheet could cause many coastal cities to be submerged around the world.\nAt present, ice loss from Thwaites contributes around four percent of all global sea-level rise. Studies have calculated that the glacier has lost more than 1,000 billion tonnes of ice since 2000, and continues to lose 50 billion tonnes of ice per year.\nThe main drivers are warm ocean water which is actively melting the Thwaites glacier from underneath it, according to several studies.\nOcean warming is caused by human-induced climate change, in particular from the burning of polluting fossil fuels.\nIn a 2019 study, the NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory discovered a giant underwater cavity beneath the Thwaites glacier that spread across an area two-thirds the size of Manhattan, which researchers said was likely speeding up the glacier’s thinning.\nRising temperatures have caused glaciers to lose about five percent of their ice since 2000, according to the European Space Agency.\nThwaites could add as much as 1.2 inches (30mm) – double the current estimates – to sea levels by 2100, even without any additional global warming, the study found.\nThe researchers said they used new computer models that paint a clearer picture of melting activity under the threatened glacier at its grounding line – the boundary where it transitions from resting entirely on bedrock to spreading out over the sea in the form of ice shelves.\nWhere other ice sheet models conclude a grounding line is fixed, the scientists said they developed the concept of a broader “grounding zone” instead. In this scenario, seawater lifts and lowers the ice in a “tidal pumping” process that allows warm water to reach “deep underneath the ice sheets and causing vigorous melting,” lead author Mattia Poinelli, a project scientist at UCLA, told media.\nThat melting process is accelerating the Thwaites’ “unpinning” from the Mouginot Ridge – an important underwater bedrock currently stabilising the glacier from below. The study concluded that there is a 60 percent chance that Thwaites could unpin from the ridge by the end of the century.\nHowever, experts say more studies are needed to accurately predict the volume of Thwaites’ ice loss.\nIn separate findings, scientists from the British Antarctic Survey concluded that Thwaites will continue losing ice for at least the next 150 years, even under the most extreme ocean cooling conditions imaginable.\nThe study, which used computer simulation models that examined the melting effects of the ocean over the next century and a half, was published in Geophysical Research Letters in July.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "8b1b0a75bfc7a547dce73305", "title": "S.Amdt.5467 to S.2 - Congress.gov", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMie0FVX3lxTE95bFZJTk5nV2VGSXpxeHdIY1VEV0ZjZmZYdHc4MDRBcDBLTkstLXVLb2pvU0RIS2VMOFo3RHppbnMyVzBMMlFYNmR2bldEdkRqQjNraVUzUWtZNG1uVWpYVkhsYy01b1oyTnplUmRfZEQyNE1KbHA3dEliNA?oc=5", "source": "official_congress_search", "published_at": "2026-10-17T07:40:20+00:00", "text": "S.Amdt.5467 to S.2 - Congress.gov\nS.Amdt.5467 to S.2 Congress.gov", "truncated": false}, {"id": "d17eb291faa6348d7c3c5c05", "title": "Earthquake of magni