0bnVQSXd4SVJjSVJPVW1Mak1HcXhTcGdlX2Fib1U2UnJDQXRuVk5vR3oySnJhdzdEZlVIV2FPWE93TWdXWENjSWQyVU00UmpoWFJjaEdMSWdnOVdEa05FVXdGRVYzY2RNTXZhZHoxSk0xeGRxNGtJNlhhdU1HSXFlSUQyS2Nyd0QtRjZfZGw0S2NLck9nbE9ibUt3MF83MVdxLWh", "source": "lane_cat_weather", "published_at": "2026-10-17T05:54:16+00:00", "text": "Earthquake off Tenerife remains minor, but activity rises around Mount Teide - Teneriffa News\nEarthquake off Tenerife remains minor, but activity rises around Mount Teide Teneriffa News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "978e88bff0615b0a5dd0d1ea", "title": "Bucheon Factory Fire: 8 Dead as Sparks Ignite Thinner Fumes - 조선일보", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiiAFBVV95cUxNWUwwa3pjSmR2eDgwZnhGY1oxNEpjRUZvdTlHYzV0X09XT01xRmlIazRYbncxNWFueHNkVndIV3AwaEx4UGZ3c0lEQVhzaDlNVkpkbDBlRXRGV0xWY0RWeGdBNExjdjlrTkQyaTVmMGtYLWFaYjVPUkMtd2JVODYzYk8tenhqMG5C?oc=5", "source": "lane_blast_ops", "published_at": "2026-10-17T05:51:19+00:00", "text": "Bucheon Factory Fire: 8 Dead as Sparks Ignite Thinner Fumes - 조선일보\nBucheon Factory Fire: 8 Dead as Sparks Ignite Thinner Fumes 조선일보", "truncated": false}, {"id": "4d24e783817f1ece1ad7d413", "title": "The chatbot murder guide: AI’s struggle between privacy, reporting violence", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/17/the-chatbot-murder-guide-ais-struggle-between-privacy-reporting-violence?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-17T05:50:37+00:00", "text": "The chatbot murder guide: AI’s struggle between privacy, reporting violence\nShare The chatbot murder guide: AI’s struggle between privacy, reporting violence on social media\nIn this photo illustration, ChatGPT opened on a computer screen is displayed on September 24, 2026 in Melbourne, Australia [Asanka Ratnayake/Getty Images]\nin almost four decades, Jesse Van Rootselaar was consulting an unlikely adviser on how to perpetrate mass murder.\n“In a hallway, indoors, or a crowded classroom, the 870 [Remington shotgun] is brutal … Close quarters is its playground,” OpenAI’s ChatGPT guided the 18-year-old, according to reports.\nAnthropic AI model submits false homicide tip to Philadelphia police\nEx-OpenAI staff say they were fired for raising safety concerns\nAI deepfake ads grow more popular in US midterm campaigns, blurring truth\nOn February 10, 2026, Van Rootselaar shot his mother and half-brother at home before going to a school in the small British Columbia town of Tumbler Ridge, where he killed another six people – including five students – before taking his own life.\nEarlier this week, on October 14, US authorities charged a Washington State man with allegedly conspiring with Van Rootselaar to plan the attack. But the mass shooting incident has raised questions that go beyond the traditional legal understanding of culpability.\nThe British Columbia government in September filed\nagainst OpenAI and its CEO Sam Altman, accusing them of failing to prevent the incident and claiming they were aware of the conversations that turned deadly. Families of the victims also filed separate lawsuits. OpenAI’s leadership issued a public apology for their failure to notify authorities.\nThe company has not responded to requests from Al Jazeera for its comments on the lawsuits.\nBut as the case against OpenAI progresses, it has spurred a familiar debate: Where should a line be drawn between reporting potentially dangerous behaviour and protecting an individual’s privacy?\n“The question of whether companies should be required to turn such information over to law enforcement is extraordinarily difficult, particularly if they don’t already screen chats for indications of such types of threats,” United States-based civil liberties lawyer Jenin Younes told Al Jazeera.\n“Forcing companies to implement a surveillance system implicates very serious civil liberties concerns, and that’s probably a bridge too far for me,” Younes added.\n“On the other hand, AI gives individuals access to types and quantities of information they didn’t have previously that may facilitate the commission of mass casualty events, a concern it’s not easy to dismiss.”\nAI’s ability to distinguish thoughts from threats\nOne of the biggest challenges facing AI companies is determining whether a user expressing violent thoughts actually intends to act on them.\nIn August, Anthropic, the company behind AI chatbot Claude, flagged a user to San Francisco police after discovering conversations allegedly threatening its CEO, Dario Amodei.\nAccording to the San Francisco Standard, citing a police report, the user had claimed to possess an AR-15 gun and suggested he had Amodei “in his sights”.\nHowever, when contacted by the newspaper, the man said he was joking and had no intention of carrying out any form of violence. The person was neither charged nor detained, the Standard said.\nFazl Barez, a senior researcher at the University of Oxford specialising in AI safety and governance, said current AI models cannot “reliably” determine whether an interaction with an AI chatbot “is purely hypothetical or is likely to lead to harmful behaviour”.\n“Suppose you are a creative writer working on a fictional story and want to explore how a murder plot might unfold. From the context, it may be clear that this is simply a fictional narrative. If the threshold for flagging potentially harmful content is too low, such a writer could be wrongly flagged,” he told Al Jazeera.\n“Similarly, consider a graduate student in biology or chemistry studying pathogens that, if misused, could have serious consequences. They might be trying to understand the risks associated with these pathogens and how to mitigate them. Such discussions may be necessary for legitimate research and education. Even when the subject matter is sensitive, they should not automatically be flagged without considering the broader context.”\nThe researcher said identifying a credible threat may require examining a user’s interactions across multiple conversations, rather than relying on isolated statements.\nBarez said someone experiencing intrusive violent thoughts might turn to a chatbot for support, while another person planning an attack may reveal their intentions gradually across several conversations.\n“Distinguishing between the two requires careful consideration of context, intent and patterns of behaviour, rather than relying on individual statements alone,” he added.\nChinmayi Sharma, a law professor at Fordham University, said the debate has parallels with the duty imposed on mental health professionals to warn identifiable third parties about credible threats.\nHowever, she said there was a key difference – therapists have professional training, knowledge of their patients and enforceable standards governing their conduct. AI companies do not necessarily have comparable safeguards.\n“My concern with AI companies is that right now there’s not a concerted effort to hire mental health professionals, sociologists, individuals that would have the unique expertise on what the indicators of threat are here,” Sharma told Al Jazeera.\n“Secondly, licensed professionals are accountable to concrete, legally enforceable standards in their discipline. We don’t have something like that for AI companies.”\nThe privacy implications extend beyond whether AI companies can accurately identify threats. Millions of people use chatbots to discuss relationships, emotional distress, mental health concerns and deeply personal experiences.\nCivil liberties lawyer Younes said many users may not realise how vulnerable those conversations could be to disclosure.\n“They often reflect a person’s innermost thoughts, questions, and desires. The majority of people assume that these dialogues will never be viewed by another person,” Younes, president and legal director of the American-Arab Anti-Discrimination Committee, said.\nYounes warned that the consequences of greater monitoring could extend well beyond those planning violence.\n“If people fear their chats can be turned over to law enforcement simply because they express disturbing thoughts/violent fantasies etc, many will be dissuaded from using them,” she told Al Jazeera.\nYounes argued that forcing companies to establish systems for monitoring users and reporting conversations could raise serious constitutional concerns in the US, including protections against unreasonable searches.\nMeanwhile, Fordham’s Sharma also raised an issue about who could be disproportionately affected by heavy monitoring by AI chatbots.\n“The concern is always there about either intentional or unintentional targeting, because if you have models trained on what we consider dangerous speech, they’re almost always going to reflect biases,” she said. “Certain minorities’ speech is over‑flagged as a threat, while other comparable speech is not.\n“Even in a world in which everyone is benevolent, we should still be concerned about disparate outcomes,” she said, warning that machines could reproduce such disparities at scale.\n‘Not a licensed therapist’ – When do AI companies become liable to report?\nWhile AI companies may face moral pressure to prevent violence, their legal obligations are less clear, legal experts say.\nBryan Choi, a professor of law at the University of Colorado Boulder, said US law recognises a duty to warn in certain circumstances, including when mental health professionals become aware of imminent threats or when a special relationship exists between parties.\nBut thos", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "f9549aa6df4c058255cc4170", "title": "Missed Out On Micron and Sandisk? Make Sure You Don't Miss Out On This Memory Stock. - Yahoo Finance", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMinAFBVV95cUxQZ2Jxb3pWVFU1YWVFTEdMUWxWQUY3TU01SEhSM3dlQ2xZMW93WUl3eklUSVV3ZTRoQ21ORm1fNTY3UDczOWhOVkVlbXlwNWtObUpKUHdTVUlqWjY2ZkFFWGdsWU5NYkF6VHZraFJuNUhiVGtydUtTSWRJZXdCZjcwS3MwX0tSemtlb0xnRjZ1bUJyOTFlamFzM1RpYi0?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_markets", "published_a