t_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-17T05:01:40+00:00", "text": "Pope's health should not be taken 'for granted', says top Vatican diplomat\nPope Leo pictured arriving at the Gemelli Hospital in Rome for his surgery on Tuesday\nPope Leo XIV's health should not be taken \"for granted\", a British senior Vatican official told the BBC.\nArchbishop Paul Gallagher, the Vatican's foreign minister since 2014, said the news of the Pope's ill health was \"a bit of a game changer\", adding \"it's going to make things a bit different\".\nfollowing surgery to remove a lower lobe of his right lung.\nSpeaking on Tuesday after the Pope's operation was announced, but before his cancer diagnosis, Archbishop Gallagher said: \"I think we were maybe taking him a little bit for granted.\"\nThe 71-year-old had surgery at Rome's Gemelli Hospital on Tuesday afternoon, which the Vatican said had been \"successful\" and proceeded without complications.\nSpeaking to Radio 4's the Sunday Programme, due to air this weekend, the Archbishop said: \"It's in some ways I think going to be a bit of a game changer after having the latter years of Pope Francis when he was in the wheelchair.\n\"And Pope Leo comes along and he's sporty and he seems to be in excellent health, and now we're told that he's not in excellent health and things have got to be sorted out.\"\nAsked how it will make things different going forward, the Archbishop said church figures might have assumed \"that he was not quite like the rest of us who are very aware of our vulnerabilities and the ageing process\".\nHe went on: \"But lots of us have been through similar experiences at different times in our lives, and we've got through them, and so we hope it's going to be the same case with Pope Leo.\n\"I am concerned. I think we've got to ask the question: how was it discovered? What happened?\"\nThe first photo of Pope Leo after surgery has been released, showing him celebrating Mass at hospital\nThe Vatican has said the Pope is showing steady improvement in hospital, and he was expected to lead Sunday prayers.\nThey had stressed that the Pope had shown no symptoms of infection or illness prior to the procedure.\nThe Angelus prayer is traditionally recited at the Vatican, overlooking crowds in St Peter's Square but has sometimes been given from a hospital window by previous popes when necessary.\nNo date has been given for Pope Leo's hospital discharge, but recovery was expected to take around a week.\nPope pictured for first time since surgery and cancer diagnosis\nLeo himself announced the news of his operation during his weekly Sunday prayers, telling the crowd in St Peter's Square that doctors had found a nodule on his lung.\nNodules are quite common and not necessarily cancerous, but after it was examined the medical team confirmed it was, \"as suspected by pre-operative tests\", the Vatican said.\nArchbishop Gallagher, who is from Liverpool, also said he expected the Pope to visit the UK at some point.\n\"We have exchanged a little bit,\" he said. \"He's just beginning to think about what he might do in 2027, but it's still very much at the examining the possibilities (stage)\".\nHe would \"bring a spirit of renewed hope\", said the Archbishop, after the Pope received a\n\"But these things do take quite a lot of planning, and so you don't just do it overnight.\"\nYou can hear more of Archbishop Gallagher's interview on The Sunday Programme on Radio 4 at 0710\nPope's cancer diagnosis leaves Catholics questioning what comes next", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "9b5a5cb00565eea437a4cdf1", "title": "how long has zepbound been fda approved - www.polskieradio.pl", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiiwFBVV95cUxQcG4zRUR4STZYR1VHU3BlNFBKeHBBa2w2OENFeEd2RW9iOXJMYjVKS0pzcGJKNmJ4bWVvbFRYTkVhUExxQWU5cjRnM0k5Nm9pZVN1bC1MXy1IckQ0NF9IUXlVeEVrcThoem15ZmF0dURmRFJ2ZDRzYkFRQ2VyNU4tUlBQUmhNQl81QW13?oc=5", "source": "lane_gate", "published_at": "2026-10-17T05:01:36+00:00", "text": "how long has zepbound been fda approved - www.polskieradio.pl\nhow long has zepbound been fda approved www.polskieradio.pl", "truncated": false}, {"id": "f2bb09b661879dd756161821", "title": "Brazil: Amazon deforestation lowest in nearly 40 years - DW.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMijwFBVV95cUxPZEQyQ3diMWtjYmFLOFBTY1I5OU1veHF4cGFtM1JBNE13QlNtWXIxRENVdlc2UmJqN1BETkJhYjBDcXdHeTZYanZtZTdTeGE1aTdGT09KaGF2aGhUWWcwU3hJaHhLa2pBNmRuZnpJeXprdVpWMnV6MFBlb1JuZWNIQ2VLTXFTSDJrcU1GYm9xMNIBjwFBVV95cUxOOXNya01NSlNpYlBtcWVIUHBZcUxtMXIyZnpZNmFPYTVYYXFXNjh", "source": "rss_google_world", "published_at": "2026-10-17T05:01:23+00:00", "text": "Brazil: Amazon deforestation lowest in nearly 40 years - DW.com\nBrazil: Amazon deforestation lowest in nearly 40 years DW.com Brazil’s Amazon deforestation hits lowest level on record Al Jazeera Brazil sees record fall in Amazon deforestation as key election looms BBC Brazilian Amazon deforestation falls to lowest on record as pivotal vote looms The Guardian Deforestation in Brazil's Amazon falls to all-time low Reuters", "truncated": false}, {"id": "0b7cb70c876dd2a0d7f73061", "title": "Three Mexicans jailed for 158 years each for killing Australian, US surfers", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/17/three-mexicans-jailed-for-158-years-each-for-killing-australian-us-surfers?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-17T05:01:14+00:00", "text": "Three Mexicans jailed for 158 years each for killing Australian, US surfers\nShare Three Mexicans jailed for 158 years each for killing Australian, US surfers on social media\nMembers of the prosecution team arrive at the civil court facilities in Ensenada, Mexico, on October 16, 2026, ahead of the sentencing of three men convicted of murdering Australian and American surfers [AFP]\nThree Mexican men have been sentenced to more than 158 years in prison each for the murder of\ntwo Australian brothers and their American friend\nduring a surf trip on Mexico’s Baja California coast in 2024.\nA Mexican judge in the coastal city of Ensenada, in Baja California state, ruled on Friday that Jesus Gerardo Garcia, alias “El Kekas”, Irineo Francisco “N” and Angel Jesus “N” would each serve 158 years and nine months in prison for homicide, robbery with violence and vehicle theft with violence.\na week ago of the murders of Australian brothers Callum and Jake Robinson, aged 33 and 30, respectively, and their American friend Jack Carter Rhoad, 30.\nThe case impacted Mexico’s tourism industry by exposing the risks foreigners face when visiting the country.\nPreviously, the Baja California State Attorney General’s Office requested the “exemplary sentence” of 210 years in prison for each defendant.\nAntonio Otanez, president of the Baja California surfers’ association, welcomed the outcome, saying “justice has been served.”\nOtanez acknowledged that the killings had caused a “major drop” in tourist visits, particularly by surfers, to the region known for its beautiful beaches.\nThe Robinson brothers and Rhoad travelled to Mexico in late April 2024 to surf at a beach in Punta San Jose, near the popular tourist city of Ensenada.\nThe three friends camped on the beach on the night of April 27, 2024. Authorities launched a multi-day search with help from the US Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) after the men were missing for several days.\nThe bodies were found on May 3 in a well more than 15 metres (50 feet) deep, all with gunshot wounds to their heads.\nLast week, the brothers’ father, Martin Robinson, said the “guilty verdict is a significant moment in our long and painful journey to seek justice for our beautiful sons, Callum and Jake, and their friend Carter Rhoad, whose lives were so cruelly taken from them in 2024.”", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "fe9476d3ed72bbfc7a6e1eb8", "title": "The Israeli politicians seeking to replace Netanyahu", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/17/the-israeli-politicians-seeking-to-replace-netanyahu?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-17T05:00:58+00:00", "text": "The Israeli politicians seeking to replace Netanyahu\nShare The Israeli politicians seeking to replace Netanyahu on social media\nDo any of these Israeli candidates support a Palestinian state?\nOn September 26, five leading members of Israel’s self-styled Zionist opposition signed a non-aggression pact, seeking to oust incumbent Benjamin Netanyahu in the October 27 election.\nGathered at the Tel Aviv home of opposition leader Yair Lapid were his co-runner, former Prime Minister Naftali Bennett, leader of Beyachad; former Chief of Staff Gadi Eisenkot, leader of the new Yashar party; Yair Golan, leader of the Democrats party; and Avigdor Liberman, head of the Yisrael Beiteinu party.\nHow genocidal war on Gaza is shaping Israel’s election\nDespite long history with Netanyahu, Trump hedges as Israel vote nears\nIsraeli opposition signal foreign policy change in style, but not substance\nIf Netanyahu goes, does anything really change for Palestinians?\nIn the statement of principles published after the hours-long meeting, the leaders said, “Despite ideological differences among us, the formation of a Zionist, statesmanlike government dedicated to repair and reconstruction is our shared overarching goal.”\nHowever, how long that pact might last — Liberman has already accused frontrunner Eisenkot of seeking a deal with Netanyahu — how meaningful a change in substance as well as style they offer, and which of them might lead a future coalition, remain\nFrom left, Israeli opposition leaders Yair Lapid, Gadi Eisenkot, Yair Golan, Naftali Bennett and Avigdor Lieberman meet on September 26, 2026 [Courtesy of Yair Lapid via X]\nWith the exception of Golan, all take a fundamentally right-wing approach to Israel’s challenges.\nNone is committed to establishing a Palestinian state, with only the Democrats acknowledging it as a distant, unspecified goal. None, Golan included, accepts that Israel’s war on Gaza amounts to genocide or that it has committed war crimes in killing more than\nthere. Nor has any questioned Israeli military actions against Lebanon, Syria, Yemen, Qatar and Iran, instead reserving criticism mostly for how those actions have allowed Israel to be perceived internationally.\n“The dominant forces in the opposition such as Bennett, Liberman or Eisenkot represent right-wing politics,” said Ahron Bregman, an Israeli analyst and senior teaching fellow at King’s College London. “They are not willing to touch the real, fundamental issue which is at the heart of everything: the Palestinian issue, and the Palestinian aspirations for independence.” He likened them to Netanyahu, but without the corruption allegations and support of far-right figures, such as National Security Minister Itamar Ben-Gvir and Finance Minister Bezalel Smotrich. “Israel as a whole is a right-wing nation,” he added.\nSo, who are the main opposition figures and what do their parties stand for?\nGadi Eisenkot, leader of the Yashar party, delivers a speech [File: Erik Marmor/Getty Images]\nCredited with originating the Dahiyeh Doctrine in 2008, involving “\n” and large-scale destruction of civilian infrastructure against groups such as Hezbollah and Hamas, Eisenkot served as Israeli military chief from 2015 to 2019.\nThe 66-year-old entered politics in 2022 with former Defence Minister Benny Gantz’s National Unity alliance and later\nassembled by Netanyahu after the Hamas-led attacks in southern Israel on October 7, 2023. In December that year, his son Gal, 25, and two nephews were killed while participating in the genocide in Gaza, losses commentators say have helped forge a connection with an electorate that has come to see itself as scarred by war.\n, accusing Netanyahu of lacking a strategy to end the war. A year later, Eisenkot founded the centrist Yashar (Straight/Honest), advocating for professional government, military service for all – including\n– and a state commission of inquiry into the failings before October 7.\nWhile notoriously ambiguous on many of Israel’s foreign policy positions, he has been critical of the government’s reliance upon the United States in achieving its war aims, while not questioning those aims themselves. In June, he characterised Washington’s mediated negotiations with Tehran to end the war against Iran that Washington had embarked upon with Israel as “a deal taking shape far from here and far from Israeli interests” and, the same month, railed against US restrictions on Israel’s freedom of action in Lebanon.\nEisenkot has ruled out including the United Arab List, or Ra’am party, in a post-election coalition. In a September 26\n, he said no Arab party would accept his conditions, including recognition of Israel as a Jewish state.\nFormer Israeli Prime Ministers Naftali Bennett, left, and Yair Lapid, right [File: Abir Sultan/EPA]\nin April 2026 by former Prime Ministers Naftali Bennett and Yair Lapid, who served for a combined 18 months in 2021-2022 between Netanyahu governments.\nBennett, a 54-year-old longtime supporter of illegal settlements in the occupied West Bank, leads the list, with Lapid, the 62-year-old founder of the centrist Yesh Atid party, in second place.\nLapid has called his partner “a right-wing politician, but an honest one”. Neither has prioritised resolving the Israeli-Palestinian conflict or supported a Palestinian state. Though their 2021 coalition included Ra’am, Bennett has acknowledged that this broke an electoral promise.\nLapid has been one of Israel’s most outspoken critics of how its genocide and multiple strikes upon its neighbours are perceived internationally, while rarely questioning the rationale behind those actions. Bennett, in contrast, is more outspoken in his hawkish views. A full-throated supporter of the country’s war on Iran, he also congratulated Israel’s military and security services for a September 2025\non Qatar’s capital, Doha, that targeted senior Hamas leaders as they met to consider a Gaza ceasefire proposal put forth by the US. A Qatari security official was killed in the attack, which\nThey now seek a government composed solely of Zionist parties, also ruling out reliance on Arab parties.\nAvigdor Lieberman speaks at a Yisrael Beiteinu party meeting [File: Amir Levy/Getty Images]\nThe most hawkish voice at Lapid’s table, Lieberman, 68, founded Yisrael Beiteinu (Israel Our Home) in 1999 to represent right-leaning Russian-speaking immigrants.\nOnce Netanyahu’s protege, Lieberman has ruled out serving under him or joining a coalition that includes the ultra-Orthodox or relies on Arab parties, which he calls anti-Zionist. He has also rejected a minority government, reportedly making him a principal obstacle to Eisenkot becoming the bloc’s prime ministerial candidate.\nIn November 2023, Lieberman repeated his 2018 assertion, made then as defence minister, that there were “no innocents in Gaza”. In February, he visited the Karem Abu Salem crossing, known as Kerem Shalom to Israelis, and accused Netanyahu’s government of\nby allowing the minimal humanitarian aid that has been permitted into Gaza.\nThe Yisrael Beiteinu leader has been a consistent supporter of Israel’s genocide and subsequent military actions. In September 2025, he joined other Israeli politicians in\nNetanyahu for the apology to Doha that he had been forced to make by the Trump administration for that month’s strike, saying the apology was instead owed to the Israeli public. Equally, he has argued against any restraint in its invasion of Lebanon, telling the Jerusalem Post in March: “If once again we go for a limited operation, some manoeuvre near the border, maybe talk about a security zone, it’s complete madness.”\n“Lieberman does not belong in this bloc,” said Shaiel Ben-Ephraim, an Israeli analyst with Atlas Global Strategies, arguing that his main motivation was hatred of Netanyahu.\nHowever, Ben-Ephraim said, Lieberman “maintains a loyal base and is a crucial force in Israeli politics”. His core constituency is the former Soviet electorate, but he has gained wider tru", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "9d5bb3c95ae89fc3e93c414e", "title": "Winless White Cloud falls to Hemlock in shutout loss, 49-0 - Local Sports Journal", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMingFBVV95cUxNbVQ2Y1lUbkl4aFRVTHdUTTNscldITEQ0Ym43c0k3ZXBRYXQ5Um12NER6azlFNkFhTDFzVEc4TDN6SUF1dTN5SXlNRURmUHpvOG1MUjZSVXRLRTRPWU93eVpBTHJoZXBsbnVoN1FRSnJCYnpRZHVXWk9PcFAxUGlOZlo4QXlLSWJBUmM5YnlfYkRfMTVhYXMyTE1IU0JnZw?oc=5", "source": "topic_technology", "published_at": "2026-10-17T05:00:46+00:00", "text": "Winless White Cloud falls to Hemlock in shutout loss, 49-0 - Local Sports Journal\nWinless White Cloud falls to Hemlock in shutout loss, 49-0 Local Sports Journal", "truncated": false}, {"id": "f2167088d201bb6410c9cb67", "title": "Some companies give AI to everyone. This CEO is doing the opposite - CNBC", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiekFVX3lxTFBrS2Y3dEp1SVBOMy1Bb290M19VN2JWVS04V2xENXdHU2dPTUY1cnExOUM3UUlJUHJjSkpXOXpIWmlvVFlkUVMxWHpCeUlVV2JWeGFuOEoxUE1aS043VjZkMklWVjZ3cjE3bFNJampEc2NMSWVaWlpJbmhB0gF_QVVfeXFMT1ZnT21FTW1GYU1MQkdmS3JYb1ptTDRJZnpkQ3NPMUdhMWhIN3RzandNbEhpcFNnZHpoOTZnbHdqWUNXRUp", "source": "topic_technology", "published_at": "2026-10-17T05:00:01+00:00", "text": "Some companies give AI to everyone. This CEO is doing the opposite - CNBC\nSome companies give AI to everyone. This CEO is doing the opposite CNBC", "truncated": false}, {"id": "0a29185213a137e541ae2d01", "title": "Trump's Russia diesel deal may offer Putin more than money — and spell trouble for Ukraine - CNBC", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMid0FVX3lxTE4yUlZma192R0hueHd1UmlZMXFPTTNxeGdGeGNNX244YjZoY3VQWHB0WUhRYkZWNFRiWlZDaGhjS0Z5Zlo2YWlxTkp4WG1NazdXZjBLeG1xc0FURWh2SVR2Y1FxMmFDNHJ5ekJibEtrQmU3bzFMVmtr0gF8QVVfeXFMTnJaMmpuYXdDUk5RbkNpaXdRRF91VjhDMW1Hd3paRUs2c2FfeEcxYmhtQXN0ZG1INHN5Qm1yZGwybTRnV1dBaEU", "source": "rss_google_us", "published_at": "2026-10-17T05:00:01+00:00", "text": "Trump's Russia diesel deal may offer Putin more than money — and spell trouble for Ukraine - CNBC\nTrump's Russia diesel deal may offer Putin more than money — and spell trouble for Ukraine CNBC House Democrat introduces bipartisan bill to block Trump’s Russian diesel deal Politico Trump Diesel Deal Faces Fresh Challenge in Congress Bloomberg.com Trump's diesel deal with Putin draws bipartisan criticism from a frustrated Congress PBS Zelenskiy blasts Trump's diesel deal and calls Russian peace moves a 'parody' Reuters", "truncated": false}, {"id": "a07ce5212f0f6be07d47ca9b", "title": "Burnham Finds His Plans to Focus on Britain Blown Up by Trump - Bloomberg.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMisgFBVV95cUxOZnRGOTI1R0lPMmkyMzg1UGdWa2tYd0JYYjc5NnhXUkFjZEhpRFVXNjY5OWVyZ1IxNDJ2d1g4SjQzLTJib3pPRlk5UmNJTEl0Sk1UcHk3YTFIcUJlMUtIdkRhVmd3UVU0aFhJSUZmTWE5RU9ZX0JEUXgxTmZmdFU4SlY3dVNObGdtZUNpMEQ1STdhRGdUc2I4MlhEVkQxd2lEYmlEbVV3Q0JDcW81TTI0RFRB?oc=5", "source": "publisher_bloomberg", "published_at": "2026-10-17T05:00:00+00:00", "text": "Burnham Finds His Plans to Focus on Britain Blown Up by Trump - Bloomberg.com\nBurnham Finds His Plans to Focus on Britain Blown Up by Trump Bloomberg.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "06f5c3b3308e77d07f4ac4c5", "title": "Christine Lagarde: IMFC Statement", "url": "https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261017~7015ea3099.en.html", "source": "official_ecb", "published_at": "2026-10-17T05:00:00+00:00", "text": "Christine Lagarde: IMFC Statement\nStatement by Christine Lagarde, President of the ECB, at the fifty-fourth meeting of the International Monetary and Financial Committee\nThe global economy has proven surprisingly resilient in the face of high uncertainty. The conflict in the Middle East continues to expose energy markets to sharp swings. Trade tensions and protectionist measures are weighing on confidence and contributing to a reconfiguration of trade flows. The global economy has held up thanks in part to the rise of artificial intelligence, with investment in technology supporting activity and trade, particularly in economies integrated into technology value chains. Risks nonetheless remain tilted to the downside for growth and to the upside for inflation. A return to an international economic order grounded in openness, predictability and the rule of law would help trade and investment flourish.\nDespite global headwinds and the ongoing energy shock, the euro area economy has so far proved resilient. Growth was broad-based across most euro area countries and sectors in the first half of 2026. The September ECB staff projections foresee growth of 0.9% in 2026, 1.4% in 2027 and 1.5% in 2028.\nAs the energy shock fades over the medium term, the projections see domestic demand benefitting from a recovery in real income over the medium term, driven by lower energy inflation and a resilient labour market. Rising government spending on infrastructure and defence, complemented by investment in AI and digital technologies, should also support growth. Export growth should pick up on the back of strengthening foreign demand, also driven by AI.\nThe medium-term outlook is surrounded by uncertainty over how intense and long-lasting the Middle East conflict will be, and how the resulting shocks might propagate through the economy. Risks to growth are assessed to be on the downside.\nFiscal sustainability remains a crucial anchor for broader economic stability. It is important to maintain sound fiscal policies, including by adhering to fiscal rules, and, where debt is high, to consolidate, re-prioritise spending and enhance the efficiency and effectiveness of public finances to rebuild buffers. Governments should strive to ensure that any fiscal response to the energy shock is temporary, targeted and tailored.\nIn addition, policies that strengthen resilience to climate change can improve the economy’s ability to absorb climate-related shocks. These efforts should be complemented by structural policies that promote labour market participation, equip the workforce with the skills needed in an AI-driven economy and facilitate the diffusion of new technologies. Priorities include advancing the savings and investments union, improving access to risk capital and creating better conditions for innovative firms to scale up across the Single Market.\nHeadline inflation increased to 3.8% in September, mostly reflecting a surge in energy inflation, and, to a lesser extent, an increase in food and services inflation. Core inflation increased slightly to 2.5%. The range of underlying inflation measures remained broadly stable in September and wages have so far shown no material response to the energy shock.\nLooking ahead, higher energy prices are expected to keep headline inflation well above target into the first half of 2027 and to gradually feed through to core and food inflation. Headline inflation is expected to decline after the first quarter as energy inflation falls, returning to around target towards the end of 2027. The risks to the inflation outlook are to the upside due, in particular, to the Middle East conflict and Russia’s unjustified war against Ukraine. A more intense or persistent energy shock could strengthen indirect and second-round effects, resulting in higher and more persistent inflation. Extreme weather could push up food prices. By contrast, a sustainable resolution of geopolitical conflicts, weaker energy pass-through or more risk-averse financial markets could lower inflation.\nThe Governing Council decided to raise the key ECB interest rates by 25 basis points in September, following an increase of the same size in June.\nOur interest rate decisions are based on our reaction function, which comprises three criteria: our assessment of the inflation outlook and the risks surrounding it, in light of the incoming economic and financial data, the dynamics of underlying inflation; and the strength of monetary policy transmission.\nThe inflation outlook points to higher inflation ahead, especially in the short term, given the latest developments in energy markets. However, we see no evidence at this stage of significant pass-through of energy prices to other prices and wages, beyond what we have already included in our projections. And while growth has been resilient, financing conditions have tightened, with long-term interest rates rising notably since our last meeting. This will slow growth and reduce pass-through by more than projected in our September exercise.\nTaking these three criteria together, the energy shock is too large to “look through” but a measured response remains appropriate to keep inflation in check.\nIn setting future policy, we will make a comprehensive assessment of the incoming data based on our reaction function. In July, the Governing Council decided on the operational features and the onboarding process for the enhanced EUREP, the Eurosystem repo facility for central banks that was announced earlier this year. The new facility supports the smooth transmission of monetary policy and reinforces the international role of the euro. More than 30 central banks have expressed interest in EUREP in the past six months.\nSources of risk and vulnerabilities for euro area financial stability remain high. Continued geopolitical tensions, fiscal challenges and uncertainties around accelerated AI adoption continue to be causes for concern. The widespread concentration of critical third-party service providers introduces single points of failure for both financial and non-financial institutions. New frontier AI models have increased cyber risks and operational vulnerabilities.\nEuro area banks maintain strong capital and liquidity buffers and robust profitability, together with healthy and stable asset quality. These solid fundamentals should help navigate ongoing geopolitical uncertainties and effectively support lending to the economy. Strengthened prudential regulation and supervision since the global financial crisis has helped leave euro area banks in a strong position to withstand the unfolding economic challenges.\nShocks could expose vulnerabilities among non-bank financial intermediaries, notably from concentrated AI-related exposures, amplifying market stress and creating spillovers across financial sectors. Banks’ exposures to non-banks and vulnerable firms could expose funding and credit vulnerabilities.\nStress in private credit markets has continued, with private credit funds reporting outflows and reactivating redemption gates. Private market “semi-liquid funds” that allow for redemptions are becoming more prevalent, which could lead to greater liquidity mismatches in the future, but euro area financial institutions have limited direct exposure to private credit. This means, for now, that private credit in isolation is unlikely to be a source of financial instability in the euro area.\nCentral bank money must evolve to fulfil the needs of an increasingly digital economy. The Eurosystem is at the forefront of this evolution, bringing the stability and trust of central bank money to digital retail payments and tokenised finance in Europe. In the retail space we are preparing to complement cash with its digital equivalent – the digital euro – for day-to-day payments. The digital euro will further stimulate innovation and competition in retail payments, ensuring that the continued co-existence of public and private solutions underpins Europe’", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "d45b26dbb3fbd4a8baf432b9", "title": "Trump waives Russia sanctions as rift with Zelenskyy widens", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/10/17/trump-waives-russia-sanctions-as-rift-with-zelenskyy-widens?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-17T04:58:32+00:00", "text": "Trump waives Russia sanctions as rift with Zelenskyy widens\nTrump waives Russia sanctions as rift with Zelenskyy widens | Russia-Ukraine war News | Al Jazeera\nTrump waives Russia sanctions as rift with Zelenskyy widens\nUS President Donald Trump has waived more sanctions on Russia in a bid to ease energy prices, leading to a chorus of condemnation from Ukraine and EU allies. The executive order comes as the rift between Trump and Ukraine’s Zelenskyy widens.\nShare Trump waives Russia sanctions as rift with Zelenskyy widens on social media", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "18c617845c74641d0afd8696", "title": "Trump signs order waiving Graham Act sanctions on Russia - ABC News - Breaking News, Latest News and Videos", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiowFBVV95cUxNaExZUlBtbDZ3YXN2cGZ0Z0RMRVcxT3hlaG8xMnRiSm16d0pSdnBHUzJzSHB1YVVFUXNSMUFGTXZHRE4yb3JyTHczam54WFAxQjRLSjcwTlBuY0pKUGVTbGtjdE5PWVlSdXVFZ1FtUlBLXy1DZUhYRVhEOEZnTHJZTWZQb2J0ejZ3bG9DU0g0cnYyVU9XUllQenJTVkdJc2xucG5r0gGoAUFVX3lxTE00QU9Jbml5eUQxemhUMzRMLVh", "source": "topic_geopolitics", "published_at": "2026-10-17T04:57:49+00:00", "text": "Trump signs order waiving Graham Act sanctions on Russia - ABC News - Breaking News, Latest News and Videos\nTrump signs order waiving Graham Act sanctions on Russia ABC News - Breaking News, Latest News and Videos", "truncated": false}, {"id": "a9d296738cfb722024821c0d", "title": "Faculty and staff at City Colleges of Chicago vote 96 percent to strike; walkout set for October 19 - World Socialist Web Site", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiaEFVX3lxTFAwektvaHBCX2tsZ2lEUXFWa1BkRS02NHdLRWNobmhMYUZOWHJtcFZORnBnVHhJSElrZl9ZaUE3LV9NbnlmRGFLcmVRU1k0bURORVp0MzJiVGpwMF9GaTFpeVk5Q1BVd1k0?oc=5", "source": "lane_labor_stop", "published_at": "2026-10-17T04:57:31+00:00", "text": "Faculty and staff at City Colleges of Chicago vote 96 percent to strike; walkout set for October 19 - World