ZVZkl4VXRMYWFQYmJiRkNsYU8xUTN0aVo2cGJWWFJkQ1VKUmIwajBEYXpDeWRZR1JJcjBUT1BtVDd2c2p0LUlmWFZhQklieGtSMU1EN0JNM1RRSm1oX0Z2eWdFcjJ6U0VLcjNoaXRMcVlROWdjX2VSZTR", "source": "lane_regime_state", "published_at": "2026-10-17T02:49:57+00:00", "text": "AI trading fears and oil prices drive U.S. market sell-off; semiconductor stocks fall for eighth consecutive day - KuCoin\nAI trading fears and oil prices drive U.S. market sell-off; semiconductor stocks fall for eighth consecutive day KuCoin", "truncated": false}, {"id": "3ec5d5c82ee06dac21196bba", "title": "JLP Holds Onto Leadership Of Clarendon Municipal Corporation Via Injunction - Radio Jamaica News Online", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMitwFBVV95cUxNbEFTSTdIb0dpcUlWZWRvUjVhN3MxcklMZktTaVlzRHpKS041eHNKY3dfZzBUTV9zS2RpRUNtVGZ1Vnl6WkFTXzVvN0pQc0IyV0xWTXBqRDJoTTA2cUJEbmhxTDBZb1ZCSDBPOWdZVjlmaVBsVHh3cC1MUzNTZXFtb1oxQ29aazFmTm1mNUFhaG5Pd0tROGkxNkxZSjk2ZG5vZTJFcEtLMlJEd0tlQVlSUlVvbXNFYU0?oc=5", "source": "lane_blast_legal", "published_at": "2026-10-17T02:49:31+00:00", "text": "JLP Holds Onto Leadership Of Clarendon Municipal Corporation Via Injunction - Radio Jamaica News Online\nJLP Holds Onto Leadership Of Clarendon Municipal Corporation Via Injunction Radio Jamaica News Online", "truncated": false}, {"id": "b793bc2d60c87cbe59e85cae", "title": "Brazil’s Amazon deforestation hits lowest level on record", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/17/brazils-amazon-deforestation-hits-lowest-level-on-record?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-17T02:49:21+00:00", "text": "Brazil’s Amazon deforestation hits lowest level on record\nShare Brazil’s Amazon deforestation hits lowest level on record on social media\nA helicopter is seen flying over an area affected by fires in the Amazon rainforest in Autazes, in the state of Amazonas, northern Brazil, on October 15, 2026 [Michael Dantas/AFP]\nDeforestation in Brazil’s Amazon rainforest has fallen to its lowest level since records began in 1988, according to official figures that give a boost to President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva days before a presidential run-off.\nBrazil’s space research agency, Inpe, said on Friday that 3,335 square kilometres (1,288 square miles) of forest were cleared in the 12 months through July, about 42 percent less than the year before.\nLula says Brazil cannot ‘accept treatment’ after new US tariffs proposed\nFlavio Bolsonaro asks Trump to delay tariffs on Brazil until after election\nUS tariffs ‘strengthen’ Lula against Bolsonaro’s son in upcoming elections\nThe previous record low was 4,571 sq km (1,765 sq miles) in 2011-12, while annual losses peaked at nearly 30,000 sq km (nearly 11,600 sq miles) in the mid-1990s, according to Inpe’s Prodes satellite programme, which measures from August to July because cloud cover is lower in mid-year.\n“It’s an absolutely stratospheric drop…it’s like a dream,” Environment Minister Joao Paulo Capobianco said at a news conference in Brasilia.\nMarcio Astrini of the climate advocacy group Observatorio do Clima called the figures “a historic achievement” and said they show that “with political will, it is possible to curb deforestation and greenhouse gas emissions”.\nPresident Lula wrote on X: “Our goal is to achieve zero illegal deforestation by 2030.”\nby 2030, after clearing soared to a 15-year high under his far-right predecessor, Jair Bolsonaro. The Amazon lost about 11,600 sq km (about 4,480 sq miles) a year on average during Bolsonaro’s 2019-2022 term, almost 60 percent more than in the previous four years.\nDeforestation has fallen every year since 2022 and is down 71.2 percent over four years. The AFP news agency attributes the drop to greater monitoring and stiffer penalties for illegal logging.\nLula faces Bolsonaro’s eldest son, Senator Flavio Bolsonaro, in the election run-off on October 25, with polls showing them neck and neck.\nFlavio pledged in his manifesto to strengthen environmental oversight in the Amazon, focus enforcement on organised crime rather than extractive workers and small farmers, and end “illegal deforestation” by 2029.\nHe has also praised his father, who is serving a 27-year sentence under house arrest for coup plotting, for opposing what he calls a “radical environmental agenda”.\nDeforestation in the Cerrado savanna, south of the Amazon, also fell by about 24 percent, to 5,517 sq km (2,130 sq miles), the lowest since records began in 2001.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "cde30690af4aab0a4287ec02", "title": "Earthquake of magnitude 4.1 hits Himachal Pradesh’s Shimla - The Times of India", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiywFBVV95cUxNbTBXM2I5V0p4TGtLcjBmNDFMSjBsRGJNd1RXSlNIZG9EUDlKeGJaaTdXYTdXM3JhcmE3MTc4bkEySk9LNGJQY2RuQjNFV3VxN19KR1NVcU4wZ19xdnZja3JtMHJWWTdrYV85LTdNN1VfX1lGck1NOVN4TnBvTzNmTy12WWVhUEdSaU8zSDdJaktDSVVVaVROaktVY1lQVzNtcEpWWVlsS3c2RS03UUVZdklHQXlha2MxYkhMWXpIcU5", "source": "lane_cat_weather", "published_at": "2026-10-17T02:49:00+00:00", "text": "Earthquake of magnitude 4.1 hits Himachal Pradesh’s Shimla - The Times of India\nEarthquake of magnitude 4.1 hits Himachal Pradesh’s Shimla The Times of India", "truncated": false}, {"id": "c7792f3d3804be9bed90d7cc", "title": "LNG Energy Group Raises Over C$1 Million as Second Private Placement Tranche Closes - The Globe and Mail", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi9gFBVV95cUxOOEpRelVyQ0ZjNHRqLVVzLUZsSzZZbnFUcXRDcGJEMk1XSFJWaWVOVVRyMDVRaTNxR0lPeDA4T2dPOFFFbUtTRVBDZ0ZMODhDdm4xVnozYlFsbkQwNk1nODhXMEVjM2xrUng5dkxDU253YUdkYWRzcGpYbHdFRzZiMHV5T1pOZk5XTkNNYl9Ud3lBZXRkU0QzdWZ4Nzl2YVh2QmtmTkdxWU5OWE5HbWVpQkplUlp5bXJEWklfU0xnMm9", "source": "lane_dilution", "published_at": "2026-10-17T02:48:54+00:00", "text": "LNG Energy Group Raises Over C$1 Million as Second Private Placement Tranche Closes - The Globe and Mail\nLNG Energy Group Raises Over C$1 Million as Second Private Placement Tranche Closes The Globe and Mail", "truncated": false}, {"id": "dbcea115daf8788923edb890", "title": "Trump waives several Russia sanctions as new US law takes effect - BBC", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiXkFVX3lxTE5BUDRsUjRjXzRSYldaUFpxVHhxTDF3WmxrU2hyRk9zT091TFhBQnlxWG9rQ2Vhb19FaEtxLVVhVTBUME5LWUZ4V2tWMzk5RVkxeDR4T0xBbkRONThHQlE?oc=5", "source": "topic_geopolitics", "published_at": "2026-10-17T02:48:36+00:00", "text": "Trump waives several Russia sanctions as new US law takes effect - BBC\nTrump waives several Russia sanctions as row with Zelensky intensifies BBC", "truncated": false}, {"id": "b677fdedabaf44798b79a308", "title": "Trump waives several Russia sanctions as new US law takes effect", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cxr5yvzv7rp0o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-17T02:48:36+00:00", "text": "Trump waives several Russia sanctions as new US law takes effect\nDonald Trump has blocked numerous sanctions on Russia two days before they were due to take effect, as a row with his Ukrainian counterpart Volodymyr Zelensky deepens.\nThe sanctions were passed by the US Congress and signed into law by the US president in September.\nBut Trump issued an executive order on Friday night saying that suspending some was in the US national interest, prompting a backlash from US lawmakers. Zelensky said it was unfortunate the US has decided to \"completely disregard\" the sanctions.\nRussian envoy Kirill Dmitriev said Trump's order was \"an important and truly historic moment\", and that the sanctions would have inflicted \"serious damage\" on the US and global market.\nRelations between Washington and Kyiv frayed after\nthe US struck a controversial deal to import Russian diesel\nlast week. Ukraine has accused the Trump administration of withdrawing key military support.\nSince then, Ukraine's president declared it was nice to be among \"real leaders\" while addressing an EU summit, after Trump said Zelensky was \"more difficult to deal with\" than Russian President Vladimir Putin.\nThe sanctions suspension seemed to add further fuel to the fire.\nZelensky said on Saturday morning that the sanctions \"could have helped enormously\" and that the law was \"supposed to impose truly effective pressure on Russia to bring this war to an end\".\nHe pointed to Russian overnight attacks on 15 Ukrainian energy facilities that had caused power cuts in several regions and said: \"Russia is trying to cause a blackout in Ukraine â right before winter.\"\nZelensky said it was \"important that everyone in America also sees what is really happening\" on the ground.\nTrump denies limiting Ukraine access to Starlink network\nTrump 'completely wrong' to restrict support for Ukraine, Italian minister tells BBC\nThe new US sanctions law took aim at Russia's economy by giving Trump broad powers to levy tariffs of up to 100% on imports from the top five purchasers of Russian oil and gas â most significantly China and India.\nIt also included sanctions against senior Russian figures, including Putin, as well as Russian financial institutions and Moscow's so-called \"shadow fleet\" of vessels used to circumvent existing embargoes.\nUS Secretary of State Marco Rubio said that the suspension would consist of a 60-day pause to allow for the Trump administration to analyse the impact they might have on the US and global economy.\nThe suspension drew criticism from US politicians from both main parties.\nDemocrats including the Virginia Congressman Don Beyer criticised the move, accusing Trump of capitulating to Moscow and turning his back on Kyiv and Zelensky.\nA handful of Republicans also came out against the move.\nTexas Senator Ted Cruz, a Trump ally, called waiving the sanctions a mistake, telling Fox News that there were other ways to address fuel prices, while the state's other Senatory John Cornyn called the move \"reckless\".\nNorth Carolina Senator Thom Tillis called it \"beyond irresponsible\", arguing that Trump could bring energy prices down by ending the Iran war instead of \"doing a solid\" for Putin.\nNebraska Representative Don Bacon indicated that US lawmakers will now resist funding Trump's agenda.\nWatch: Will Ukraine get the support needed as tensions with US rise?\nZelensky has long stressed the need for stringent sanctions to limit funding that Russia can use in its war effort.\nHe told reporters on Saturday that he believed the Trump administration would lift more sanctions against Russia.\nZelensky and Ukraine's European allies initially criticised Trump for his plans to import Russian diesel in the hope of bringing down US energy prices, which have skyrocketed during the Iran war.\nTrump has spent months grappling with the political consequences of rising fuel costs ahead of the US midterm elections, with inflation souring his standing with the American public.\nZelensky suggested the deal was tantamount to the US investing in Russia's war effort.\nA few days later, Trump claimed that both Russia and Ukraine had agreed to an \"energy ceasefire\" to facilitate the export of Russian diesel. Both nations have been intensively striking one another's energy infrastructure.\nZelensky expressed surprise at the announcement, and said Kyiv would stop the strikes if Russia did the same. After three days of fresh attacks from Russia, Kyiv resumed its strikes on Russian oil refineries.\nTrump condemned the Ukrainian strikes as a disgrace, but Zelensky said he was \"only standing up for Ukraine's interests\".\nZaporizhzhia was among regions hit by Russian strikes overnight\nConcerns that Trump is siding with Russia have been heightened by disruption to a US-provided satellite system used by Ukraine.\nStarlink suddenly and unexpectedly restricted\nthe speed at which drones could fly while still maintaining internet connection. This disrupted some battlefield missions, Ukraine said, and hampered Kyiv's ability to protect itself from incoming Russian attacks.\nZelensky told reporters on Saturday that Ukraine had been told by US officials the speed restrictions were linked to technical issues.\nHowever, he said Ukraine had tested Starlink on EU territory and found \"these problems do not exist\" and the system was operating at higher speeds.\nTrump has denied deliberately slowing down the network and denied another accusation by Zelensky that US had withdrawn intelligence-sharing.\nUkrainian authorities said that at least nine people have been killed and around 60 injured in Russian strikes across the country since Friday. That included an ambulance driver killed and 20 others injured in a Russian strike on the city of Kherson, they said.\nAdditional reporting by Helen Sullivan and Sareen Habeshian\nZelensky says Ukraine has right to defend itself after Trump calls latest strikes a 'disgrace'\nTrump faces growing backlash over plans to import diesel from Russia\nTrump signs sweeping Russia sanctions bill\nRussia accuses Ukraine of attacking oil refinery after Zelensky says strikes to resume", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "e3de4adb87a2905f8d75831b", "title": "High Roller Restates Q1 2026, Cuts Net Loss by $1.0 Million on Warrant Accounting - Minichart", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMivAFBVV95cUxQNHNubW01aFdsMHZJYlA0el91NFkxVUlTUmpXNmJoMWRXTnQ1YklSRU9QZ2R6RVVwVEJ1bHpsRkZzcEN5dWJVTmJLRWJyeTVoejExbzIza2hxSTVOWkpUekhDdlN0OWtXRGROeUNQeG9MSUc4allmd2VNTEZFU0RGV2o5aF94NU42ZHluN3hXSldVc2VncENOdkpIamcwcDQwMUFoOFlhR2xmYlJWazVoQXhQbDZEWEhPdWZDUA?oc=5", "source": "lane_integrity", "published_at": "2026-10-17T02:48:00+00:00", "text": "High Roller Restates Q1 2026, Cuts Net Loss by $1.0 Million on Warrant Accounting - Minichart\nHigh Roller Restates Q1 2026, Cuts Net Loss by $1.0 Million on Warrant Accounting Minichart", "truncated": false}, {"id": "6dc77b04b357e58c4049276b", "title": "Magical ‘Wildwood’ portal opens up at Powell’s Books ahead of film debut - AOL.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihwFBVV95cUxQYTVNSGtYMHB