": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiVkFVX3lxTE9oTlJtZFQ5N281MnBuWlhqS0o1ZHg5NnhLWDByWlNCbEFRaXU4Y2ZhOXpJbkczVW15dGxKN1hxcWF4endhMzBWVlVfMVBGVmhpeHVkTkx30gFWQVVfeXFMT2hOUm1kVDk3bzUycG5aWGpLSjVkeDk2eEtYMHJaU0JsQVFpdThjZmE5ekluRzNVbXl0bEo3WHFxYXh6d2EzMFZWVV8xUEZWaGl4dWROTHc?oc=5", "source": "lane_listing_flow", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:44:00+00:00", "text": "Nasdaq Sued Over Delisting Of Social Media Co. - Law360\nNasdaq Sued Over Delisting Of Social Media Co. Law360", "truncated": false}, {"id": "6fde37afe6a4e44df7de4d0e", "title": "What Are Starbucks Stock Bulls Not Worried About? - Yahoo Finance", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMinwFBVV95cUxOWlI4YUlpR1lkNXdPZVA4NXRCcUNWMDFES2Z3MVB2cjV2TDE5RzJHNUh0Z1N6TVRESFFNbnY4WmdxdzdDZjFTeGFlUUdXTFdEeXVOZXFHR2ZFUWp6VWVDYnNJaWVZWmdZb2dTR3lBeEZrcWFrSnhpd0FJY3BTanJkRFU1aFhiZFJYekxrN3B5UWJTWGtScURfOUN3SU9QNlU?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_markets", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:44:00+00:00", "text": "What Are Starbucks Stock Bulls Not Worried About? - Yahoo Finance\nWhat Are Starbucks Stock Bulls Not Worried About? Yahoo Finance", "truncated": false}, {"id": "5c1c5c8113485db8a599878e", "title": "Kospi slump drives forced sell-offs as Koreans shift to bonds and U.S. stocks - CHOSUNBIZ - Chosunbiz", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMigAFBVV95cUxQMW9PV0hjQWFXQkFremNuTTQtaEJpTlFwSjBKcno0YlJsNmxocFh3WTBwazFrSG9NR0RJRFNyUnlmbWZmNmRzd1EtU19PWnZrWWNDZGlOMlRrR2s4V0tQaVNsRnJuazlZV1J0bExkU2NUcEFrREFXWTh0ejJydndfMdIBlAFBVV95cUxPRWx6dmtHVUdKMEZrTEVBUklBN211WkNBelNFSUY0Q2F2ZHMzaXlMQUw5ZFFiLTdVY3NqWHh", "source": "lane_flow_forced_liq", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:43:00+00:00", "text": "Kospi slump drives forced sell-offs as Koreans shift to bonds and U.S. stocks - CHOSUNBIZ - Chosunbiz\nKospi slump drives forced sell-offs as Koreans shift to bonds and U.S. stocks - CHOSUNBIZ Chosunbiz", "truncated": false}, {"id": "88386493be35012956f71b20", "title": "A thousand slogans echoed here - but it could be the end for iconic Delhi protest site", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6kgqjvdv4n2o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:41:58+00:00", "text": "A thousand slogans echoed here - but it could be the end for iconic Delhi protest site\nThe \"cockroach\" demonstrations over the summer of 2026 could well become the last time Jantar Mantar was home to a sustained protest movement\nTo raise your voice against injustice? To draw attention to a cause? Or to make those in power uncomfortable enough to listen?\nFor more than three decades, Indians have tried to do all three in a narrow, tree-lined road near Jantar Mantar, an 18th Century astronomical obervatory in Delhi.\nThis week, Delhi Police said Jantar Mantar, located close to parliament, would no longer be a designated protest site.\nThe decision comes months after the Gen Z-led Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) - not a political party but a collective\n- led a massive protest there demanding the resignation of federal education minister Dharmendra Pradhan. Tens of thousands turned up and Pradhan subsequently resigned.\nPolice barricaded Jantar Mantar soon afterwards, preventing further demonstrations. Last week, an attempt by CJP supporters to march there, this time demanding the resignation of India's election chief,\nProtesters have now been directed to three other sites. Two are far from parliament, while the third, the sprawling Ramlila Ground, is often booked months in advance. Unlike Jantar Mantar, which is free, all three charge tens of thousands of rupees a day.\nit acted after the Supreme Court suggested identifying alternative sites with adequate infrastructure. But critics say the move could push protests beyond the reach of ordinary Indians, further from the seat of power and into venues many cannot afford.\nOver the years, Jantar Mantar has witnessed everything from mass movements that shook governments to small, lonely demonstrations by people desperate to be heard. Farmers, students, grieving parents and ex-soldiers have gathered under its trees, holding placards and shouting slogans. Not every protest made headlines, but for many, it was a place to turn to when other doors had closed.\nThis week, when the BBC visited, those scenes seemed a distant memory.\nJantar Mantar is an 18th Century astronomical observatory in Delhi - the protests were held not in it, but in the road next to it\nYellow barricades blocked both ends of the road, with hundreds of paramilitary personnel standing guard, batons in hand.\nIn a nearby lane, uniformed men sat in shops drinking tea or scrolling through their phones. At one of the few shops without policemen, the shopkeeper folded his hands and said he did not want to answer any questions.\nBuilt by Maharaja Sawai Jai Singh II in the 1720s, Jantar Mantar was once better known as a tourist attraction than a place where a thousand revolutions were imagined.\nIts story as a protest site began in 1993, when the then-Congress government stopped allowing demonstrations at the Boat Club lawns opposite the old parliament.\nHistorian Harleen Kaur has traced how, from the 1960s onwards, successive governments brought in new rules that shifted the site of protest in Delhi further away from parliament.\n\"Jantar Mantar, situated 3km (1.86 miles) from the Parliament House, [allowed the government] to contain protesting crowds at some distance from the Parliament House and PM's residence,\" she wrote in\nYet, the road saw countless spirited protests over the years.\nRachna Dhingra, an activist with the Bhopal Group for Information and Action, an advocacy group for survivors of a 1984 gas leak disaster, camped at Jantar Mantar with hundreds of others for around five months in 2008, trying to get the government to agree to demands including setting up a commission to allocate resources for rehabilitation schemes.\nThrough a scorching summer, they held press conferences, met politicians and even chained themselves to railings opposite then Prime Minister Manmohan Singh's residence to draw attention to their cause.\nIt was also where she found solidarity with others - Tibetans protesting against China, activists opposing the displacement of people over large dam projects and even just lone protesters who caught a train from their home towns with nothing but a placard and a grievance.\n\"It can be lonely to protest but that place became a home for so many of us. We shared each other's stories, struggles and victories,\" says Dhingra.\nIn 2008, Rachna Dhingra and other activists camped for months at Jantar Mantar demanding justice for survivors of the deadly 1984 gas leak in Bhopal - when it rained, they sheltered under tarpaulin sheets\nDipa Sinha, an academic associated with the Right to Food campaign, has seen ground-breaking laws - a landmark\n, the National Food Safety Act and Forest Rights Act - take shape at Jantar Mantar.\n\"It was a place where you could discuss issues with people who came to Delhi from across the country,\" she says.\nProtesters would demonstrate outside government offices, including the erstwhile Planning Commission and the agriculture ministry, where they were often detained by police, before returning to Jantar Mantar to \"gather, discuss and strategise\".\nBut sustaining a protest for months was not easy.\nDhingra says they sheltered under tarpaulin sheets when it rained, used public toilets near the site and went to nearby gurdwaras to freshen up.\nIn 2017, the National Green Tribunal cited the lack of infrastructure, along with complaints from locals, to have Jantar Mantar shut down for protests.\nA year later, the Supreme Court overturned this, citing the rights to free speech and peaceful assembly guaranteed under the constitution.\nWhen asked whether the proximity to parliament made a difference to their cause, Dhingra quoted the reactions of her co-protesters who had come to Delhi for the first time.\n\"That's the president's house? Does the prime minister live there? How can they not listen to us when we are at their gates?\" she remembered them saying.\nSinha points out that Jantar Mantar was also near institutions like the Press Club and Constitution Club, making it easier to get media coverage, and for politicians to drop by and meet protesters.\nIt was a simpler time - sometimes they would ask for permission from police only on the morning of the protest.\nIn recent years, when Dhingra tried to protest at Jantar Mantar, she was told no-one could stay the night.\n\"Where do you go with hundreds of people who have travelled all the way to the capital to protest?\" she asks.\nIn 2006, Narmada Bachao Andolan (Save the Narmada river movement) leader Medha Patkar (grey sari), went on a 20-day hunger strike at Jantar Mantar\nIn 2015, retired soldiers held protests at Jantar Mantar against the delay in the implementation of a pension scheme\nWhile Jantar Mantar has its supporters, others share memories of an even more vibrant protest culture.\nMary E John, a gender studies scholar who lived in Delhi both as a student and as an academic, says that before 1993, she attended protests at the Boat Club on issues ranging from workers' rights and women's issues to topics of international significance.\n\"We lived in a climate where protest was part of life. In fact, we took it for granted,\" she says.\nWhen the spacious Boat Club lawns made way for Jantar Mantar, it actually felt \"claustrophobic\", she said.\nShe pointed out that they, and others before and after them, protested at multiple sites - universities, colleges, streets - instead of just at Jantar Mantar.\nwho camped at Delhi's borders for months, and people\nwho sat at the Shaheen Bagh neighbourhood\ndemanding the repeal of a controversial citizenship law.\nEven the 2012 anti-rape protests, she points out, were not restricted to Jantar Mantar but almost reached the Indian president's heavily guarded complex and beyond into other parts of Delhi.\nIt's not clear what Jantar Mantar's future is - the Supreme Court is hearing pleas challenging its removal as a protest site.\nOpposition leader Rahul Gandhi and CJP leaders have said that Jantar Mantar belongs to the people and will be \"reclaimed\".\nAs the debate ", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "389ee7a6748e74e95cce2d91", "title": "New York City Public Schools banning AI use through middle school starting this year - ABC News - Breaking News, Latest News and Videos", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMinAFBVV95cUxPQVFUVERKNGMyTDlwWUQ2Z29SYldYc1pyRHZvZHNFXzhsRFVROWE2T05vZ3lfLUE3Nm5tYWtxbU9fZVhIMUNXWFZ0aktrTHBUNHlFQll2QW94ZGdBbWFUT0tUcElMT1NMb2VkNDM3NkN4ZGYtb3E0eThhVHhsbkZkZEJLM2RSM2d1M3piNThHQWp4QzRDZjJfdl9qM0nSAaIBQVVfeXFMTl9KZ3YzOW9vdV9PcWhjTmV6bDZxTjZZYmR", "source": "topic_technology", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:41:35+00:00", "text": "New York City Public Schools banning AI use through middle school starting this year - ABC News - Breaking News, Latest News and Videos\nNew York City Public Schools banning AI use through middle school starting this year ABC News - Breaking News, Latest News and Videos", "truncated": false}, {"id": "60d0f45eb475ce790d25f515", "title": "Groups seek injunction over Camp East Montana conditions - KTSM 9 News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiaEFVX3lxTE9hMUQ2MVhPVk1NRDRhNlR0SXdoMERrMkgxcE1FUzlGekdOc0pkczVFV0pTZktRTG1wc05WWGpxRXludG4xWVZCeEE4RWhteS1aSVB1bzVEaVVIWF9Xc0tDenJNY1RKQWVM0gFuQVVfeXFMTkttWFNSdjNmRGVZZS1fZzNUbEk2RkhxN0dTeGJucHd3dnpMQkVtTVdCdzUwWDNNZWVJb3E0c0RqZHBnRDFPMXItdWRzaVhlbEZZZndPX3I", "source": "lane_blast_legal", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:41:18+00:00", "text": "Groups seek injunction over Camp East Montana conditions - KTSM 9 News\nGroups seek injunction over Camp East Montana conditions KTSM 9 News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "fb7df1154492f941a64bb0f2", "title": "Trump waives some sanctions, restrictions, duties on Russia in Graham Act - TradingView", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi2AFBVV95cUxPM1BDOF9QcGhRRXk1dldJRkJOVkR2VU1BYlMxLUNQcEgxeTdMdUFBSElzSjdXZ3pvMW9xVG1IaGJGQUJwUWpBWGEzcm1iUjA4NlJqbGd5eXlCbXdBbmpnWmFVMHJzU0lLUHNnWWxwdFV6cnVWaFFxUHlmalVwMGxESnFqUkF3Z3llbzFSWENYZmxQOVJaVEdxZzk4eENuQ3ZPblp1alRXN3F6OTgwUzZ1N2c3R2RPTmE4RG9DSlZkY1Z", "source": "topic_geopolitics", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:41:00+00:00", "text": "Trump waives some sanctions, restrictions, duties on Russia in Graham Act - TradingView\nTrump waives some sanctions, restrictions, duties on Russia in Graham Act TradingView", "truncated": false}, {"id": "fbc7a8e964033caaf97a7ae7", "title": "'Bully': Feud erupts between Trump and Ontario's Doug Ford over US-Canada trade war - ABC News - Breaking News, Latest News and Videos", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimAFBVV95cUxORng2eU5QQWk1a21qSHlmMjZGR1ExSDh6NXAyNW1HZkd3ZE9HWl81SFRyT0xEcjJQTVg4bFBoT3ZMekNwN3pHd1cxZXBvSndhRkpseFZOM2hpalJidkd4VUl6bUpuTHN6cFgzUkszSVdzeFdLYlZ4NTJ5TFozNWYxdG5sWVhYaVJSN1hydWk3RzlERE1HNHVGUNIBngFBVV95cUxNWmljYjB6Vms3NUV3Qzg0MWxQcUo5VFI2RzFTTzN", "source": "topic_geopolitics", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:40:18+00:00", "text": "'Bully': Feud erupts between Trump and Ontario's Doug Ford over US-Canada trade war - ABC News - Breaking News, Latest News and Videos\n'Bully': Feud erupts between Trump and Ontario's Doug Ford over US-Canada trade war ABC News - Breaking News, Latest News and Videos", "truncated": false}, {"id": "6e4744cedbcd92a992100818", "title": "The World’s Oldest Shipwreck? Maybe, but ‘We’re All Excited by Maybe.’ - The New York Times", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMikgFBVV95cUxPM3BsRXkweDh4cmtRSVZzZVJBem5aQUJ6TVd6RTE5cXRLR1FhU1Yxb1FxMkJHZldIb3JOZkdoYl8ybk8wZ29iUnMwMVhNd3dxQTdLSEU0ZWk0dkxKRF9OTUVLX3hGN2JSdi1UeWRmOHpESjM5RzJ2SngyQ3J5Uzc4LTVpZTdmX1ZfQUJGU3k4QU9wUQ?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_world", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:40:18+00:00", "text": "The World’s Oldest Shipwreck? Maybe, but ‘We’re All Excited by Maybe.’ - The New York Times\nThe World’s Oldest Shipwreck? Maybe, but ‘We’re All Excited by Maybe.’ The New York Times World’s oldest shipwreck found off coast of Turkey CNN Archaeologists in Turkey may have found the world’s oldest shipwreck FOX Weather Could this ancient shipwreck be the oldest ever found? Scientific American Divers Found the Oldest Shipwreck on Earth 100 Feet Down—Then They Looked Inside Its Jars Popular Mechanics", "truncated": false}, {"id": "9e9d7567554131d7c7e5c12f", "title": "NHF introduces double subsidy for persons living with disabilities - Jamaica Gleaner", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiqgFBVV95cUxOT2MycTFwS2U4ZDZsbEhuSExRTmE3ZTl2a3dXMl9GR1RJb2JwZ25zQi01OUlfeGxWWFBROExXME83eEhKY2JtX25jcnNQaGFwNkg3Tk1ZNWMtLXVTamdIWklCLTVMaE1nam9qVlc4cUVRU3lZTTg3Y1pPbmJXSkIyeVotYkE5WFdKNC1vU1htNW5ZcDFYWlIyc3lndUJBcG9IR0twQnpvVmduZw?oc=5", "source": "lane_subsidy", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:39:01+00:00", "text": "NHF introduces double subsidy for persons living with disabilities - Jamaica Gleaner\nNHF introduces double subsidy for persons living with disabilities Jamaica Gleaner", "truncated": false}, {"id": "7380c158af8ff1c29e4881df", "title": "Judge throws out child sex abuse charges against actor Timothy Busfield", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c8vgxwq4q5r2o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:38:34+00:00", "text": "Judge throws out child sex abuse charges against actor Timothy Busfield\nA judge in the US state of New Mexico has thrown out charges against Emmy-winning actor Timothy Busfield, saying there were issues with how prosecutors presented the case.\nThe West Wing and Field of Dreams actor was indicted in February on four counts of criminal sexual contact of a child. Busfield has denied all the allegations.\nThe charges stemmed from accusations brought by child actors he worked with on the Fox series The Cleaning Lady, which he directed and produced.\nThe judge said on Friday that the prosecutor \"failed to act in a fair and impartial manner\". The district attorney's office said it was evaluating options on how to move forward.\nActor Timothy Busfield indicted on child sexual contact charges\nState District Judge Joseph Montano granted the defence's motion to dismiss the indictment on Friday, citing mistakes that undermined the grand jury process.\n\"Our office has received the order and is evaluating our options to determine how we will proceed,\" a spokesperson for the Office of the Second Judicial District Attorney in Bernalillo County told the BBC on Friday.\nThe BBC has contacted a legal representative for Busfield.\nin January when a warrant was issued for his arrest. A judge later ordered his release pending trial.\nBusfield had originally been taken into custody in Albuquerque on two counts of criminal sexual contact with a minor and one count of child abuse.\nThe indictment against him, reviewed by the BBC, said that on or between 19 October 2022 and 10 September 2023, Busfield \"touched or applied force to the intimate parts\" of one of the unnamed boys when he was under the age of 13.\nThe Thirtysomething actor's defence team submitted more than 70 letters from people vouching for his character and said that he underwent and passed a polygraph test pertaining to the allegations against him.\nIn a warrant for Busfield's arrest, prosecutors had said the parents of the boys alleged that he groomed and sexually abused the youngsters while working on The Cleaning Lady.\nAuthorities claimed the boys referred to Busfield as \"Uncle Tim\" and alleged that he \"would tickle them on the stomach and legs\". Both boys told authorities they were touched inappropriately for years, his arrest affidavit stated.\nBusfield's lawyers had said the actor was being accused as an act of revenge after the children were fired from the series, which angered their mother.\nActor Timothy Busfield to be released from jail in sex abuse case\nActor Timothy Busfield charged with sexually abusing boys on set of TV series", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "3ca7072e41a75e5af80d590a", "title": "Norman Powell gets the basket plus the foul - ESPN", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiiAFBVV95cUxQczBfRXpqR1BtSkZISUk4UEtONzhaR3lQLU1xakZ5aDB4YW1QSUxqUlZVbFdWZzZHSkZTTjhvWWdsWkwwSmY3NGZzZFNMUDlRWUlxTFlhYWd5NENvYzlmQUxNMGU2Ymg4cGpqaGk1Vmt1bWluaU1BaWp4VmlWSmpnRXpqdnFnSjF5?oc=5", "source": "topic_fed", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:38:31+00:00", "text": "Norman Powell gets the basket plus the foul - ESPN\nNorman Powell gets the basket plus the foul ESPN", "truncated": false}, {"id": "c1276e25ee01f2dec2f74a17", "title": "FDA approves Moderna's mRNA seasonal flu vaccine - ABC News - Breaking News, Latest News and Videos", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimAFBVV95cUxOU1BQaW1BVDVnZmczM0Z2LVJBR0RuZEFxc25uSW10QTIzSlhBX0VGQ282YU5EMWI2M3FoTnJmTGU0WGY2RVdFXzNoanREejdsUHVDWUZXMWdJUldzUHB0R1ZWYjUydHU1YTRfYnVqdnlZU0VsTXNjS203TG8zWDI4OHgtOFBqcXgxYUIzanBDU1h3WjFmdlhiUtIBngFBVV95cUxQbVdvZXgyUFJhSlVYU2c2UzlnTzJGY25ubnBHSlV", "source": "lane_gate", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:38:28+00:00", "text": "FDA approves Moderna's mRNA seasonal flu vaccine - ABC News - Breaking News, Latest News and Videos\nFDA approves Moderna's mRNA seasonal flu vaccine ABC News - Breaking News, Latest News and Videos", "truncated": false}, {"id": "5e58cae7e75f98b668c4aa37", "title": "As tensions escalate in the Strait of Hormuz, US and Iran both fire at ships - ABC News - Breaking News, Latest News and Videos", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMipgFBVV95cUxOVXh5c1VJbHBFMmEzNXh6RE8wUE1tQmtNdzJ1aVZGWVViN3haTXJjUHdvSzdRNXdsTHBVSzdvZFRxVlU0SHVyY3NXa1hnVHcxcS0xd0lzQ1NzQ2cwRDJtTmlSMlY1X0Z4QXk1dk4tdlFWMHpFeUtSTUZoTmZWU3BaN3hkbXpGRUpNakg2c3hBbDJETEJGaFhFbERIc2E1QXZBUGhDYldn0gGrAUFVX3lxTE44TFFXSGY4Q0RsS3BqWEY", "source": "topic_geopolitics", "published_at": "2026-10-17T00:38:21+00:00",