tal heart disease WPXI", "truncated": false}, {"id": "1797673a65dcacdc2e73dd8f", "title": "Albanian PM blames ‘foreign hands’ for unrest as student protests intensify", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/16/albanian-pm-blames-foreign-hands-for-unrest-as-student-protests-intensify?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-16T23:27:01+00:00", "text": "Albanian PM blames ‘foreign hands’ for unrest as student protests intensify\nShare Albanian PM blames ‘foreign hands’ for unrest as student protests intensify on social media\nProtesters run away from riot police during an anti-government demonstration in central Tirana, Albania, on October 16, 2026 [AFP]\nAlbanian Prime Minister Edi Rama has blamed “foreign hands” for the increasingly violent\nRiot police clashed with protesters outside the education ministry for the fourth day in a row, as thousands of young Albanians marched through the capital, Tirana, on Friday to protest government corruption and poor university conditions.\n‘Each country goes its own way’: How Balkan politics shape ties with Israel\n‘Boiling point’: Why students are protesting against Albania’s Edi Rama\nAlbania’s PM dismisses calls to resign as student protests spread\n“This is not a student phenomenon,” Rama said in a video address on Friday. He said there were “foreign hands that do not wish for Albania to be at peace”.\nRama insisted that the protest had “absolutely nothing to do with the hardships, problems or legitimate aspirations of anyone who desires a better life”.\nPolice officers used water cannon and tear gas as demonstrators hurled rocks.\nThe protests have spread beyond the capital to Elbasan, Shkodra and Korca, where demonstrators broke windows and set fire to rubbish bins, local media reported.\nThe ongoing escalation follows months of mostly peaceful demonstrations, originally sparked by the sale of part of Albania’s coastline to a luxury resort development\nto Jared Kushner, US President Donald Trump’s son-in-law.\nDays of unrest have increasingly targeted banks and government buildings. Protesters hurled bricks and concrete blocks at the education ministry on Tuesday. They also set fire to several buildings across the city, including the prime minister’s office, the finance and justice ministry buildings and branches of several banks.\nAccording to a police statement, 160 people were detained over alleged acts of violence on Thursday evening.\nDemonstrators are demanding Rama’s resignation, as well as electoral and education reforms.\nThe prime minister, whose government denies corruption,\nhe would be ready to discuss rising education costs but rejected the calls to resign.\nHe said on Thursday, “My responsibility is to listen, address legitimate concerns and keep Albania moving forward.”\nHis dismissal “would be a betrayal of the Albanians who gave me a strong mandate to complete negotiations on European Union membership”, he added.\nCorruption scandals have complicated Albania’s efforts to join the EU. Analysts say the country is a hub for gangs seeking to launder money from drugs and weapons trafficking.\nRama won a fourth term last year on promises to take Albania into the EU, aiming for membership as early as 2028.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "50c13797cae1991708408079", "title": "Ransomware Recovery Isn’t a Backup Problem. It’s an Identity Problem. - Security Boulevard", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMipwFBVV95cUxQOFFyNWtkTGc1YlBSNGY5TzNBQTlWa0NONDVyODBmbE82ZFNlUE1iQTZmZWpfVTU5MkdyREtTcnpDLWw3QkJiVU9uRk1EZk9fYy1JX0VHb1NqcGk1ekVoOTEwRjBNdGhsU0RySkUwMHp2VXdZc3NVeS11Mml4RjY0cF91amtHQmZLYzRGdVpXNy10d1RZZkhzdWRMNTlRc05pcVdNRmdwOA?oc=5", "source": "lane_blast_cyber", "published_at": "2026-10-16T23:26:15+00:00", "text": "Ransomware Recovery Isn’t a Backup Problem. It’s an Identity Problem. - Security Boulevard\nRansomware Recovery Isn’t a Backup Problem. It’s an Identity Problem. Security Boulevard", "