ptions - SmartAsset.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiXkFVX3lxTFBXWFpOVDMwNTBpNjJSbDBiUUFXNS1GSWZvZVdORXFLcDZzaEJ4elhlbVg3RXdhTUhlcng0V1VzV19meTBycW5Xb0VYbFdkSUViWXhUMmtuMnl2cFJGRkE?oc=5", "source": "lane_listing_flow", "published_at": "2026-10-16T23:08:33+00:00", "text": "Anduril IPO: Timeline, Valuation and Investment Options - SmartAsset.com\nAnduril IPO: Timeline, Valuation and Investment Options SmartAsset.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "218cf1a4fd0add80aeb51e8b", "title": "2026 Midterm Election: Could the Stock Buyback Tax Jump to 4%? - SmartAsset.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMilgFBVV95cUxPOFp2d0tfYXJETkRUaUJiRFluTGRUd2hIVEc4YUU3bzZUYkxlSXhfY25TVlY4WjRsckl2MFM5UFZwRDNiaVVUQzhDdjR1TnZKbm9kSm94UE1zYm8tMElLMG5xbVlMbk96NkZCREJWamVRcU16M0lsM1JkcFNEYS1YVkRqUVlpd2pSemtTLXVqZjUwcHJVUFE?oc=5", "source": "lane_capital_return", "published_at": "2026-10-16T23:08:33+00:00", "text": "2026 Midterm Election: Could the Stock Buyback Tax Jump to 4%? - SmartAsset.com\n2026 Midterm Election: Could the Stock Buyback Tax Jump to 4%? SmartAsset.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "02baa469d67c092161db802e", "title": "'The Diplomat' Season 4 Ending Explained: Why Kate Resigns, and What Hal Was About to Say - FanBolt", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuAFBVV95cUxQdUgtLV9pMmdUaTE4d3JpRFN2clU5akpqWklISm9mSnRNN014V3JnWGdjdzQ4X0ZSejFWZWNRZmpUbG5CdG5nMnFoazVkcC1NWHNPSzVlTDhhUjZuSS1tSmRmYWdvWFlNc2VYWGVRLTh5azVJNVpsT25yWUtOcHhwR19acXpVckRaNHJDQTZlMUMwNlVLX1Zld3pCYkhfX0J6N1dhdUgySHdSUGJMSUM1elI4VzlJbVZr?oc=5", "source": "lane_key_person", "published_at": "2026-10-16T23:07:50+00:00", "text": "'The Diplomat' Season 4 Ending Explained: Why Kate Resigns, and What Hal Was About to Say - FanBolt\n'The Diplomat' Season 4 Ending Explained: Why Kate Resigns, and What Hal Was About to Say FanBolt", "truncated": false}, {"id": "2c85031267de30640f731afd", "title": "Oregon teacher fought school attacker before armed dad shot suspect 'multiple times'", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cv98zw1k8kp3o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T23:07:38+00:00", "text": "Oregon teacher fought school attacker before armed dad shot suspect 'multiple times'\nTwo of the victims were five-year-old Rhoam Summer (left) and teacher Meghan Lambert, 25 (right)\nSome readers may find this article distressing.\nA female teacher in the US state of Oregon tackled a teenage shooter who entered her classroom and tried to wrestle the gun from his grasp, police have said.\nThe teacher, who has not been named, threw her mobile phone before trying to disarm the 16-year-old on the floor of a classroom at Silver Rail Elementary in the city of Bend.\nThe attacker killed three children and a teacher after shooting his way into the building on Thursday.\nHe was shot dead by a father with two daughters at the school, who was driving past as the attack started to unfold and, armed with a pistol, ran in to confront the suspect.\nPolice said the attacker used to be a student at the school. His motive remains unclear.\nHe had been armed with about 44 more rounds of shotgun ammunition when he was stopped, police said. He fired as many as 15 rounds in the class.\n\"To the people who showed courage in the face of such violence and intervened to prevent this tragedy from being any worse, we owe you a debt of gratitude,\" Bend Police Chief Mike Krantz said.\nPolice used a news conference on Friday to identify two of the four victims of the school shooting. They were five-year-old Rhoam Summer and 25-year-old teacher Meghan Lambert.\nLambert's family said in a statement shared by the police that her goal was to be a teacher in the district in which she grew up and that she was \"compassionate and had a huge heart\".\n\"She always fought for the underdog and those with special needs,\" they said.\nBryan Jolly, Summer's grandfather, said the family was devastated by his death.\n\"The whole thing is senseless, and a shock,\" he told the New York Times, saying his grandson was \"was very bright and very mischievous and very fun-loving, and totally without fear\".\nThe families of the two other students who were killed, an eight-year-old and 11-year-old, did not wish to have them publicly identified yet, police said.\nTwo others were injured in the attack. Another teacher, a 54-year-old, was still being treated at hospital, and a seven-year-old student, who was in serious but stable condition.\nWatch: Mother of Oregon school shooting 'hero' recounts call with son\nKrantz said the heroic actions of both the teacher and the father who ran inside the school had saved lives.\nHe said the teacher who wrestled with the gunman had not been hurt but \"almost certainly helped prevent more bloodshed\".\nKrantz identified the suspect as 16-year-old Griffin J John II, who had been a special educational needs student at the school.\nA search warrant has been executed at his home and a search is ongoing, the police chief said.\nPolice believe the suspect travelled to the elementary school on foot, but they are still investigating his exact movements and how he acquired the gun.\nOfficials did not confirm the name of the father who intervened and fatally shot the suspect.\nBut Kaylub Giamanco's family has identified him to US media as the person who sprang into action.\nThe 36-year-old carpenter had just finished lunch and was on his way home when he passed the school, his brother told NBC News. He saw the suspect open fire on a vehicle outside the school and then shoot through a window to gain entry.\nPolice said he had a legally concealed .40 calibre in his vehicle, while his brother said he grabbed the pistol \"and decided to go in after\" the suspect. Police say he fired at the suspect \"multiple times\", killing him.\nGiamanco's mother, Kenda Freeman, who lives in Sacramento, California, told a local CBS station that her son had called her moments after the shooting and that he was \"traumatised\".\n\"He said 'a man went into my daughter's school',\" Freeman recalled, pausing as she started to tear up. \"And, um, shot kids. And he said, 'and I looked at him point blank, and I shot and killed him, mum'.\"\nShe added: \"I told him he saved a lot of lives, and he said, 'mum, I couldn't save them all'.\"\nUS President Donald Trump praised the bystander on Friday, calling him a \"great hero\" and telling reporters that the situation could have become \"much worse\".\n\"It's a terrible thing, but thank goodness for a man that had a gun and knew how to use it,\" he said.\nWithout naming the bystander, Oregon Governor Tina Kotek acknowledged the man's heroic actions as having stopped further loss of life, but noted they \"carry a heavy emotional burden and I just want you to know that my thoughts are with you as well\".\nCandlelit vigil held for Oregon school shooting victims\n'It's really hard to believe' - How Oregon primary school shooting unfolded\nCommunity 'heartbroken and horrified' after Oregon school shooting", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "4187462d94eb3ac286a9d9a1", "title": "An investor has exposure tied to about 13% of Funko (FNKO) shares, separate from its ownership stake. - Stock Titan", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuwFBVV95cUxOb29mS1d5UlBKWFZlWnhGdDNodnFZT09icWJiVXR6S0NaTWpPNVJzeWxSVlhBWE1VNVpwTW5OY205cFJ6elg3UDhwT29ETUdQZW55ZHRxRHRGM2JfWm9SZXlhN3I2LUFYeFNRdURYVm5WTTVHdnVWZGN4V2ZLYThiNVl3bkhKYXRJS1I4ZVRMTTRCQ29EUEwwUk8wbXY5RGJsYXJMWnpuOE5vZWI5X1VTdUZsREI5NkIzMkdN?oc=5", "source": "sec_SCHEDULE_13D:google_fallback", "published_at": "2026-10-16T23:07:29+00:00", "text": "An investor has exposure tied to about 13% of Funko (FNKO) shares, separate from its ownership stake. - Stock Titan\nAn investor has exposure tied to about 13% of Funko (FNKO) shares, separate from its ownership stake. Stock Titan", "truncated": false}, {"id": "2900f260915f0ea7ece867c8", "title": "Stolen password caused North Platte cyberattack - knopnews2.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMilgFBVV95cUxQQ2NpQVM2bjhqRjlCQ3ZpWmhJT2ZkU011YUdlNWJnUFBVZHZIRzV1RU9uLWY4ZDRLY01sRndONk9HaGhucXM3UVN0VVdBMzY0TUlnSU9KdUdDT1BnMXN3Q2lfaWRmSkQ5ZkpUWWY0UGZjVWRaVTdpMlpxa1pva2ltM01NWTRVNnBHdmw4dXRPMmdCSElyM3c?oc=5", "source": "lane_blast_cyber", "published_at": "2026-10-16T23:07:22+00:00", "text": "Stolen password caused North Platte cyberattack - knopnews2.com\nStolen password caused North Platte cyberattack knopnews2.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "00f30d32b87311f1e9dfd73e", "title": "Trial of German steakhouse heiress that sparked a huge spy scandal", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwvgdn3d656vo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T23:07:18+00:00", "text": "Trial of German steakhouse heiress that sparked a huge spy scandal\nChristina Block, 53, denies knowing about the planned abduction of her children\nIt is a trial that has gripped Germany, with a wealthy heiress in the dock for months and a case that triggered the country's biggest espionage scandal in recent history.\nChristina Block, heir to a popular chain of steakhouses, is accused of ordering an Israeli security firm to carry out the violent abduction of her children from Denmark, following a bitter custody battle with her former husband.\nThere are allegedly connections with at least two former secret agents, from Germany and Israel.\nIn the latest twist, investigators in the case stumbled across evidence that has led to Germany's former intelligence chief, August Hanning, being arrested this month for treason and espionage.\nHanning's arrest stunned the intelligence community. Although his case has nothing to do with the Hamburg trial, he has denied allegations of involvement in an earlier attempted abduction of the children.\nThere was enormous media interest in Germany when Christina Block and six others went on trial in a high-security courtroom in Hamburg in July last year.\nBlock House restaurants, specialising in steak and baked potatoes, are a feature of many German towns. Their success over the past half century has helped make Christina Block's father Eugen a multimillionaire.\nProsecutors allege that she and a family lawyer, Andreas Costard, organised the forcible abduction of two of her children, who were staying with her former husband, Stephan Hensel, at his home in Denmark.\nThey say the operation was carried out by a 36-year-old Israeli citizen named only as Jonathan G and five others.\nThe abduction took place during the New Year celebrations of 2023-24, as the two children and their father were watching a firework display, according to the indictment.\nProsecutors say several men ambushed and beat up Stephan Hensel, and dragged the boy and the girl, then 10 and 13 years old, into a car.\nOn the approach to the border with Germany, they are alleged to have changed vehicles, taped up the children's mouths and tied the girl's hands.\nThe group are then said to have travelled on to Baden-WÃ¼rttemberg in a campervan. Prosecutors say the children were held there against their will until their mother arrived to pick them up on 2 January 2024 and took them to her home in Hamburg.\nAfter Stephan Hensel and Christina Block separated, the children lived with their mother\nChristina Block, Andreas Costard and Jonathan G are charged with the abduction of minors, grievous bodily harm and unlawful detention.\nBlock's partner, a former TV sports presenter called Gerhard Delling, and three others are charged with aiding and abetting.\nAll of them deny wrongdoing, except for Jonathan G, who has admitted his involvement. However, he told the court he thought he was taking part in a rescue operation.\nThe trial comes after years of legal battles between Christina Block and her former husband, Stephan Hensel, over who had the right to look after the children.\nImmediately after their separation, all four children lived with their mother in Hamburg. Hensel, who remarried, later moved to Denmark, where they regularly visited him.\nIn 2021, the two youngest children went to visit their father in Denmark, but then were not brought back to Hamburg as agreed.\nA court in Hamburg ruled that Christina had custody and that the children should be returned. However, in February 2023, a Danish court ruled that, although the boy and the girl had been brought to Denmark unlawfully, they could not be taken back.\nIn May 2023, Hensel was charged with the abduction of minors, in a case which is still pending.\nHe has accused Christina of being violent with the children, charges she denies. She told the court Hensel wanted to take revenge on her.\nShe also denied knowing anything about plans for the abduction of the children from Denmark. Her defence argues that the Israeli security team had acted on its own initiative.\nShe told the court it was \"a catastrophe\" and that she had never wanted anything like that.\nThe head of the Israeli security team, David Barkay, who is not on trial but faces future legal proceedings, told the court that Christina Block and Costard had known about the planned repatriation operation.\nFormer foreign intelligence chief August Hanning was arrested after police investigating the Christina Block case searched his home\nBarkay, who was described by another witness as a former agent with Mossad, the Israeli secret service, said he had not shared all the details because he \"didn't trust them.\"\nThe alleged connection with Germany's former spy chief, August Hanning, relates to accusations of an earlier attempt to abduct the children and bring them back from Denmark in 2022.\nProsecutors have said Hanning was responsible for arranging security staff sent to Denmark with Christina Block. His lawyer denies the accusations. Hanning has not been charged over the current Block court case.\nHowever, while investigators were searching his home, they came across evidence to suggest\nHanning was involved in buying some 2,000 documents\nfrom his former chief of staff and then passing on state secrets to foreign powers.\nWhich foreign power and who he is alleged to have given the secrets to is unspecified, but it has prompted widespread speculation.\nNow 80, Hanning is in custody charged with treasonous espionage. His lawyer says he is being \"unjustly persecuted\" and that the charges are \"unfounded\".\nMeanwhile the trial against Christina Block is currently on hold for two weeks - one of numerous delays and extensions over the course of the past 15 months.\nThe latest delay is because a witness, Stephan Hensel's new wife, failed to appear in court.\nNord Stream trial: How German police followed the evidence - to Ukraine\nSabotage suspect held in Germany after spate of attacks on power grid", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "e35feeae92dd2c8ae0de8ce3", "title": "Painting Eisenhower Building would diminish historic character of White House complex, report says - AP News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMirAFBVV95cUxPTmtnQmJhZUdYenI5NG1feE1iZnZTYjFiYTRaSm1mdlNORXlYRlpTMzJNaExzRERwejJzQTZYWnlQMzl5d1lYaFhMUThuMWlWTnktcHczOWkzYlZKdnctMTA5UFIxOVBHWEJxOG13cjhLLU1hTXc2TEo4YW8xUWFjam5SaUtXMnFodUNFRUFFQV9ybG9QNTBTMm1hYUt4bHZNbW8wNE1hNFlBR2gw?oc=5", "source": "rss_ap_business:google_fallback", "published_at": "2026-10-16T23:07:00+00:00", "text": "Painting Eisenhower Building would diminish historic character of White House complex, report says - AP News\nPainting Eisenhower Building would diminish historic character of White House complex, report says AP News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "024d3b9530d0f157b8c9e8d5", "title": "Acorns Acquires Frich to Scale Financial Education - PYMNTS.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimgFBVV95cUxQVDRfSl9FbVVZSkp0S01DeHJPUDEydXhEMUprVkZrWGt0aERaM2hpT3I4VTIwRWFCUGhjRUREQVZnVmxleEQ5Ry1QQUZGdHFwTGdCbVFPd1ZkRDNGM3E4TXFnVGJROXczMTRrb25TRGUwYjRmaWJFVnQ1WUVoXzE0ZGw1dnp6dk