WUxRnc5QUw5VTdkaF9ObUR", "source": "topic_technology", "published_at": "2026-10-16T21:05:22+00:00", "text": "Why investing in social services technology means investing in caseworkers - Federal News Network\nWhy investing in social services technology means investing in caseworkers Federal News Network", "truncated": false}, {"id": "b35844d57c5dbbcc509a48f5", "title": "Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent to join Sen. Jon Husted at Ohio tax credit roundtable - myfox28columbus.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMirgJBVV95cUxQSEJqN2R1RnhIaFNVS2tqUmd6SUU3NFAxTFhMdFlNdnZaOE1SWUJSQTFMM0FqanZoQkl0QjY3WkoteEthMVoyREtvVGl6YjFUa0VtRUFmdGpBVzhQWGtzS0tFVW4yYUhzNTZBdHlBTzRXc1dDOXFuV0VYc3RsbE1JVnRjbEFTSXFwZk16T3NTNEhnWUNDVTN1SmQ1Nm9BNzhsakVWa0hzY3VOcDRoWFpXa3diTUZaTnFOSkxqalBaaHV", "source": "topic_government", "published_at": "2026-10-16T21:05:15+00:00", "text": "Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent to join Sen. Jon Husted at Ohio tax credit roundtable - myfox28columbus.com\nTreasury Secretary Scott Bessent to join Sen. Jon Husted at Ohio tax credit roundtable myfox28columbus.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "94eab1f0bb1f57a53a3917bb", "title": "Katie Zacharia leaving her White House role less than five days on the job", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c32l4ekpvzl8o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T21:05:05+00:00", "text": "Katie Zacharia leaving her White House role less than five days on the job\nTrump: New White House press secretary should 'reconsider' job\nKatie Zacharia will be leaving her post as White House press secretary, just five days after her shaky public debut.\nUS President Donald Trump told reporters moments before news of her exit was announced that he had told her \"you don't have to do this if you don't want to\" and \"honestly, if I were you, I'd reconsider\".\nZacharia faced heavy criticism for her first press briefing on Tuesday, which lasted less than 15 minutes and included only three questions from reporters.\nThe 42-year-old spent the days following playing defence, fielding tough questions from news outlets about her credentials and preparedness for the job.\nTrump hinted at her impending exit early on Friday afternoon, during an impromptu news conference in front of the Marine One helicopter.\n\"I said, 'Is this for you, Katie? Because honestly, you don't need this',\" Trump said.\n\"You don't have to be treated this badly,\" he continued, adding that he believed she had been treated \"pretty nastily\" by the media.\nHours later, she praised Trump as \"kind, wonderful, gracious, thoughtful, enthusiastic, and the most hardworking man\" on X, writing that it had been an honour to work for him.\nAs the November midterm elections, which will decide who controls the US Congress, draw closer, Democrats are seizing on Zacharia's abrupt departure as a sign of failure in the Republican White House.\n\"The White House Press Secretary is gone. Maga extremists continue to fall apart,\" the top Democrat in the House of Representatives, Hakeem Jeffries, posted on X.\nZacharia appeared visibly nervous during her inaugural White House press briefing, in which she read talking points from a notebook, answered only a few questions and referred to her husband as the president.\nDuring the briefing, a reporter asked Zacharia - who had been called an attorney by Trump and others and who graduated from Pepperdine University's law school - about where she passed the bar exam. To practise law in the US, an attorney must be admitted to a state's bar association by passing a rigorous exam.\nAfter intense scrutiny and digging by the media, Zacharia admitted on Thursday that she did not pass the bar exam.\nShe did say in a statement that she had practised international transaction law in China with the \"appropriate credentials\".\nQuestions about her legal credentials became a major talking point throughout the week, with Sean Hannity, from conservative-leaning Fox News, lobbing particularly pointed questions at her during an interview on Thursday.\nWhen she said she had not passed the bar, he replied: \"Why didn't you just say, 'I never practiced law?'\"\nZacharia also described how quickly she had taken on the press secretary role - having been hired less than a week before her debut - prompting him to ask: \"Am I hearing that you think the staff should have better prepped you?\"\nWhen she appeared on Fox & Friends, which Trump often watches, on Friday, Zacharia faced a similar line of questioning.\nShe told the show's hosts that she did not disclose to Trump that she was not an attorney because \"those questions weren't asked\", saying she was hired for a communications role, not a legal one.\nHost Lawrence Jones then asked her: \"Do you think you still have the confidence of the president?\"\nShe avoided offering a direct answer but ended by saying: \"I hope to be able to build the confidence and trust of everyone around me.\"\nZacharia's early exit is the quickest among Trump's White House press team.\nAnthony Scaramucci left his post as White House communications director after only 10 days during Trump's first term.\n\"Records are made to be broken,\" Scaramucci wrote on X on Friday. \"But I take no pleasure in other people's pain.\"\nPrior to her appointment as press secretary, Zacharia was a frequent fixture on right-wing US networks such as Fox and Newsmax.\nShe had a brief stint as a spokeswoman at the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) and worked as a communications adviser for Trump's social media platform Truth Social, according to her LinkedIn.\nIn her early career, Zacharia worked as an intern for the Republican National Committee and the White House Office of Political Affairs during the Bush administration in 2006.\nWhite House defends new press secretary over her legal credentials", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "bb4b171f64706e51af60dc82", "title": "Getty Images Files for Bankruptcy: