es Papa John's Veteran Jenna Bromberg CMO Amid Inspire Brands' IPO Push 10/19/2026 - MediaPost\nArby's Names Papa John's Veteran Jenna Bromberg CMO Amid Inspire Brands' IPO Push 10/19/2026 MediaPost", "truncated": false}, {"id": "2d8532e663cbdf1f574f979e", "title": "Kenya records third Ebola case, linked to first patient who died", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/16/kenya-reports-third-bundibugyo-strain-ebola-case?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-16T18:57:40+00:00", "text": "Kenya records third Ebola case, linked to first patient who died\nShare Kenya records third Ebola case, linked to first patient who died on social media\nHealth workers and officials stand outside an Ebola isolation facility in Kenya, October 7, 2026 [File: Andrew Kasuku/AP Photo]\nKenya has recorded its third case of the Bundibugyo virus disease – a strain of Ebola that has killed thousands of people in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) – 10 days after reporting its\nThe latest confirmed case – a 65-year-old woman – was a “primary contact and a close relative of the first and second cases”, Kenya’s Health Ministry said in a statement on Friday, adding that the patient has been in quarantine since October 6.\nWhy single Ebola death in Kenya has prompted fears of wider African spread\nKenyan patient suspected of Ebola infection tests negative\nEbola spreads in eastern DRC as Kenya confirms imported case\nAfter developing symptoms, she was isolated and then tested positive for the virus, it added in a statement.\n“Once she developed fever, sore throat and malaise, she was isolated and specimen collected for testing. She tested positive for Ebola,” the Health Ministry said. “She was diagnosed early and is currently receiving ⁠⁠supportive care improving her ⁠⁠chances of recovery.”\nKenya’s cases are linked to the Ebola epidemic in the DRC, which is the second-largest on record globally ⁠ ⁠with more than\nand over 4,300 deaths since it was officially detected in May.\nKenya’s ⁠⁠first case emerged last ⁠⁠week after a Kenyan man, who had been ill in the DRC about a month before travelling by road ‌‌to Uganda, flew to Nairobi on October 3, where a relative drove him to ‌‌the ‌‌hospital. He died on October 5.\nIn its statement on X, the Health Ministry moved to reassure the public that there was no community spread of the disease and that the risk to the Kenyan public remained low.\nOut of 130 contacts listed, 127 had been traced and were in quarantine, it said.\nEfforts to control the spread of the Ebola outbreak have been complicated because the Bundibugyo strain has no licensed vaccine. Research is under way to develop one or more vaccines that work against the strain and some of these vaccines are already in the early phases of testing in humans.\nA nurse wears personal protective equipment (PPE) at a Nairobi hospital isolation facility that is preparing to screen patients exposed to Ebola, October 7, 2026 [AFP]\nTransparency demands over Ebola response funds\nThe new case in Kenya was reported as Africa’s ‌‌top public health body and Congolese health authorities called ⁠⁠for greater transparency in how Ebola response funds are used.\nThe Africa Centres for Disease Control and Prevention (Africa CDC) said financial information provided by aid organisations indicated that ⁠⁠more than $700m of donor money was unaccounted for.\nDr Jean Kaseya, the director general of ‌‌the Africa CDC, told the Reuters news agency that international donors disbursed about $962m to organisations ⁠⁠implementing Ebola response activities in DRC, rather ⁠⁠than to the government.\nKaseya said those organisations reported receiving $228m, of which $127m had ⁠⁠been spent and roughly $100m remained available.\nBut he said it was too early to speak of embezzlement.\nIn a post on X, Congolese Health Minister Samuel Roger Kamba warned aid groups were required to provide details on funding ‌‌sources to his ministry or risk being barred from working ‌‌in ‌‌DRC and blacklisted across Africa.\nMeanwhile, on the ground in the DRC, Ebola cases have been detected in a new province bordering the Central African Republic, two health officials told Reuters on Thursday.\nThe two cases were confirmed in North Ubangi, the eighth Congolese province affected by the Bundibugyo outbreak, the officials said. Congolese authorities have not yet officially announced the outbreak has spread to North Ubangi.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "6f05b80f0289ad019782dde9", "title": "Trade Desk Is Now Down 67% This Year: Is TTD Stock Dead Money or Due for a Bounce? - Yahoo Finance", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMikAFBVV95cUxOUzBHUUJLejE1SGxtbmlGT3RpdE1aZHRXOFM5UTdYQWE1QnJKNFUzYUcwQnUtR1dPY1Z6NVR6azExeGNnMDhHZld4RUpyeGFCTUozMlNVbWw5Nm5LMUxiTkh1Z0ZZODJ6ejF5c3FUWXlvZjdNaFRWb1RFTW9EQk9BdUtOeGxBeGZxSWVrRXQ5U2g?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_markets", "published_at": "2026-10-16T18:57:28+00:00", "text": "Trade Desk Is Now Down 67% This Year: Is TTD Stock Dead Money or Due for a Bounce? - Yahoo Finance\nTrade Desk Is Now Down 67% This Year: Is TTD Stock Dead Money or Due for a Bounce? Yahoo Finance", "truncated": false}, {"id": "bfae62784c18e383d1eff47f", "title": "[NFHS Network]TV!] Powell vs Jefferson County Ｌｉｖｅ Ｔｖ Ｃｈａｎｎｅｌ 16 October 2026 - Universidad de Córdoba (UCO)", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuAJBVV95cUxQcTlkN0tTeFZOd2RxbkZMSkFfd1FybzNCLXB6QW1qNHBwbk40R3A1MUYzVno1T18yV21FV0VVNmFJa1Mxclk1RktRTGRNZ1NfNkRVVG92QTFGNG1FRDFhUkJtUlk1MjZLcmlmSWY0Q2hITDFnajA4RFNpLTVlb0ppeFZqY3N0RXo0a1pqS1NzLUJ2VDNwbjVoVEpMY1NyTkNqRjZ5Y2ZNMTJ3Vmh4czE3RlVWTDIxcXJCdHEwd2J3Q0l", "source": "topic_fed", "published_at": "2026-10-16T18:57:09+00:00", "text": "[NFHS Network]TV!] Powell vs Jefferson County Ｌｉｖｅ Ｔｖ Ｃｈａｎｎｅｌ 16 October 2026 - Universidad de Córdoba (UCO)\n[NFHS Network]TV!] Powell vs Jefferson County Ｌｉｖｅ Ｔｖ Ｃｈａｎｎｅｌ 16 October 2026 Universidad de Córdoba (UCO)", "truncated": fals