JZEJlTnEwTFZpX0dTbDNEaXdxREhmd2h1Q0NjYXpXMFFBVW1Ba0dqOFM1Y2d", "source": "topic_government", "published_at": "2026-10-16T18:00:15+00:00", "text": "U.S. budget deficit swells to almost $2 trillion for fiscal 2026, up 12% in a single year - CNBC\nU.S. budget deficit swells to almost $2 trillion for fiscal 2026, up 12% in a single year CNBC", "truncated": false}, {"id": "7f8dfcf8c9f53586369bf3fe", "title": "U.S. budget deficit swells to almost $2 trillion for fiscal 2026, up 12% in a single year", "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/16/us-budget-deficit-2026.html", "source": "rss_cnbc_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-16T18:00:15+00:00", "text": "U.S. budget deficit swells to almost $2 trillion for fiscal 2026, up 12% in a single year\nThe Treasury Department said the U.S. budget deficit came in at nearly $2 trillion for the 2026 fiscal year.\nThe U.S. officially recorded its 25th straight fiscal year operating at a deficit.\nAn electronic display shows the national debt in Washington, Aug. 19, 2026.\nThe U.S. finished the 2026 fiscal year with a\nof nearly $2 trillion, the latest measurement to underscore the extent of the country's financial stress.\nThe $1.992 trillion figure was about 12% higher than the $1.775 trillion deficit in the prior fiscal year, the\nIt's the third-highest annual deficit in U.S. history, behind only 2020 and 2021, when the federal government was doling out trillions of dollars in financial stimulus amid the Covid-19 pandemic. The 2026 fiscal year saw record sums for both receipts and outlays, according to a Treasury Department official.\nWith Friday's release, the U.S. officially recorded its\noperating at a deficit. The government last recorded a surplus — meaning its revenues were larger than debts — in 2001.\nFriday's report offers fresh evidence of how the U.S.'s fast-growing debt pile has dragged on the nation's overall financial health. The rising debt bill has helped exacerbate angst among bond traders, who sent the\nSocial Security amounted to the biggest slice of government spending at nearly $1.7 trillion for the full fiscal year, up modestly from the roughly $1.6 trillion spent in 2025.\nMedicare was the second biggest contributor, costing almost $1.1 trillion for the fiscal year. By comparison, the government-run healthcare program required slightly under $1 trillion in fiscal 2025.\nNet interest – a calculation of the Treasury's gross interest minus the interest it receives – also topped $1 trillion for the fiscal year. In the 2025 fiscal year, net interest totaled $970 billion.\nIndividual incomes taxes helped lessen the total deficit by bringing in more than $2.8 trillion, an increase of about 7% year over year. Corporate income tax provided $381 billion in receipts during the 2026 fiscal year. That was a decrease of about $70 billion from fiscal 2025 stemming from the tax cuts included in the One Big Beautiful Bill act passed by Congress last year.\nIn the 2026 fiscal year, the U.S. shelled out more than $1.3 trillion in interest on the national public debt. The total owed\nearlier this year and has more than doubled over the last decade.\nTreasury Secretary Scott Bessent told CNBC in August that the U.S. can\n\"Our message to our allies, our trading partners, is that global growth is the way to take care of this mountain of debt,\" Bessent said in August.\npitched his plans for broad and steep tariffs as a way to drive up revenue and shrink the government's debt. The White House has pushed forward with its strategy of increasing levies on trading partners despite the\nearlier this year that many of those instituted by Trump were unconstitutional.\nThe Treasury saw a record high for gross customs duties in the 2026 fiscal year, according to a department official. But the total revenue from customs took a hit due to\nfollowing the Supreme Court ruling, which sent billions of dollars back to retailers.\nThe Treasury also reported Friday that September's monthly deficit came in at $27 billion, a reversal from the prior year, when the department saw a surplus of $198 billion in the month.\nChoose CNBC as your preferred source on Google and never miss a moment from the most trusted name in business news.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "9e640bb2d727c8b10ad6658f", "title": "FDA approves Genentech's Enspryng as first at-home TED injection - Glance by Eyes On Eyecare", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuAFBVV95cUxNejJucWdNWTItZlltYzFJT2J3RjNIazZwS1ZuVnpleUkxME1BRXRTV1VPdGNmVWhRRUotVnphLTdlZnJiN0JiSm5uOE9ZclhMUGJDcWRaSHdOQWJDekFCVWFyVHhoTkl3dUxnYmRpd3E5cjFfcGMxeGJtOFZFdmJNdWp2LUFIWU1kUWFVbjFyMmp0eVluUGRSbnE0MWRsYlE3NFl3MGNpY1o4clg0WWxvUEJQOHRnSjdC?oc=5", "source": "lane_gate", "published_at": "2026-10-16T18:00:10+00:00", "text": "FDA approves Genentech's Enspryng as first at-home TED injection - Glance by Eyes On Eyecare\nFDA approves Genentech's Enspryng as first at-home TED injection Glance by Eyes On Eyecare", "truncated": false}, {"id": "8b6d10bd879a6f88bc7c81ef", "title": "Why Nobody Was Ready for the Earthquake in Colombia - Finger Lakes Times", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi2AFBVV95cUxNVzBqY1FFLUV4RFFPd3BNMkUxcGVkUDBUWTRtRVRhWVZVNGhhWHJNeXAydkxyTTJxVmZuZ2piLU5QaTcwMjdGODQ2b0lXVVNLeHpPSjFPN2U1MEktT2ZHR3JwOThnT0dBbDZfc0pWUzFQMzNmbFF5Ykl4T3llUkFLWGJoZnhtTVc4MzJmVjQtcDlTMHV4bkUwSTVwVEkwRXRMUGFqV0hSNzBpNDhVN1hUQmZyekdKcEZKTkNaTFdBT1p", "source": "lane_cat_weather", "published_at": "2026-10-16T18:00:09+00:00", "text": "Why Nobody Was Ready for the Earthquake in Colombia - Finger Lakes Times\nWhy Nobody Was Ready for the Earthquake in Colombia Finger Lakes Times", "truncated": false}, {"id": "38a6365bbd1de7addc214cb6", "title": "The $44bn Firmus float web just got even more tangled - The Australian", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiiAJBVV95cUxNNlNoU1JmR3A0eW1abkhRb1FZMXFvcmtYY3JWN3NEalpZT3pvQlRzSll0cTJnSTY1QkNET0FTOTVOdldLbTFydGk3UWg4Ul8yQW1jZUZfcTFpNXQ4YWN2QWZyUngyTDV0V1U0UHFFdkIzbFFtZ