VFaaEg2V3hFMGc5Z3NzRWNsbkd4UmpYeENMcFJoMzJrYkJPVDNQLUxRVXhyQzBKTVJuaWs1a3BONmZFMmhTQnBHbFlpcm1lVlZldFhmbk5RWDRHLUxSQXc2NWZQdVE4VmUzYlY0V19lTnF5U3ZPQzgwektfcDFXRHFxNmh2REFONkNfYXREd0V", "source": "lane_listing_flow", "published_at": "2026-10-16T16:46:33+00:00", "text": "CoreWeave's CEO sold nearly $1 billion of stock since IPO, filings show - TradingView\nCoreWeave's CEO sold nearly $1 billion of stock since IPO, filings show TradingView", "truncated": false}, {"id": "eae5a55c9be34a436e4da280", "title": "U.S. presses China on Mastercard, Citigroup market access in bilateral Bangkok talks, official says", "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/16/us-china-mastercard-citigroup-market-access.html", "source": "rss_cnbc_finance", "published_at": "2026-10-16T16:46:18+00:00", "text": "U.S. presses China on Mastercard, Citigroup market access in bilateral Bangkok talks, official says\nU.S. officials raised market access issues for Mastercard and Citigroup in China during bilateral meetings in Bangkok this week, according to a U.S. Treasury official.\nThe regulatory approval was agreed to during Chinese leader Xi’s September Washington visit with President Trump, the official said.\nThe progress follows China's Oct. 13 approval for Visa to begin preparing a wholly owned domestic card-clearing business.\nA Citibank branch in New York, US, on Sunday, Oct. 11, 2026.\nBANGKOK – U.S. officials pressed Beijing on market access issues for\nduring at least two bilateral meetings with Chinese counterparts in Bangkok this week, according to a U.S. Treasury official.\nThe Treasury delegation, led by Deputy Secretary Francis Brooke, held bilateral meetings with officials from the People's Bank of China and the finance ministry on the sidelines of the Group of 20 finance meetings and the IMF-World Bank annual gathering this week, the official said, speaking on condition of anonymity to discuss the closed-door meetings.\nThe talks included market access for U.S. companies, including Mastercard,\nand Citigroup, the official said, adding that regulatory approval was part of the agreement reached during the meeting between\nand Xi Jinping during the latter's state visit in Washington last month.\nThe sideline meetings in Bangkok continued discussions on implementing these deals, the official said.\nThe PBOC, China's National Financial Regulatory Administration, China Securities Regulatory Commission, Chinese embassies in Singapore and Thailand, the U.S. Treasury Department press office and Visa did not immediately respond to CNBC's request for comment. Citigroup and Mastercard declined to comment.\nThe progress follows China's Oct. 13 approval for Visa Inc. to\nbegin preparing a wholly owned domestic card-clearing business\n. Visa has up to a year to prepare before it applies for an operating license,\nChina has opened much of its financial sector to foreign firms since 2018, gradually lifting foreign ownership caps on services such as securities, futures and fund management. It still limits cross-border capital flows and has kept state-backed domestic players dominant in payments.\nMastercard currently operates yuan card payments in China through a local joint venture with NetsUnion Clearing Corp., which began operating in 2024. The pending approval would allow it to operate as an independent entity, the person said.\nCitigroup — which has hired several dozen employees for its China unit — is seeking a wholly owned securities license to conduct A-share brokerage, underwriting, research and principal trading businesses in the onshore market, two people familiar with the industry said, requesting anonymity for discussing private matters.\nOne of the two people close to Citigroup said the bank planned to increase its China securities staff to about 100.\nTrump and Xi are expected to meet in person for the third time this year in Shenzhen, China, for the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation summit in November.\n\"The move suggests the US-China trade environment remains benign, and Beijing is keen to keep up the momentum,\" said Dan Wang, China director at Eurasia Group.\nLicensing U.S. financial firms is a \"limited concession\" on market access, she said, though it signals China is continuing to open up, and \"this should marginally bolster investor confidence in Chinese assets.\"\nChoose CNBC as your preferred source on Google and never miss a moment from the most trusted name in business news.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "cbfe2105ed97cddc069d2cc5", "title": "Form S-1/A REGISTRATION STATEMENT UNDER THE SECURITIES ACTOF 1933 For: 16 October - Investing.com UK", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi1gFBVV95cUxNUmQ4LTI5b0NkUEJzN2NKTzhVd2dJcjB0VTktVW1OY0gwZDgtVmt6OFpYdjVvOUhTaTFTTWFNR3NpOThhMVNnOG02bTQwZGhqcnh5Z1FfMnJXUlFpcWpoV01zaExaa0NmQWdzYjhGX2lQMk1KdVNuNEh5NzZMMFM1aEhlR2xNaC1lQWpENHNBNDlKckNhZ3FZaXdLN3hhcG1rX19FNXA3UnF6bHpzbGtlS3hOZm11YUpqSGlWSE1icVZ", "source": "sec_S-1:google_fallback", "published_at": "2026-10-16T16:46:00+00:00", "text": "Form S-1/A REGISTRATION STATEMENT UNDER THE SECURITIES ACTOF 1933 For: 16 October - Investing.com UK\nForm S-1/A REGISTRATION STATEMENT UNDER THE SECURITIES ACTOF 1933 For: 16 October Investing.com UK", "truncated": false}, {"id": "c89c6f8616ae8adc257bc200", "title": "CRE Appraisal Processes Advancing Rapidly with Technology, AI - Globest", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMinAFBVV95cUxOdGlLWTV2SFdueU1VU1h5cy1kSjBZMEczWjl3UEIxdGsxcnFrYmlCWjJlNWdOQllJbTRwTFc1aUVoZjM5SXVlNXVyd1JuS0Z2NkRHOFdVS2haNURzZHJYVXlhcmJyb2xQNHlRTU1xUHRsbUdZQWY5ZWtBZnFZMk1JZ1ZISU9TckN4eXVBRHNnUWxoT0p5V1ZjUFNSYmU?oc=5", "source": "topic_technology", "published_at": "2026-10-16T16:45:37+00:00", "text": "CRE Appraisal Processes Advancing Rapidly with Technology, AI - Globest\nCRE Appraisal Processes Advancing Rapidly with Technology, AI Globest", "truncated": false}, {"id": "3d112db7b3975b14f452934d", "title": "Alphabet’s Cloud Surge and AI Dominance Overshadow Capex 