 partner's real estate businesses: \"With a clear conscience\" - El Constitucional", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMikwJBVV95cUxNNnZtS0R6LWpfMzZ0WmVuUlFJZEx0REVLZ2ZPUGl3MDk3eGVXTUNmTlVvM0dYS1I2cUhiOF9xdTU5aTRTS29KVlpHV185Z2luLU1oVzBtVGhURUs0R09tZ0FDaVNZcXZFYmU3REo3WDdJNGxwLWxPWXZHOTRyYUNST0xHVVVLMnNJRlVWMkNjaU8zSFA4WU82NVBlYTFzSjZoeWVFMnRRXzFZdTM4Qnd6TVF6eWZpRTc1SEl6ZFdWVmM", "source": "lane_key_person", "published_at": "2026-10-16T16:28:00+00:00", "text": "Montse Mínguez resigns as PSOE spokesperson after the controversy over her partner's real estate businesses: \"With a clear conscience\" - El Constitucional\nMontse Mínguez resigns as PSOE spokesperson after the controversy over her partner's real estate businesses: \"With a clear conscience\" El Constitucional", "truncated": false}, {"id": "ca2ef61f2e92d036f1973ccd", "title": "Pope Leo urges ceasefire to deliver aid to Ukrainian city in call with Putin - Anadolu Ajansı", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMitgFBVV95cUxPdTJNRkZUSktSRXhGeC1iR2xfUkVuVnczOVZmQ05Xa2ZpYTMtaFJzNUFJNlM0QWhyUjVQWEdyWFQ1WlFMekE0ZGJXbmFWYWVUb2hTRi1WU2FXOWpnVnBHU2ZoR29QZkJTTVh0VFVmZkVpb3ZFdjRzSzZ2a29NNzZQRG43WUZLR3FDeGs2WEtKdjVMRjhwQlBwcFNjV0gyOGtxWkdaU0hXWGQydm9QU0RVZDdzYTV4Zw?oc=5", "source": "topic_geopolitics", "published_at": "2026-10-16T16:27:55+00:00", "text": "Pope Leo urges ceasefire to deliver aid to Ukrainian city in call with Putin - Anadolu Ajansı\nPope Leo urges ceasefire to deliver aid to Ukrainian city in call with Putin Anadolu Ajansı", "truncated": false}, {"id": "3bbae259c523355189eff76d", "title": "Analysis: Everyone expects the Fed to hold rates in October. Kevin Warsh could surprise us all", "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/16/kevin-warsh-fed-october-rate-hike-interest-rates-markets.html", "source": "rss_cnbc_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-16T16:27:31+00:00", "text": "Analysis: Everyone expects the Fed to hold rates in October. Kevin Warsh could surprise us all\nFutures markets assigned a 15% probability of an October rate hike Friday morning.\nOther top Fed officials have signaled comfort with holding rates steady.\nFed Chair Kevin Warsh has rejected traditional forward guidance and distanced himself from other officials' rate forecasts.\nElevated inflation and relatively loose financial conditions could still inform the case for another increase.\nFederal Reserve Chairman Kevin Warsh holds a press conference following a two-day meeting of the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) at the Federal Reserve in Washington, D.C., U.S. September 16, 2026.\nTraders are betting overwhelmingly that the\nwon't raise interest rates when it meets later this month. But that judgment rests in part on assumptions about how the Fed communicates that may no longer hold under its new chairman.\nhas left room to maneuver toward a hike, should he choose to use it.\nTo be clear, Warsh said outright at an International Monetary Fund event in Bangkok Friday morning that\none way or the other. His remarks nonetheless reinforced a judgment in the markets that the Fed would pause its rate increases, with the\nassigning a 15% probability to an October hike as of Friday morning. That judgment follows remarks by other Fed speakers, including the chair's traditional top lieutenants, who indicated they were comfortable holding.\nThere are good reasons for investors to expect the Fed to wait. But there is also a case in the inflation data that Warsh could invoke to push the Federal Open Market Committee toward an increase on Oct. 28. Investors are overlooking that case based on longstanding past experience with the Fed.\nthe convention of telling markets what he plans to do before the committee meets. That raises the possibility that investors are dangerously complacent about the probability of a rate hike in October.\nAnalysis: Why a $2 trillion deficit and 5% Treasury yields are becoming America’s biggest economic challenge\nEconomists: Hot PPI shows Fed’s work is not done\nHow high inflation became a ‘fact of life’ in the U.S.\nWholesale prices jumped 6.3% annually in September, biggest gain since 2022\nThat complacency rests on a few bases. One is that the chairman's top lieutenants speak for him, or at least wouldn't contradict him in public. That was true in the past, but may not be so anymore.\nIn recent weeks, both Fed Vice Chairman Philip Jefferson and New York Fed President John Williams gave remarks that indicated they see little urgency in raising interest rates. The markets quickly repriced in favor of a hold in October.\nWarsh, Jefferson and Williams make up an informal decision-making team atop the Fed known as the troika. Warsh could arguably coordinate with the troika even if he doesn't believe in communicating his own views. But outsiders can't be certain that traditional conventions surrounding the public statements of the New York Fed president and the Fed vice chair have survived the leadership transition.\nAnother assumption is that Warsh broadly accepts the FOMC's view of the economy. \"The world has broadly conformed to our expectations,\" Warsh said in Bangkok of the economy since September.\nFed’s Warsh in Bangkok: 'Not in that business anymore' of broadcasting decisions\nThat can be read as saying that relatively hot inflation readings from the consumer and producer price indexes in recent days don't disconfirm the view expressed in the Fed's most recent\nreleased at the conclusion of the September meeting. At the time, the median Fed official believed there would be one more rate hike in 2026 — which left room to pause in October and raise again in December.\nBut Warsh went out of his way at the September press conference to disclaim the SEP. \"Those aren't my forecasts,\" he told a reporter.\nWarsh set out his own idiosyncratic reading of the economy in detail at a closely watched speech in\nat the end of August. It described the economy as growing strongly, with inflation a greater worry than the jobs market. Warsh's strongly hawkish views then set up the FOMC to unanimously raise interest rates in September. That hawkish position relied heavily on what he sees as loose credit conditions.\nhas risen about 0.05 points since Jackson Hole but remains negative, indicating financial conditions have tightened but remain loose overall.\nDoes Warsh believe the market has done his work for him? Is having \"removed a dose of accommodation,\" as he put it Thursday, enough? We can infer these answers, but we won't really know them until the FOMC press conference.\nrose 3.7% for the year through September, while the\ngained 6.3%. The new data implies that the next reading of the Fed's preferred inflation reading, core personal consumption expenditures, will come in at 3.1% year over year. Headline PCE, which will be affected by energy prices, would be higher.\nWarsh has gone out of his way to say that figures at that level are a problem. After economists questioned whether Warsh might tolerate inflation above 2%, he was unequivocal in Jackson Hole: \"There should be no misunderstanding,\" Warsh said. The Fed's 2% PCE goal \"is a firm, fixed target.\"\nWarsh is visibly learning on the job. He may well be coming to understand that the Fed chairman can't operate alone and must rely on the staff and the rest of the FOMC to help him do his job. Likewise, he may discover that markets don't issue a clear signal independently of the Fed.\nRaising rates while the markets strongly expect otherwise is a recipe for volatility. That factor has prompted interventions by officials like Jefferson and Williams in the past. A surprise decision after they were seen to guide the market could dent their credibility and make the Fed's decisions even harder to predict.\nBut how Warsh will settle these matters isn't clear yet. Warsh could go into the October meeting primed to muscle up wavering committee members based on booming demand for AI spending and warm readings in the inflation data. Or he could 