ce": "topic_products", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:34:00+00:00", "text": "Venice Launches Survey Over New Parks - ABC7 WWSB\nVenice Launches Survey Over New Parks ABC7 WWSB", "truncated": false}, {"id": "b8ca153d59482c1ac42c5e11", "title": "Norwegian teen sentenced to 20 years in prison for plotting murder in UK for Iran-linked gang - AP News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMipgFBVV95cUxPMXVGUWJ5OFpmSHYwLXR1eGd4MGZNVTlHNGZYXzJSRWNzQ19EcGNPaWR3d3JUcG5KdzhtZnp2Zk1BTVp1TmthNUtQN082LUo5VUlNS2FiU1IzcTlWcG4zSHdXRGpxRkxidDEyYUZOSkYyc0pBc3k4UFd5NzNpQkhvOVYwMHB2dVdHVjdic0d3V1pIcVJyTU9xNTlFd1FwQlZLS1ZFNXF3?oc=5", "source": "rss_ap_business:google_fallback", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:34:00+00:00", "text": "Norwegian teen sentenced to 20 years in prison for plotting murder in UK for Iran-linked gang - AP News\nNorwegian teen sentenced to 20 years in prison for plotting murder in UK for Iran-linked gang AP News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "8975c0ac1d5dbca600c7328f", "title": "Water service outage planned in Hardeeville, hundreds of customers impacted - WJCL", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihAFBVV95cUxQVTNDc2V0Y1ZnTlN6VWpIeXVFbHpSQ0x6OTZBTnlzSG92bTNZU0Z6eElkeElPR3J5VUFUUFpEdlk1d1pnTE1xQnpLc2hjaXFJMzFLOTZCY2FTYVFQcm11SXlQUDZXZUVKal9ydmZkUmRvSlhXS0REWXB5T3p0QVBIaVhZQ3I?oc=5", "source": "lane_blast_ops", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:34:00+00:00", "text": "Water service outage planned in Hardeeville, hundreds of customers impacted - WJCL\nWater service outage planned in Hardeeville, hundreds of customers impacted WJCL", "truncated": false}, {"id": "175bba3042f3ba76932e8b09", "title": "Don't Expect A U.S.-Mexico Trade Deal Any Time Soon - Jalopnik", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidkFVX3lxTE50LW5Wekw0ZlVESVF1SVRLcHNaVmpUYnh2T2k1eDJQb2p6aXA0LVJfVFhDYWlFLTBGdTdMcUd2X01DRkRIaVdLckNSZUE3NnFYV3hBR3BmczE2Z3FLOWdyaE9RcXpsMEZyUWhMTm5Way14QUt0TFE?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_us", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:33:48+00:00", "text": "Don't Expect A U.S.-Mexico Trade Deal Any Time Soon - Jalopnik\nDon't Expect A U.S.-Mexico Trade Deal Any Time Soon Jalopnik Mexico Negotiations Stall Following Canada Breakdown, Calling Into Question the Future of USMCA WWD Exclusive | Lutnick Throws the Future of U.S.-Mexico Trade Talks Into Further Doubt WSJ Progress toward a US-Mexico tariff deal has stalled, WSJ reports Reuters US commerce secretary’s resistance to cutting tariffs stalls trade talks with Mexico Mexico News Daily", "truncated": false}, {"id": "7078f1786cbe57e01b5f8785", "title": "Albanian protests turn violent: What we know", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqdjv90d1jpko?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:33:32+00:00", "text": "Albanian protests turn violent: What we know\nTirana has seen four days of clashes and the protests are spreading\nProtesters and anti-riot police have clashed in Albania's main cities in scenes not witnessed for decades.\nGovernment buildings have been attacked and some private businesses, too.\nAlbania's capital Tirana has seen months of protests from an anti-government \"Flamingo\" movement, but what started on Tuesday this week was on a different scale.\nAnd the clashes have spread beyond Tirana - to include the main cities of DurrÃ«s, ShkodÃ«r, LezhÃ« and Vlora in the west, GjirokastÃ«r in the south and KorÃ§Ã« in the east.\nThe new demonstrations were announced as a student movement, ostensibly demanding action by the government to help with the high cost of education fees and books - and the higher cost of living.\nSecondary schools joined in, swelling the numbers.\nBefore too long, masked protesters threw stones at police, and they responded with water cannon, tear gas and batons.\nMany were incensed by scenes of a young man being carried away, his broken leg dangling with him in agony.\nApologies were expressed but failed to calm the situation.\nA floor of the education ministry was set on fire, with other government institutions ablaze, too.\nThere have been violent scenes involving protesters and police in central Tirana\nEvery evening, throughout the summer for some 135 days in a row, Albanians in central Tirana would hold pictures of a pink flamingo, calling on Prime Minister Edi Rama - now in his fourth term - to resign.\nThe protests began over a mega-tourism project on the Adriatic coast linked to US President Donald Trump's son-in-law, Jared Kushner. Demonstrators said the bird would become extinct on an island near the coastal city of Vlora, if the plans for a luxury development went ahead.\nThe public anger comes as allegations of corruption among ministers and other senior officials grow louder.\nOne deputy prime minister has fled the country, another has been ordered to step down as investigations continue.\nFor almost five months over the summer, Albanians carrying images of flamingoes called on the prime minister to resign\nStudent protesters attack government buildings in Albania\n'Flamingo Revolution' takes off as thousands demand Albanian PM's resignation\nKushner-backed luxury resort plan sparks protests in Albania\nSo has the Flamingo movement radicalised?\nThe flamingo protesters say they have no links to any political party - one of their slogans being \"Rama in jail, Berisha in jail\". While Rama is prime minister, Sali Berisha is Albania's main opposition leader - as well as a former prime minister and president.\nThey were attended by young and old, parents with children and sometimes their grandparents. Some of the participants took pride in cleaning up after every night.\nBut after months and an unusually hot summer, their numbers dwindled - with no sign that the government was meeting their demands.\nThe current movement appears far more violent. Dozens of police have been hurt, and the eyes of Europe are now watching what happens next in a country viewed as one of the top candidates for EU membership.\nSeveral young protesters told the BBC that what was going on now was a new phase born of the previous movement.\nFive months of peaceful protest had been \"met with humiliation, ridicule, mockery,\" complained one young woman called Livia. \"That was a clear message from the government that our voice will not be allowed to be heard.\"\ntold the BBC's What in the World programme\nshe was worried about what might happen next: \"I am very terrified because the media and the government is reacting, especially now. It was not just my voice, it is the voice of Albanian youth.\"\nAn overlap between the two sets of protests may well have occurred, even though flamingo leaders have not publicly stated so and no representative of the student movement has come forward.\nAfter all, their main concerns relate to corruption, which the prime minister says must be investigated by the justice system.\nHow is the government handling the unrest?\nRama has been touring some of the damaged buildings and blaming Sali Berisha for instigating chaos.\n\"All of this is the work of the very same hand - the hand that has gripped the Albanian opposition by the throat, the hand that has burned and destroyed Albania more than once, and is now prepared to burn and destroy it again to prevent any progress. It is a blind struggle, fuelled by the most irrational hatred,\" he said on Friday.\nThe prime minister has ruled out resigning, promising to \"listen, address legitimate concerns and keep Albania moving forward\".\nBerisha's Democratic Party has often tried to get people out to oust Rama.\nBut the number of his loyal supporters, who have also protested for months and at times clashed with police, has been low in comparison to the current attendance.\nSo there is some scepticism about Berisha's ability to mastermind the trouble, though he may well seek to capitalise on it.\nOn the other hand, the Albanian media have suggested that secret services of foreign countries may be involved.\nUnfounded allegations have been made against Serbia, with commentators suggesting Belgrade is envious of Albania being often cited as potentially next in line to join the EU.\nIndeed, without mentioning names, Prime Minister Rama echoed the allegation on Friday.\n\"These are foreign hands that do not wish for Albania to be at peace... they do not want Albania to be part of the united European family,\" he said, adding: \"Do you think it is a coincidence that every time Albania receives another green light... or draws closer to that great ambition - that historic objective - flames suddenly erupt? It is no coincidence at all! None of what is happening now is a coincidence.\"\nAlthough the street violence has taken place in many cities, it remains contained for the time being.\nThe UK government has urged visitors to check media\nfor latest information, stay away from \"demonstrations, large-scale gatherings or political rallies\" and follow advice of local authorities.\nAlbania is a popular tourist destination, and the government in Tirana has warned that violence tarnishes the country's image, affecting both tourism and its European path.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "4d287224f79f466d215bff19", "title": "Albanian protests turn violent: What we know - BBC", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiW0FVX3lxTE1ySEtzaHdiM1ZHbERROV9lYm9MTkFvT2FhTGJBcThrTDhuSDRNYjZZek9ndVlVSjF1TEZEN0pMWXBJYlIwcFdjTDk0MWJRTFc1LUxVSG5sVVJCLU0?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:33:32+00:00", "text": "Albanian protests turn violent: What we know - BBC\nAlbanian protests turn violent: What we know BBC Thousands march in Albanian capital as anti-government protests spread Reuters Albania PM refuses to resign as student protesters attack government buildings Financial Times ‘Boiling point’: Why students are protesting against Albania’s Edi Rama Al Jazeera Albanian PM blames 'foreign hands' for violent protests across country Euronews.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "db