Liga president resigns after comparing attacks on referees with Nazi propaganda - Toronto Star\nMadrid demands La Liga president resigns after comparing attacks on referees with Nazi propaganda Toronto Star", "truncated": false}, {"id": "90c35b7d95568d42c02b76e1", "title": "RAF remains liable for medical expenses paid by schemes, SCA rules - Moonstone Information Refinery", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimgFBVV95cUxQYXVnOGNJWkJDNTNoa0JfWDJPTEhfWVZaLXpzQXh3NktlV1ZqYzhZdVNaUmFrd0lJU18xTEh4VUgxMFJVR2JoNGZfSm1pT21BN1V2T0dBbHNJVlp3ejRzb0RiVDktbGt6eXpiSkNZeHR2SVItWDNFNWNGc2lUaWxSQzUwUG91WE1iODdxZ1hsTFlSaFg0a00zM19B?oc=5", "source": "lane_blast_legal", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:27:12+00:00", "text": "RAF remains liable for medical expenses paid by schemes, SCA rules - Moonstone Information Refinery\nRAF remains liable for medical expenses paid by schemes, SCA rules Moonstone Information Refinery", "truncated": false}, {"id": "a8983c7916eea107ce41e196", "title": "Femicide in Iran: When 'honor' becomes a death sentence", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/femicide-in-iran-when-honor-becomes-a-death-sentence/a-79681977?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf", "source": "rss_dw_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:27:00+00:00", "text": "Femicide in Iran: When 'honor' becomes a death sentence\nThe 2022 death of Mahsa Amini in Iranian police custody gave rise to the global Women, Life, Freedom movement against gender-based violence and discrimination\nImage: Andrea Ronchini/NurPhoto/picture alliance\nThe life of Maryam Yarmohammadi came to a violent end in the early hours of October 5, when the 28-year-old mother of two was attacked with knives by her brother and husband on Qeshm Island in southern\n. Her genitalia were mutilated. She died shortly afterward in hospital. Both her husband and brother were arrested.\nMedia reported that about 50 relatives gathered outside the hospital to protest the arrests. They reportedly believed that death had been the \"least severe punishment\" Maryam Yarmohammadi deserved.\nWhat the young woman's relatives considered her guilty of has not been publicly reported. Yet both the circumstances of the case and the extreme violence involved follow a familiar pattern seen\nIn such cases, the underlying belief is often that a man must erase a perceived \"dishonor\" brought upon the family by a sister, daughter or wife. Failure to do so may expose him to rejection, humiliation or social exclusion within his community.\nAll the Ayatollah's men — ensuring the regime's survival\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nWhat constitutes \"honor\" or \"proper conduct\" is often defined by family expectations or social and cultural norms. In some traditionally conservative regions, even laughing loudly or engaging in a friendly conversation with an unrelated man may be viewed as a violation of these norms.\nWomen accused of such transgressions are frequently denied any meaningful opportunity to defend themselves against the allegations.\nFor years, women's rights activists in Iran have sought to establish nationwide support networks offering counseling and protection to women at risk. One such initiative is the Campaign to End Honor Killings.\nRezvan Moghaddam, a spokesperson for the campaign, told DW that there appeared to be no activist or local group in the region capable of supporting Maryam Yarmohammadi.\nThrough civil society networks, the campaign has in some cases succeeded in relocating threatened women to safe locations and securing shelter for them.\nMoghaddam said that Maryam Yarmohammadi's death should not be interpreted as criticism of women's rights activists in those regions: \"I myself come from Khuzestan in southern Iran and am fully aware of the restrictions that exist there.\"\nBased on interpretations of Shariah, or Islamic law, Iran's laws do not treat women as equal citizens in many areas of life. Their ability to make decisions about education, employment or travel may depend on the consent of their fathers or, later, their husbands.\nEven after a woman's death, male relatives can, under certain legal provisions, influence the course of criminal proceedings.\nIranian criminal law applies the principle of retribution in certain offenses, including murder. In homicide cases, the victim's relatives may choose either to demand retribution, which can result in the death penalty, or to pardon the perpetrator.\nThese provisions raise particular concerns in\n. When an alleged perpetrator is also among the relatives empowered to influence decisions on punishment or forgiveness, a serious conflict of interest emerges.\nSuch situations expose the protection gaps critics say exist within Iran's justice system regarding violence against women.\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nAccording to a 2020 study by Iran's Police Academy, honor killings — defined as the murder of a woman by male relatives such as her father, brother or uncle for allegedly violating social norms of modesty and family honor — accounted for up to 45% of all murders in some traditionally conservative provinces in western and southern Iran.\nIn March 2025, the UN Special Rapporteur on human rights in Iran reported that at least 179 women were murdered by men within their family circles in 2024. These cases were recorded as femicides.\nIt was the brutality of Maryam Yarmohammadi's murder that shocked Iranians. The murder triggered a wave of grief and outrage on social media. Many women reacted strongly to what they saw as a femicide driven by hatred in the name of \"honor\" and \"shame.\"\nAt the same time, numerous comments focused on victim-blaming, or holding Maryam Yarmohammadi responsible for her own death. Others described the killing as the \"punishment of an unfaithful woman\" or attempted to justify it by \"understanding the men's anger.\"\nMen have been socialized to believe they are responsible for controlling women and monitoring their behavior and decisions, the psychoanalyst Saba Alaleh said. Male identity, she said, is tied to a woman's \"obedience.\"\nViolence becomes normalized when beliefs, social approval and silence reinforce one another within this structure, Alaleh said.\n\"When cultural, social, political, economic, legal and educational factors are closely intertwined, they acquire enormous power,\" she said. \"Under such conditions, women are also highly economically dependent and lack sufficient social support. Preventive mechanisms are absent, as well. All of this increases the risk of violence.\"\nIranian women unveiled: A shift in hijab enforcement?\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nAtefeh Rangriz, a sociologist and women's rights activist, told DW: \"Femicides have always existed, and their political manifestations can be traced throughout history, from forced unveiling [under Reza Shah Pahlavi in the 1930s] to the slogan 'Either the headscarf or blows to the head' in the Islamic Republic.\"\nRangriz said women understood the path toward liberation and, despite tremendous obstacles, had achieved significant progress over the past decades.\nWomen in Iran have been driving since 1940. They have been allowed to vote and run for political office since 1963. Women account for half of all university graduates in the country. The number of women filing for divorce or mutually ending unhappy marriages has also continued to rise.\n, which emerged from the nationwide protests\nfollowing the death of Jina Mahsa Amini in police custody in 2022\n, has further transformed public perceptions of women in Iran.\n\"Women have learned to focus on the present rather than postponing justice to the future. They demand it now,\" Rangriz said. \"They want to exercise autonomy over their own bodies, live as they choose, and free themselves from the structures of domination, oppression and exploitation. It is a kind of radical revolution.\"\nno longer wear the mandatory hijab in public\n, openly defying the Islamic Republic's dress regulations.\nYet it remains unknown what the young mother Maryam Yarmohammadi wanted, or how she wished to live. She was brutally killed.\nIf you or someone you know have experienced gender-based violence, the website\nlists trustworthy helplines and NGOs that offer support in almost every country in the world.\nThis article was originally written in German.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "0b1f3b77f1c5e61ddcd33ac2", "title": "Iran: When 'honor' becomes a death sentence for women", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/iran-when-honor-becomes-a-death-sentence-for-women/a-79681977?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf", "source": "rss_dw_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:27:00+00:00", "text": "Iran: When 'honor' becomes a death sentence for women\nThe 2022 death of Mahsa Amini in Iranian police custody gave rise to the global Women, Life, Freedom movement against gender-based violence and discrimination\nImage: Andrea Ronchini/NurPhoto/picture alliance\nThe life of Maryam Yarmohammadi came to a violent end in the early hours of October 5, when the 28-year-old mother of two was attacked with knives by her brother and husband on Qeshm Island in southern\n. Her genitalia were mutilated. She died shortly afterward in hospital. Both her husband and brother were arrested.\nMedia reported that about 50 relatives gathered outside the hospital to protest the arrests. They reportedly believed that death had been the \"least severe punishment\" Maryam Yarmohammadi deserved.\nWhat the young woman's relatives considered her guilty of has not been publicly reported. Yet both the circumstances of the case and the extreme violence involved follow a familiar pattern seen\nIn such cases, the underlying belief is often that a man must erase a perceived \"dishonor\" brought upon the family by a sister, daughter or wife. Failure to do so may expose him to rejection, humiliation or social exclusion within his community.\nAll the Ayatollah's men — ensuring the regime's survival\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nWhat constitutes \"honor\" or \"proper conduct\" is often defined by family expectations or social and cultural norms. In some traditionally conservative regions, even laughing loudly or engaging in a friendly conversation with an unrelated man may be viewed as a violation of these norms.\nWomen accused of such transgressions are frequently denied any meaningful opportunity to defend themselves against the allegations.\nFor years, women's rights activists in Iran have sought to establish nationwide support networks offering counseling and protection to women at risk. One such initiative is the Campaign to End Honor Killings.\nRezvan Moghaddam, a spokesperson for the campaign, told DW that there appeared to be no activist or local group in the region capable of supporting Maryam Yarmohammadi.\nThrough civil society networks, the campaign has in some cases succeeded in relocating threatened women to safe locations and securing shelter for them.\nMoghaddam said that Maryam Yarmohammadi's death should not be interpreted as criticism of women's rights activists in those regions: \"I myself come from Khuzestan in southern Iran and am fully aware of the restrictions that exist there.\"\nBased on interpretations of Shariah, or Islamic law, Iran's laws do not treat women as equal citizens in many areas of life. Their ability to make decisions about education, employment or travel may depend on the consent of their fathers or, later, their husbands.\nEven after a woman's death, male relatives can, under certain legal provisions, influence the course of criminal proceedings.\nIranian criminal law applies the principle of retribution in certain offenses, including murder. In homicide cases, the victim's relatives may choose either to demand retribution, which can result in the death penalty, or to pardon the perpetrator.\nThese provisions raise particular concerns in\n. When an alleged perpetrator is also among the relatives empowered to influence decisions on punishment or forgiveness, a serious conflict of interest emerges.\nSuch situations expose the protection gaps critics say exist within Iran's justice system regarding violence against women.\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nAccording to a 2020 study by Iran's Police Academy, honor killings — defined as the murder of a woman by male relatives such as her father, brother or uncle for allegedly violating social norms of modesty and family honor — accounted for up to 45% of all murders in some traditionally conservative provinces in western and southern Iran.\nIn March 2025, the UN Special Rapporteur on human rights in Iran reported that at least 179 women were murdered by men within their family circles in 2024. These cases were recorded as femicides.\nIt was the brutality of Maryam Yarmohammadi's murder that shocked Iranians. The murder triggered a wave of grief and outrage on social media. Many women reacted strongly to what they saw as a femicide driven by hatred in the name of \"honor\" and \"shame.\"\nAt the same time, numerous comments focused on victim-blaming, or holding Maryam Yarmohammadi responsible for her own death. Others described the killing as the \"punishment of an unfaithful woman\" or attempted to justify it by \"understanding the men's anger.\"\nMen have been socialized to believe they are responsible for controlling women and monitoring their behavior and decisions, the psychoanalyst Saba Alaleh said. Male identity, she said, is tied to a woman's \"obedience.\"\nViolence becomes normalized when beliefs, social approval and silence reinforce one another within this structure, Alaleh said.\n\"When cultural, social, political, economic, legal and educational factors are closely intertwined, they acquire enormous power,\" she said. \"Under such conditions, women are also highly economically dependent and lack sufficient social support. Preventive mechanisms are absent, as well. All of this increases the risk of violence.\"\nIranian women unveiled: A shift in hijab enforcement?\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nAtefeh Rangriz, a sociologist and women's rights activist, told DW: \"Femicides have always existed, and their political manifestations can be traced throughout history, from forced unveiling [under Reza Shah Pahlavi in the 1930s] to the slogan 'Either the headscarf or blows to the head' in the Islamic Republic.\"\nRangriz said women understood the path toward liberation and, despite tremendous obstacles, had achieved significant progress over the past decades.\nWomen in Iran have been driving since 1940. They have been allowed to vote and run for political office since 1963. Women account for half of all university graduates in the country. The number of women filing for divorce or mutually ending unhappy marriages has also continued to rise.\n, which emerged from the nationwide protests\nfollowing the death of Jina Mahsa Amini in police custody in 2022\n, has further transformed public perceptions of women in Iran.\n\"Women have learned to focus on the present rather than postponing justice to the future. They demand it now,\" Rangriz said. \"They want to exercise autonomy over their own bodies, live as they choose, and free themselves from the structures of domination, oppression and exploitation. It is a kind of radical revolution.\"\nno longer wear the mandatory hijab in public\n, openly defying the Islamic Republic's dress regulations.\nYet it remains unknown what the young mother Maryam Yarmohammadi wanted, or how she wished to live. She was brutally killed.\nIf you or someone you know have experienced gender-based violence, the website\nlists trustworthy helplines 