news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi4wFBVV95cUxQWjhqbDJ4SnRCY3NDTG02eEpQN0xvSFBmLWJ5SHIwLVRhSjk5YURub3dKd2owb2huZ2FsekJKcDlGenR0UndybkIza2VFdEJuenp3bnhlZTlEbklpQV9uNm5ja1pTNjVfV2E4NmN0X0RnVkpJNVljaVp5bzNGV2d3cEQ1cGl1Z3pkM2REV1g3bVgwU0o5Z0FReThyaWtrdUZGZ2g2SGRRR1VETFhEdnVINlFLZ3FwVTZsbmc1N3VrLTR", "source": "lane_factor_impulse", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:21:58+00:00", "text": "State regulators approve nearly $140M rate hike for Consumers Energy natural gas customers - ClickOnDetroit | WDIV Local 4\nState regulators approve nearly $140M rate hike for Consumers Energy natural gas customers ClickOnDetroit | WDIV Local 4 Michigan approves 4.3% natural gas rate hike for Consumers Energy customers amid AG criticism WZZM13.com MPSC approves nearly $140 million Consumers Energy natural gas rate increase WILX Natural gas rates to rise by 4.3% following $139M Consumers Energy rate hike approval WWMT Natural gas rates set to increase for 2.1M Michigan utility customers MLive.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "8d0fd2ec2b13a5d3856d521c", "title": "Tekfen Holding Says Kenan Alpdundar Steps Down As CFO As Of October 15 - TradingView", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi1wFBVV95cUxPV1JHMEY5SWphNTZHQmhTMnNmNl9KWU9kNGcwZmRUQ0t4WWEtVjJZSkhFSTlJellZU2N4c2t0ZnlxUkhFcHRYM2pQdHpIYTJUWVZfWk8yME8yMXZBN0poQm9oT1lJNHZqSHVtZzhIWVJqUXFpTzhIZjJhaTlBYVBZZUZWNGk5dVNvalRCdmhacnVTTkhmU0RkN0lIM3NaSUU4NERNdjNnNWZ1LXlrRGhYNnB2LUFlVmZTaVpwU25LRTZ", "source": "lane_key_person", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:21:42+00:00", "text": "Tekfen Holding Says Kenan Alpdundar Steps Down As CFO As Of October 15 - TradingView\nTekfen Holding Says Kenan Alpdundar Steps Down As CFO As Of October 15 TradingView", "truncated": false}, {"id": "bbfd72c7e959fc3e1efbee32", "title": "Dad fined after son, 2, died in Cyprus hotel fall", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cvj6j1klez88o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:21:32+00:00", "text": "Dad fined after son, 2, died in Cyprus hotel fall\nJoshua Foster \"lost control\" of his son after placing him on a windowsill, a court in Cyprus heard\nA father whose two-year-old boy fell from a fourth floor window of a Cyprus hotel has been fined â¬3,000 (Â£2,500) for causing his death.\nJoshua Foster thought the window was closed when he placed his son on a sill to show him the view of a waterpark\n, Paphos District Court previously heard.\nThe toddler fell nearly 40ft (12.1m) at the five-star King Evelthon Beach Hotel and Resort on 12 July, the first day of the family's holiday in Chlorakas.\nFoster, 37, from Essex, admitted causing death through a reckless, rash or dangerous act and was sentenced earlier.\nHe told reporters afterwards: \"Thank you for the support. I'm looking forward to going home and being with my wife and daughter.\"\nLater, the family said in a statement: \"We are relieved that this process is now over, but for our family, the consequences of this tragedy will never end.\n\"Our son was deeply loved and will be forever missed. His loss is something we carry with us every day.\"\nFoster, an auditor, arrived in Cyprus hours before the incident with his wife, two children and in-laws.\nThey enjoyed various activities and swimming, before getting ready for dinner in the evening.\nThe incident happened on the first day of the family's holiday at the five-star King Evelthon Beach Hotel and Resort on the Mediterranean island's west coast\nAt about 18:00, Foster, his father-in-law and son were walking towards a lift when the boy noticed a waterpark outside.\nFoster pushed the lift button, placed the child on the windowsill and the \"inconceivable\" unfolded before he could react, the court heard.\nThe boy was taken to Paphos General Hospital and later pronounced dead.\n\"The window did not appear to carry markings that would necessarily have alerted the defendant to the fact that one section was open,\" said District Judge Froso Christodoulidou.\nHis failure to establish that the window was not closed led to the tragic result, the judge added.\n\"It was a momentary error which created a dangerous situation with fatal consequences,\" he said.\nThe judge added there was \"no evidence that the defendant deliberately exposed the child to danger\", according to a translation of proceedings.\nShe added no sentence could outweigh the \"personal consequences of the loss of his child\".\nLawyer Alexandros Alexandrou said his client's remorse was \"clear\"\nIn a voluntary statement to police, Foster said when he placed his son on the windowsill, the boy \"fell before he had a chance to grab him\".\nWhile Foster had alcohol in his system, it did not form a significant part of the case against him and his lawyer Alexandros Alexandrou said there was no testimony he was drunk.\nAfter the sentencing, Foster's lawyer, Alexandros Alexandrou, told reporters the case should have \"never gone to court\".\nHe said Foster was held in custody for eight days immediately after the incident and he had to call his daughter from custody to explain what had happened.\n\"Within this three months, there was a lost opportunity for this man to grieve as he wanted to, to be with his family,\" he said.\nHe described his client as an \"incredible father\".\n\"[He is] an exemplary family man with a beautiful family behind him, that support him, that embraces him, that has always been by his side.\"\nDo you have a story suggestion for Essex? Contact us below.\nDad sees son fall to death from Cyprus hotel window\nFather admits causing death of his son in hotel fall", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "c78e18cd5b7ceb3bc7357589", "title": "Tesla: Roadster Shows Unique Technology That Will Make a Strong Halo Car - Morningstar", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiswFBVV95cUxPejdoVUZHa0tnblJTUnllOUQ3MlpPTERfUUZMWkl5T2ZBbl9FS1JiZU1ZdkxjRFozeWN1QzNOSE5wa2dHLVg2TUZlMEVSaGxFYUpVUmRULVA4YU03bEdhN2lDU2xCYm4xRGxLUnJNMzZTTXMtQjNmUmliT18xWE