ls CNBC CNBC Senators Demand Investigation Into Ukraine Satellite Disruption The New York Times", "truncated": false}, {"id": "0446e5ab7c62b132400021a8", "title": "US senators call for investigation into Ukraine's Starlink restrictions", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cq398zl8jmvdo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:19:50+00:00", "text": "US senators call for investigation into Ukraine's Starlink restrictions\nDemocratic Party Senator Jeanne Shaheen has spearheaded the cross-party statement from several US senators\nA bipartisan group of US senators has signed a letter demanding an urgent investigation into disruptions to SpaceX's Starlink internet network in Ukraine.\nStarlink is a vital tool allowing Kyiv to guide drones to Russian targets and protect its own skies, but on Tuesday night Ukraine said it had unexpectedly restricted how fast drones could fly while maintaining internet connection.\nIn the letter, sent to the US defence department inspector general, the senators express \"grave concern\" about reports of speed curbs between 12 and 14 October.\nAsked whether the US might have had a role in the slowdown, a US official told the BBC \"no such request was ever made by the president [Donald Trump]\".\nThe letter, led by Jeanne Shaheen - a Democrat on the Senate's Foreign Relations Committee - calls for the inspector general to find out whether the restrictions were caused by the US defence department or its contractors.\nIt says \"restrictions reportedly degraded Ukraine's ability to conduct defensive operations against Russian drone and missile attacks on civilian infrastructure\".\nStarlink, which is owned by Elon Musk's SpaceX, restricted speed limits on Tuesday night to 100mph (160km/h), meaning any weapons that travelled faster than that lost connectivity to the network.\nOn Friday, Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelensky told a news conference in Brussels that Starlink services were still being restored in his country.\n\"Has the speed been fully restored to what it was before? No. Is the speed being restored now? Yes,\" he said.\nThe senators also say they are \"deeply concerned\" by reports of disruptions or \"glitches\" affecting intelligence-sharing and threat-warning capabilities relied upon by personnel operating in Ukraine.\n\"We are particularly alarmed by incidents in which U.S. diplomatic, military, and security personnel were unable to reach designated bunkers during Russian air raids,\" the statement says.\n\"Given the potentially deadly consequences, these incidents require immediate investigation,\" the senators add.\nThe letter's signatories include Republican Senators Thom Tillis, Susan Collins, Lisa Murkowski and John Cornyn, alongside Senators Richard Durbin, Jack Reed, Richard Blumenthal and Christopher Coons from the Democrats.\nMusk says US government may be behind slowdown of Ukraine drone speeds\nWhy is Starlink internet so important on the battlefield in Ukraine?\nthe US government may be behind restrictions.\nOn Wednesday Musk wrote on X that \"Starshield systems are owned and controlled by the US government. Any limits on speed or usage are decided by the US military and ultimately [the US president]\".\nStarlink is a civilian service that uses satellites in low Earth orbit to provide internet access, particularly in remote areas. Starshield, which relies on the Starlink network, is designed for government and military use. Both are owned by Musk's SpaceX.\nZelensky has accused the US of withdrawing intelligence-sharing\nto pressure Kyiv into ending attacks against Russian energy sites.\nOn Thursday, Zelensky said Ukraine's attacks would continue after Russia had continued striking Ukraine's energy infrastructure during an \"energy ceasefire\" announced by Trump on Sunday.\nZelensky said he had restricted attacks for three days and would agree to the \"energy ceasefire\" if Moscow also abided by it. However, he accused Russia of ignoring the proposed deal within hours of it being announced.\nThe Kremlin never said it had agreed to the deal and Russia said Kyiv had also tried to hit refineries on Wednesday - which Ukraine denied.\nZelensky has also criticised the agreement between Russia and the US to release 300,000 tonnes of diesel to the US and global marketplace, struck last week, as the US effectively investing in Russia's war effort.\nThat deal was the latest effort by Trump to lower fuel prices in the US ahead of the midterm elections, after months spent grappling with the political consequences of the Iran war, which has sent fuel costs skyrocketing.\nWatch: What does Starlink have to do with the Ukraine-Russia war?", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "290a4465238e8a63f28de5f0", "title": "France's Macron warns EU leaders they need budget deal before year-end, say diplomats - Reuters", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiugFBVV95cUxPSjFjRDcxQzRjUmMyTTlYYktfaDhIVlJWc3NjRDhoMTNObl9PeVd0S1NYNm9DTVE0WktocXh3VVZDQ0p4RWI0T2R2cElFbzRIblhwc2xsRi1QRVlndm1BNnJ0Q1ZfM3k1WVIzZy1INjM2cV84VU93Qnp6T1VwVGJ4Tjg4WmltazNKY1lRbGpvamJIRTU0MnNVQ3RaMjh6ZzQwVFFzWXRBM1JRcmdnZ1pYN25CY1B3R3BoT0E?oc=5", "source": "publisher_reuters", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:19:43+00:00", "text": "France's Macron warns EU leaders they need budget deal before year-end, say diplomats - Reuters\nFrance's Macron warns EU leaders they need budget deal before year-end, say diplomats Reuters", "truncated": false}, {"id": "a46fb33ff69de8a7fc307f73", "title": "Qatar announces ‘massive’ solar plant project in Lebanon", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/16/qatar-announces-massive-solar-plant-project-in-lebanon?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:19:36+00:00", "text": "Qatar announces ‘massive’ solar plant project in Lebanon\nShare Qatar announces ‘massive’ solar plant project in Lebanon on social media\nLebanon's prime minister welcomed Qatar's PM in Beirut on Friday [Hassan Ammar/AP]\nQatar’s prime minister has announced plans to set up what he described as a “massive” solar plant in Lebanon to help ease its\nSheikh Mohammed bin Abdulrahman bin Jassim Al Thani made the announcement during his visit to Beirut on Friday, after meeting his Lebanese counterpart, Nawaf Salam.\nHow Israel’s ‘Yellow Line’ is reshaping southern Lebanon\nIsraeli quadcopter drone drops bomb in Lebanon\nRubio presses Lebanon on confiscation of weapons from Hezbollah\n“We have agreed to establish a comprehensive framework for cooperation in the energy sector and to present viable solutions,” Sheikh Mohammed said.\nQatarEnergy has reached an agreement with French company TotalEnergies to jointly develop a solar power plant in Lebanon as an initial project.\nThe aim is to develop a major investment project “that would benefit the Lebanese economy, as well as the Qatari companies and entities participating in it”, he added.\nPrime Minister Salam said he would put the plans for Lebanon’s electricity sector to the Lebanese Cabinet soon.\n“The Qatari side will fund, implement and activate the project without any cost on the Lebanese state,” Lebanon’s leader said, adding that such a move is important because of the country’s financial crisis.\nLebanon’s state electricity company is one of the biggest sources of debt, haemorrhaging about $40bn over past decades with a bloated workforce and outdated infrastructure. The company provides only a few hours of electricity a day and the state, until a year ago, took advances from the Central Bank when diesel fuel ran out.\nSheikh Mohammed said Doha stands in “solidarity with the Lebanese people” and the entire region “in this dire hour”. Lebanon has been struggling with multiple energy crises in recent years and the situation has been compounded by the country’s financial collapse and\nEarlier in the day, the Qatari PM also met Lebanon’s president and parliament speaker.\nAmong the topics discussed was the situation in southern Lebanon, where Israel continues its assault, capturing dozens of towns and villages.\nSheikh Mohammed said “Israeli violations within the Lebanese territories” were a large part of his talks.\nHe said that Israel should withdraw from Lebanon and cease its attacks, adding that Qatar is working in coordination with the United States to help lay the groundwork for future reconstruction in southern Lebanon to allow displaced people to return to their homes.\nIsraeli attacks on southern Lebanon have continued despite a ceasefire agreed in June. Under the agreement brokered by the US, the Lebanese government is to disarm Iran-backed Hezbollah. In return, Israeli forces will gradually withdraw from southern areas that they occupy. However, Hezbollah is not part of the agreement and has refused to disarm until Israel withdraws from the country.\nLebanese authorities say Israeli attacks have killed more than 4,300 people in the country since March. About 1.2 million people were also displaced in Lebanon at the height of the war.\nAlthough most have returned home since the June ceasefire, more than 300,000 people remain displaced, according to the International Organization for Migration.\nLebanon’s PM Salam thanked Qatar for its “continuous” support during some of Lebanon’s “darkest hours”.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "81ef68f20ce0f717227a41b6", "title": "Senate Passes Bill To Establish Federal College Of Agriculture In Borno - News Agency of Nigeria", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiQEFVX3lxTE5fclBCelliLXpTYXpZdnN6RF9wVE1ub2lCVDA0VldhN0FoT3BMX2xZQnJZRDlXZFMyTlZNSzZVTkc?oc=5", "source": "topic_legislation", "published_at": "2026-10-16T14:19:19+00:00", "text": "Senate Passes Bill To Establish Federal College Of Agriculture In Borno - News Agency of Nigeria\nSenate Passes Bill To Establish Federal College Of Agriculture In Borno News Agency of Nigeria", "truncated": false}, {"id": "0d820e53ef64c87b5182