d Proposed REG-120137-19 Under Section 6311 - Current Federal Tax Developments", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimwJBVV95cUxPM0dMdFpNM2x5c0FHcDZObk5lUV9NaFJnYzZTbUR2eThEQXBzU2Rad3VSOHYtLXFSS2dVTngzWV9hWUtKZXZkSl9lZVdieFJRUkpGR29PNzJJR0RWQ1M5SExvUjVtM3pvZDFwX1hiUmFlYzNsWnFwWjl2Qi1zSnZzMXdlOUtpcDdTbkZybDU1OFJDMzlYZzlpMGF5RVZEU0l0dFJNakJpWHRzS2Z2N3IycXpta290eXZRWHZFc1A1NGp", "source": "topic_government", "published_at": "2026-10-16T13:26:55+00:00", "text": "IRS Modernizes Tax Payment Regulations: An Analysis of Final Treasury Decision 10060 and Proposed REG-120137-19 Under Section 6311 - Current Federal Tax Developments\nIRS Modernizes Tax Payment Regulations: An Analysis of Final Treasury Decision 10060 and Proposed REG-120137-19 Under Section 6311 Current Federal Tax Developments", "truncated": false}, {"id": "b3c55876a31ec01c7bfed1b3", "title": "Palestinians, US journalist attacked in West Bank pogrom: What we know", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/16/palestinians-us-journalist-attacked-in-west-bank-pogrom-what-we-know?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-16T13:26:47+00:00", "text": "Palestinians, US journalist attacked in West Bank pogrom: What we know\nShare Palestinians, US journalist attacked in West Bank pogrom: What we know on social media\nHeidi Levine, a photojournalist with The Washington Post, speaks to journalists at the hospital after sustaining multiple injuries during an attack by Israeli settlers on Palestinians harvesting olives, in Ramallah in the Israeli-occupied West Bank, October 16, 2026. [Reuters/ Ali Sawafta]\nPalestinian olive pickers and a photojournalist working for The Washington Post in the occupied West Bank on Friday, beating villagers and setting olive groves ablaze.\nThe attack was among several targeting Palestinians trying to gather their olive harvest, with further assaults and arson reported in nearby communities as well.\nMalaysia’s welcome for Min Aung Hlaing undermines its stand on justice\nPakistan orders school inspections following deadly roof collapse\nFuel tanker fire engulfs market in southern Nigeria\nThese incidents are just the latest in weeks of intensifying pogroms on local communities in the West Bank by Israelis from illegal settlements who are largely protected – or at least unrestricted – by Israeli forces.\nVeteran journalist Heidi Levine, 64, was taken to hospital after the assault on Friday in Abu Falah, northeast of Ramallah. A Palestinian man was also shot in the thigh by Israeli soldiers, according to a witness who spoke to Al Jazeera and the Palestinian news agency Wafa.\nDozens of masked settlers attacked Palestinian farmers harvesting olives, throwing stones, using slingshots and setting trees on fire.\nLevine, an American freelance photojournalist who works for The Washington Post, fell to the ground during the attack before she was set upon, witnesses said. “We looked and saw that there was a journalist … on the ground with people surrounding her, beating her, clubbing her, robbing her, pulling her up, beating her again and kicking her,” Jasper Nathaniel, an independent journalist who witnessed the attack, told Al Jazeera.\nLevine later told reporters that her attackers “just kept kicking me and kicking me, like probably 50 times”.\nLevine was wearing a protective vest marked “PRESS” on the front and back. Her cameras and phone were taken from her during the assault, the Washington Post reported.\nNathaniel said he and others managed to reach her when the settlers briefly backed away, but she struggled to stand. “She was in very, very bad shape. She couldn’t really get up off the ground,” he said.\nAs they helped her towards paramedics, settlers charged again, throwing more stones, he added. Levine was eventually taken to hospital in Ramallah.\nNathaniel said Israeli soldiers arrived after the settler assault and opened fire and used tear gas against Palestinians, shooting a man in the leg. “They do nothing to stop the settlers, and they speed into town and start firing their guns and tear-gassing, and they shoot a Palestinian man in the leg,” he said.\n“They’re pointing their guns at the paramedics who are helping him, shoving journalists, including myself,” Nathaniel said, adding that the man was eventually taken away in an ambulance.\nWafa also reported that Israeli forces fired live ammunition and tear gas at Palestinians during the attack. After the soldiers left, Palestinians returned to harvesting their olives, Nathaniel added.\nMatt Murray, executive editor of The Washington Post, said in a statement: “We are outraged by this unwarranted attack on a journalist doing her job and urge a thorough investigation and follow-through from authorities.”\nUS Senator Chris Van Hollen wrote in a post on X, that the “repeated violence and brazen impunity for attacks” against Americans and Palestinians alike are “unacceptable”. “When will Trump and Rubio DO SOMETHING?”\nHe urged the administration to hold those responsible to account.\nIn posts on X, US Congressman Ro Khanna highlighted the assault on Levine, saying that attackers “picked her up, then threw her down again and kept kicking”.\nKhanna called for an end to US assistance, saying: “We need to cut off aid to Israel that is used to fund settler activity, and stop the sales of these weapons.”\nCongresswoman Rosa DeLauro also called for accountability. “If the settlers will do this to an American journalist on camera, in broad daylight, imagine what Palestinian families face when no one is watching,” she wrote in a post on X.\n“Those responsible for this attack must be held accountable.”\nWhere else have olive pickers been attacked?\nThere was violence in other parts of the occupied West Bank on Friday, as well. In Burqa, east of Ramallah, four Palestinians were reported to have been injured in a settler attack on olive pickers. In nearby Turmus Aya, settlers assaulted farmers and burned vehicles and olive trees. A civil defence volunteer was also injured, according to Wafa.\nThe attacks follow the killing of Ali Hassan Yasser al-Bazzari, 44, during an assault on Palestinians gathering olives near Burqa, northwest of Nablus, on October 7. Witnesses reported that settlers and Israeli soldiers attacked the harvesters.\nJournalists documenting the violence have also been targeted. On October 6, armed settlers beat Palestinian AFP photographer Jaafar Ashtiyeh, 58, with rifle butts near Aqraba, south of Nablus. He was taken to hospital with injuries to his head and torso, according to witnesses, relatives and colleagues.\nFirefighters douse a burned car with water after Israelis from illegal settlements attacked a group of Palestinians harvesting olives near Turmus Aya in the Israeli-occupied West Bank, on October 16, 2026 [File: Ammar Awad/Reuters]\nThe Israeli settler pogroms have been going on for many weeks, and have stepped up as the seasonal Palestinian olive harvest gets under way.\nThe Israeli rights group Yesh Din has recorded at least 170 pogroms since September 27, around the start of the annual olive harvest, Reuters reported.\nThe violence threatens Palestinian livelihoods and access to their land. Farmers endure assaults, the destruction of trees and the theft of crops, while settlers have also begun stealing olives from Palestinian-owned groves themselves.\nAl Jazeera correspondent Tareq Abu Azzoum reported that the intimidation extends beyond agricultural land to Palestinian homes and vehicles, creating pressure on communities to leave.\n“People there are still living under threat of attack, not just on their agricultural lands, but also on their properties,” he said.\n“Vehicles and houses have been repeatedly hit by settler attacks, which have been used by the Israeli military, apparently, to create a deterrent environment with Palestinians expected to leave these areas so they can later turn them into Israeli settlements.”", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "d68278bb935abba22c00c67a", "title": "Crowds throng New Delhi and wave Indian flags amid escalating protests - NPR", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiswFBVV95cUxQY04xSW5DWjNncnJaR19qY3dHRm9YWkU4UXJFS291Zm40UXJFeXBsZUhRNVFmbDgzd0lfTnp3RnV2Rkhhamh6NjV4MlRFdXE2SUtYQmxNd0t5T3U3ZHZTV2R0UWNkeF9VY09RSVhINWlGRVpjVlJHN2NmUGQxOFJQc0pBZEhjS1YtRkV2YUszdjkxTWp0RjdhX0Z4THlqbnR6SGRUbkpNNl9vVnk1YzdjRnMtdw?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T13:26:43+00:00", "text": "Crowds throng New Delhi and wave Indian flags amid escalating protests - NPR\nCrowds throng New Delhi and wave Indian flags amid escalating protests NPR Indian police again detain ‘cockroach’ movement founder ahead of protest CNN Delhi protest: Dipke held under preventive detention, to face magistrate; AISA's Neha Bora released The Times of India India’s Cockroach Janta party leader detained amid crackdown on protests The Guardian CJP, Abhijeet Dipke: 'Cockroach' party founder detained ahead of Central Park Delhi protest BBC", "truncated": false}, {"id": "740947fe1474e120866726d4", "title": "Market Euphoria Distracts From Brazil’s Mounting Debt Problem - Bloomberg.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMisgFBVV95cUxONG9xUDlPb2g0Y1ZJUzRuS2xZRndRZW9ucHkyeGZwM3RaU2IybE9NajlIc1d3R1dMMUVJSEhoQUZZYzhGZDhSV2lVZGlsbHNmSkFMRjJJME95dTFxTzBKanJPWW5qRVlWZ3FpX05ZZnVFNFBqVmdvdlIzT0hoTGp4S0pwZWljdWV2cVU1eFc2OWZLQmdzVXBLQnJvSnM1T3ZNZHNzdmQyQkhtNzFSbnRMYV9n?oc=5", "source": "publisher_bloomberg", "published_at": "2026-10-16T13:26:43+00:00", "text": "Market Euphoria Distracts From Brazil’s Mounting Debt Problem - Bloomberg.com\nMarket Euphoria Distracts From Brazil’s Mounting Debt Problem Bloomberg.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "d59461fe1fae232f4b52b49e", "title": "Roche makes class-first push into thyroid eye disease with new FDA nod for Enspryng - Fierce Pharma", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiqAFBVV95cUxPY01xdGFxMzBtR3Bna0FYRWxRc