ee Turkish companies frozen out of global indexes - CNBC", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMie0FVX3lxTFB5dklEWTRNUjlCc21TbTU4UmQzYXRzX2k5SS11a2hBMDQ0dEx1MGl1cEVzTmZVUllId0k3eThxNmN0Qnk1aVNVMTUxdlpLWXo1d1RpekZ0SUxFVndVaFZjUUF2Vk1VTU9hU2JGaUwxd3RudEhsR2ladXVFb9IBgAFBVV95cUxQQUJpZVNTbWZfbEIxSG8wSDAtVU9ldnQ3d2xMQ1VtNU1idXhmRnptVkhTa2loN3VVV0xJZlpRSnI3VEV", "source": "rss_google_markets", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:58:15+00:00", "text": "Stock fixing scandal could see Turkish companies frozen out of global indexes - CNBC\nStock fixing scandal could see Turkish companies frozen out of global indexes CNBC", "truncated": false}, {"id": "2f95041a1d7a62a52cbb724a", "title": "Fed chair Warsh says price stability is key but shies away from signals about rates - AP News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMikAFBVV95cUxObUdoZ1dEUV9yMWY2UHVhSmtrdWRyeUYxUDlWRTN1b2EwbGVtSUJZM1lVTnAyMEJybzBnM1hMdU5iZV9Ta3pvXzkzcXk1dGRlcWoycGJzVktfc25UNmM4SEhSZWVQdUw2ekw0RU5kRUNSendlWElacnlvQm43R3d4MnlTZzFZNE8xTjRuN0l3QXg?oc=5", "source": "rss_ap_business:google_fallback", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:58:06+00:00", "text": "Fed chair Warsh says price stability is key but shies away from signals about rates - AP News\nFed chair Warsh says price stability is key but shies away from signals about rates AP News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "25eaa552b4e599a68d464e57", "title": "How UN chief Antonio Guterres tests the limits of quiet diplomacy", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/features/longform/2026/10/16/how-un-chief-antonio-guterres-tests-the-limits-of-quiet-diplomacy?traffic_source=rss", "source": "rss_aljazeera_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:57:47+00:00", "text": "How UN chief Antonio Guterres tests the limits of quiet diplomacy\nHow UN chief Antonio Guterres tests the limits of quiet diplomacy\nAn illustration of United Nations Secretary-General Antonio Guterres [Muhammet Okur/Al Jazeera]\nAn illustration of United Nations Secretary-General Antonio Guterres [Muhammet Okur/Al Jazeera]\nShare How UN chief Antonio Guterres tests the limits of quiet diplomacy on social media\nPortugal's Prime Minister Antonio Guterres called President Bill Clinton from Lisbon on August 30 and September 8, 1999, to discuss the growing crisis in East Timor. The small Southeast Asian territory, which had endured a brutal Indonesian occupation since 1975, was again engulfed in violence after voters overwhelmingly chose independence in a referendum. Pro-Indonesian militias responded to the vote for independence by unleashing a so-called \"scorched-earth\" campaign, destroying infrastructure, displacing up to half a million people and killing thousands.\nThe crisis resonated in Portugal, where linguistic, Catholic, and historical ties to its former colony helped fuel a solidarity movement. An estimated 10,000 demonstrators, dressed in white, formed human chains through Lisbon’s city streets.\nGuterres had raised the issue before. At a 1996 summit in Bangkok, he met Indonesia’s President Suharto and broached the politically sensitive topic of East Timor, offering to restore diplomatic relations (severed since the 1975 invasion) in\nthe release of Timorese resistance leader Xanana Gusmao. The proposal was met with an awkward silence.\nThis time, Guterres was asking the United States to support a UN mission in East Timor, but Washington was reluctant to confront Indonesia. Guterres recalled in his 2022 biography, “Honest Broker”, that he warned Clinton during the second call that Portugal could reconsider its Balkan deployments if the international community failed to intervene. Portugal’s contribution to NATO was small but politically well-placed, with its troops serving in the alliance’s prominent post-Cold War missions in Bosnia and Kosovo.\nGuterres offered Clinton a choice: risk a public falling-out with a founding NATO ally, or use Washington’s influence to make Indonesia accept an international force in East Timor. The next day, amid growing international pressure, the White House stated that Indonesia “must” allow international assistance.\nFor Guterres, it was an early test of multilateral crisis diplomacy, a skill set he would later bring to his post as UN secretary-general in 2017. East Timor, also known as Timor-Leste, finally won its freedom in 2002. For spearheading diplomatic efforts during the 1999 crisis, Guterres was awarded Timorese citizenship in 2024.\n“Antonio Guterres pushed a lot for what we now call the referendum, and when violence broke out he was one of the people calling for a UN intervention,” said Christine Cabasset, a teacher at Lille Catholic University and an expert on East Timor, reflecting on how the East Timor crisis foreshadowed Guterres’s later career.\n“Guterres had a critical role all along the way,” she added, noting how he later pushed for the creation of the UN Transitional Administration in East Timor.\n“There is also a kind of love story with the UN, I mean, that strong link between Timor-Leste and the UN… Multilateralism, which is still really the purpose of the UN, has been critical in the independence of Timor-Leste. And Guterres [himself] became very important in the UN starting from 2005.”\nGuterres’s response to the East Timor crisis reflected an approach shaped by his Catholic faith, his early work with Portugal’s poor and his political coming of age after the fall of a dictatorship. He carried those experiences into a career built on finding common ground and helping people caught in crises. But as UN secretary-general, he has faced the limits of that approach, struggling to turn appeals for peace into action when the world’s most powerful countries are at odds.\nUS President Bill Clinton is welcomed in Lisbon by Portugal's Prime Minister Antonio Guterres for a three-day official visit, May 30, 2000 [Armando Franca/AP]\nU.S. President Bill Clinton shakes hands with Portuguese Prime Minister Antonio Guterres on the tarmac of Lisbon's airport upon arriving in Portugal for a three-day official visit on May 30, 2000 [Armando Franca/AP] (AP)\nAntonio Manuel de Oliveira Guterres was born in Lisbon on April 30, 1949, to Ilda Candida de Oliveira Guterres and Virgilio Dias Guterres, a senior employee of the Lisbon gas and electricity company. He spent summers and extended periods in the countryside with his grandparents in the small village of Donas. His childhood friend Vitor Dias told the newspaper\nthat Guterres’s grandmother stressed the importance of education and by “three or four years old, he already knew how to write his name.” The grandparents’ house was right next to the church where “Little Toni” often served as an altar boy.\nPortugal was then under the rule of dictator Antonio Salazar, whose Estado Novo regime closely resembled Italian fascism, but with less emphasis on brute force, personality cults and revolutionary zeal, and more on Catholic conservatism. As a result, the country was politically and culturally cut off from much of democratic Western Europe.\n“As dictator, [Salazar] was very much marked by corporatism, the idea of an organic society without conflict and also by Roman Catholic conservative values,” said Antonio Costa Pinto, a historian at the University of Lisbon.\n“But especially in the late 60s and early 70s … it basically became a Cold War dictatorship, an anti-communist one.”\nAmong the regime’s core beliefs was that the seafaring Portuguese were innately suited to colonialism and it stubbornly clung to the centuries-old Portuguese empire, fighting bloody rebellions in Angola, Mozambique and Guinea-Bissau.\n“We were a very closed country. We had censorship everywhere. We had the political police,” remembered Joao Goulao, a retired Portuguese doctor who grew up in that era.\n“There was a very limited mobility of citizens; we were not able to travel abroad without a lot of formalities. We were not a very sexy destination for tourism, so people didn’t come to us and we were very isolated.”\nStill, Portugal was a founding member of NATO, a US ally and a participant in Western economic institutions.\n“In the late 60s especially, there was a huge contradiction between the type of censorship and the type of values – very conservative – supported by the dictatorship and the structure of Portuguese society that was already much more modern,” said Costa Pinto.\n“There was a contradiction between the values of the political elite and the values of a more developed, educated middle class.”\nPortuguese citizens saw those contradictions in everyday life: Coca-Cola was banned from being made in Portugal, and until 1970, anyone who owned a cigarette lighter needed an annual licence — a regulation imposed\nAlthough literacy rates improved under Salazar, outside the major cities, many Portuguese children received only\nfrom teachers who often had only had a fourth-grade education themselves. The instructors’ main qualification was loyalty to the regime.\nBut Guterres’ grandmother’s efforts to ensure he had a proper education paid off. He earned top marks at his high school in Lisbon and was reportedly recognised with a national academic prize in 1965. The future statesman went on to study electrical engineering at Lisbon’s Instituto Superior Tecnico, graduating in 1971.\nWhile there, Guterres led the university’s Social Action Centre, which conducted social work in the capital’s poorest neighbourhoods. He also joined the Grupo da Luz (Group of Light), a Catholic student club led by Father Vitor Melicias, who became a close personal friend and confidant.\nAlthough the Group of Light never directly challenged the regime, Melicias told World Mission Magazine that its members regularly changed meeting locations to", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "bdbe234566a353a00253acea", "title": "From hospital, pope pushes Putin for ceasefire in contested Ukraine city - RFI", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiqAFBVV95cUxPQV9ILVdFS21EM0pPWGFfbGlHdFFFTFVrV