hambers, break GOP supermajorities, analysis finds Yahoo", "truncated": false}, {"id": "23b7f80d47742ad72eebe590", "title": "Solid Impact Investments Corp. Announces Further Extension to Private Placement - TradingView", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi6gFBVV95cUxPUzItSzdqNExLUlVSamRyU19rLVpGQzBIWXdLd1o0eUo4T0UzdnNfdjdFN0ZIOVZtM0FqS1d6OFJJdVRIY2lMXzJabHZrWnZPbEJ1WjJlOGVNM25STTQzckpzcU9pT0RzcXN6aHpyOExSUnhTZEhRQ3pELVBiRzBSZks4ajV3MkxzUjBKd2p1TUNPTkVDNzNwM3pEaVd0SzJqVjRhWTA4VU9xT051S09WNXFwWTJwMlVKUHY1NjhORm9", "source": "lane_dilution", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:09+00:00", "text": "Solid Impact Investments Corp. Announces Further Extension to Private Placement - TradingView\nSolid Impact Investments Corp. Announces Further Extension to Private Placement TradingView", "truncated": false}, {"id": "fee0f1ee6c34406b846cbc2f", "title": "Oil prices are still poised for a 'super spike': Energy analyst explains - Yahoo Finance", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiggFBVV95cUxQTkpEMjAzbHZuek5kWkpiWE9TZ2NDN0hFZDBtbER2MnJYaldiOVZVMnMwTV9NYmxhTTQ4NWp1YVpmNGFLN3N4a1dxdVQtZFVjeFVON0IxVk16MnNpSDh4ekl2U1ljVE9faGFhLTlVSTVFWkRudDZpRGYwSXN0bW9BWndB?oc=5", "source": "lane_regime_state", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Oil prices are still poised for a 'super spike': Energy analyst explains - Yahoo Finance\nOil prices are still poised for a 'super spike': Energy analyst explains Yahoo Finance", "truncated": false}, {"id": "fbc6f137b66965c4afea8af2", "title": "The United States Attorney’s Office for the Middle District of Tennessee Joins DOJ Fraud Division, IRS, and IRS-CI in National Tax Enforcement Surge Exposing Tax Fraud", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-mdtn/pr/united-states-attorneys-office-middle-district-tennessee-joins-doj-fraud-division-irs", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "The United States Attorney’s Office for the Middle District of Tennessee Joins DOJ Fraud Division, IRS, and IRS-CI in National Tax Enforcement Surge Exposing Tax Fraud\nPress Release The United States Attorney’s Office for the Middle District of Tennessee Joins DOJ Fraud Division, IRS, and IRS-CI in National Tax Enforcement Surge Exposing Tax Fraud Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Middle District of Tennessee More than 30 U.S. Attorney’s Offices joined the Fraud Division in bringing enforcement actions against alleged fraudsters NASHVILLE – The United States Attorney’s Office for the Middle District of Tennessee announced charges as part of nationwide enforcement actions led by the Justice Department’s National Fraud Enforcement Division, Internal Revenue Service, and Internal Revenue Service Criminal Investigation targeting tax fraud and related offenses. The United States Attorney’s Office for the Middle District of Tennessee was a key participant in this surge effort. Between early September and today, federal prosecutors across the country facilitated fraud enforcement actions spanning over 76 criminal defendants reaching more than $490,000,000 in intended loss to American taxpayers . According to court records, Thomas William Jones, 59 years old, of Brentwood, Tennessee, pleaded guilty last month to one count of failing to collect and pay taxes to the Internal Revenue Service (IRS), announced United States Attorney for the Middle District of Tennessee Braden H. Boucek. Jones was the owner and operator of Alternative Solutions Consulting Corporation (“ASCC”), a software consulting company in Franklin, Tennessee, where he employed others and maintained the ASCC’s finances. Jones controlled ASCC’s bank account and paid his employees from this account. As an employer, Jones had a duty to truthfully account for and pay the required federal income and employment taxes. Between 2014 and 2023, Jones failed to file the appropriate forms with the IRS for ASCC to report his employees’ wages. Jones also did not make payments to the IRS for federal income and Federal Insurance Contributions Act (FICA) tax withholdings which were required to be taken from his employees’ paychecks. In addition, Jones did not make payments to the IRS for the matching portion of the FICA taxes ( i.e ., the employer contributions to Social Security and Medicare) that he was required to make. The total amount of taxes Jones failed to collect and pay is not more than $1,975,964.24. Jones was charged by an Information on July 7, 2026. He pled guilty on September 1, 2026. He faces up to five years in federal prison. U.S. District Court Judge Eli J. Richardson scheduled Jones’s sentencing for January 15, 2027. *** On April 7, the Department of Justice announced the creation of the Fraud Division. The Fraud Division is laser-focused on investigating and prosecuting those who commit fraud against the ", "truncated": true}, {"id": "fa5d4caaa7bd0601a3dd8a6d", "title": "Federal Jury Finds Minnetonka Man Guilty on All Counts for Threatening to Murder Federal Judges and Elected Officials", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-mn/pr/federal-jury-finds-minnetonka-man-guilty-all-counts-threatening-murder-federal-judges", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Federal Jury Finds Minnetonka Man Guilty on All Counts for Threatening to Murder Federal Judges and Elected Officials\nPress Release Federal Jury Finds Minnetonka Man Guilty on All Counts for Threatening to Murder Federal Judges and Elected Officials Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Minnesota MINNEAPOLIS – United States Attorney Daniel N. Rosen announced that Jeffrey Allen Petersen, 68, of Minnetonka, Minnesota, was found guilty on 10 counts of threatening to murder U.S. judges and elected officials, and 10 counts of interstate transmission of threats to injure. A federal jury found Petersen guilty of all 20 counts on October 15, 2026, after a trial before U.S. District Judge Patrick J. Schiltz. A sentencing hearing will be scheduled by the Court at a date yet to be determined. “My office has no tolerance for political violence and threats of political violence,” said United States Attorney Daniel N. Rosen. “We will prosecute—to the fullest extent of the law—those who intimidate or threaten public servants who are just doing their jobs.” “Threats against federal judges and lawmakers strike at the heart of our judicial system and our democracy,” said FBI Minneapolis Special Agent in Charge Christopher D. Dotson. “The FBI Minneapolis Joint Terrorism Task Force will investigate and disrupt individuals who attempt to intimidate or harm public officials. At a time when Minnesota communities are especially mindful of political violence, this conviction on all 20 counts reflects the strength of our partnerships and our unwavering commitment to protecting those who serve the public.” According to court documents and evidence introduced at trial, in July 2025, the U.S. Marshals Service for the District of Maryland received a report from their judicial security intelligence unit regarding threatening comments posted to an article on a website referencing a federal judge in that District. On at least one website, Petersen used the username, “Adam Lanza,” the mass murderer who shot and killed 26 people, 20 of whom were school children between the ages of six and seven years old, at Sandy Hook Elementary School in 2012. Image 1: Online post from Petersen Image 2: Online post from Petersen The U.S. Marshals Service investigated the post and found it was made with an IP address assigned to Petersen with a service address in Minnetonka, Minnesota. The U.S. Marshals Service then referred the matter to the FBI. During the investigation, law enforcement received additional reports concerning similar threatening comments posted on other sites toward federal judges and various elected officials throughout the United States. The username of some of the posts was “Behring.a,” and the full registered account name was, “Anders Breivik Behring.” Breivik is the name of the mass murderer who shot and killed 77 people in 2011 at a summer camp in Norway in “support of white supremac", "truncated": true}, {"id": "f833564347a3b9eff7c2c564", "title": "Foreign National Pleads Guilty to Illegally Obtaining U.S. Citizenship and U.S. Passport", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-nj/pr/foreign-national-pleads-guilty-illegally-obtaining-us-citizenship-and-us-passport", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Foreign National Pleads Guilty to Illegally Obtaining U.S. Citizenship and U.S. Passport\nPress Release Foreign National Pleads Guilty to Illegally Obtaining U.S. Citizenship and U.S. Passport Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of New Jersey NEWARK, N.J. – A foreign national and convicted felon pleaded guilty to unlawful procurement of naturalization and passport fraud, U.S. Attorney Robert Frazer announced. Miguel Dario Bautista Jimenez, a/k/a “Carlos Chapman Hernandez,” a/k/a “Carlos Hernandez,” 42, of Hoboken, New Jersey pleaded guilty to a two-count Indictment charging him with unlawful procurement of naturalization, in violation of Title 18, United States Code, Sections 1425(b) and 2, and passport fraud, in violation of Title 18, United States Code, Sections 1542 and 2, on October 14, 2026 before U.S. District Judge Stanley R. Chesler in Newark federal court. According to documents filed in this case and statements made in court: Miguel Dario Bautista Jimenez, a citizen of the Dominican Republic, was removed from the United States in or around 2013, following a criminal conviction in New York for an aggravated felony.  In or around June 2014, Bautista re-entered the United States using a false identity.  Bautista applied for legal permanent residence and naturalization falsely stating that his name was “Carlos Chapman Hernandez,” a person born in Cuba, and falsely stating that he had never been convicted of a crime, had never been removed from the United States, and had never been to prison.  Bautista used the false identity to fraudulently obtain U.S. citizenship documents through naturalization, including a U.S. passport. The charges of unlawful procurement of naturalization and passport fraud each carry a maximum penalty of ten years’ imprisonment.  Each offense carries a maximum fine of $250,000, or twice the gross loss or gain from the offense, whichever is greatest. U.S. Attorney Frazer credited special agents of Homeland Security Investigations in Newark, under the direction of Special Agent in Charge Michael S. McCarthy, the Social Security Administration, Office of the Inspector General, New York Field Division, under the direction of Special Agent-in-Charge Amy Connelly, the U.S. Department of State’s Diplomatic Security Service, New York Field Office, under the direction of Special Agent in Charge Nicholas Fanelli, and the Federal Bureau of Investigation, under the direction of Special Agent in Charge Stefanie Roddy in Newark, with the investigation leading to the guilty plea. The government is represented by Special Assistant U.S. Attorney Priscilla Gabela and Assistant U.S. Attorney Katherine M. Romano of the Criminal Division in Newark. On April 7, the Department of Justice announced the creation of the National Fraud Enforcement Division (Fraud Division). The Fraud Division is investigating and prosecuting those who commit fraud against t", "truncated": true}, {"id": "f66a649127203b9a8126adbd", "title": "Missouri Sex Offender Admits Possessing Child Sexual Abuse Material", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-edmo/pr/missouri-sex-offender-admits-possessing-child-sexual-abuse-material", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Missouri Sex Offender Admits Possessing Child Sexual Abuse Material\nPress Release Missouri Sex Offender Admits Possessing Child Sexual Abuse Material Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Eastern District of Missouri ST. LOUIS – A registered sex offender on Friday admitted viewing child sexual abuse material that he found online. Corey Adam Oliver, 53, pleaded guilty Friday in U.S. District Court in St. Louis to one count of possession of child pornography. In December of 2024, a Cyber Tipline report to the National Center for Missing and Exploited Children indicated that Oliver had uploaded child sexual abuse material (CSAM) to Bing’s image search application. On January 14, 2025, the Missouri State Highway Patrol obtained a search warrant for Oliver’s home near Bismarck, in St. Francois County. In an interview with law enforcement, Oliver initially denied viewing or receiving CSAM. After he failed a consensual polygraph examination, he admitted using a tablet to do so. Oliver was required to register as a sex offender after having been convicted in Louisiana in 2000 of the felony offenses of molestation of a juvenile and carnal knowledge of a juvenile and in Georgia in 2007 of the felony offenses of enticing a child for indecent purposes and interference with child custody. Oliver is scheduled to be sentenced in January. The U.S. Attorney’s office will recommend 15 years in prison, followed by a lifetime on supervised release. The Missouri State Highway Patrol investigated the case. Assistant U.S. Attorney Jillian Anderson prosecuted the case. This case was brought as part of Project Safe Childhood, a nationwide initiative to combat the growing epidemic of child sexual exploitation and abuse launched in May 2006 by the Department of Justice. Led by U.S. Attorneys’ Offices and the Department of Justice Criminal Division's Child Exploitation and Obscenity Section, Project Safe Childhood marshals federal, state and local resources to better locate, apprehend and prosecute individuals who exploit children via the Internet, as well as to identify and rescue victims. For more information about Project Safe Childhood, please visit www.justice.gov/psc . Contact Robert Patrick, Public Affairs Officer, robert.patrick@usdoj.gov. Updated October 16, 2026 Topic Project Safe Childhood Component USAO - Missouri, Eastern", "truncated": false}, {"id": "f54a6cc0444b6bf15bca8187", "title": "Texas residents charged with promoting Illegal tax shelter", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-ndtx/pr/texas-residents-charged-promoting-illegal-tax-shelter", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Texas residents charged with promoting Illegal tax shelter\nPress Release Texas residents charged with promoting Illegal tax shelter Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Northern District of Texas Caused an estimated tax loss to United States of more than $200 Million – one defendant also charged with fraudulently soliciting $2 million investment in the shelter DALLAS — United States Attorney for the Northern District of Texas Ryan Raybould announced that a federal grand jury in Dallas, Texas returned an indictment on Sept. 22, 2026, charging four Texas residents with conspiracy to defraud the United States and other crimes for promoting an illegal tax shelter. According to the indictment, Texas residents Duncan John MacDonald, Sarah Duren, Richard Ronald Cayce and Thomas Weickgenant promoted and sold a fraudulent trust scheme that was used to shelter more than $1.5 billion of income, causing an estimated tax loss to the IRS of more than $200 million. The defendants allegedly sold clients the tax shelter for fees that often exceeded $100,000 by fraudulently telling clients that they could assign their income to sham trusts to defer paying taxes in perpetuity, all while continuing to enjoy the benefit and control over their assets and income. The defendants also allegedly encouraged some clients to backdate their trusts and file amended income tax returns claiming refunds for prior years’ taxes. The indictment also alleges that Weickgenant, who allegedly filed false tax returns for tax shelter clients, obstructed IRS efforts to audit those clients. Weickgenant’s alleged obstruction included instructing clients not to speak with the IRS and encouraging at least one client not to comply with a U.S. District Court order to enforce an IRS summons. The indictment further alleges that MacDonald and Duren, common-law spouses, evaded income taxes on the millions they received in fees from selling the tax shelter. To do so, the couple allegedly concealed MacDonald’s ownership of the tax shelter business and lied to federal investigators. They also allegedly filed false tax returns, used trusts to conceal their receipt of income, and paid personal living expenses through trusts and nominee accounts held in the names of third parties. Allegedly, Cayce, who owned the distributorship licensing the trust tax shelter, likewise received millions of dollars in fees, which he failed to report on his income tax returns. Additionally, according to the allegations, MacDonald fraudulently solicited $2 million from investors to purportedly purchase shares in a business promoting the tax shelter by allegedly developing digital trusts. Instead, MacDonald allegedly used the $2 million to purchase luxury goods for himself, including a Rolls Royce Ghost (pictured below top with MacDonald) and the downpayment for a yacht (pictured below bottom). At the time he received funds from investors, MacDona", "truncated": true}, {"id": "f26ca871859887398502c06e", "title": "East Hartford Man Charged with Drug Distribution and Gun Possession Offenses", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-ct/pr/east-hartford-man-charged-drug-distribution-and-gun-possession-offenses", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "East Hartford Man Charged with Drug Distribution and Gun Possession Offenses\nPress Release East Hartford Man Charged with Drug Distribution and Gun Possession Offenses Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Connecticut David X. Sullivan, United States Attorney for the District of Connecticut, and P.J. O’Brien, Special Agent in Charge of the New Haven Division of the Federal Bureau of Investigation, today announced that a federal grand jury in New Haven has returned an indictment charging VASHAWN RAY, 38, of East Hartford, with drug distribution and firearm possession offenses. The indictment was returned on October 13, 2026, and Ray was arrested yesterday.  He appeared before U.S. Magistrate Judge Robert A. Richardson in Hartford and was ordered detained As alleged in court documents and statements made in court, on October 25, 2025, Ray and a companion drove a Mercedes SUV to the Westfarms Mall in West Hartford.  While they were inside the mall, a tow truck operator took the vehicle as part of a repossession.  After arriving at the impoundment lot, the tow truck operator found narcotics and a bag containing a firearm in the SUV, and contacted police.  Responding police arrived and recovered approximately 160 wax folds of fentanyl, a quantity of crack cocaine, and a loaded 9mm handgun.  The bag that contained that firearm also contained Ray’s wallet, identification, and credit cards. The indictment alleges that Ray’s criminal history includes state felony convictions for robbery and drug offenses, and a federal conviction in 2013 for a crack cocaine trafficking offense. The indictment charges Ray with possession with intent to distribute fentanyl and cocaine, an offense that carries a maximum term of imprisonment of 20 years, and unlawful possession of a firearm by a felon, an offense that carries a maximum term of imprisonment of 15 years. U.S. Attorney Sullivan stressed that an indictment is not evidence of guilt.  Charges are only allegations, and each defendant is presumed innocent unless and until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt. This investigation is being conducted by the FBI’s Northern Connecticut Gang Task Force with the assistance of the Newington and Bloomfield Police Departments.  The Task Force includes members from the Connecticut State Police, the Connecticut Department of Correction, and the East Hartford, Hartford, Manchester, Naugatuck, New Britain, Newington, Waterbury, West Hartford, and Wethersfield Police Departments.  The case is being prosecuted by Assistant U.S. Attorneys Christopher Lembo and Daniel Makofsky. This case is part of Operation Take Back America, a nationwide initiative that marshals the full resources of the Department of Justice to repel the invasion of illegal immigration, achieve the total elimination of cartels and transnational criminal organizations (TCOs), and protect our communities from the perpetrators", "truncated": true}, {"id": "ee778ae6ffbbb48c10642710", "title": "Brazilian Man Sentenced to 12 Years in Prison for Managerial Role in Conspiracy to Launder Tens of Millions of Dollars in Illicit Cash Proceeds", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdfl/pr/brazilian-man-sentenced-12-years-prison-managerial-role-conspiracy-launder-tens", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Brazilian Man Sentenced to 12 Years in Prison for Managerial Role in Conspiracy to Launder Tens of Millions of Dollars in Illicit Cash Proceeds\nPress Release Brazilian Man Sentenced to 12 Years in Prison for Managerial Role in Conspiracy to Launder Tens of Millions of Dollars in Illicit Cash Proceeds Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Southern District of Florida MIAMI – Ygor Fokin Saviolli, 35, a Brazilian national, was sentenced today to 12 years in prison for his role as a manager and supervisor in a money laundering organization that laundered more than $30 million in illicit proceeds, including the proceeds of drug trafficking. On Sept. 24, five co-defendants received sentences ranging from 94 months in prison to 12 months and one day in prison. Gabriel Cezar Menezes, 29, a Brazilian national, was sentenced to 94 months; Omar Aliperti De Mello Correa, 34, a U.S. citizen, was sentenced to 30 months; Leandro De Avila Goncalves, 43, a Brazilian national, was sentenced to 24 months; and Joao Andrade De Mello, 29, and Tadeu Sebastiane Rabelo Alves Barbosa, 30, both Brazilian nationals, were each sentenced to 12 months and one day. The Brazilian national defendants were illegally residing in the U.S. at the time of their arrests. “Drug trafficking organizations depend on sophisticated money launderers to turn dirty cash into usable profits,” said U.S. Attorney Jason A. Reding Quiñones for the Southern District of Florida. “Ygor Fokin Saviolli helped manage an international operation that moved and concealed more than $30 million in illicit proceeds, including drug money, across the United States and beyond. We will continue targeting not only the traffickers who poison our communities, but also the financial networks that make their criminal enterprises possible. His sentence of 12 years in federal prison is a consequence of helping turn the proceeds of drug trafficking into the lifeblood of international criminal organizations.” According to court documents, Saviolli, Menezes, Correa, Goncalves, De Mello, and Barbosa were part of a sophisticated money laundering organization that operated across the U.S. and spanned several countries. Members of the conspiracy arranged for U.S.-based couriers to receive bulk cash proceeds from the sale of controlled substances and then to deposit the cash at banks across the country in order to conceal and return those profits to, among others, the sources of drug supply operating outside the U.S. Dozens of pickups across the country were coordinated through WhatsApp message chains that included facilitators and couriers. Saviolli provided upfront funds to facilitate the operations of the money laundering organization and oversaw the receipt and laundering of the bulk cash drug proceeds. Menezes also served as a facilitator, providing direction to and oversight of couriers, and personally picked up bulk cash on multiple occasions. C", "truncated": true}, {"id": "ed68eb47618fdac0809e146e", "title": "Scinai Immunotherapeutics Announces Closing of $4.0 Million Financing Through a Private Placement and Warrant Inducement - PR Newswire", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihgJBVV95cUxPQ3czZlNNMXZ4eWRWZDZtNXJYeVpCTW84U01PS2NWMDZsQ2w2VkdUSHpNUXFOc01jdWhzYkNiSWQtZFRkcGlWSnlUTkVUWmRxdTVseE9YcTROcWxDUVRNRXhtYXFDd0NhRE9fNU54YVZpeGl0OHlROHk1NnFnUUVKNnJHb3l5ek5KVThNZXZ4NHRVdERsZlFCT0lwQTNseWVVRzE5UTZoLW41ZFpXSGc3N1VkYjFVZzJfZUVuemc0Y1d", "source": "lane_dilution", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Scinai Immunotherapeutics Announces Closing of $4.0 Million Financing Through a Private Placement and Warrant Inducement - PR Newswire\nScinai Immunotherapeutics Announces Closing of $4.0 Million Financing Through a Private Placement and Warrant Inducement PR Newswire", "truncated": false}, {"id": "e8e2c5d3b55b82a10b18e8ca", "title": "AG Blanche, USMS Director Host Roundtable on Local Impact of Memphis Safe Task Force", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/opa/video/ag-blanche-usms-director-host-roundtable-local-impact-memphis-safe-task-force", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "AG Blanche, USMS Director Host Roundtable on Local Impact of Memphis Safe Task Force\nAG Blanche, USMS Director Host Roundtable on Local Impact of Memphis Safe Task Force\nAttorney General Todd Blanche hosted a roundtable with U.S. Senator Marsha Blackburn and U.S. Marshals Service Director Gadyaces Serralta in Memphis, Tennessee, following the one-year anniversary of the Memphis Safe Task Force. Participants included federal, state, and local officials as well as two special guests.\nDOJ, ATF, Law Enforcement Partners Conclude Operation Show Me Justice\nThe Department of Justice, Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives (ATF), U.S. Marshals Service (USMS), U.S. Attorney’s Office for the Eastern District of Missouri, and others today announced the...\nJustice Department, Law Enforcement Partners Announce Results of 8-Week Law Enforcement Surge Focused on Violent Crime Reduction\nAttorney General Blanche Hosts Roundtable on Violent Crime Reduction Efforts", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "e714bae7b356ccf0b8c51dfc", "title": "North Carolina Man Admits to Producing and Transporting Child Pornography into the United States", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-nj/pr/north-carolina-man-admits-producing-and-transporting-child-pornography-united-states", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "North Carolina Man Admits to Producing and Transporting Child Pornography into the United States\nPress Release North Carolina Man Admits to Producing and Transporting Child Pornography into the United States Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of New Jersey NEWARK, N.J. – A North Carolina man admitted to producing child pornography in a foreign country and transporting it into the United States, U.S. Attorney Robert Frazer announced. Barry Grant BeVier, 63, formerly of Wilmington, North Carolina, pleaded guilty on October 14, 2026 before U.S. District Judge Claire C. Cecchi in Newark federal court to an Information charging him with one count of sexually exploiting a child outside the United States for the purpose of producing child pornography and transporting that child pornography into the United States, in violation of Title 18, United States Code, Sections 2251(c) and (e). According to documents filed in this case and statements made in court: In November 2024, BeVier was outside of the United States and twice persuaded a child to engage in sexually explicit conduct with him by offering the child things of value including a mobile phone.  BeVier filmed videos of himself engaging in the sexually explicit conduct with the child and stored the videos on his cell phone.  He then took an international flight on a commercial airliner into the United States and landed at Newark Liberty International Airport while carrying his cell phone containing the videos.  BeVier also agreed as part of his plea to acknowledge that in November 2024 he produced child pornography involving one additional child. The charge of sexually exploiting a child outside the United States for the purpose of producing child pornography and transporting child pornography into the United States carries a mandatory minimum term of 15 years imprisonment and a maximum term of 30 years imprisonment, and a fine of $250,000. Sentencing is scheduled for February 17, 2027. U.S. Attorney Frazer credited special agents of Homeland Security Investigations (HSI), under the direction of Special Agent in Charge Michael S. McCarthy and officers from Customs and Border Protection (CBP), under the direction of New York Director of Field Operation Frank Russo, with the investigation leading to the guilty plea. This case was brought as part of Project Safe Childhood, a nationwide initiative to combat the growing epidemic of child sexual exploitation and abuse launched in May 2006 by the Department of Justice. Led by U.S. Attorneys’ Offices and the Child Exploitation and Obscenity Section (CEOS) in the Justice Department’s Criminal Division, Project Safe Childhood marshals federal, state and local resources to better locate, apprehend and prosecute individuals who exploit children as well as to identify and rescue victims. For more information about Project Safe Childhood, please visit: https://www.justice.go", "truncated": true}, {"id": "e70d23db3a008931128b3523", "title": "Indonesia's $5 billion bet on its own stock market - Fortune", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMifkFVX3lxTE1fdjduZF80WTZScjAzdTV2bWR0dnpEdHNraThYS0VhS0IzTGpjV0hyVUpFbDR5UTh0bXdPUmgwQ2tTclB5OWIyeEtmUVBKOFhEeWlYblVxa21xaEhrZWJGb2lON19GSmFwd3dPd1BBcHJzMG9JY05RdFV5RkhhQQ?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_markets", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Indonesia's $5 billion bet on its own stock market - Fortune\nIndonesia's $5 billion bet on its own stock market Fortune", "truncated": false}, {"id": "e3ce7918f70488f5585e19b4", "title": "Panama City Drug Trafficking Organization Dismantled by Homeland Security Task Force Prosecution", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-ndfl/pr/panama-city-drug-trafficking-organization-dismantled-homeland-security-task-force", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Panama City Drug Trafficking Organization Dismantled by Homeland Security Task Force Prosecution\nTallahassee, Florida – Mario Swanston, 45, of Miami, Florida; Santana Wyvonne Harris, 41, of Panama City, Florida; Shannon Jarbar Clayton, 46, of Panama City, Florida; Elizabeth Gray, 37, of Panama City, Florida; and Shontell Fedd, 49, of Panama City, Florida were each sentenced by U.S. District Judge Mark E. Walker in federal court after previously pleading guilty to conspiracy to possess with intent to distribute cocaine and marijuana.", "truncated": false}, {"id": "ded216e3387249daf6729a8b", "title": "Beretta's cash offer for Ruger drew more shares than it can buy; allocation awaits confirmation. - Stock Titan", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiugFBVV95cUxNdTNKS0FTUGRydDhFaVlVVEI4R2kwN3RGOW9OMUtxYy16YWlKaHVvMC1LOWQ2bjR0NTFmUkRyNW9SS1dUVDE3anpzandoSGVPakpFMmY5YVo4OGdEZHVFUjdsZEtzZnpIRTFGcW1fcmt2YVVrT0daSEkyMWMxQ011d3BJMTIyeHBHSmdvQjVmSUVOSFRReWRvVENoWHdrTV91VS1QQ3c2MWxJcjYxV2pUUDFYY21BUTBZZVE?oc=5", "source": "lane_preannounce", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Beretta's cash offer for Ruger drew more shares than it can buy; allocation awaits confirmation. - Stock Titan\nBeretta's cash offer for Ruger drew more shares than it can buy; allocation awaits confirmation. Stock Titan", "truncated": false}, {"id": "dada606528087f322a670065", "title": "Jacksonville Man Sentenced to Ten Years in Prison for Drug Offenses", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-mdfl/pr/jacksonville-man-sentenced-ten-years-prison-drug-offenses", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Jacksonville Man Sentenced to Ten Years in Prison for Drug Offenses\nPress Release Jacksonville Man Sentenced to Ten Years in Prison for Drug Offenses Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Middle District of Florida Jacksonville, Florida – Jamal Jason James (40, Jacksonville) has been sentenced by Chief U.S. District Judge Marcia Morales Howard to 10 years in federal prison for participating in a drug trafficking conspiracy and possessing MDMA with the intent to distribute it. James pleaded guilty on December 12, 2025. U.S. Attorney Gregory W. Kehoe made the announcement. According to court documents, on July 29, 2024, members of the Jacksonville Sherriff’s Office responded to Ivy Street in Jacksonville regarding a call for a male lying in the road. When officers arrived, they observed James lying outside the open door of a vehicle used by him. While arranging for aid to tend to James, officers noticed a small bag of a rock-like substance in the vehicle, which laboratory testing later revealed was 6 grams of MDMA, an amount inconsistent with personal use. James was placed in handcuffs and searched. Officers found more than $20,000 in cash on James and inside the vehicle. After James was arrested, he was briefly incarcerated at the Duval County Jail where he made a series of phone calls to Brian Brightman and Anthony Smokes. The calls concerned finding and moving drugs belonging to James being stored in at least one trap house accessible by Brightman and Smokes. They also discussed collecting drug debts owed to James by other members of the conspiracy. In related cases, Brightman and Smokes previously pleaded guilty to federal drug offenses and are awaiting sentencing. This case was investigated by the Drug Enforcement Administration, the Nassau County Sherriff’s Office, and the Jacksonville Sherriff’s Office. It was prosecuted by Assistant United States Attorney Rachel Lasry. Updated October 16, 2026 Topic Drug Trafficking Component USAO - Florida, Middle", "truncated": false}, {"id": "d81cea96e91a2782afc04f09", "title": "New helium facility boosts Saskatchewan’s production capacity - DiscoverHumboldt", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMinwFBVV95cUxQZW94SDlkVUVyQzljc0cxRm1EUnUzbmFMT2VXRzJoSWVscnkyLTNVajZoZTlJeHFYY1IxVjl1ZDBQSE1FT0dMN0JJV1djZndjbV8zajhiWlRWWGVVLWs3UndBX3NTUGkxaW84VVRyM296MUk4QTBnU0tPNjlCcXp5VlMtNlBwMGZGcHdLeUtWdkhrWTRHX2RGbU9HNGFOTE0?oc=5", "source": "lane_capacity", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "New helium facility boosts Saskatchewan’s production capacity - DiscoverHumboldt\nNew helium facility boosts Saskatchewan’s production capacity DiscoverHumboldt", "truncated": false}, {"id": "d53883ad137d4d4b7cbe5b61", "title": "Fort Thompson Woman Sentenced to 5 Years in Federal Prison  for Assault Resulting in Serious Bodily Injury", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-sd/pr/fort-thompson-woman-sentenced-5-years-federal-prison-assault-resulting-serious-bodily", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Fort Thompson Woman Sentenced to 5 Years in Federal Prison  for Assault Resulting in Serious Bodily Injury\nPress Release Fort Thompson Woman Sentenced to 5 Years in Federal Prison  for Assault Resulting in Serious Bodily Injury Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of South Dakota PIERRE - United States Attorney Ron Parsons announced today that U.S. District Judge Eric C. Schulte has sentenced a Fort Thompson, South Dakota, woman convicted of Assault Resulting in Serious Bodily Injury.  The sentencing took place on October 5, 2026. Rosilee Grey Owl, 30, was sentenced to five years in federal prison, followed by three years of supervised release, and ordered to pay a $100 special assessment to the Federal Crime Victims Fund. Grey Owl was indicted by a federal grand jury in November 2025. She pleaded guilty on July 1, 2026. On the evening of September 2, 2025, Grey Owl was involved in an argument with the victim, an adult male, at his residence in Fort Thompson, SD, within the Crow Creek Sioux Indian Reservation.  Grey Owl became irate and swung a shovel at the victim, striking him in the head and causing a deep laceration to the back of his head.  The victim was seen by an ambulance crew but refused additional medical help and went back to his room.  The next morning, the victim was found deceased. This matter is being prosecuted by the U.S. Attorney’s Office because the Major Crimes Act, a federal statute, mandates that certain violent crimes alleged to have occurred in Indian Country be prosecuted in federal court as opposed to State court. This case is part of the Department of Justice’s Missing or Murdered Indigenous Persons (MMIP) Regional Outreach Program, which aims to aid in the prevention and response to missing or murdered Indigenous people through the resolution of MMIP cases and communication, coordination, and collaboration with federal, Tribal, state, and local partners.  The Department views this work as a priority for its law enforcement components.  Through the MMIP Regional Outreach Program, a broad spectrum of stakeholders work together to identify MMIP cases and issues in Tribal communities and develop comprehensive solutions to address them.  This prosecution upholds the Department’s mission to the unwavering pursuit of justice on behalf of victims and their families despite the passage of time. This case was investigated by the Bureau of Indian Affairs-Office of Justice Services.  Missing and Murdered Indigenous Persons Assistant U.S. Attorney for the Great Plains Troy R. Morley prosecuted the case. Grey Owl was immediately remanded to the custody of the U.S. Marshals Service. Contact usasd.press@usdoj.gov Updated October 16, 2026 Topic Indian Country Law and Justice Component USAO - South Dakota", "truncated": false}, {"id": "d49f18ce7ae47d825ae78d67", "title": "Nashville Man Convicted of Operating Major Cocaine and Fentanyl Trafficking Operation from Downtown Nashville Apartment", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-mdtn/pr/nashville-man-convicted-operating-major-cocaine-and-fentanyl-trafficking-operation", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Nashville Man Convicted of Operating Major Cocaine and Fentanyl Trafficking Operation from Downtown Nashville Apartment\nPress Release Nashville Man Convicted of Operating Major Cocaine and Fentanyl Trafficking Operation from Downtown Nashville Apartment Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Middle District of Tennessee NASHVILLE – Brandon Ostein, 40, of Nashville, Tennessee, was convicted by a federal jury of multiple drug trafficking and firearms offenses following a jury trial in Nashville, announced United States Attorney for the Middle District of Tennessee Braden H. Boucek. The jury found Ostein guilty of conspiracy to possess with intent to distribute 400 grams or more of fentanyl and five kilograms or more of cocaine; two counts involving the distribution of cocaine; possession with intent to distribute 400 grams or more of fentanyl and five kilograms or more of cocaine; possession of firearms in furtherance of a drug trafficking crime; and possession of a firearm by a convicted felon. According to the evidence presented at trial, Ostein operated a large-scale drug trafficking business from an apartment at 1200 Broadway in downtown Nashville. The apartment, served as Ostein’s central location for storing substantial quantities of cocaine and fentanyl, cash, firearms, and equipment used to prepare and package the drugs for distribution. The investigation began in April 2023 and involved controlled purchases of cocaine, physical surveillance, and video recordings. The investigation revealed Ostein’s distribution of cocaine and fentanyl in the Middle Tennessee area. The evidence presented at trial established that Ostein worked with another individual who sold smaller quantities of cocaine while Ostein supplied the cocaine and oversaw the operation. Ostein was also present during several of the cocaine transactions. On August 31, 2023, law enforcement executed a search warrant at the apartment at 1200 Broadway. Inside the apartment, investigators recovered approximately 35 pounds of cocaine, four pounds of fentanyl, $350,000 in cash, and two loaded firearms. Investigators also recovered equipment associated with drug trafficking, including a kilogram press, packaging materials, and other items used in preparing drugs for distribution. The evidence further established Ostein’s access to and control over the apartment through building access records, surveillance footage, and personal belongings recovered inside. Investigators also discovered a telescope positioned toward a nearby parking area, which was used to monitor cocaine deals. The jury convicted Ostein on all counts charged in the indictment. In a separate phase of the trial, the jury also determined that Ostein had qualifying prior felony drug convictions, including Davidson County convictions for possession of more than 300 grams of cocaine, supporting an enhanced statutory penalty. Ostein is set to be sent", "truncated": true}, {"id": "d1acae045e9d2271e1aea9b1", "title": "Illegal Alien Sentenced to 13 Years in Prison for 2025 Armed Carjacking, Attempted Carjacking in Philadelphia", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-edpa/pr/illegal-alien-sentenced-13-years-prison-2025-armed-carjacking-attempted-carjacking", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Illegal Alien Sentenced to 13 Years in Prison for 2025 Armed Carjacking, Attempted Carjacking in Philadelphia\nPress Release Illegal Alien Sentenced to 13 Years in Prison for 2025 Armed Carjacking, Attempted Carjacking in Philadelphia Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Eastern District of Pennsylvania PHILADELPHIA – United States Attorney David Metcalf announced that Mory Kante, 21, a French national illegally in the U.S., who has been residing in Wilmington, Delaware, was sentenced today to 13 years in prison and five years of supervised release by United States District Judge Gerald J. Pappert for the carjacking and attempted carjacking of two women in one night. The defendant was arrested on a criminal complaint and warrant in January of this year and charged by indictment the same month. He pleaded guilty in June to carjacking, attempted carjacking, and using, carrying, and brandishing a firearm during and in relation to a crime of violence. As detailed in court filings, on November 20, 2025, at approximately 9:15 p.m., a woman (“Victim 1”) parked her 2018 Honda Civic on the 1300 block of North Lawrence Street, got out of the vehicle, and walked away. She then saw the defendant run across the street and point a gun at her, saying “gimme your car keys” repeatedly. Victim 1 dropped her purse, and the defendant scooped it up and ran from the scene. Police subsequently found the purse on the street with some of the contents dumped out. Victim 1’s camera, driver’s license, two credit cards, debit card, wallet, and some cash were missing. At approximately 11:00 p.m. that night, police responded to a report of an armed carjacking on the 700 block of West Girard Avenue, approximately seven blocks from where Victim 1 was robbed. A woman (“Victim 2”) reported that a black male wearing all black clothing and a gray hat carjacked her at gunpoint. Victim 2 provided police with location data showing that her car, a 2019 Kia Optima, was near the intersection of Marvin Street and Wyoming Avenue, about four and a half miles north of the carjacking scene. Police located the car and activated their lights and sirens. Kante fled in the vehicle and a brief chase ensued, before the defendant abandoned the car and fled on foot. Pursuing officers saw him toss away a gun, jump on the porch of a nearby residence, and push his way into the home. A SWAT team was called and located the defendant hiding in some bushes in the backyard. This case was investigated by the Philadelphia Police Department and the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives and prosecuted by Assistant United States Attorney Justin Oshana. Contact USAPAE.PressBox@usdoj.gov 215-861-8300 Updated October 16, 2026 Topics Firearms Offenses Immigration Violent Crime Components Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives (ATF) USAO - Pennsylvania, Eastern", "truncated": false}, {"id": "d0bdbc15d14248bb1003a2c5", "title": "ModMed Launches New ModMed AI Analytics at MOMENTUM 2026 - Business Wire", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiswFBVV95cUxONkk1bURkSzE2VlhmUjZVeC0tM01RZXZGQ1pmTEpIQlQ0cFhIWi1xVWFYOXBXY09XOUZzTEJZMXZNNDBmaEswWnhNSFhsZFhtaUNXZFpvRWtXVFNINHYxSWJod1RoNERGUTJFRVlid0lSXy16YzdsaUVZODNIVVF3YmdLN2xwekxhaEJlMHBndTJwTG9xS3o2MTE3WHBkOWNabVBFb2wxZUhmUml6OW16RXM0Zw?oc=5", "source": "topic_products", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "ModMed Launches New ModMed AI Analytics at MOMENTUM 2026 - Business Wire\nModMed Launches New ModMed AI Analytics at MOMENTUM 2026 Business Wire", "truncated": false}, {"id": "ca8baa0b8987e0dba52a9258", "title": "Onyx Launches OnyxPay as Commission Payment Fight Heats Up - Luxury Travel Advisor", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMipAFBVV95cUxQbkRGUkZTRVlncGdpZ0drV25seXl5dzhjdDdMczZLOFJZMU5sSmdzUUFhZW1BeEdNUGgtYjV2T0ZQMnV4aDRrLWVQZlV3ekpveGlOcV82TGNrZ2NXM0VpallXQU1ESzhLZUtRMUFwR1d0c040Nm5JeTNEdTRHekEzRVVuZXFrLTlNSW9ZdjE0SmEyeUJkSDZpQk5IQTd4djRnQWtrLQ?oc=5", "source": "topic_products", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Onyx Launches OnyxPay as Commission Payment Fight Heats Up - Luxury Travel Advisor\nOnyx Launches OnyxPay as Commission Payment Fight Heats Up Luxury Travel Advisor", "truncated": false}, {"id": "c9b0d392fd97f5902539b8c7", "title": "Jamaican Fraudster Extradited To Michigan Sentenced To Prison For His Role In Nationwide Fraud Scheme", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-wdmi/pr/2026_1016_Stewart_Sentencing_PR", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Jamaican Fraudster Extradited To Michigan Sentenced To Prison For His Role In Nationwide Fraud Scheme\nPress Release Jamaican Fraudster Extradited To Michigan Sentenced To Prison For His Role In Nationwide Fraud Scheme Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Western District of Michigan GRAND RAPIDS, MICHIGAN – U.S. Attorney for the Western District of Michigan Timothy VerHey today announced that Sefton Olivair Stewart, also known as “Powa,” age 41 of Montego Bay, Jamaica, was sentenced to 72 months in federal prison for his role in a nationwide scheme that fraudulently obtained $3.4 million from 28 victims, including a victim who resides in West Michigan. Stewart was sentenced by the Honorable Paul Maloney, United States District Judge. Stewart will be removed from the United States after he serves his prison sentence. In July 2026, after being extradited from Jamaica to Grand Rapids by the U.S. Marshals Service, Stewart pleaded guilty to participating in a conspiracy to commit mail and wire fraud. Between October 2019 and September 2024, Stewart, co-conspirator Danielle Diarbakerly, and others participated in a scheme to defraud people in the United States by falsely telling the victims that they had won large prizes (cash and luxury vehicles) in a multistate lottery. To claim the prizes, the conspirators told the victims that they first had to pay taxes and fees associated with their winnings. All of this was false and the conspirators used fake bank and U.S. government documents to convince some victims to believe the fraudulent representations and promises. Diarbakerly, a Florida resident, previously pleaded guilty to conspiracy to commit money laundering and was sentenced to 37 months in federal prison for her role in the scheme. U.S. Attorney Timothy VerHey said, “There are people out there, like Stewart and Diarbakerly, who have decided to make a career out of defrauding U.S. citizens. We are doing everything we can to send the message that such careers have no future. If you engage in fraud, we will find you no matter where you are, charge you, convict you and persuade a judge to put you in prison.  Stewart got that message today.  Other con artists out there need to get the message that it’s time to change careers.” “Fraudsters who prey on victims with false promises of life-changing prizes will be held accountable, regardless of where they operate,” said HSI Detroit acting Special Agent in Charge Jeremy Pierczynski. “HSI is committed to working with our law enforcement partners to pursue transnational fraud schemes, bring offenders to justice, and protect communities from financial exploitation.” “Fraud schemes like this leave real victims with significant financial and emotional losses,” said Acting Special Agent in Charge Todd Strom, IRS Criminal Investigation’s Detroit Field Office. \"Stewart and his co-conspirators used deception, fake documents, and false p", "truncated": true}, {"id": "c158cad5ecc9f2d57385e61c", "title": "Escaped Shreveport Inmate Who Triggered Multi-Agency Manhunt Indicted for Violent Carjacking", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-wdla/pr/escaped-shreveport-inmate-who-triggered-multi-agency-manhunt-indicted-violent", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Escaped Shreveport Inmate Who Triggered Multi-Agency Manhunt Indicted for Violent Carjacking\nPress Release Escaped Shreveport Inmate Who Triggered Multi-Agency Manhunt Indicted for Violent Carjacking Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Western District of Louisiana SHREVEPORT – On October 7, 2026, a federal grand jury returned an indictment charging Tre'veon Jackson , 24, of Shreveport, with Carjacking Resulting in Serious Bodily Injury. Jackson faces up to 25 years in federal prison for the charge. U.S. Attorney Zachary A. Keller for the Western District of Louisiana made the announcement. According to court documents, Jackson escaped from custody during a medical transport on September 14, 2026, and, shortly thereafter, approached a man outside a hotel in Bossier City, violently assaulted him, and stole the victim’s vehicle. Jackson then allegedly led law enforcement officers on a high speed pursuit exceeding 110 miles per hour before crashing the vehicle and fleeing on foot. A multi-agency manhunt followed, and Jackson remained at large for several days until he was rearrested in Marshall, Texas, by the United States Marshals Service. He is now in federal custody. The FBI, U.S. Marshals Service, Bossier City Police Department, Marshall Police Department, Harrison County Sheriff’s Office, Texas State Technical College Police Department, and the Texas Rangers investigated the case. It is being prosecuted by Assistant U.S. Attorneys Allison Foster and Samuel Crichton, with assistance from Legal Assistant Emily Favrot. An indictment is merely an allegation. All defendants are presumed innocent until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt in a court of law. This case is part of the Western District of Louisiana’s GATOR (Gun Crime Abatement and Targeted Offender Response) Task Force initiative, which focuses on investigating, prosecuting, and preventing violent crime through a unified, multi-agency strategy in the Shreveport and Bossier City area. The Task Force is built around a core group of FBI Special Agents and FBI Task Force Officers from Louisiana State Police, Caddo Parish Sheriff’s Office, and Shreveport Police Department, who work closely with the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms, and Explosives, the U.S. Department of Homeland Security’s Homeland Security Investigations, Sabine Parish Sheriff’s Office, Louisiana State Probation & Parole, the Shreveport City Marshal, and Caddo Parish Juvenile Services. You may find a copy of this press release (and any updates) on the website of the United States Attorney’s Office for the Western District of Louisiana at www.justice.gov/usao-wdla. Related court documents and information may be found on the website of the District Court for the Western District of Louisiana at www.lawd.uscourts.gov or at https://www.lawd.uscourts.gov/cmecf-pacer. The case number for this matter is 5:26-cr-00399-01. ### CONTACT: Public Affa", "truncated": true}, {"id": "bf3575c42690bd47ea70cd06", "title": "America's Greatest Opportunities: How Simmonds Stock Stood Out - Investor's Business Daily", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimgFBVV95cUxNT2pNQks1MHpMaFlHR3B1QjA2aHhJTldCTGtIUDdYMkFmeWR0TGxrTks4T2ZRZFhFMW51OTNEUXlHV0JqZ1V2azJxRU1IRDdXSWtMQl9tUkZMMkprWlJWWldRSGhZekNXdUpxV001MGM1YWdpYWlSbk03c1QxbWNJeTNzTHdRSzhOWm1OVkotVGRXdUZuVnlEQ2Vn?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_markets", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "America's Greatest Opportunities: How Simmonds Stock Stood Out - Investor's Business Daily\nAmerica's Greatest Opportunities: How Simmonds Stock Stood Out Investor's Business Daily", "truncated": false}, {"id": "b837f147ca8a4a180245c2d0", "title": "Georgia Inmates Sentenced in Police‑Impersonation Jury‑Duty Call Scheme", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-mdga/pr/georgia-inmates-sentenced-police-impersonation-jury-duty-call-scheme", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Georgia Inmates Sentenced in Police‑Impersonation Jury‑Duty Call Scheme\nPress Release Georgia Inmates Sentenced in Police‑Impersonation Jury‑Duty Call Scheme Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Middle District of Georgia Inmates Posed as Police, Threatening Citizens and Collecting Payments from Prison ALBANY, Ga. – Two Georgia inmates who orchestrated a phone scheme from behind bars, extorting at least 119 citizens by demanding payment for supposed missed jury‑duty fines, have been sentenced to federal prison following an investigation conducted by the FBI. Lance Riddle, aka “C-Port,” was sentenced to serve a total of 200 months in prison, followed by three years of supervised release, to run consecutively to any state sentence. Co-defendant Joey Amour Jackson, aka “Apes---,” was sentenced to serve a total of 120 months in prison, followed by three years of supervised release, to run consecutively to any state sentence. The Court will determine restitution for both defendants in 90 days. Senior U.S. District Judge W. Louis Sands presided over the sentencing hearings on October 14. Jackson and Riddle were found guilty of one count of conspiracy to commit wire fraud and one count of conspiracy to commit money laundering on January 9, after a five-day trial that began on January 5. There is no parole in the federal system. “Even from prison, the defendants preyed on citizens through threats and deceit, scams that too many Americans are confronted with today,” said U.S. Attorney William R. ‘Will’ Keyes. “Our office and our law enforcement partners remain firmly committed to protecting the public and ensuring that those who commit fraud face full accountability.” “Not even prison cells prevented these criminals from harming dozens of innocent people. Hopefully, this sentence will bring some level of comfort to the victims and prevent others from falling for similar scams,” said Marlo Graham, Special Agent in Charge of FBI Atlanta. “The FBI will continue to work with our law enforcement partners in not only addressing these types of inmate-based crimes but also in preventing inmates’ access to contraband cell phones that provide the means to do so much harm to the unsuspecting public.” According to court documents and statements referenced at trial, FBI agents in Columbus, Georgia, received information from law enforcement agencies across the U.S. about wire fraud by Georgia inmates, including local law enforcement in Colorado, Florida, Virginia, Nevada, California, and Ohio. Agents discovered that inmates at Calhoun State Prison, including Jackson and Riddle, were using contraband cellular telephones and advanced electronic techniques such as Voice Over Internet Protocol (VOIP) to contact victims from a number that mirrored the numbers of their local police departments. The victims were told they had one or more warrants for their arrest due to missing jury duty. The defe", "truncated": true}, {"id": "b1edef2f35a2107fd5038b57", "title": "Grand jury indicts Wichita man for trafficking methamphetamine", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-ks/pr/grand-jury-indicts-wichita-man-trafficking-methamphetamine-0", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Grand jury indicts Wichita man for trafficking methamphetamine\nPress Release Grand jury indicts Wichita man for trafficking methamphetamine Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Kansas TOPEKA, KAN. – A federal grand jury in Topeka returned a superseding indictment charging a Kansas man with methamphetamine trafficking offenses. According to court documents, Zachery W. Thomas, 46, of Wichita was indicted on: One count of Distribution of a Controlled Substance; Two counts of Possession of a Controlled Substance; Two counts of Possession with intent to Distribute a Controlled Substance; The Wichita Police Department (WPD) and the Sedgwick County Sheriff’s Office are investigating the case. OTHER INDICTMENTS Donald Ince, 47, of Riley County, was indicted on one count of one count of possession of child pornography and one count of committing an offense while on release. The Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) is investigating the case. Kyler B. Slavens, 26, of Shawnee County, a superseding indictment charging Slavens for one count of distribution of child pornography, one count of receiving child pornography, one count of possession of child pornography, one count of publication of digital forgeries, one count of production of child pornography, committing a sex offense by a registered sex offender, one count of production of obscene visual representation of sexual abuse of children. The Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) is investigating the case. Deonne Stinson, 28, of Geary County, a superseding indictment charging Stinson for one count of production of child pornography, one count of distribution of child pornography, one count of receipt and one count of possession of child pornography, one count of transfer of child pornography to a minor and transportation with the intent to engage in sexual criminal activity. The Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) is investigating the case. Project Safe Childhood This case was brought as part of Project Safe Childhood, a nationwide initiative to combat the growing epidemic of child sexual exploitation and abuse launched in May 2006 by the Department of Justice. Led by U.S. Attorneys’ Offices and CEOS, Project Safe Childhood marshals federal, state, and local resources to better locate, apprehend and prosecute individuals who exploit children via the Internet, as well as to identify and rescue victims. For more information about Project Safe Childhood, please visit https://www.justice.gov/psc An indictment is merely an allegation, and all defendants are presumed innocent until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt in a court of law. ### Updated October 16, 2026 Topics Project Safe Childhood Drug Trafficking Component USAO - Kansas", "truncated": false}, {"id": "b1e079f391544ff01a80a97a", "title": "Previously convicted sex offender sentenced to 35 years in prison", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-edva/pr/previously-convicted-sex-offender-sentenced-35-years-prison", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Previously convicted sex offender sentenced to 35 years in prison\nPress Release Previously convicted sex offender sentenced to 35 years in prison Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Eastern District of Virginia NORFOLK, Va. – A Portsmouth man was sentenced today to 35 years in prison for production of child sexual abuse material (CSAM), coercion and enticement of a minor, receipt of CSAM, and possession of CSAM. According to court records and evidence presented at trial, Brian James Backues, 46, sent sexually explicit chats to entice an 11-year-old girl to produce a video of sexually explicit conduct and to commit sexual acts between February and March of 2025. Backues also sent sexually explicit videos of himself to the victim. Backues was previously convicted of child sex crimes in the City of Norfolk in 2020. Theophani K. Stamos, First Assistant U.S. Attorney for the Eastern District of Virginia, and Ian Kaufmann, Special Agent in Charge of the FBI’s Richmond Field Office, made the announcement after sentencing by Senior U.S. District Judge John A. Gibney Jr. Assistant U.S. Attorney Matthew J. Heck and Special Assistant U.S. Attorney Nikolas Nelson prosecuted the case. This case was brought as part of Project Safe Childhood, a nationwide initiative launched in May 2006 by the Department of Justice to combat the growing epidemic of child sexual exploitation and abuse. Led by U.S. Attorneys’ Offices and the Child Exploitation and Obscenity Section (CEOS), Project Safe Childhood marshals federal, state, and local resources to better locate, apprehend, and prosecute individuals who exploit children via the internet, as well as to identify and rescue victims. For more information about Project Safe Childhood, please visit www.justice.gov/psc . Related court documents and information are located on the website of the District Court for the Eastern District of Virginia or on PACER by searching for Case No. 2:25-cr-136. Contact USAVAE.Press@usdoj.gov Updated October 16, 2026 Topic Project Safe Childhood Components Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) USAO - Virginia, Eastern", "truncated": false}, {"id": "afae8bc5a6e0d30dfd20195a", "title": "Mexican National Charged with Possession with Intent to Distribute Cocaine and Methamphetamine in Lafayette", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-wdla/pr/mexican-national-charged-possession-intent-distribute-cocaine-and-methamphetamine", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Mexican National Charged with Possession with Intent to Distribute Cocaine and Methamphetamine in Lafayette\nPress Release Mexican National Charged with Possession with Intent to Distribute Cocaine and Methamphetamine in Lafayette Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Western District of Louisiana On October 13, 2026, the United States Attorney’s Office for the Western District of Louisiana charged Manuel De Jesus Arce Cruz , 42, of Mexico, by criminal complaint with Possession with Intent to Distribute Cocaine and Methamphetamine. According to court documents, on October 8, 2026, investigators began surveillance at Arce Cruz’s Lafayette-area residence in connection with a drug trafficking investigation. Investigators saw Arce Cruz, an illegal alien, depart his residence by car with a work bag. After law enforcement stopped Arce Cruz for a traffic violation, the officers conducted a lawful search of the vehicle and recovered approximately 1.5 kilograms of cocaine and half a kilogram of methamphetamine. A search warrant executed at Arce Cruz’s residence allegedly led to the recovery of an additional half of a kilogram of cocaine along with scales, baggies, cash, and other items typically associated with drug distribution. U.S. Attorney Zachary A. Keller for the Western District of Louisiana made the announcement. This case was investigated by the Drug Enforcement Administration and the Louisiana State Police. It is being prosecuted by Assistant U.S. Attorney Lauren Sarver, with assistance from Paralegal Specialist Joanne Henry-Mills. A criminal complaint is merely an allegation. All defendants are presumed innocent until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt in a court of law. You may find a copy of this press release (and any updates) on the website of the United States Attorney’s Office for the Western District of Louisiana at www.justice.gov/usao-wdla. Related court documents and information may be found on the website of the District Court for the Western District of Louisiana at www.lawd.uscourts.gov or https://www.lawd.uscourts.gov/cmecf-pacer. The case number for this matter is 6:26-mj-00249. ### CONTACT: Public Affairs (337) 262-6704 Cara.Alexander@usdoj.gov www.justice.gov/usao-wdla Updated October 16, 2026 Topics Drug Trafficking Immigration Components Drug Enforcement Administration (DEA) USAO - Louisiana, Western", "truncated": false}, {"id": "ab16480f15aafb4cf441d903", "title": "Muncie Man Accused of Falsely Requesting over $1 Million in COVID-19 Relief Tax Credits", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdin/pr/muncie-man-accused-falsely-requesting-over-1-million-covid-19-relief-tax-credits", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Muncie Man Accused of Falsely Requesting over $1 Million in COVID-19 Relief Tax Credits\nPress Release Muncie Man Accused of Falsely Requesting over $1 Million in COVID-19 Relief Tax Credits Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Southern District of Indiana INDIANAPOLIS- Gregory Vernon Dupree, 38, of Muncie, has been charged in a federal indictment with eight counts of filing false COVID-19 claims,including claims for the Employee Retention Credit (ERC) and COVID‑related Sick and Family Leave credits. These charges were brought as part of the Trump Administration’s Task Force to Eliminate Fraud. According to the indictment, between January 2021 and January 2022, Dupree allegedly filed eight Forms 941 (Employer’s Quarterly Federal Tax Return) with the Internal Revenue Service. Through these filings, Dupree made claims against the United States requesting refunds totaling over $1 million, knowing those claims to be false. The Internal Revenue Service-Criminal Investigations investigated this case. U.S. Attorney Wheeler thanked Assistant U.S. Attorney Adam Eakman, who is prosecuting this case. On April 7, the Department of Justice announced the creation of the National Fraud Enforcement Division (“Fraud Division”). The Fraud Division is investigating and prosecuting those who commit fraud against the American people. The Department’s work to combat fraud supports President Trump’s Task Force to Eliminate Fraud, a whole-of-government effort chaired by Vice President J.D. Vance to eliminate fraud, waste, and abuse within Federal benefit programs. An indictment or criminal complaint are merely allegations, and all defendants are presumed innocent until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt in a court of law. ### Updated October 16, 2026 Topics Fraud COVID-Related Fraud Components National Fraud Enforcement Division USAO - Indiana, Southern", "truncated": false}, {"id": "a78c57e6316e576e58dcd6ea", "title": "How AI Chips Will Power Google Cloud Revenue In 2027 And Beyond - Investor's Business Daily", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMikwFBVV95cUxNbHBNeURLVlZKc1N4bXVoU2t2OUVwaUY2OFNQemQ0VU93R3NIeGJLWi10SlQ5cktKdmpMTmREemZQVzZfYWx0Mkpia3FPTW1yUGR1TUY0VnlQT3JPVmtQNEFJNm11VXdMN0hiUmt5MDNSUzBEbnBNa1U0UkV4SFBOUzFWY1Q3RUZIazRLSzY0UVk5UW8?oc=5", "source": "topic_technology", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "How AI Chips Will Power Google Cloud Revenue In 2027 And Beyond - Investor's Business Daily\nHow AI Chips Will Power Google Cloud Revenue In 2027 And Beyond Investor's Business Daily", "truncated": false}, {"id": "a236a5881e817d8166b4d254", "title": "North Fort Myers Man Pleads Guilty to Illegally Possessing a Gun and Drug Offenses", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-mdfl/pr/north-fort-myers-man-pleads-guilty-illegally-possessing-gun-and-drug-offenses", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "North Fort Myers Man Pleads Guilty to Illegally Possessing a Gun and Drug Offenses\nPress Release North Fort Myers Man Pleads Guilty to Illegally Possessing a Gun and Drug Offenses Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Middle District of Florida Fort Myers, Florida – Olivier Emanuel Fevrier-Sildor (27, North Fort Myers) has pleaded guilty to conspiracy to possess with intent to distribute cocaine, possession with intent to distribute cocaine, possession of a firearm in furtherance of a drug trafficking crime, possession of a firearm by a convicted felon, and three counts of distribution of cocaine. He faces up to life imprisonment, a mandatory minimum term of 5 years, consecutive to any other sentence, as well as a $1,000,000 fine. Fevrier-Sildor remains detained pending sentencing. U.S. Attorney Gregory W. Kehoe made the announcement. According to court records, Fevrier-Sildor was arrested on March 31, 2026, in North Fort Myers, while possessing a distributable quantity of cocaine along with a firearm. Fevrier-Sildor has a prior felony conviction for robbery by sudden snatching. Therefore, he is prohibited from possessing a firearm under federal law. After his arrest and while incarcerated, Fevrier-Sildor recruited Ramiro Jimenez-Flores (20, North Fort Myers) to utilize Fevrier-Sildor’s cell phones and continue to distribute cocaine on his behalf. Law enforcement subsequently conducted three controlled buys of approximately 112 grams of cocaine from Jimenez-Flores on April 10, April 17, and April 29. During the April 29 controlled buy, law enforcement conducted surveillance on Jimenez-Flores as he met up with his source of supply, Christopher Antonio Sierra Lopez (26, Lehigh Acres), and obtained approximately 56 grams of cocaine, which he then sold to an undercover agent. Jimenez-Flores pleaded guilty to conspiracy to possess with intent to distribute cocaine on July 28, 2026. Sierra Lopez pleaded guilty to distribution of cocaine on August 18, 2026. This case was investigated by the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives and the Lee County Sheriff’s Office. It is being prosecuted by Assistant United States Attorney Matthew Redavid. Updated October 16, 2026 Topics Drug Trafficking Firearms Offenses Component USAO - Florida, Middle", "truncated": false}, {"id": "9f635582467f6e14e0e3d970", "title": "Where will Tacoma see more buses if Pierce Transit’s sales tax increase passes? - Tacoma News Tribune", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMicEFVX3lxTE4zdWJEZnJvRU5FZUx0N2xENDdxN2xwc3NDQ2RqQlZRbkUtQlJPcHBYWXRGXzZaay1yWU9zZ05ad3VJQUJsRFBwbzNMREdBeE02NE43N2phU1dGejRGemJZdnlKRGFGWFlYOUJXX1U1Vm7SAXBBVV95cUxNb0RXangzd2lGU1o3Y3FRbGNLcklVbG9TYVZ5Uzg1eVVEUm4ybjVfUEZfYXBLc2p0OFo5RHZJZWt5aUNCSkNuaXdQUjJxeUN", "source": "lane_tax_fiscal", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Where will Tacoma see more buses if Pierce Transit’s sales tax increase passes? - Tacoma News Tribune\nWhere will Tacoma see more buses if Pierce Transit’s sales tax increase passes? Tacoma News Tribune", "truncated": false}, {"id": "9ea9fd6fd41d91b3559f6223", "title": "Justice Department Reaches Settlement Agreement with City of Lexington, Mississippi, Regarding Lexington Police Department’s Unlawful Police Practices", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-reaches-settlement-agreement-city-lexington-mississippi-regarding", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Justice Department Reaches Settlement Agreement with City of Lexington, Mississippi, Regarding Lexington Police Department’s Unlawful Police Practices\nJustice Department Reaches Settlement Agreement with City of Lexington, Mississippi, Regarding Lexington Police Department’s Unlawful Police Practices\nThe Justice Department announced today that it has obtained an out-of-court settlement agreement with the City of Lexington, Mississippi, to remedy certain violations described in the Department’s Sept. 26, 2024, findings report. Specifically, the Department found that the Lexington Police Department (LPD) engages in a pattern or practice of civil rights violations.\n“The Department of Justice supports local law enforcement” said Assistant Attorney General Harmeet K. Dhillon of the Justice Department’s Civil Rights division. “We are very pleased that the City of Lexington and the Lexington Police Department reached an agreement to enhance the lawful and essential activities of the police department and its officers as they continue to protect and serve the citizens of Lexington and its surrounding areas.”\n“Today’s agreement reflects a shared commitment to meaningful and sustainable change” said U.S. Attorney J.E. Baxter Kruger for the Southern District of Mississippi. “Through these reforms, we seek not only to address the concerns identified in our investigation, but also to strengthen public trust and promote practices that respect the rights and dignity of every person.”\nAs part of the agreement, the City and LPD will amend policies and practices regarding fines and fees, bail and release criteria, and potential conflict of interests. LPD will also revise its policies and practices regarding stops, searches, arrests, and use of force policies and update its reporting and review of such activities by officers.\nThe Department looks forward to working cooperatively with the City and LPD to ensure that they remedy the alleged violations.\nThe Civil Rights Division’s Special Litigation Section and the U.S. Attorney’s Office for the Southern District of Mississippi initiated the investigation in March 2023 under 34 U.S.C. 12601 which authorizes the Department to take action to remedy a pattern or practice of deprivation of constitutional rights by state or local law enforcement agencies.\nAdditional information about the Civil Rights Division’s work regarding the conduct of law enforcement agencies is available on its website at:\nwww.justice.gov/crt/conduct-law-enforcement-agencies\nJustice Department Announces Settlement Ending Antisemitism and Race Discrimination at UNC\nThis week the U.S. Department of Justice, Civil Rights Division and the U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights (ED-OCR) entered a voluntary university-wide settlement agreement with the University...\nVA Home Loan Servicer Village Capital to Pay Over $500,000 for Violating Servicemembers’ Rights\nThe Justice Department today announced that Village Capital & Investment LLC, a servicer of home loans guaranteed by the Department of Veterans Affairs, will pay over $500,000 to resolve allegations that...\nJustice Department Notifies the District of Columbia of Investigation into Whether Conditions in the D.C. Department of Corrections Correctional Facilities Violate the Constitution\nDistrict of Columbia (D.C.) Mayor Muriel Bowser of the initiation of a federal investigation into D.C. Department of Corrections (D.C. DOC) correctional facilities. The Justice...", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "9dba44e74c53cefba242bf76", "title": "U.S. Attorney’s Office for the District of Puerto Rico Announces Creation of the Interagency Fraud Enforcement Task Force", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-pr/pr/us-attorneys-office-district-puerto-rico-announces-creation-interagency-fraud", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "U.S. Attorney’s Office for the District of Puerto Rico Announces Creation of the Interagency Fraud Enforcement Task Force\nPress Release U.S. Attorney’s Office for the District of Puerto Rico Announces Creation of the Interagency Fraud Enforcement Task Force Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Puerto Rico SAN JUAN, Puerto Rico – Acting United States Attorney Héctor Ramírez Carbó announced the creation of the Puerto Rico Interagency Fraud Enforcement Task Force, bringing together federal law enforcement agencies, state partners, and inspectors general to strengthen efforts to identify, investigate, and prosecute fraud against government programs and American taxpayers. The U.S. Attorney’s Office hosted the first task force meeting on Thursday, October 15, attended by federal prosecutors and representatives from participating agencies. The meeting focused on establishing an all-hands approach to investigations and prosecutions, expanding intelligence and data sharing, discussing efficient methods for identifying fraud schemes, analysis of investigative leads, and ensuring law enforcement resources are directed toward significant fraud schemes. “Fraud targeting federal government programs steals from taxpayers and deprives intended recipients of essential resources, weakening the impact of programs meant to serve the public,” said Acting U.S. Attorney Héctor Ramírez Carbó. “Our office—working alongside our federal, state, and local partners—will apply every criminal, civil, and administrative tool at our disposal to uncover fraud, stop it, and hold offenders accountable for the harm they cause.” The agencies joining the Puerto Rico Interagency Fraud Enforcement Task Force include: Federal Bureau of Investigation Homeland Security Investigations U.S. Secret Service Drug Enforcement Administration Office of Inspectors General: IRS Criminal Investigation (IRS-CI) U.S. Postal Inspection Service (USPIS) U.S. Postal Service Office of Inspector General (USPS-OIG) U.S. Department of Health and Human Services Office of Inspector General (HHS-OIG) U.S. Small Business Administration Office of Inspector General (SBA-OIG) U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development Office of Inspector General (HUD-OIG) U.S. Department of Education Office of Inspector General (Education-OIG) U.S. Department of Labor Office of Inspector General (DOL-OIG) U. S. Department of Agriculture Office of Inspector General (USDA-OIG) Social Security Administration Office of Inspector General (SSA-OIG) Veterans Affairs, Office of Inspector General (VA-OIG) Department of the Army Criminal Investigation Division (Army CID) State Partners Puerto Rico Department of Justice (PRDOJ) PRDOJ –  Medicaid Fraud Control Unit (MFCU) Puerto Rico Inspector General The Puerto Rico Interagency Fraud Enforcement Task Force employs an all‑hands approach and leverages expertise across various federal programs to adv", "truncated": true}, {"id": "97a91b425b00a4a412b8e85c", "title": "Oahu Girls’ Wrestling Coach Indicted for Production, Distribution, Receipt, and Possession of Child Pornography and Child Sex Trafficking", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-hi/pr/oahu-girls-wrestling-coach-indicted-production-distribution-receipt-and-possession-child", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Oahu Girls’ Wrestling Coach Indicted for Production, Distribution, Receipt, and Possession of Child Pornography and Child Sex Trafficking\nPress Release Oahu Girls’ Wrestling Coach Indicted for Production, Distribution, Receipt, and Possession of Child Pornography and Child Sex Trafficking Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Hawaii HONOLULU – United States Attorney Ken Sorenson announced that Sean Sakaida, 44, of Honolulu, Hawaii, was indicted on October 15, 2026, and charged with four counts of production of child pornography, four counts of distribution of child pornography, one count of receipt of child pornography, four counts of possession of child pornography, and one count of child sex trafficking.  He was previously arrested and taken into custody on September 24, 2026, after being charged by criminal complaint. According to court records, Sakaida, a former coach of the girls’ wrestling team at Moanalua High School, frequently communicated with a minor victim using the messaging application WhatsApp between 2025 and 2026.  In these communications, Sakaida received multiple sexually explicit photographs of the minor victim.  As charged in the indictment, he also produced and distributed multiple videos of himself engaged in sexual acts with the minor victim.  Sakaida was also charged with enticing a second minor victim into engaging in a commercial sex act. If convicted, Sakaida faces at least 15 years and up to 30 years in prison on each production count, at least 5 years and up to 20 years in prison on each distribution and receipt count, up to 10 years in prison on each possession count, up to life in prison on the sex trafficking count, a fine of up to $250,000, plus a term of supervised release. The charges in the indictment are merely accusations, and the defendant is presumed innocent unless and until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt in a court of law.  In the case of conviction, any sentence would be imposed by a United States District Judge based on the statutory sentencing factors and the advisory United States Sentencing Guidelines. The FBI is investigating the case. Special Assistant U.S. Attorney Heidi Turner is prosecuting the case. The FBI’s Honolulu Field Office is seeking to identify potential victims of Sean Sakaida. If you and/or your minor dependent(s) were victimized by Sean Sakaida or have information relevant to this investigation, please fill out this short form: https://forms.fbi.gov/victims/SakaidaVictims/view If you know of someone else who has possibly been victimized by Sean Sakaida, please encourage them to complete the form themselves. This case was brought as part of Project Safe Childhood, a nationwide initiative to combat the growing epidemic of child sexual exploitation and abuse launched in May 2006 by the Department of Justice. Led by U.S. Attorneys' Offices and CEOS, Project Safe Childhood marshals federa", "truncated": true}, {"id": "95ddbfb76823ee7e2654bf93", "title": "Justice Department Announces Charges Involving 16 Aliens for Illegal Voting", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-charges-involving-16-aliens-illegal-voting", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Justice Department Announces Charges Involving 16 Aliens for Illegal Voting\nJustice Department Announces Charges Involving 16 Aliens for Illegal Voting\nToday, the Justice Department announced charges involving 16 aliens in North Carolina, New York, and Idaho, including an illegal alien, in connection with illegal voting, false claims of U.S. citizenship, and related election crimes. The cases include voting by aliens in federal elections and false claims of U.S. citizenship to register to vote or to vote.\n10 aliens have been charged for illegally voting in the 2024 elections.\nEduardd Omar Majano Sosa, 22, an illegal alien from El Salvador, has been charged with one count of falsely claiming citizenship to vote and one count of voting by an alien. He registered to vote in 2024 and voted in the 2024 General Election.\nEduardd Omar Majano Sosa, 22, an illegal alien from El Salvador\nA national of former Yugoslavia Gjina Gojcaj McDonald, 60, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with one count of falsely claiming citizenship to vote and one count of voting by an alien. She registered to vote in 2008 and voted in the 2024, 2022, 2020, 2016, 2012, 2010, and 2008 General Elections, as well as the 2019 Special Election and the 2012 Primary Election.\nGerman national James Edward Herrmann, 47, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with one count of falsely claiming citizenship to vote and one count of voting by an alien. He registered to vote in 2018 and voted in the 2024 and 2020 General Elections.\nGerman national Karl Fout, 73, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with one count of falsely claiming citizenship to vote and one count of voting by an alien. He registered to vote in 2008 and voted in the 2024, 2020, 2018, 2016, 2014, 2012, and 2008 General Elections.\nGerman national Karl Fout, 73, a lawful permanent resident\nJamaican national Lawton Caswell McKenzie, 46, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with one count of falsely claiming citizenship to vote and one count of voting by an alien. He registered to vote in 2007 and 2012 and voted in the 2024, 2020, and 2012 General Elections.\nJamaican national Lawton Caswell McKenzie, 46, a lawful permanent resident\nHaitian national Lionel Paul, 62, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with one count of falsely claiming citizenship to vote and one count of voting by an alien. He registered to vote in 2024 and voted in the 2024 General Election.\nHaitian national Lionel Paul, 62, a lawful permanent resident\nPanamanian national Ludgardys Olena Matthews, 62, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with one count of falsely claiming citizenship to vote and one count of voting by an alien. She registered to vote in 2009 and voted in the 2024, 2020, and 2018 General Elections, as well as the 2020 Primary Election.\nPanamanian national Ludgardys Olena Matthews, 62, a lawful permanent resident\nJamaican national Mervin Sylvester Bogle, 60, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with one count of falsely claiming citizenship to vote. He registered to vote in 2024 and completed a ballot in the 2024 General Election and voted in the 2012 and 2008 General Elections.\nGerman national Ramona Abreu-Exia, 71, a lawful permanent, has been charged with one count of falsely claiming citizenship to vote and one count of voting by an alien. She registered to vote in 2004 and voted in the 2024, 2020, 2018, 2016, 2012, 2010, 2008, and 2004 General Elections, as well as the 2008 Primary Election.\nCanadian national Robert Harold Corbett, 69, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with one count of falsely claiming citizenship to vote and one count of voting by an alien. He voted in 2024, 2016, 2004, 2002, 2000, 1998, and General Elections. He registered to vote in 1998.\nFive aliens have been arrested for voter fraud.\nBritish national Vikki Bradt, 51, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with voting by an alien. Bradt allegedly voted in an election held in part for the purpose of electing a candidate for President, Vice President, Member of the United States Senate, or Member of the United States House of Representatives.\nBritish national Vikki Bradt, age 51, a lawful permanent resident\nBritish national Samantha Merkle, 55, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with voting by an alien. Merkle allegedly voted in an election held in part for the purpose of electing a candidate for President, Vice President, Member of the United States Senate, or Member of the United States House of Representatives.\nBritish national Samantha Merkle, age 55, a lawful permanent resident\nBosnian national Suad Sasic, 30, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with voting by an alien. Sasic allegedly voted in an election held in part for the purpose of electing a candidate for President, Vice President, Member of the United States Senate, or Member of the United States House of Representatives.\nBosnian national Suad Sasic, age 30, a lawful permanent resident\nCanadian national Jacques Patry, 64, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with voting by an alien. Patry allegedly voted in an election held in part for the purpose of electing a candidate for President, Vice President, Member of the United States Senate, or Member of the United States House of Representatives.\nCanadian national Jacques Patry, age 64, a lawful permanent resident\nCanadian national Paula Stitt, 57, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with voting by an alien. Stitt allegedly voted in an election held in part for the purpose of electing a candidate for President, Vice President, Member of the United States Senate, or Member of the United States House of Representatives.\nCanadian national Paula Stitt, age 57, a lawful permanent resident\nCanadian national, Taralynn Bosco, 48, a lawful permanent resident, has been charged with illegal voting by alien. Bosco allegedly voted in the 2022 general election even though she was not a United States citizen.\nA complaint, indictment, and information merely contain accusations. All defendants are presumed innocent until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt in a court of law.\nJustice Department Charges 28 Foreign Nationals in California for Election Crimes\nFederal law enforcement throughout California today arrested 11 foreign nationals living in the United States who are among 28 total charged in criminal complaints with federal crimes, including that they...\nIllegal Alien Admits to Illegally Voting in Federal Election in New Jersey\nAn illegal alien from Jamaica admitted to illegally voting in a federal election, U.S. Attorney Robert Frazer for the District of New Jersey announced today.\nJustice Department Sues State of California over Voter List\nThe Justice Department, working with the Department of Homeland Security (DHS), today filed a complaint against the State of California over its voter list after determining that more than 20,000...", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "9542ffe155fa8b5ef2e430c9", "title": "New York Man Sentenced to Five Years in Prison for Participating in a Nationwide Bank Fraud Conspiracy", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdal/pr/new-york-man-sentenced-five-years-prison-participating-nationwide-bank-fraud", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "New York Man Sentenced to Five Years in Prison for Participating in a Nationwide Bank Fraud Conspiracy\nPress Release New York Man Sentenced to Five Years in Prison for Participating in a Nationwide Bank Fraud Conspiracy Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Southern District of Alabama MOBILE, AL – A New York man was sentenced to 60 months in prison for participating in a nationwide bank fraud conspiracy involving stolen and counterfeited financial instruments. According to court documents, Curtis Jackson, 31, of Staten Island, New York, admitted that, between February 2023 and June 2024, he conspired with LaEric Marques Williams-Beverly and other conspirators based in Mobile, Alabama, to commit mail theft and bank fraud involving stolen and counterfeited checks. The checks bore the personal identifying information of numerous identity theft victims. Jackson and Williams-Beverly administered an illicit Telegram channel called “House of Pain & Drank,” where millions of dollars’ worth of stolen financial instruments were posted for sale to other fraudsters. In January 2024, agents obtained video footage from a Mobile business (“Business-1”) showing Williams-Beverly stealing checks from the business’s mailbox. Williams-Beverly then coordinated with other coconspirators to counterfeit the checks and fraudulently deposit them into a bank account. ATM surveillance footage captured Williams-Beverly making the fraudulent deposits. Later that month, police in New York City arrested Jackson in possession of a counterfeited check for $43,686.45, drawn on Business-1’s bank account. Jackson had received the check from Williams-Beverly. Jackson’s iCloud account also contained images of stolen and counterfeited checks, including a Citibank deposit slip for a $62,673.46 check drawn on Business-1’s bank account. In June 2024, federal agents executed a search warrant at Williams-Beverly’s home in Mobile. Agents seized numerous pieces of stolen mail, counterfeited checks, debit and credit cards belonging to other individuals, counterfeiting tools, two pistols, and Williams-Beverly’s cell phone. Williams-Beverly admitted to agents that he produced counterfeited checks and coordinated the purchase and sale of stolen checks through the fraudulent Telegram channel he operated with Jackson. Electronic evidence recovered from the conspirators’ cell phones, iCloud accounts, and social media accounts demonstrated the extensive scope of the conspiracy. U.S. District Judge Kristi K. DuBose sentenced Jackson to serve 60 months in federal prison, emphasizing that he was a recidivist with several prior state arrests and convictions for theft, larceny, possession of forged instruments, possession of drugs, and bail jumping. In addition to the five-year prison term, Judge DuBose ordered Jackson to serve a five-year term of supervised release upon his release from prison, during which time he will be su", "truncated": true}, {"id": "924d6567ade55916e8670e74", "title": "Former New Orleans Personal Injury Attorney Sentenced in Connection with Staged Accident Probe", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-edla/pr/former-new-orleans-personal-injury-attorney-sentenced-connection-staged-accident-probe", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Former New Orleans Personal Injury Attorney Sentenced in Connection with Staged Accident Probe\nPress Release Former New Orleans Personal Injury Attorney Sentenced in Connection with Staged Accident Probe Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Eastern District of Louisiana shane.jones@usdoj.gov NEW ORLEANS, LOUISIANA – DANNY PATRICK KEATING, JR. , age 57, of New Orleans, was sentenced on October 15, 2026 for Conspiracy to Commit Mail and Wire Fraud, in violation of Title 18, United States Code, Section 371, arising out of staged automobile accidents with tractor-trailers occurring in New Orleans, announced United States Attorney David I. Courcelle. KEATING was indicted on November 5, 2020, and charged with knowingly engaging in a vast conspiracy involving the intentional staging of motor vehicle collisions with tractor-trailers and commercial vehicles in the metropolitan New Orleans area. Thus far, sixty-three (63) defendants have been charged in the federal probe into the staging of such collisions. KEATING was a personal injury attorney licensed to practice law in Louisiana. At the time of his plea on June 17, 2021, KEATING admitted he knowingly paid Damian Labeaud for thirty-one (31) illegally staged tractor-trailer accidents. KEATING represented seventy-seven (77) plaintiffs involved in the thirty-one (31) accidents staged by Labeaud. KEATING settled seventeen (17) of the thirty-one (31) staged accidents. The indictment charged that KEATING and his clients received a total of approximately $1,500,000.00 in settlement monies resulting from his representation of these clients, and that KEATING kept approximately $358,000.00 of these monies as his attorney’s fees. KEATING admitted filing lawsuits in state and federal court in Louisiana, on behalf of his clients who were involved in the staged accidents. The lawsuits he filed on behalf of the seventy-seven (77) plaintiffs fraudulently misrepresented the identity of the driver of these vehicles, fraudulently misrepresented who was at fault in the staged accidents, and fraudulently claimed injuries. KEATING used the U.S Postal Service to mail settlement demands on behalf of his clients from the Eastern District of Louisiana to various out-of-state locations. In many cases, KEATING ’s clients provided false testimony in depositions taken in conjunction with lawsuits filed by KEATING . United States District Judge Nanette Jolivette Brown sentenced KEATING to thirty-two (32) months of imprisonment, followed by three (3) years of supervised release, as well as the payment of $657,405.34 in restitution, and a $100.00 mandatory special assessment fee. The U.S. Attorney’s Office would like to acknowledge the assistance of the Federal Bureau of Investigation, Louisiana State Police, and the Metropolitan Crime Commission with this matter. The prosecution of this case was handled by Brian M. Klebba, Deputy Chief of the Criminal Divi", "truncated": true}, {"id": "90c9ffe68951eb7c239da4a0", "title": "Milwaukee Man Sentenced to 38 Years in Federal Prison for  Murder of Letter Carrier", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-edwi/pr/milwaukee-man-sentenced-38-years-federal-prison-murder-letter-carrier", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Milwaukee Man Sentenced to 38 Years in Federal Prison for  Murder of Letter Carrier\nPress Release Milwaukee Man Sentenced to 38 Years in Federal Prison for  Murder of Letter Carrier Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Eastern District of Wisconsin Brad D. Schimel, First Assistant United States Attorney for the Eastern District of Wisconsin, announced that on October 14, 2026, United States District Court Chief Judge Pamela Pepper sentenced Kevin McCaa (age 40) of Milwaukee, Wisconsin, to 38 years’ imprisonment, followed by five years of supervised release. On July 8, 2026, Kevin McCaa pled guilty to the murder of a United States Postal Employee and discharge of a firearm during a crime of violence. As detailed in the court record, veteran United States Postal Service letter carrier Aundre Cross was delivering mail in Milwaukee on the afternoon of December 9, 2022, when he was shot in the head and killed. Evidence established that Kevin McCaa and Charles Ducksworth Jr. were responsible for the homicide. McCaa and Ducksworth surveilled the letter carrier for over 30 minutes prior to the homicide and targeted him because they thought he had stolen their drug package. After the homicide, they threw the firearm in a nearby creek, changed their phone numbers, and disposed of clothing.  McCaa and Ducksworth have been in federal custody since their arrest for these offenses on December 27, 2022. At the sentencing hearing, Judge Pepper credited the “old-fashioned” investigative work that led to the arrest of McCaa and Ducksworth. Law enforcement utilized warrants for phone record data, automated license plate reader technology and surveillance footage from numerous residences and businesses in the area of the homicide to show the suspects’ activities before and after the homicide. Judge Pepper further determined that the facts of the case reflected that the crime was “premeditated, planned beforehand, with malice aforethought” and called it “incomprehensible.” “As the Chief Judge noted, these defendants were brought to justice through excellent work by investigators and prosecutors,” said First Assistant U.S. Attorney Schimel. “This was a cold-blooded, senseless murder of a beloved member of his family and the community he served. McCaa will rightly spend virtually the rest of his life in federal prison.” “Holding those accountable who commit violence against employees of the U.S. Postal Service is our agency’s number one priority. Today’s sentencing hopefully brings a small amount of justice and closure for the family and friends of Aundre Cross. The U.S. Postal Inspection Service would like to thank the Milwaukee Police Department and FBI Milwaukee for their unwavering assistance with this investigation.” stated Nicholas Bucciarelli, Inspector in Charge, Chicago Division of U.S. Postal Inspection Service. “Kevin McCaa’s heinous crime was deserving of the significant sentenc", "truncated": true}, {"id": "8fab7f6eb4b40a8df26bc59d", "title": "BERETTA HOLDING S.A. ANNOUNCES PRELIMINARY RESULTS OF CASH TENDER OFFER FOR SHARES OF STURM, RUGER & COMPANY, INC. - TradingView", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi_gFBVV95cUxPelBhaG5OX215Si1SaXR3aThSMk1iMkMwZlFsdjNhMHdkcEpzSGt1bkhIaU5fVy1NQ0pfYk1mM2diMGhKMHVoeU1QZWdDMjMwc1BZSzdCMUtiS3Myb0xQR2MwcjFQbUF1NE1ScGxjRTQ2cGpudU9BUWNTRWN2MmlDaHkzdVJ4UVRQdjJpV2F1MEtza1BhVjhkRnBhX0JrTGtGVWxVNWFPTFk2dGtURlNrSXh1SldRMjQ4UDJEdWdKTGp", "source": "sec_SC_TO-T:google_fallback", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "BERETTA HOLDING S.A. ANNOUNCES PRELIMINARY RESULTS OF CASH TENDER OFFER FOR SHARES OF STURM, RUGER & COMPANY, INC. - TradingView\nBERETTA HOLDING S.A. ANNOUNCES PRELIMINARY RESULTS OF CASH TENDER OFFER FOR SHARES OF STURM, RUGER & COMPANY, INC. TradingView", "truncated": false}, {"id": "8ec84b5f88088a1d92fc5241", "title": "Scotland County Man Convicted on Drug and Firearm Charges", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-mdnc/pr/scotland-county-man-convicted-drug-and-firearm-charges", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Scotland County Man Convicted on Drug and Firearm Charges\nPress Release Scotland County Man Convicted on Drug and Firearm Charges Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Middle District of North Carolina GREENSBORO, N.C. – A jury in federal district court convicted VERNON SCOTT NORTON today of multiple drug and firearm charges, announced United States Attorney Dan Bishop of the Middle District of North Carolina. According to court documents, NORTON, age 34, was initially indicted on October 27, 2025, charged in Count One with conspiracy to distribute 500 grams or more of a mixture and substance containing a detectable amount of methamphetamine; in Count Two with possession of a firearm in furtherance of the offense charged in Count One; in Count Three with possession of a machinegun; and in Counts Four and Five with felon in possession of firearms. Evidence at trial showed that NORTON was a previously convicted felon who possessed 31 firearms and a significant quantity of methamphetamine at his trailer in Laurel Hill, North Carolina. On December 29, 2023, law enforcement officers executed a search warrant at the property early in the morning. Officers located eight firearms inside the trailer and 23 additional firearms in a 2005 Infiniti parked in the yard. One of the firearms in the Infiniti was a machinegun—a Glock Model 19X 9mm pistol equipped with a machinegun conversion device. Inside the trailer, officers recovered nearly 1.4 kilograms of methamphetamine hydrochloride. At the time of his arrest, NORTON had more than $6,000 in cash on his person. A readily accessible firearm—a Sturm/Ruger & Co. Inc. Model Ruger P94 9mm pistol—was found resting on the arm of a sofa next to a loaded magazine. Before executing the warrant, law enforcement had conducted multiple controlled purchases of narcotics from the property. A jury convicted NORTON on all counts of the Indictment after a four-day trial. At sentencing, NORTON faces a term of imprisonment of 15 years to life, not less than 5 years of supervised release, fines, and restitution. Sentencing is scheduled for February 17, 2027, before Senior United States District Judge William L. Osteen, Jr. in Greensboro. The case was investigated by the Scotland County Sheriff’s Office and the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms, and Explosives. The case was prosecuted by Assistant United States Attorneys Frank J. Chut and Chase Stevens. ### Updated October 16, 2026 Component USAO - North Carolina, Middle", "truncated": false}, {"id": "8e4ccd63587b244e2eb8c33d", "title": "Evansville Tax Preparer Lands in Federal Prison for $965,000 COVID 19 Tax Credit Fraud", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdin/pr/evansville-tax-preparer-lands-federal-prison-965000-covid-19-tax-credit-fraud", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Evansville Tax Preparer Lands in Federal Prison for $965,000 COVID 19 Tax Credit Fraud\nPress Release Evansville Tax Preparer Lands in Federal Prison for $965,000 COVID 19 Tax Credit Fraud Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Southern District of Indiana EVANSVILLE- Tonda Avery, 55, of Evansville, has been sentenced to one year and one day in federal prison, followed by one year of supervised release, after pleading guilty to five counts of Aiding and Assisting in the Preparation and Presentation of a False and Fraudulent Tax Return. Avery was also ordered to pay $1,318,455.43 in restitution. This case was brought as part of the Trump Administration’s Task Force to Eliminate Fraud. According to court documents, Avery operated Diversity Tax Service (DTS) in Evansville, Indiana. Avery prepared federal income tax returns for clients and employed other tax return preparers at DTS. Between 2020 and 2021, Avery prepared and filed approximately 123 tax returns containing false Forms 7202, causing a total tax loss of $965,203. Form 7202 allowed eligible self-employed individuals to claim refundable tax credits for certain periods in which they were unable to work because of circumstances related to COVID-19. As part of her scheme, Avery manufactured or inflated her clients’ eligibility for these credits. For clients who did not own legitimate businesses, Avery created fictitious businesses and prepared false Schedule C forms reporting nonexistent business income and expenses. She then used the fictitious businesses to support fraudulent Forms 7202 and claim COVID-19 sick and family leave tax credits to which the clients were not entitled. For clients who did have legitimate businesses, Avery inflated information used to calculate the credits, including the number of days the clients purportedly were unable to work and their net earnings from self-employment. These false figures resulted in the clients claiming larger tax credits and receiving refunds greater than they were entitled to receive. The prevalence of Forms 7202 in Avery’s returns was dramatically higher than among taxpayers generally. In 2020, approximately 33.82% of the returns Avery filed included a Form 7202, compared with approximately 0.90% of returns nationwide and 0.50% of returns in Indiana. In 2021, Forms 7202 appeared on approximately 61.14% of Avery’s returns, compared with approximately 0.90% nationwide and 0.40% in Indiana. Avery also instructed and encouraged other tax return preparers at DTS to prepare fraudulent returns, including returns containing false Forms 7202. Avery charged fees based on the number of forms and schedules included in a tax return. When Avery added a fraudulent Schedule C and Form 7202 to a client’s return, she increased her fee by $800. “Ms. Avery’s scheme exploited pandemic relief programs designed to help Americans in genuine need,” said Tom Wheeler, United States Attorney fo", "truncated": true}, {"id": "8da1289aac555dd483227871", "title": "Yerevan says enclave issue will not prevent signing of peace agreement with Baku - Today.Az", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiS0FVX3lxTE1qUlgyeWRiYkRVQ0kxNjVSaWlHNmtHVGwxRXJFSU5kcFk1MnliUUN1TmthN2NsQjN0eUN6bV9ReVFyNmdiS21BRjZzcw?oc=5", "source": "lane_regime_break", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Yerevan says enclave issue will not prevent signing of peace agreement with Baku - Today.Az\nYerevan says enclave issue will not prevent signing of peace agreement with Baku Today.Az", "truncated": false}, {"id": "8a3a38d93cc861daad473f6e", "title": "11th Circuit Affirms Conviction, 30-Year Sentence in Cold Case Prosecution of 2001 Murder of Pregnant Army Private in Germany", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-ndfl/pr/11th-circuit-affirms-conviction-30-year-sentence-cold-case-prosecution-2001-murder", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "11th Circuit Affirms Conviction, 30-Year Sentence in Cold Case Prosecution of 2001 Murder of Pregnant Army Private in Germany\nPress Release 11th Circuit Affirms Conviction, 30-Year Sentence in Cold Case Prosecution of 2001 Murder of Pregnant Army Private in Germany Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Northern District of Florida Pensacola, Florida – The United States Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit affirmed the conviction and sentence of Shannon L. Wilkerson, 46, for the 2001 murder of 19-year-old Army Private First Class Amanda Gonzales and her unborn child. In May 2024, a Pensacola jury convicted Wilkerson, a former U.S. solider, of second-degree murder. According to court documents, Wilkerson beat and strangled Gonzales to death on November 3, 2001, in her barracks room at Fliegerhorst Kaserne, then a U.S. Army base in Hanau, Germany. Evidence introduced at trial indicated that Wilkerson feared he was the father of Gonzales’ unborn child and that her pregnancy would interfere with his military career and his marriage to another soldier on the base. Wilkerson was a member of the U.S. Armed Forces at the time of the offense but was later discharged. The district court sentenced Wilkerson to 30 years’ imprisonment. United States Attorney John P. Heekin said: “This cold case prosecution demonstrated the lengths to which my office will go to achieve justice for crime victims.  I am extremely proud that we successfully defended this conviction and sentence against the defendant’s appeal. Nothing can bring back the innocent lives taken by this defendant’s heinous crime, but defending this successful prosecution against his appeal ensures he will not escape accountability.” “This case serves as a perfect example of the Criminal Division’s pursuit of justice no matter the challenge,” said Assistant Attorney General A. Tysen Duva of the Justice Department’s Criminal Division.  “I am proud of the work of the Criminal Division prosecutors, the Northern District of Florida, and the federal agents that worked tirelessly to solve this terrible cold case murder.  I also want to express my deepest condolences to the family of PFC Amanda Gonzales and hope that the affirming of Shannon Wilkerson’s conviction by the Court of Appeals brings some sense of closure to this two-decade pursuit of justice.” Following his conviction and sentencing, Wilkerson appealed to the Eleventh Circuit. After briefing by the U.S. Attorney’s Office for the Northern District of Florida defending the conviction and sentence, the Court rejected each of Wilkerson’s arguments in an unpublished opinion without oral argument. It concluded that the district court acted appropriately in keeping out purely speculative alternative-perpetrator evidence claiming that another soldier committed and pleaded to an unrelated double-homicide in 2006, refusing to entertain the defense’s discovery “fishing expediti", "truncated": true}, {"id": "894b8f115242c63b9dc08a5e", "title": "Previously Convicted Bank Robber Charged Again for Robbing Banks  Wearing “Old Man” Mask", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-mdfl/pr/previously-convicted-bank-robber-charged-again-robbing-banks-wearing-old-man-mask", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Previously Convicted Bank Robber Charged Again for Robbing Banks  Wearing “Old Man” Mask\nPress Release Previously Convicted Bank Robber Charged Again for Robbing Banks  Wearing “Old Man” Mask Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Middle District of Florida Tampa, Florida – David Sartin, Jr. (40, St. Petersburg) has been charged by criminal complaint with two counts of bank robbery. If convicted, he faces up to 20 years in federal prison on each count. United States Attorney Gregory W. Kehoe made the announcement. According to court documents, on August 24, 2026, Sartin robbed a bank in Largo. On September 15, 2026, he robbed a bank in St. Petersburg. During both robberies, Sartin entered the bank wearing a prosthetic “old man” mask, approached the teller counter and demanded money, and instructed the teller not to include dye packs. The investigation revealed that Sartin had arrived at a nearby gas station shortly before the first robbery, then drove through the surrounding area before parking, switching to a bicycle, and approaching the bank. After the robbery, Sartin fled on the bicycle, retrieved the staged vehicle, and drove away. The vehicle was later seen on multiple nearby roadways heading in the direction of his residence. Video surveillance captured a vehicle leaving Sartin’s home before the second robbery and returning afterwards. When the vehicle returned to the residence, a white male resembling Sartin was seen unloading a bicycle from the vehicle and rolling it toward the residence. The next day, investigators observed Sartin spray paint the vehicle’s white roof black, making the entire vehicle appear black in color. A search of Sartin’s residence uncovered a bicycle, a blue suit, and a black bag, all similar to the items used during the first robbery. A black suit similar to the one used during the second robbery was also recovered. Investigators also seized materials covered in black spray paint and a receipt for clothing purchased the day before the second robbery. A search of Sartin’s storage unit revealed a backpack and laptop bag containing disguise items matching those seen in the surveillance footage from the robberies, including the old‑man mask with grey facial hair, dark‑framed glasses, and work gloves with black paint on them. The bags also contained a fake handgun and clothing consistent with the disguise worn during the second robbery, including a white dress shirt with black studs, a pinstriped sports coat, a dark blue flat cap, and black dress shoes and pants. Agents also searched another residence where Sartin occasionally stayed. There, they found several items, including a prosthetic mask different from the one used in the robberies and a journal titled, “God’s Best friend is a Bank Robber! Uncle Henry Cops and Robbers the world through the eyes of a Bank Robber.” The journal was dated “Jan 1, 2021,” and was “written by: David Sartin.” O", "truncated": true}, {"id": "88bc8123419e5f09c7fb83ee", "title": "Illegal Alien from Brazil Sentenced for Illegally Reentering the US After Prior Removal", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-me/pr/illegal-alien-brazil-sentenced-illegally-reentering-us-after-prior-removal", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Illegal Alien from Brazil Sentenced for Illegally Reentering the US After Prior Removal\nPress Release Illegal Alien from Brazil Sentenced for Illegally Reentering the US After Prior Removal Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Maine Jose Carlos Ferreira was previously removed in 2010 BANGOR, Maine: A Brazilian national was sentenced today in U.S. District Court in Bangor for reentering the United States after a prior removal. U.S. District Judge John A. Woodcock, Jr. sentenced Jose Carlos Ferreria , 50, to time served (59 days). Ferreira pleaded guilty on September 4, 2026. According to court records, on August 15, 2026, U.S. Border Patrol Agents in Franklin County observed a car registered to an alien who had been ordered removed from the United States. During a subsequent roadside immigration inspection, agents determined that the car’s three occupants were, in fact, illegal aliens without lawful authority to be in the country. They were transported for processing to the Rangeley Border Patrol Station, where agents determined that Ferreira had been ordered removed in 2005, and subsequently removed from the U.S. twice in 2010. The U.S. Border Patrol investigated the case. Operation Take Back America : This case is part of Operation Take Back America , a nationwide initiative that marshals the full resources of the Department of Justice to repel the invasion of illegal immigration, achieve the total elimination of cartels and transnational criminal organizations (TCOs), and protect our communities from the perpetrators of violent crime. ### Contact Chris Ruge, Assistant United States Attorney (Tel: 207-945-0373) Updated October 16, 2026 Topics Operation Take Back America Immigration Component USAO - Maine", "truncated": false}, {"id": "8682ff383569f01acb1e8548", "title": "Miracle-Ear and its Foundation Agree to Pay over $6.4 Million to Resolve False Claims Allegations Concerning the Paycheck Protection Program", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-edwi/pr/miracle-ear-and-its-foundation-agree-pay-over-64-million-resolve-false-claims", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Miracle-Ear and its Foundation Agree to Pay over $6.4 Million to Resolve False Claims Allegations Concerning the Paycheck Protection Program\nPress Release Miracle-Ear and its Foundation Agree to Pay over $6.4 Million to Resolve False Claims Allegations Concerning the Paycheck Protection Program Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Eastern District of Wisconsin Brad D. Schimel, First Assistant United States Attorney for the Eastern District of Wisconsin, announced a settlement with Miracle-Ear, Inc., and the Miracle-Ear Foundation to settle claims that they improperly obtained three loans through the Paycheck Protection Program (“PPP”).  Miracle-Ear and its foundation agreed to pay $6,473,222 to resolve the government’s claims. Congress enacted the PPP in March 2020 to provide emergency assistance to small businesses suffering from the economic impact of the COVID-19 pandemic. The program was operated by the Small Business Administration (SBA) and allowed eligible small businesses and nonprofits to receive loans guaranteed by the federal government.  If the borrower spent the funds on qualified expenses, the government repaid the loan on the borrower’s behalf.  To qualify as a small business eligible for a PPP loan, a borrower and any affiliated companies generally could not employ more than 500 individuals.  In its loan application, moreover, the borrower was required to certify that it was eligible for the PPP and that the information provided in the application was accurate. Miracle-Ear distributes and sells hearing aids and associated products through stores owned by Miracle-Ear as well as stores owned and operated by franchisees.  Miracle-Ear Foundation is a nonprofit organization that provides free hearing aids to low-income individuals.  Miracle-Ear received a first-draw PPP loan in the amount of $1,962,000 in June 2020 and a second-draw PPP loan in the amount of $1,962,000 in February 2021. Miracle-Ear Foundation received a single PPP loan in the amount of $58,100 in May 2020. Miracle-Ear is wholly owned by Amplifon (USA) Inc., which, in turn, is wholly owned by Amplifon S.p.A., an Italian public company with thousands of employees and annual sales exceeding 1.5 billion euros in 2020 and 2021. Miracle-Ear Foundation is likewise affiliated with Amplifon S.p.A.  The government alleges that Miracle-Ear and its foundation falsely certified that they were eligible for the PPP loans.  Specifically, the United States alleges that Miracle-Ear and its foundation were not eligible for their PPP loans because they were affiliated with Amplifon S.p.A. and that the Amplifon corporate group employed too many individuals for Miracle-Ear and its foundation to qualify for PPP loans.  The United States further contends that Miracle-Ear provided false information in its loan applications to make it appear that Miracle-Ear was covered by an exemption to the SBA’s affiliation rules", "truncated": true}, {"id": "8613d36e39e97e35c500b46a", "title": "Three People Charged in Conspiracy to Commit Sex Trafficking of Minors and Related Charges", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/three-people-charged-conspiracy-commit-sex-trafficking-minors-and-related-charges", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Three People Charged in Conspiracy to Commit Sex Trafficking of Minors and Related Charges\nThree People Charged in Conspiracy to Commit Sex Trafficking of Minors and Related Charges\nAn indictment was unsealed today in the District of Delaware charging Jamar Geeter, 39, of San Francisco, California, Kalvin Carter Jr., 44, of Waldorf, Maryland, and Jada Hasty, 29, of Coatesville, Pennsylvania, with conspiracy to commit sex trafficking of a minor by force, fraud, and coercion, sex trafficking, and coercion and enticement of a minor. Geeter and Hasty are also charged with producing child sexual abuse material (CSAM).\n“These three individuals are alleged to have exploited and abused four victims — including three children — for their financial gain,” said Assistant Attorney General A. Tysen Duva of the Justice Department’s Criminal Division. “Human trafficking is a serious crime that strips people of their dignity and safety. Anyone who engages in this heinous conduct will be investigated and prosecuted.”\n“Human-trafficking schemes don’t stop at state lines, and neither does our work to investigate them,” said U.S. Attorney Benjamin L. Wallace for the District of Delaware. “Alongside the Criminal Division and the FBI, my office will pursue anyone charged with exploiting minors in Delaware, no matter where the perpetrators reside.”\n“According to court documents, Jamar Geeter, already a convicted sex trafficker, continued directing abuse from behind bars with Jada Hasty and Kalvin Carter Jr. as his willing accomplices,” said Special Agent in Charge Jimmy Paul of FBI Baltimore. “I am proud of the work done by our agents, analysts and victim specialists who handled this case with urgency and care. Their investigation spanned coast- to-coast, rescuing three children and one woman from the clutches of human trafficking and guaranteeing their alleged abusers will feel the full weight of the federal justice system.”\nAccording to court documents, Geeter is currently serving a 97-year sentence in California for unrelated state sex trafficking convictions. It is alleged that Geeter used contraband cell phones to engage in some of the alleged criminal conduct while incarcerated.\nIt is also alleged that the defendants conspired to compel three minors and one adult to engage in commercial sex acts in Delaware and elsewhere. Geeter and Hasty recruited, enticed, harbored, transported, provided, obtained, and maintained two minors knowing or in reckless disregard of the fact that they were under the age of 18 and would be caused to engage in commercial sex acts. Geeter and Hasty also used facilities of interstate commerce, including cell phones and the internet, to induce and entice three minors to engage in prostitution. Geeter and Hasty produced CSAM images of two of the minor victims as well. The defendants involved in the scheme used force, threats of force, fraud, or coercion to compel the victims to engage in commercial sex acts.\nAll three defendants are charged with conspiracy to traffic a minor and by force, fraud, and coercion and sex trafficking by force, fraud, or coercion. Geeter and Hasty are also charged with sex trafficking of a minor and by force, fraud, or coercion, coercion and enticement of a minor, and production of CSAM materials. Geeter is charged with additional counts of production and receipt of CSAM materials. If convicted, all three defendants face a minimum penalty of 15 years in prison and a maximum penalty of life in prison on the sex trafficking by force, fraud, and coercion charges.\nIn addition, Geeter and Hasty face a minimum penalty of 15 years in prison and a maximum penalty of life in prison for sex trafficking of a minor charges and coercion and enticement of a minor. Geeter faces a minimum penalty of 25 years in prison and a maximum penalty of 50 years in prison while Hasty faces a minimum penalty of 15 years in prison and a maximum penalty of 30 years in prison on the CSAM charges.\nThe FBI’s Delaware Violent Crime and Safe Streets Task Force and Delaware State Police are investigating the case.\nTrial Attorney Nicole Raspa of the Criminal Division’s Human Rights and Special Prosecutions Section and Assistant U.S. Attorney Claudia Pare for the District of Delaware are prosecuting the case.\nAnyone who has information about human trafficking should report that information to the National Human Trafficking Hotline toll-free at 1-888-373-7888, which is available 24 hours a day, seven days a week. For more information about human trafficking, please visit\n. Information on the Department of Justice’s efforts to combat human trafficking can be found at\nAn indictment is merely an allegation. All defendants are presumed innocent until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt in a court of law.\nCriminal - Human Rights and Special Prosecution Section\nCalifornia Man Pleads Guilty to Sex Trafficking a Minor\nKeynin McGee, 27, of Oakland, California, pleaded guilty to sex trafficking a minor in the Eastern District of California.\nTexas Man Convicted of Sex Trafficking in the District of Columbia\nA federal jury in the District of Columbia convicted Devontee Parker, 38, of Dallas, today for sex trafficking by force, fraud, or coercion, interstate transportation for purpose of prostitution, and...\nIowa Man Sentenced to Life in Prison for Sex Trafficking Three Women, Drug Distribution, and Obstruction\nDave Shumpert III, 30, of Des Moines, Iowa, was sentenced today in the U.S. District Court for the Southern District of Iowa to life in prison after being found guilty...", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "857b01a18fc3ed904fe3f869", "title": "U.S. Attorney’s Office Announces Election Integrity Plan for 2026 General Election and Early Voting in Nevada", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-nv/pr/us-attorneys-office-announces-election-integrity-plan-2026-general-election-and-early", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "U.S. Attorney’s Office Announces Election Integrity Plan for 2026 General Election and Early Voting in Nevada\nPress Release U.S. Attorney’s Office Announces Election Integrity Plan for 2026 General Election and Early Voting in Nevada Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Nevada LAS VEGAS – The U.S. Attorney’s Office for the District of Nevada , in coordination with the Department of Justice’s Civil Rights Division , announced its comprehensive strategy today to oversee federal voting protections and investigate potential election fraud as early voting begins in Nevada for the 2026 midterm elections . The initiative is part of a nationwide effort to ensure a secure, transparent, and accessible voting process. The U.S. Attorney’s Office, alongside federal law enforcement partners, is prepared to enforce federal election laws vigorously while protecting the right of every legal voter to cast their ballot without intimidation or interference. Voters can consult official Department of Justice and state portals to review federal election laws and eligibility requirements. “Maintaining public confidence in our democratic institutions is a top priority. As Nevadans head to the polls for early voting and utilize the state's mail-in ballot options, our office will work hand-in-hand with federal, state, and local partners to safeguard the integrity of the vote. We are committed to a fair election process where every legal ballot is counted accurately. Every single fraudulent, duplicate, or unlawful ballot cast completely cancels out the vote of an honest citizen. Protecting election integrity isn't about keeping people out; it’s about making sure every legal vote counts,” said First Assistant United States Attorney Sigal Chattah for the District of Nevada. Key Enforcement Priorities for the 2026 Election: Protecting Voter Rights: Ensuring that all eligible voters can participate in early voting, mail-in voting, and Election Day voting free from discrimination, harassment, or unlawful coercion. Voter Eligibility and Enforcement: Monitoring compliance with federal laws regarding voter registration and eligibility. For detailed information on ongoing reviews, eligibility laws, or federal penalties, please consult official Department of Justice guidelines and state election authority resources. Combating Election Fraud: Investigating actionable allegations of ballot harvesting, voter impersonation, or intentional disruption of polling places and mail-in ballot processing. Mail-In and Early Voting in Nevada Nevada state election officials have confirmed that early in-person voting and mail-in voting will proceed as scheduled under standard state protocols. Federal oversight will remain active throughout the early voting period to monitor for compliance with the Voting Rights Act and ensure that secure ballot drop boxes and polling locations are unhindered. Reporting Electi", "truncated": true}, {"id": "7e4f591d512c5f5c19d366e3", "title": "Capitol agenda: What the House’s new leftists learned from the Squad - Politico", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMijgFBVV95cUxOZ1pJX2V1TmdCVEhYb190OU96eDluUTNoS01iaGVTQ2RSLU5YeEZuTGoyWFRRVHdEc0k0Ry1vM3VJaHg4c0UwdVhCOFdEdkJXQXBpZkRlekVpUmRLejFFNG93MmRLcWIydXl5ekJIWmIxbE5iUXAzdGFLWWNCUTRxakZUUVlCLWxjMmJUQjln?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_us", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Capitol agenda: What the House’s new leftists learned from the Squad - Politico\nCapitol agenda: What the House’s new leftists learned from the Squad Politico Darializa Avila Chevalier vows to show DSA can ‘deliver’ in Congress New York Daily News Avila Chevalier Pledges Pragmatic Progressive Agenda in Congress SuaraGarut.ID Inside Congress Live Politico What new leftists learned from the Squad Politico", "truncated": false}, {"id": "786987d2779acb657a4b7f72", "title": "Mobile Man Sentenced to More Than Five Years in Prison for a Cocaine and Crack Cocaine-Trafficking Conspiracy", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdal/pr/mobile-man-sentenced-more-five-years-prison-cocaine-and-crack-cocaine-trafficking", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Mobile Man Sentenced to More Than Five Years in Prison for a Cocaine and Crack Cocaine-Trafficking Conspiracy\nPress Release Mobile Man Sentenced to More Than Five Years in Prison for a Cocaine and Crack Cocaine-Trafficking Conspiracy Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Southern District of Alabama MOBILE, AL – A Mobile man was sentenced to 63 months in federal prison for participating in a conspiracy to traffic cocaine and crack cocaine. According to court documents, Rodney Antwaine Davis, 42, was arrested on November 9, 2023, after leading police officers on a high-speed chase through a residential neighborhood in Mobile. Davis crashed into a ditch and attempted to flee on foot but was apprehended by officers. During the arrest, officers seized $6,145 in cash and a plastic bag containing crack cocaine from Davis’s underwear. A chemist confirmed that the crack cocaine weighed 52.69 grams. On March 20, 2025, federal agents obtained a warrant to search a package mailed from Houston, Texas, to an address on Johnston Street in Mobile under a fictitious name. The package contained more than two kilograms of vacuum-sealed powder cocaine and a bottle containing 300 alprazolam pills, as confirmed by laboratory testing. The following day, an undercover agent delivered the package to the residence on Johnston Street. Davis arrived and retrieved the package. Agents moved in to arrest him, but Davis fled into the house before being apprehended in a bedroom. At the time of his arrest, Davis had $5,734 in cash in his pocket. Agents also discovered a small amount of methamphetamine on a bed near where Davis was arrested and three bottles of promethazine syrup in Davis’s car, which was parked outside the residence. Agents seized and searched Davis’s cell phone, which contained messages and other data related to his drug-trafficking conspiracy. U.S. District Judge Kristi K. DuBose sentenced Davis to a total of 63 months in prison, with his sentence adjusted to account for the 19 months he has spent in custody since March 2025. Upon his release from prison, Davis will serve four years of supervised release, during which he will be subject to drug testing. The ordered Davis to pay $100 in special assessments. U.S. Attorney Sean P. Costello of the Southern District of Alabama made the announcement. Homeland Security Investigations, the United States Postal Inspection Service, the Mobile Police Department, and the Mobile County Sheriff’s Office investigated the case. Assistant U.S. Attorney Justin Roller prosecuted the case on behalf of the United States. Updated October 16, 2026 Component USAO - Alabama, Southern", "truncated": false}, {"id": "76845d8fe41ecce5fe7f0ee6", "title": "Shiprock Man Sentenced to 20 Years in Prison for Second-Degree Murder", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-nm/pr/shiprock-man-sentenced-20-years-prison-second-degree-murder", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Shiprock Man Sentenced to 20 Years in Prison for Second-Degree Murder\nPress Release Shiprock Man Sentenced to 20 Years in Prison for Second-Degree Murder Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of New Mexico ALBUQUERQUE – A Shiprock man was sentenced to 20 years in prison for fatally stabbing a man in broad daylight near a community grocery store in Shiprock, New Mexico. According to publicly available court documents, on the afternoon of July 25, 2024, officers with the Navajo Nation Police Department responded to a fight in progress near a high-traffic Navajo Nation shopping center in Shiprock, New Mexico. Upon arrival, they found a man lying on the ground with wounds to his face and chest. Witnesses reported that Edison Brady , 57, an enrolled member of the Navajo Nation, had stabbed the man in the chest after the man was already badly beaten, hardly conscious, lying on the ground, and bleeding from the head. According to multiple witnesses, Brady attacked the victim without provocation, repeatedly punching and kicking him as he remained on the ground and attempted to shield his face. Brady then walked away, retrieved a long kitchen knife from his nearby bag, and returned to stab the victim in the chest as the victim lay bleeding and barely conscious. The victim died from his injuries shortly after arriving at the Northern Navajo Medical Center. Officers recovered the knife, which was covered in the victim’s blood, from Brady ’s bag minutes after the attack. Brady pleaded guilty without a plea agreement to an indictment charging him with one count of second-degree murder. Upon his release from prison, Brady will be subject to five years of supervised release. There is no parole in the federal system. Brady must also compensate the New Mexico Crime Victim Reparations Commission for the $6,000.00 it expended paying for the victim’s funeral expenses. First Assistant U.S. Attorney Ryan Ellison and Special Agent in Charge Justin A. Garris of the FBI’s Albuquerque Field Office made the announcement today. The Farmington Resident Agency of the FBI’s Albuquerque Field Office investigated this case with assistance from Navajo Nation Police Department and Navajo Nation Department of Criminal Investigations. Assistant United States Attorneys Jena Ritchey and Zachary C. Jones are prosecuting this case. Updated October 16, 2026 Topic Indian Country Law and Justice Components Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) USAO - New Mexico Press Release Number: 26-290", "truncated": false}, {"id": "7051595149f5b12aec0e9298", "title": "Philadelphia Man Convicted for his Role in a $2 Million Theft", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-nj/pr/philadelphia-man-convicted-his-role-2-million-theft", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Philadelphia Man Convicted for his Role in a $2 Million Theft\nPress Release Philadelphia Man Convicted for his Role in a $2 Million Theft Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of New Jersey CAMDEN, N.J. – A Philadelphia man was convicted on October 15, 2026, for his role in stealing $2 million from a Glassboro business and laundering the proceeds of his theft, U.S. Attorney Robert Frazer announced. Ruben Cruz, 33, of Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, and Davenport, Florida, was convicted of one count of conspiracy to transport stolen property across state lines, one count of interstate transportation of stolen property, one count of conspiracy to launder money, and seven counts of money laundering.  Cruz was convicted following a three-day trial before U.S. District Judge Christine P. O’Hearn in Camden federal court.  Sentencing is set for February 24, 2027. According to court documents and evidence presented at trial: Over the 2021 July 4 th holiday weekend, Cruz and co-conspirator Ricky Rivas-Ortiz burglarized a Glassboro business, stealing a safe that contained more than $2 million in money orders and cash and taking it to Philadelphia.  After the burglary, Cruz and another co-conspirator negotiated money orders from the stolen safe in a manner designed to conceal that they were stolen.  One method they used to launder the proceeds was altering the stolen money orders and depositing them in piecemeal fashion.  For example, Cruz deposited some of the stolen money orders on the same day at different banks that were next door to each other and deposited other stolen money orders at the same bank branch over several consecutive days. Rivas-Ortiz previously pleaded guilty to an information charging him with transporting stolen property interstate and was sentenced by U.S. District Judge Christine P. O’Hearn to 21 months’ imprisonment. For Cruz, the count of conspiracy to transport stolen property interstate carries a maximum penalty of 5 years in prison and a fine of up to $250,000, or twice the gross loss to the victim or gain to the defendant, whichever is greatest.  The count of interstate transportation of stolen property carries a maximum penalty of 10 years in prison and a fine of up to $250,000, or twice the gross loss to the victim or gain to the defendant, whichever is greatest.  The count of conspiracy to launder money and each money laundering count carry a maximum penalty of 20 years in prison and a fine of up to $500,000, or twice the value of the laundered money, whichever is greatest. U.S. Attorney Frazer credited postal inspectors of the U.S. Postal Inspection Service, Philadelphia Division, under the direction of Inspector in Charge Christopher A. Nielsen, and agents of the Internal Revenue Service – Criminal Investigation, Newark Field Office, under the direction of Special Agent in Charge Jenifer L. Piovesan, with the investigation leading to the co", "truncated": true}, {"id": "6b70db663a1bd46f9f25313b", "title": "Spokane Man Sentenced to 5 Years for  Receipt of Child Pornography", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-edwa/pr/spokane-man-sentenced-5-years-receipt-child-pornography", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Spokane Man Sentenced to 5 Years for  Receipt of Child Pornography\nPress Release Spokane Man Sentenced to 5 Years for  Receipt of Child Pornography Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Eastern District of Washington Spokane, Washington – First Assistant United States Attorney Pete Serrano announced that on October 14, 2026, United States District Court Judge Rebecca L. Pennell sentenced Nicholas Howard to five years in custody, to be followed by a life term of federal supervised release. Judge Pennell also ordered Howard to pay $31,000 in restitution to his victims and a $5,000 special assessment under the Amy, Vicky, and Andy Child Pornography Assistance Act of 2018. The United States argued for a term of imprisonment of 210 months, while Howard’s attorney, Bevan Maxey, requested the 60-month term ultimately imposed by the Court. As reflected in court documents and public proceedings, Howard had a long history of viewing child pornography and hid his sexual interest in minors from friends and family for 20 years. On the Dark Web, Howard was a “senior member” of a website dedicated to child sex abuse and images memorializing that abuse. The Federal Bureau of Investigation reviewed 179 posts by Howard, where he communicated with other users and traded images and videos of child sex abuse material. Howard wrote in his posts of his attraction to children aged “7–14 years old” and said that that he “understood the appeal of being sexually active with a minor female” and that he “fantasized about this for decades.” Howard also shared that he “became sexually aroused” when explaining sex to his nine-year old daughter, that he “masturbated to minor females he knows or comes across in real life,” and that he “followed multiple minor aged females on Instagram to whom he masturbates to.” Howard wrote that “he would engage in sexual intercourse with his [11-year-old] daughter’s friend if he could” and appreciated that the website was “truly full of [his] people.” The FBI executed a search warrant at Howard’s residence and seized a number of Howard’s devices. On Howard’s phone, the FBI discovered 223 images and 23 videos of child sex abuse material, including depictions of minors as young as toddlers. The footage included sadistic abuse of young, prepubescent girls. Howard also possessed videos of Nazi ideology and violent footage of adult women being hung and strangled. Howard’s phone further revealed that he had been viewing child sex abuse material just minutes before picking up his young daughters on the day of his arrest and used anti-forensic software to help delete and conceal evidence of his crime. AUSA Wick relied upon these aggravated facts to support the recommended sentence of 210 months. “This defendant posed a significant risk to the community,” stated Serrano. “Without the extraordinary investigative efforts of the FBI and my office, Howard would have continued to ", "truncated": true}, {"id": "6668371f20488890e24610f3", "title": "U.S Departments of Justice and Labor Secure Settlement with Cloudera, Enforce Two-Year PERM Debarment & Back Pay Following Discrimination Against U.S. Workers", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/us-departments-justice-and-labor-secure-settlement-cloudera-enforce-two-year-perm-debarment", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "U.S Departments of Justice and Labor Secure Settlement with Cloudera, Enforce Two-Year PERM Debarment & Back Pay Following Discrimination Against U.S. Workers\nU.S Departments of Justice and Labor Secure Settlement with Cloudera, Enforce Two-Year PERM Debarment & Back Pay Following Discrimination Against U.S. Workers\nThe U.S. Department of Justice’s Civil Rights Division (CRT) and the U.S. Department of Labor (DOL) today announced a coordinated enforcement\nwith Cloudera Inc., a Santa Clara, California-based data management company, following allegations that the company discriminated against qualified U.S. workers during recruitment conducted under the Permanent Labor Certification (PERM) program.\n“There are no exceptions: the Justice Department will hold accountable employers who discriminate against U.S. workers,” said Assistant Attorney General Harmeet K. Dhillon of the Justice Department’s Civil Rights Division. “This substantial settlement, in partnership with DOL, sends a strong signal to employers that the federal government will work together to ensure employers treat U.S. workers fairly.”\nAs part of the resolution, Cloudera will be barred from participating in the PERM program for two years, one of the most significant sanctions available for DOL to protect the integrity of the employment-based immigration system and safeguard opportunities for U.S. workers. The debarment follows DOL’s\nof Cloudera’s PERM processing and reflects DOL’s commitment to aggressively enforcing labor certification requirements when employers fail to provide U.S. workers a fair opportunity to compete for jobs.\nDOJ filed in April of this year, alleging that Cloudera violated the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA) by discriminating against U.S. workers when recruiting and hiring for seven positions under the PERM process. Under the terms of the settlement with the Justice Department, Cloudera will establish a back-pay fund of $1.4 million to compensate qualified U.S. workers who tried to apply for the jobs at issue but were not given fair consideration. Cloudera also will pay $600,000 in civil penalties to the United States.\nIn addition to the two-year debarment, Cloudera will be subject to compliance obligations designed to ensure future adherence to federal labor and anti-discrimination laws. These measures include enhanced monitoring, revised PERM compliance procedures, and reporting requirements intended to prevent future violations.\nThis debarment and compliance framework establish an important enforcement precedent and strengthen future efforts to protect U.S. workers. The PERM program allows employers to sponsor aliens for permanent residence only after demonstrating that qualified U.S. workers were provided a genuine opportunity to apply and that no qualified, willing, and available U.S. workers could be found. Employers who evade these requirements undermine both program integrity and the interests of U.S. workers. This resolution is one of many examples of coordinated federal enforcement designed to ensure employers comply with labor market protections established by Congress.\nThis settlement is also the fourteenth settlement since DOJ re-launched its Protecting U.S. Workers Initiative in 2025 to enforce the INA’s prohibition on citizenship status discrimination against companies that illegally discriminate against U.S. workers in favor of those with temporary employment visas. Under these settlements, DOJ obtains civil penalties for each violation and will continue to appropriately seek the maximum penalty permitted by law. The settlements also involve awards of back pay, when warranted, and require employers to conduct comprehensive training for relevant staff and recruiters and cease restricting consideration for job opportunities based on workers’ citizenship status without a lawful reason.\nFor information about additional settlements under the Protecting U.S. Workers Initiative, visit IER’s\nFor informal assistance, the public can call IER’s free\nat 1-800-255-7688 for workers or at 1-800-255-8155 for employers (1-800-237-2515, TTY for hearing impaired) between 9am and 5pm Eastern Time, Monday through Friday; sign up for a live webinar or watch an on-demand\nJustice Department Announces Settlement Ending Antisemitism and Race Discrimination at UNC\nThis week the U.S. Department of Justice, Civil Rights Division and the U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights (ED-OCR) entered a voluntary university-wide settlement agreement with the University...\nVA Home Loan Servicer Village Capital to Pay Over $500,000 for Violating Servicemembers’ Rights\nThe Justice Department today announced that Village Capital & Investment LLC, a servicer of home loans guaranteed by the Department of Veterans Affairs, will pay over $500,000 to resolve allegations that...\nJustice Department Notifies the District of Columbia of Investigation into Whether Conditions in the D.C. Department of Corrections Correctional Facilities Violate the Constitution\nDistrict of Columbia (D.C.) Mayor Muriel Bowser of the initiation of a federal investigation into D.C. Department of Corrections (D.C. DOC) correctional facilities. The Justice...", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "63758d9c965f7de9d77841c0", "title": "Aduro Clean Technologies Reports First Quarter Fiscal Year 2027 Results and Provides Business Update - GlobeNewswire", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiggJBVV95cUxONVFqcE4xWnk5ZklzaG5MdkNBakI4NktRZVdFNHZ0SjlRR3Y5cXVGVGhKcVM0MDJBaHN2MjB2WUZfZER0RG13dzNRZ0xIWXRvVnIxTnRoRzA5bjg0QXdheWNjYnV0UEgzTUQtV0tkZkdlamV3eTZoaGJidDBYNWRrMzJYaTNXdndxdkotUkdLQVFTYjh1MnpIaDI2WW1XcTQ0QUJheW42cDNMbVowSmNVVXdZTm10UENGNTB2Q3Qxa1V", "source": "topic_business", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Aduro Clean Technologies Reports First Quarter Fiscal Year 2027 Results and Provides Business Update - GlobeNewswire\nAduro Clean Technologies Reports First Quarter Fiscal Year 2027 Results and Provides Business Update GlobeNewswire", "truncated": false}, {"id": "5f33e0d456a930ba25b76791", "title": "The District of Alaska Joins DOJ Fraud Division, IRS, and IRS-CI in National Tax Enforcement Surge Exposing Over $490 Million in Tax Fraud", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-ak/pr/district-alaska-joins-doj-fraud-division-irs-and-irs-ci-national-tax-enforcement-surge", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "The District of Alaska Joins DOJ Fraud Division, IRS, and IRS-CI in National Tax Enforcement Surge Exposing Over $490 Million in Tax Fraud\nPress Release The District of Alaska Joins DOJ Fraud Division, IRS, and IRS-CI in National Tax Enforcement Surge Exposing Over $490 Million in Tax Fraud Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Alaska ANCHORAGE, Alaska – A federal grand jury in Alaska returned an indictment charging a doctor with five counts of attempting to evade and defeat taxes. According to court documents, between January 2018 and May 2024, John Michael Koller, 66, of Kodiak, Alaska, willfully attempted to evade and defeat income tax owed to the United States. During this time, Dr. Koller was the owner and manager of Kodiak Island Ambulatory Care Clinic (KIACC). The indictment alleges that in attempting to evade taxes, Dr. Koller submitted fraudulent corporate and individual income tax returns to the IRS, maintained falsified accounting records, provided tax preparers with incomplete and inaccurate financial information, and made false statements to investigators. Dr. Koller also concealed cash income generated by KIACC by directing employees to record the cash income in separate places, destroying cash receipts and cash deposit slips, and not depositing cash income into bank accounts. Dr. Koller’s initial appearance was held on October 9, 2026, before U.S. Magistrate Judge Kyle F. Reardon of the U.S. District Court for the District of Alaska. If convicted, he faces a maximum penalty of 5 years in prison. A federal district court judge will determine any sentence after considering the U.S. Sentencing Guidelines and other statutory factors. U.S. Attorney Michael J. Heyman of the District of Alaska; and Special Agent in Charge Adam Jobes of the IRS Criminal Investigation, Seattle Field Office made the announcement. The IRS Criminal Investigation, Seattle Field Office is investigating the case. Assistant U.S. Attorney Ainsley McNerney is prosecuting the case. This press release details one way in which this office has contributed to ensuring that all Americans pay their fair share. To see the successes of other offices, please see this press release: https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/dojs-fraud-division-irs-and-irs-ci-expose-more-490m-tax-fraud-national-tax-enforcement-surge . On April 7, 2026, the Department of Justice announced the creation of the National Fraud Enforcement Division (“Fraud Division”). The Fraud Division is laser-focused on investigating and prosecuting those who commit fraud against the American people. The Department’s work to combat fraud supports President Trump’s Task Force to Eliminate Fraud, a whole-of-government effort chaired by Vice President J.D. Vance to eliminate fraud, waste, and abuse within Federal benefit programs. An indictment is merely an allegation, and all defendants are presumed innocent until proven guilty beyond a ", "truncated": true}, {"id": "5d5e049d275bf13f27dde0ae", "title": "Eastham, Bender Partner on Value-Add Acquisition in Twin Cities Metro - Multifamily & Affordable Housing Business", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMirwFBVV95cUxNSEltUFgxekRZU0dQODR4UExmeUJrWUp6bHdrZXZuSGpfeUJUUUItVFFpT2RGZEZBbUVNSEx2QzNUeE54aFZSdzZpenItOW05RzVPbEE3Y2JRS2NLaVlLcVRxRmM3LWMxR2lyRVlKR0pybnVLOUppWTAtcy15YkM5X3RqaXRGRFNRbE5aOGpVZHRDWHJLR3ZKSFM5Vy1iaWlWNmtGQllwQjVTMFVKbGYw?oc=5", "source": "lane_corporate_action_mna", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Eastham, Bender Partner on Value-Add Acquisition in Twin Cities Metro - Multifamily & Affordable Housing Business\nEastham, Bender Partner on Value-Add Acquisition in Twin Cities Metro Multifamily & Affordable Housing Business", "truncated": false}, {"id": "589326ce36aada7294d87766", "title": "Red Roof leans into technology, design to drive returns - Hotel Management", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimwFBVV95cUxQd2VibEo0Q0VDRUNERjdjc1o5aVNRQUhoNjgxamFjOEZkZ1hQNFhaOV9HLXZZRmlpZHN6N2dwMlBwWWw2UV9LMU9XNE4wc2VDR1BCRWliMnRMM1l2b0lxTlI1eE0wNkxfZ0xjcnUySGVOamh6elh3SEFRcl93LUozSW05OHpyZVptMWFHRGJzTXVFSnN2cDQxTklkWQ?oc=5", "source": "topic_technology", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Red Roof leans into technology, design to drive returns - Hotel Management\nRed Roof leans into technology, design to drive returns Hotel Management", "truncated": false}, {"id": "57f1cd73e1dd023dad356f4b", "title": "Five Non-Citizens Arrested for Voter Fraud Across the Northern District", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-ndny/pr/five-non-citizens-arrested-voter-fraud-across-northern-district", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Five Non-Citizens Arrested for Voter Fraud Across the Northern District\nPress Release Five Non-Citizens Arrested for Voter Fraud Across the Northern District Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Northern District of New York USANYN-MediaInquiry@usa.doj.gov On October 13 and 14, 2026, Vikki Bradt, age 51, a British National, of Ballston Spa, NY, Samantha Merkle, age 55, a British National of Glens Falls, NY, Suad Sasic, age 30, a Bosnian National of Clifton Park, NY, Jacques Patry, age 64, a Canadian National of Tupper Lake, NY, and Paula Stitt, age 57, a Canadian National of Albany, NY, appeared on charges of voting by aliens. All defendants are lawful permanent residents. First Assistant United States Attorney John A. Sarcone III and Anthony Patrone, Acting Special Agent in Charge of the Buffalo Field Office of Homeland Security Investigations (HSI) made the announcement. The complaints against all five defendants allege that they are aliens, who knew they were not United States citizens and that they knowingly voted in an election held in part for the purpose of electing a candidate for President, Vice President, Member of the United States Senate, or Member of the United States House of Representatives. These charges carry a maximum sentence of one year in prison and a fine up to $100,000.  A defendant’s sentence is imposed by a judge based on the particular statute(s) the defendant is convicted of violating, the U.S. Sentencing Guidelines, and other factors. First Assistant United States Attorney John A. Sarcone III stated, “the law is clear that only U.S. citizens can vote in elections that have candidates running for federal office.  When non-citizens vote, the public loses confidence in the integrity of the election process and its outcome.  Our office, working with our federal law enforcement partners, will work tirelessly to prosecute those who should not have voted, but did.  Just this week, our office filed charges against five individuals, all of whom were non-citizens and unlawfully voted in federal elections within the last few years.” \"The integrity of lawful voting and government processes is essential to maintaining public confidence in our institutions,\" said Homeland Security Investigations (HSI) Buffalo Acting Special Agent in Charge Anthony J. Patrone.\" From the North Country to the Capital Region, residents expect that the systems affecting their communities, including the electoral process, operate fairly, lawfully, and with accountability. Today’s charges reflect that coordinated effort and HSI's commitment to investigate alleged voter fraud and protect the integrity of government processes that communities in this region rely upon.\" The charges in the complaints are merely accusations. The defendants are presumed innocent unless and until proven guilty. Assistant U.S. Attorneys Richard Belliss and Benjamin Loefke are prosecuting the case. Upda", "truncated": true}, {"id": "56d660625bdc03f1f6aa74b6", "title": "Regions Financial (NYSE:RF) Releases Quarterly Earnings Results, Beats Estimates By $0.07 EPS - MarketBeat", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi5gFBVV95cUxQaXR0T0xSTHM3T2YtOXVCb0pnNnBpR0pGcjVhZlNabUp1c1R4dTh3c1JkWTQwRUFZckw1M2pwR0t3NzhqS0w1UndFQTJJVHgxWnQzN25aRzVKZmp6ckhlNzlLcUlKOWZCTlpsZ19Db1ZTaWxHdVVGMWcyVU9IN1pUREdJbk1QVllZbjdUdUNYUkNZT3pzWnd6azNXa2cyZ3FuZXJuTEl3MmlZazZmWnlleTJLQ2NjSTBaem5VT09oOGY", "source": "lane_print_vs_priced", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Regions Financial (NYSE:RF) Releases Quarterly Earnings Results, Beats Estimates By $0.07 EPS - MarketBeat\nRegions Financial (NYSE:RF) Releases Quarterly Earnings Results, Beats Estimates By $0.07 EPS MarketBeat", "truncated": false}, {"id": "55f4e0622acbc2625b3b1ef0", "title": "California law change clears financing path for West Sacramento’s MLB expansion bid - Sacramento Bee", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiZ0FVX3lxTFBkZjh6NFduNlhOU1VwMXZ4Mi1lZ3l3OHBYVjZ6X2gwYnM3YWFfZUtnTGs5M29rSWVkd0ZLWHNKOVZqRDAtSS1nTHhyaE9UWTlPbS1BWndkUEg1ZU01STh2Mlg1amFFaWPSAWdBVV95cUxOVjByZWQwSTk0R3huR09aTTBfY0hldlJURmNQZlJ2VkdyM3o1NVVGNWhmejFhbGFYUDhpRFlJbXdVa1RnZU1ycXRFbDMzdnN0S1c3aFVTTlN", "source": "lane_tax_fiscal", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "California law change clears financing path for West Sacramento’s MLB expansion bid - Sacramento Bee\nCalifornia law change clears financing path for West Sacramento’s MLB expansion bid Sacramento Bee", "truncated": false}, {"id": "554dd658f5667813e1b76808", "title": "Two Plead Guilty to Unemployment Insurance Benefits Fraud Conspiracy", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-edca/pr/two-plead-guilty-unemployment-insurance-benefits-fraud-conspiracy", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Two Plead Guilty to Unemployment Insurance Benefits Fraud Conspiracy\nPress Release Two Plead Guilty to Unemployment Insurance Benefits Fraud Conspiracy Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Eastern District of California SACRAMENTO, Calif. — Las Vegas residents Ricky Lee Ware, 43, and Kimberly Ann Wallace, 30, pleaded guilty today to conspiracy to commit access device fraud, U.S. Attorney Eric Grant announced. According to court documents, between July 2020 and January 2021, the defendants conspired to obtain debit cards that allowed them to access unemployment benefits. The unemployment benefits were in the names of people who did not know the defendants. The defendants then used the debit cards to withdraw more than $900,000 in unemployment benefits from ATMs. The U.S. Department of Labor Office of Inspector General, Homeland Security Investigations, the Federal Bureau of Investigation; and the Department of Homeland Security Office of Inspector General conducted the investigation. Assistant U.S. Attorney Nicholas M. Fogg is prosecuting the case. Ware and Wallace are scheduled to be sentenced by U.S. District Judge Dena Coggins on Feb. 19, 2027, and face a maximum of 7.5 years in prison. The actual sentence, however, will be determined at the discretion of the court after consideration of any applicable statutory factors and the federal Sentencing Guidelines, which take into account a number of variables. The third defendant in the case, Carlos Lee Sanchez Jr., 46, of Sacramento, pleaded guilty to conspiracy to commit access device fraud in June 2026 and is scheduled to be sentenced Nov. 13, 2026. This effort is part of a California COVID-19 Fraud Enforcement Strike Force operation, one of five interagency COVID-19 fraud strike force teams established by the U.S. Department of Justice. The California Strike Force combines law enforcement and prosecutorial resources in the Eastern and Central Districts of California and focuses on large-scale, multistate pandemic relief fraud perpetrated by criminal organizations and transnational actors. The strike forces use prosecutor-led and data analyst-driven teams to identify and bring to justice those who stole pandemic relief funds. Updated October 16, 2026 Topics COVID-Related Fraud Financial Fraud Identity Theft Component USAO - California, Eastern", "truncated": false}, {"id": "5375689afffc79652fb3cbf7", "title": "D.C. Mother Pleads Guilty to Perjury for Lying to Grand Jury During Investigation into Her 2-Year-Old Son’s Murder", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-dc/pr/dc-mother-pleads-guilty-perjury-lying-grand-jury-during-investigation-her-2-year-old", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "D.C. Mother Pleads Guilty to Perjury for Lying to Grand Jury During Investigation into Her 2-Year-Old Son’s Murder\nPress Release D.C. Mother Pleads Guilty to Perjury for Lying to Grand Jury During Investigation into Her 2-Year-Old Son’s Murder Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Columbia USADC.Media@usdoj.gov WASHINGTON – Monet Jones, 28, of Washington, D.C., pleaded guilty today for lying to the grand jury about the beating death of her 2-year-old child, Mars Jones, announced U.S. Attorney Jeanine Ferris Pirro. “Two-year-old Mars deserved a mother who would fight for justice, not protect the man who beat her child to death,” said U.S. Attorney Pirro. “Instead, she misled investigators and deleted evidence to shield him from accountability. If you obstruct justice to protect someone who kills a child, you will answer for it. No exceptions.” Jones pleaded guilty before D.C. Superior Court Judge Rainey Brandt to one count of perjury and one count of tampering with evidence. Judge Park scheduled sentencing for February 1, 2027, with her co-defendant, Dasean Matthews. According to the government’s evidence, on October 13, 2022, 2-year-old Mars, Jones, and Matthews, (co-defendant Jones’s romantic partner) drove to a Dunkin Donuts just before noon. While in the Dunkin Donuts, video evidence shows Matthews striking the young child with a closed fist in the upper body causing Mars to fall and slam against a metal railing. Later that day, while in a parking garage, video evidence shows Matthews grabbing Mars by the neck of his clothing, swing him by the clothing, and minutes later, strike Mars in the upper body or face, causing the young child to again fall to the floor. Later the same day, while alone in a vehicle with Mars, video evidence shows defendant Matthews repeatedly strike the young child so forcefully that the vehicle shakes from the impact of the blows. Immediately thereafter, Matthews delivers an unconscious and bloody baby Mars to Jones and then flees the scene. An autopsy determined that Mars died from complications of inflicted head trauma. Throughout the pendency of the case, Jones repeatedly lied to law enforcement and the grand jury about witnessing defendant Matthews’ assaulting her child or being in the Dunkin Donuts on October 13, 2022, in an attempt to hinder the investigation. Jones also deleted messages between her and Matthews from her phone in an attempt to protect Matthews. Joining the announcement was Interim Chief Jeffery Carroll of the Metropolitan Police Department. This case was investigated by the Special Victims Unit of the Homicide Section at the Metropolitan Police Department and was prosecuted by Assistant U.S. Attorneys Emma McArthur and Shauna Payyappilly. 2023 CF2 000873 Contact USADC.Media@usdoj.gov Updated October 16, 2026 Topic Violent Crime Component USAO - District of Columbia", "truncated": false}, {"id": "531c0b5cf3d1bc9ccba8b3b5", "title": "Waipahu Women Sentenced to Two Years in Prison for Pandemic and Tax Fraud While Earning Millions from an Unlicensed Construction Business", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-hi/pr/waipahu-women-sentenced-two-years-prison-pandemic-and-tax-fraud-while-earning-millions", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Waipahu Women Sentenced to Two Years in Prison for Pandemic and Tax Fraud While Earning Millions from an Unlicensed Construction Business\nPress Release Waipahu Women Sentenced to Two Years in Prison for Pandemic and Tax Fraud While Earning Millions from an Unlicensed Construction Business Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Hawaii HONOLULU – United States Attorney Ken Sorenson announced that Heidi Cafirma, 46, of Waipahu, Hawaii, was sentenced on September 30, 2026, by United States District Judge Shanlyn A.S. Park to 24 months in prison followed by 3 years of supervised release for filing false tax returns and wire fraud.  Cafirma was also ordered to forfeit $70,500 and pay up to over $1 million in restitution, which total amount will be determined by the court at a later date.  Cafirma pled guilty pursuant to a plea agreement in December 2025. In 2020, the Coronavirus Aid, Relief, and Economic Security (CARES) Act created the Pandemic Unemployment Assistance (PUA) unemployment insurance program to provide emergency unemployment payments to certain workers whose livelihoods were impacted by the COVID-19 pandemic but who were ineligible for traditional state unemployment insurance benefits, such as business owners, self-employed workers, independent contractors, and those with a limited work history who were out of business or had significantly reduced their services as a direct result of the pandemic.  To obtain either traditional unemployment insurance (UI) or PUA UI benefit payments, claimants could submit a claim online.  If approved, the U.S. Treasury typically deposited payments every week into a claimant’s bank account.  To continue to receive benefit payments, claimants were required to certify on a weekly basis under penalty of law that they remained unemployed or partially employed, and eligible to receive benefit payments. According to court documents, Cafirma admitted that between June 2020 and September 2021, she submitted 22 false claims online for UI benefit payments for herself by repeatedly falsely certifying under penalty of law that she was unemployed and not receiving income, despite knowing that her certifications were false because she received over $1 million in income during that period from her and her husband’s unlicensed construction business.  Cafirma fraudulently obtained at least $70,500 in unemployment insurance and PUA UI benefits to which she was not entitled. Between 2016 and 2020, Cafirma also willfully failed to pay federal and state taxes on over $3.4 million in income from her and her husband’s construction business.  She provided false documents to her tax return preparer that omitted income and then filed false tax returns with the IRS.  Over at least four years, Cafirma took steps to conceal her income by directing construction customers to write checks to her personally rather than to her business, funneling incom", "truncated": true}, {"id": "5206f725d168a3b79cb5448e", "title": "Odyssey Marine Exploration Announces Corporate Update on Proposed Merger with American Ocean Minerals Corporation - FinancialContent", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMigwJBVV95cUxOR1ZZOEFWbTNzM2ZBcjItZ1dJbzFRTmtnckYyNFpZdUVJWmJQNXRqcV9UaGc4QTM2ZmU3ZVl3Z2RsN0RtN1JhU0VLdkdfUkQyQ2xSVVo5OTAxWmxtNGh4QkZaT1BkLVVVNjV2T21iTmNTcmFFd3lDSk9lTkJCVnQ5ODl4bzAwS3NtY1A2NXp4REc1TVN4amF6LTdGM1hkc1psMmFJNVExcDdHT1RBQk01U0NVUXlRY3Q2ajNzY1hDN3N", "source": "lane_corporate_action_mna", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Odyssey Marine Exploration Announces Corporate Update on Proposed Merger with American Ocean Minerals Corporation - FinancialContent\nOdyssey Marine Exploration Announces Corporate Update on Proposed Merger with American Ocean Minerals Corporation FinancialContent", "truncated": false}, {"id": "50bc3041f8760deec8eadf17", "title": "Massachusetts Man Charged for His Role in Marriage and Immigration Document Fraud", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-ndoh/pr/massachusetts-man-charged-his-role-marriage-and-immigration-document-fraud", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Massachusetts Man Charged for His Role in Marriage and Immigration Document Fraud\nPress Release Massachusetts Man Charged for His Role in Marriage and Immigration Document Fraud Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Northern District of Ohio CLEVELAND – A 31-year-old man has been charged for his role in a conspiracy where he allegedly agreed to be paid to enter into a sham marriage to allow a co-conspirator to illegally obtain a green card. A federal grand jury returned an indictment charging Jingming Chen, 31, of Quincy, Massachusetts, with Conspiracy to Commit Marriage Fraud and Immigration Document Fraud. According to allegations in the indictment, Chen, a U.S. citizen, was paid by a Chinese national co-conspirator residing in Aurora, Ohio, to enter into a sham marriage and commit immigration document fraud by fabricating evidence to support the false claim that their marriage was legitimate. They submitted false statements to the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Service (USCIS) to obtain lawful permanent resident status—commonly known as a Green Card—and other immigration benefits for the co-conspirator. To carry out the conspiracy, Chen allegedly agreed to receive partial payment at each step of the green card process as they were completed. Chen and the co-conspirator discussed creating a false backstory of their online romance and long-distance relationship, including how they met and who introduced them. They also went so far as to discuss obtaining a prenuptial agreement and opening various joint accounts for banking, cellphone service, insurance, and a discount store membership card. Eventually, the two filed an intent to get married in Quincy, Massachusetts, and Chen directed the co-conspirator to buy a cheap, fake diamond ring to use for the wedding ceremony.  After they married, Chen directed a preparer to submit his USCIS Form I-130, “Petition for Alien Relative,” to document his relationship with a foreign national which would pave the way for the co-conspirator to obtain a green card. The Homeland Security Investigations Cleveland office and the USCIS-Fraud Detection and National Security Directorate in Boston conducted the investigation preceding the indictment. Assistant United States Attorney Brian M. McDonough is leading the prosecution for the Northern District of Ohio. If convicted, the defendant faces up to five years in prison. The court will determine the sentence after reviewing the factors unique to this case, including the defendant’s prior criminal record, if any, the defendant’s role in the offenses, and the characteristics of the violations. On April 7, the Department of Justice announced the creation of the National Fraud Enforcement Division. The Fraud Division investigates and prosecutes those who commit fraud against the American people. The Department’s work to combat fraud supports President Trump’s Task Force to Eliminate Fraud, a ", "truncated": true}, {"id": "4f1010219af7c18adce046f6", "title": "Connecticut Physician and His Practices Pay More Than $860K to Settle False Claims Act Allegations", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-ct/pr/connecticut-physician-and-his-practices-pay-more-860k-settle-false-claims-act", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Connecticut Physician and His Practices Pay More Than $860K to Settle False Claims Act Allegations\nPress Release Connecticut Physician and His Practices Pay More Than $860K to Settle False Claims Act Allegations Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Connecticut STEVEN MURPHY, M.D. and his practices, MURPHY MEDICAL ASSOCIATES, LLC and DIAGNOSTIC AND MEDICAL SPECIALISTS OF GREENWICH, LLC, located in Stamford, have entered into a civil settlement agreement with the federal and state governments in which they will pay $866,539 to settle allegations of COVID-19 related fraud and health care fraud.  As part of the settlement, Murphy and his practices agreed to be excluded from all federal health care programs for a period of three years. The government alleges that Murphy and his practices submitted false and fraudulent claims to the Health Resources Services Administration (HRSA), which is a sub-agency of the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, to reimburse Murphy for “uninsured” patients treated during the COVID-19 pandemic.  The government also alleges that Murphy submitted false claims to Medicaid and Medicare for blood testing “panels” allegedly performed by Murphy’s in-house lab. COVID-19 Uninsured Program Fraud In response to the COVID-19 pandemic, Congress passed a series of bills to provide funds to health care providers for COVID-19 testing and treatment for uninsured individuals (the “Uninsured Program”).  The Uninsured Program reimbursed eligible health care providers for COVID-19 testing, treatment, and vaccine administration provided to uninsured individuals.  HRSA was selected to provide oversight and management of the Uninsured Program. An uninsured individual was defined as someone who was “not enrolled” in a government or private health insurance program as of the date of service in question.  Prior to seeking reimbursement from the Uninsured Program, health care providers were required to attest to the government that they had confirmed that the patients for whom they were seeking reimbursement were uninsured. Murphy and his practices participated in the Uninsured Program, attested that the patients for whom they sought payment were uninsured, and received reimbursement from the Uninsured Program for those patients.  The government alleges that Murphy and his practices had information, including copies of insurance cards and other documents, in their files indicating that certain patients for which they made attestations and submitted claims for reimbursement to the Uninsured Program were, in fact, enrolled in government or private health insurance programs.  In addition, Murphy and his practices changed the patient’s insurance identifying information to “Covid19 HRSA Uninsured” in their internal electronic medical records system and then submitted claims and received reimbursement from the Uninsured Program for those patients. Blood ", "truncated": true}, {"id": "4e770f750522c93d59d5d6eb", "title": "Rocket Lab pushes 1st launch of next-gen Neutron rocket to 2027 - Space", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiwAFBVV95cUxOVGhxUG9YQldRN3paOTNCWV9DSy0zNnFzZlRySk1sWUh5RG1aWHUxZDJJaVFITWJ3SDBEUUdaX3dtYms4T2dxX1NaMnBwTVlpcjd1aEJYWXg2VlBDWDVUUjR1N1UxSWtQMEV1NGJZVVQybm42eEQ2RWdObVFoLThpMmNfcmk4NHhLTUNEVkJTMU5oZHBnMjlMZk1oYkJVbTRhdHlOV2RyU3dCT0FrNS1OcVB2WDhxcllDcDlibDlkUEw", "source": "topic_products", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Rocket Lab pushes 1st launch of next-gen Neutron rocket to 2027 - Space\nRocket Lab pushes 1st launch of next-gen Neutron rocket to 2027 Space", "truncated": false}, {"id": "4e07acb4b6a25266c83f3474", "title": "Tucson Man Sentenced to 20 Years in Prison for Child Exploitation Crimes", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-az/pr/tucson-man-sentenced-20-years-prison-child-exploitation-crimes", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Tucson Man Sentenced to 20 Years in Prison for Child Exploitation Crimes\nPress Release Tucson Man Sentenced to 20 Years in Prison for Child Exploitation Crimes Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Arizona TUCSON, Ariz. – A Tucson man will spend two decades in prison after fueling an international child-exploitation ring that broadcasted the real-time abuse of minors. On Oct. 14, David Berry Garmarnik, 48, of Tucson, was sentenced by U.S. District Court Judge Rosemary Marquez to 20 years in prison. Garmarnik previously pleaded guilty to one count of Attempted Production of Child Pornography. “Live‑streamed child sexual abuse is one of the most urgent and devastating forms of exploitation, and offenders who fuel it will be met with swift, aggressive, and unrelenting prosecution,” said U.S. Attorney Timothy Courchaine. “These crimes inflict real‑time harm on real children, and our office will pursue anyone who participates in this abuse, no matter where they hide or how they operate. We are grateful to HSI for its relentless work in dismantling international trafficking networks and for ensuring that those who prey on children are brought to justice.” “This case demonstrates the devastating reality of child exploitation, and the lengths offenders will go to abuse children and conceal their crimes. HSI is committed to identifying these offenders, dismantling the networks that enable their abuse, and working with our domestic and international law enforcement partners to ensure they are held accountable. Protecting children and pursuing those who exploit them remains a priority for HSI, wherever these crimes lead us,” said Jason Stevens, Special Agent in Charge of HSI Phoenix. According to the plea agreement, Garmarnik admitted that he requested and directed sexually explicit conduct of children under the age of 12 and in some cases as young as 7. Additional court records show that HSI identified David Berry Garmarnik as a participant in an international child‑exploitation network that live‑streamed the sexual abuse of minors for paying offenders around the world. Investigators linked Garmarnik to more than 100 online interactions with known or suspected traffickers between 2017 and 2022. Records obtained from Microsoft showed thousands of messages, media files, and call logs consistent with child‑exploitation activity. In addition, financial subpoenas revealed that Garmarnik sent over 100 payments to individuals in the Philippines with no legitimate connection to him, including a known trafficker whose operation involved the abuse of children as young as two years old. After search warrants were executed, Garmarnik admitted he had paid for approximately 100 live‑streamed shows involving minors. He also acknowledged downloading child sexual abuse material and communicating with traffickers using his Skype account. This case was brought as part of Project Safe C", "truncated": true}, {"id": "474a3e8f6576c2b25aa8e068", "title": "Curis, Inc. Announces Pricing of $8.5 Million Private Placement Priced At-the-Market Under Nasdaq Rules with Existing Healthcare Focused Institutional Investors - PR Newswire", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuAJBVV95cUxNbkZtMEhQRUF5SVhUOUFaa1RSN1lIRGhRck9NdTlrRFlGX0pnZldjSTZMUUtIWkRPVWFMcnJ6RVU1bThuUGQzNi0tU3NkWDJZUjZrRGQwdnhibTBIQzltRnhFdVR1TnJhNW9tOHhiNHg4Mm1yLXVDalAxVXZqb1U2YTJZRXZDTUFUdXI5TXZZeXVxSUFHQURYX3g0bUxUWGtaRVhVRGNVS05vZ0hpdXVHWmM2Y1EzbVRvRmZYNF9GTlR", "source": "lane_dilution", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Curis, Inc. Announces Pricing of $8.5 Million Private Placement Priced At-the-Market Under Nasdaq Rules with Existing Healthcare Focused Institutional Investors - PR Newswire\nCuris, Inc. Announces Pricing of $8.5 Million Private Placement Priced At-the-Market Under Nasdaq Rules with Existing Healthcare Focused Institutional Investors PR Newswire", "truncated": false}, {"id": "3a8b21c50932a959f6531233", "title": "New Jersey Man Indicted on Arson Charges Connected to Retail Store Fire", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-md/pr/new-jersey-man-indicted-arson-charges-connected-retail-store-fire", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "New Jersey Man Indicted on Arson Charges Connected to Retail Store Fire\nPress Release New Jersey Man Indicted on Arson Charges Connected to Retail Store Fire Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Maryland Baltimore, Maryland – A New Jersey man is facing indictment, today, after using fire and explosive materials in a Maryland retail store. Anthony John Rhodes, 37, of Short Pump, is charged with malicious destruction of property by fire. The charges stem from an incident in which Rhodes set a fire in an Elkton retail store during business hours while customers shopped. Kelly O. Hayes, U.S. Attorney for the District of Maryland, announced the indictment with Special Agent in Charge Charles Doerrer, Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms, and Explosives (ATF), and Chief Caroll H. Spriggs, II, Office of the Maryland State Fire Marshal. According to the indictment, on April 29, 2026, Rhodes maliciously damaged and destroyed the store building, along with its merchandise and inventory. An indictment is not a finding of guilt.  Individuals charged by indictment are presumed innocent until proven guilty at a later criminal proceeding. If convicted, Rhodes faces a minimum of five years and a maximum of 20 years in federal prison.  Actual sentences for federal crimes are typically less than the maximum penalties. A federal district court judge determines sentencing after considering the U.S. Sentencing Guidelines and other statutory factors. U.S. Attorney Hayes commended the ATF and Office of the Maryland State Fire Marshal for their work in the investigation, along with Office of the State’s Attorney for Cecil County, for its valuable assistance.  Ms. Hayes also thanked Assistant U.S. Attorney Victoria Liu who is prosecuting the federal case. For more information about the Maryland U.S. Attorney’s Office, its priorities, and resources available to help the community, visit justice.gov/usao-md . # # # Contact Kevin Nash USAMD.Press@usdoj.gov 410-209-4946 Updated October 16, 2026 Components Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives (ATF) USAO - Maryland", "truncated": false}, {"id": "32af8a6a29a4ffd81a359ce7", "title": "St. Louis County Man Admits Using Fraudulent Deed Filings to Steal At Least 14 Properties", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-edmo/pr/st-louis-county-man-admits-using-fraudulent-deed-filings-steal-least-14-properties", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "St. Louis County Man Admits Using Fraudulent Deed Filings to Steal At Least 14 Properties\nPress Release St. Louis County Man Admits Using Fraudulent Deed Filings to Steal At Least 14 Properties Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Eastern District of Missouri ST. LOUIS – A man on Thursday admitted conspiring to steal at least 14 properties in St. Louis and St. Louis County by filing fraudulent deeds. James L. Townes Jr., 51, of Berkeley, pleaded guilty in U.S. District Court in St. Louis to a conspiracy charge. Townes admitted that beginning as early as Sept. 25, 2018, he began filing fraudulent deeds in St. Louis and St. Louis County on properties that belonged to others. The filings falsely claimed that Townes or his relatives had purchased the properties. Townes and his co-defendant, Charnay Bartlett, used fraudulently obtained notary seals to “authenticate” the forged signatures of the true owners of the properties. Townes and Bartlett transferred at least 14 properties to Townes, his companies or his friends and family, his plea agreement says. Townes admitted that his scheme involved the use of the names of 10 or more individuals and that the intended losses associated with his actions exceeded $1,500,000. The real owners had to expend considerable resources to regain the titles to their properties. Others abandoned their properties because they lacked the financial resources to initiate legal action. At least three of the property owners were deceased at the time when their purported signatures were authenticated by Townes and Bartlett. In some cases, Townes gained access to the properties by forcing entry through locked doors and squatting there. Bartlett, 30, pleaded guilty in April to the same conspiracy charge and is scheduled to be sentenced on December 2. Townes is set to be sentenced on January 21. The charge carries a penalty of up to five years in prison, a fine of up to $250,000 or both prison and a fine. The U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development Office of Inspector General, the Social Security Administration Office of Inspector General, the St. Louis County Police Department and the Hazelwood Police Department investigated the case. Assistant U.S. Attorney Gwen Carroll is prosecuting the case. Contact Robert Patrick, Public Affairs Officer, robert.patrick@usdoj.gov. Updated October 16, 2026 Topic Financial Fraud Component USAO - Missouri, Eastern", "truncated": false}, {"id": "31b7685391a579abe0afafa1", "title": "Twice-Deported Illegal Alien from Mexico Arrested on Federal Complaint Charging Him with Possessing 360 Kilograms of Cocaine", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-cdca/pr/twice-deported-illegal-alien-mexico-arrested-federal-complaint-charging-him-possessing", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Twice-Deported Illegal Alien from Mexico Arrested on Federal Complaint Charging Him with Possessing 360 Kilograms of Cocaine\nPress Release Twice-Deported Illegal Alien from Mexico Arrested on Federal Complaint Charging Him with Possessing 360 Kilograms of Cocaine Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Central District of California RIVERSIDE, California – A twice-deported illegal immigrant from Mexico has been arrested on a federal criminal complaint charging him with possessing approximately 360 kilograms (793.7 pounds) of cocaine in his San Bernardino County apartment, the Justice Department announced today. José María Mena Rosales, 38, of Fontana, was arrested on Wednesday and is charged with possession with intent to distribute cocaine. Rosales made his initial appearance on Thursday in United States District Court in Riverside. United States Magistrate Judge David T. Bristow ordered Rosales released on $50,000 bond. No plea was taken. Rosales’s arraignment is scheduled for November 10. According to an affidavit filed with the complaint, in February 2026, law enforcement investigating a Mexico-based drug trafficking organization learned that a large quantity of cocaine was to be delivered in four suitcases to Rosales at a public parking lot in Fontana. Rosales later retrieved the cocaine from the parking lot and drove to his apartment, where he unloaded the suitcases of narcotics into a garage. Law enforcement executed a search warrant at Rosales’s garage and apartment and seized approximately 360 kilograms of cocaine spread across 17 cardboard boxes. The four empty suitcases were found in the garage. Law enforcement officers confirmed Rosales’s identity with his California driver’s license and found several cell phones that contained text messages between Rosales and the individual who arranged the cocaine delivery. According to court documents, Rosales was deported from the United States in May 2014 and June 2014. A criminal complaint is merely an allegation. All defendants are presumed innocent until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt in a court of law. If convicted, Mena would face a mandatory minimum sentence of 10 years in federal prison and a statutory maximum sentence of life in federal prison. The FBI is investigating this matter with assistance from the San Bernardino County Sheriff’s Department. Assistant United States Attorney Courtney N. Williams of the Transnational Organized Crime Section is prosecuting this case. Contact Ciaran McEvoy Public Information Officer ciaran.mcevoy@usdoj.gov (213) 894-4465 Updated October 16, 2026 Topics Drug Trafficking Immigration Component USAO - California, Central Press Release Number: 26-199", "truncated": false}, {"id": "2b944db81a5c227191ccd3fd", "title": "Two Florida defendants sentenced to prison for nearly $900,000 health care scam", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-ks/pr/two-florida-defendants-sentenced-prison-nearly-900000-health-care-scam", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Two Florida defendants sentenced to prison for nearly $900,000 health care scam\nPress Release Two Florida defendants sentenced to prison for nearly $900,000 health care scam Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Kansas TOPEKA, KAN. – Two defendants received prison sentences following an $897,405 health care scheme where they used their pharmaceutical service to bill Medicare for thousands of unnecessary and illegal prescriptions. On April 7, the Department of Justice announced the creation of the National Fraud Enforcement Division (Fraud Division). The Fraud Division is laser-focused on investigating and prosecuting those who commit fraud against the American people. The Department’s work to combat fraud supports President Trump’s Task Force to Eliminate Fraud, a whole-of-government effort chaired by Vice President J.D. Vance to eliminate fraud, waste, and abuse within Federal benefit programs. According to court documents, the following defendants pleaded guilty to one count of conspiracy to commit health care fraud, mail fraud, and wire fraud: Steven Anthony Churchill, 39, of Boca Raton, Florida, was sentenced to 24 months in prison; Samson Kollelil Solomon, 28, of Pompano Beach, Florida, was sentenced to 24 months in prison; and Fawn Lickteig, 46, of Lawrence, Kansas, will be sentenced on November 10, 2026. In March 2020, Steven Churchill bought Free State Pharmacy (FSP) in Lawrence, Kansas, but under the name of a now deceased, accused coconspirator. FSP operated as a mail-order pharmacy despite representing itself to Medicare as a retail pharmacy. FSP contracted with Fawn Lickteig’s company, Pharmacy Consulting, to complete and submit its applications to Medicare and other health care related government entities. As part of the scheme, FSP acquired information from telemedicine companies, including names and addresses of patients. FSP employees then filled and shipped medications to these people even though the doctors who wrote the prescriptions had not physically examined or conducted proper telemedicine visits with the patients. FSP received calls from patients unexpectedly receiving medical products, and some patients returned the products to FSP. FSP prioritized the filling of prescriptions based on the amount of profit Medicare would ultimately pay the pharmacy. The majority of the medications prescribed were high-dollar dermatological products. Although FSP was based in Kansas, most of the beneficiaries were Floridians, with 948 claims for Florida residents and 381 claims for Kansas residents. Of the 40 prescribing physicians who worked with FSP, 15 were under investigation at the time for referring medically unnecessary services. Between September 2020 and February 2021, Churchill, Solomon, and Lickteig submitted 2,699 false claims causing Medicare to make fraudulent payments to FSP totaling $897,405. “Medicare exists to help senior citizens o", "truncated": true}, {"id": "296dea4df5bd8fbad436b13a", "title": "Lincoln Man Sentenced for Conspiracy to Distribute Methamphetamine", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-ne/pr/lincoln-man-sentenced-conspiracy-distribute-methamphetamine-55", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Lincoln Man Sentenced for Conspiracy to Distribute Methamphetamine\nPress Release Lincoln Man Sentenced for Conspiracy to Distribute Methamphetamine Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Nebraska United States Attorney Lesley A. Woods announced that Jessie Feaster, Jr., 50, of Lincoln, Nebraska, was sentenced on October 15, 2026, in federal court in Lincoln for conspiracy to distribute/possession with intent to distribute 500 grams or more of methamphetamine mixture and 50 grams or more of methamphetamine actual. United States District Judge Susan M. Bazis sentenced Feaster to 130 months’ imprisonment. There is no parole in the federal system. After Feaster’s release from prison, he will begin a 5-year term of supervised release. On October 10, 2025, investigators with the Lincoln Lancaster County Narcotics Task Force were conducting surveillance on co-defendant David Leibbrandt’s Lincoln residence.  They saw a U.S. Postal Service truck arrive and the driver left a package on the porch.  A short time later, someone came out of the house and retrieved the package.  About an hour later, Feaster, Jr. (“Feaster”) arrived at Leibbrandt’s residence and went inside.  After a few minutes, Feaster returned to his vehicle and left. Investigators followed Feaster to another location in Lincoln where Feaster was contacted and he agreed to let them search his car.  A sandwich bag was found on the front passenger seat which contained approximately one pound of methamphetamine.  Purity testing at the Nebraska State Patrol Crime Lab showed at least 410 grams of actual (pure) methamphetamine. Later the same day, investigators executed a search warrant at Leibbrandt’s residence.  During that search, they found seven bags containing methamphetamine with a total weight of more than 2.7 kilograms.  Purity testing on one of the packages showed at least 1.8 kilograms of actual methamphetamine.  Leibbrandt was arrested and agreed to talk to investigators.  He admitted receiving 10 pounds of methamphetamine earlier that day and said he had already sold more than three pounds from that quantity, including the pound he sold to Feaster. Leibbrandt pleaded guilty to the same conspiracy charge and was sentenced on September 24, 2026, to 135 months’ imprisonment and a 5-year term of supervised release. This case was investigated by the Lincoln/Lancaster County Narcotics Task Force. Contact Amy Donato 402-661-3700 Updated October 16, 2026 Component USAO - Nebraska", "truncated": false}, {"id": "2230e82ae148de0a70a808d7", "title": "White & Case advises A.P. Moller Capital on acquisition of Portek International - White & Case LLP", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMitAFBVV95cUxOcm9pbDhpa28wOVRvMXVUbDJzSDJIMk5QSGdZRGdLQU9neFY0WmFBUE5XY2twNHJKczlNU21SOWlaVFI0WEtudE9TdUY0aFpLMGtqRHVpRHo2LXZYS05aUk1lUHY5bnBzX3JQNFRHanJBMnc1NmZpeFE1bVBFNVBGOExPSWJOa3hOWmp3MWprb1pVRjVLTElERVpDVWZZSjRCVVEwY3BkeFVkX1BWVlZBamFoTnM?oc=5", "source": "lane_corporate_action_mna", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "White & Case advises A.P. Moller Capital on acquisition of Portek International - White & Case LLP\nWhite & Case advises A.P. Moller Capital on acquisition of Portek International White & Case LLP", "truncated": false}, {"id": "21e3b729cf22964584bfbf47", "title": "Northern Nevada Man Indicted for Federal Child Sexual Abuse Material Crimes", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-nv/pr/northern-nevada-man-indicted-federal-child-sexual-abuse-material-crimes", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Northern Nevada Man Indicted for Federal Child Sexual Abuse Material Crimes\nPress Release Northern Nevada Man Indicted for Federal Child Sexual Abuse Material Crimes Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Nevada RENO – A Reno resident has been arrested and made his initial appearance in federal court yesterday for allegedly distributing and possessing child sexual abuse material (CSAM). “There is absolutely no room for leniency when it comes to the exploitation of children,” said First Assistant United States Attorney Sigal Chattah for the District of Nevada. “Our office, alongside our law enforcement partners, will use every aggressive legal avenue available to dismantle this destructive cycle and ensure that predators face the maximum severity of federal law.” “Not only did Mr. Stockinger exploit these children, he further victimized them by distributing vile images of their abuse to other predators,” said Special Agent in Charge Christopher S. Delzotto for the FBI Las Vegas Field Office. “His arrest marks the first step toward ensuring he is held fully accountable for these horrific crimes. The FBI will continue to deploy every available resource through our Child Exploitation and Human Trafficking Task Force to investigate, pursue, and bring to justice the dangerous predators who target our most vulnerable.” According to allegations in the indictment, beginning about June 5, 2026, to about June 30, 2026, Zachary Steven Stockinger distributed and possessed child sexual abuse material of children younger than 12 years old engaged in sexually explicit conduct. Stockinger is charged with one count of distribution of child pornography and one count of possession of child pornography. A trial has been scheduled for December 15, 2026, before United States District Judge Miranda M. Du. If convicted, Stockinger faces a minimum penalty of five years in prison and a maximum penalty of 30 years in prison. This case was investigated by the FBI. Assistant United States Attorney Afroza Yeasmin is prosecuting the case. This case was brought as part of Project Safe Childhood, a nationwide initiative to combat the epidemic of child sexual exploitation and abuse, launched in May 2006 by the Department of Justice. Led by U.S. Attorneys’ Offices and CEOS, Project Safe Childhood marshals federal, state, and local resources to better locate, apprehend and prosecute individuals who exploit children via the internet, as well as to identify and rescue victims. For more information about Project Safe Childhood, please visit www.justice.gov/psc . ### Updated October 16, 2026 Topic Project Safe Childhood Component USAO - Nevada", "truncated": false}, {"id": "204f578c657d40a1d1d718f0", "title": "Mike Cloud Paints the Abject in \"Suitcase Portraits\" - Newcity Art", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMijwFBVV95cUxNTkpxZUZtZUFmc2doejU4OVpMYm9fZW1KVWlTN3pzNXgzRXIxd05YMXg2cUEyWU93djViNEdrSEg3WXlBUmRBLWljV25zNUR5cG1IcTRNS084VmdIOU11a1RiQVAzcnQwcFRsTmtKcWc3V25iM05VaG1fcmtaU2dma0l1NjRCXzV0cXV4UWVIVQ?oc=5", "source": "topic_technology", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Mike Cloud Paints the Abject in \"Suitcase Portraits\" - Newcity Art\nMike Cloud Paints the Abject in \"Suitcase Portraits\" Newcity Art", "truncated": false}, {"id": "1fe127c886442c84b9b1210d", "title": "Lincoln Man Sentenced for Possession with Intent to Distribute Methamphetamine", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-ne/pr/lincoln-man-sentenced-possession-intent-distribute-methamphetamine-19", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Lincoln Man Sentenced for Possession with Intent to Distribute Methamphetamine\nPress Release Lincoln Man Sentenced for Possession with Intent to Distribute Methamphetamine Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Nebraska United States Attorney Lesley A. Woods announced that Johnathan Klein, 31, of Lincoln, Nebraska, was sentenced on October 15, 2026, in federal court in Lincoln for possession with intent to distribute 50 grams or more of methamphetamine actual. United States District Judge Susan M. Bazis sentenced Klein to 130 months’ imprisonment. There is no parole in the federal system. After Klein’s release from prison, he will begin a 5-year term of supervised release. Law enforcement learned that Klein had been dealing methamphetamine to a person between October of 2024 and March 13, 2025, in varying amounts.  They stated they bought 1/8 ounce 10 times, an ounce 30 times, four ounces twice, and 5 ½ ounces once. On March 13, 2025, a Lincoln Police Department officer committed a traffic stop on a vehicle with Klein as a passenger.  During a search of the driver’s person, a small bag of methamphetamine was found, which resulted in a search of the vehicle. A larger bag, containing two baggies of methamphetamine, was found on the front floorboard where the co-defendant driver and Klein had been sitting.  Syringes, scales, and baggies were also found in the vehicle. The two baggies of suspected methamphetamine found on the floorboard were sent to the Nebraska State Patrol Crime Laboratory. Further testing showed one of the baggies containing 57.72 grams of methamphetamine with a purity of 99.3%, resulting in at least 54 grams of methamphetamine actual.  The second bag contained 54.73 gram of methamphetamine. No purity analysis was conducted on that bag. The case is pending against the co-defendant. This case was investigated by the Lincoln/Lancaster County Narcotics Task Force. Contact Amy Donato 402-661-3700 Updated October 16, 2026 Component USAO - Nebraska", "truncated": false}, {"id": "1a6e2612e7ca4351b2c74931", "title": "Wake County Public Libraries launches New STEAM Labs to support student success - Wake County Government (.gov)", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMipAFBVV95cUxOd0d6MWEwaDkzbExRNFRkdjhZLVdNOUlRSnpVNVRveld3RUsxRUw5S2NjcVppZUFkRmtxWGp6d243bUFDTlFNbVpVa2g1Y2N3Z1pKTHdCZWlnTWtGR1hyVDQ5UmkwcUpTMmJMNGV5OElmVlROOFg3emlzZEZERTd4WkdPMjdMU0lNeDhiX0xRUkk0VDkxRnlXbGtTcU4ySm1GMUdZMg?oc=5", "source": "topic_products", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Wake County Public Libraries launches New STEAM Labs to support student success - Wake County Government (.gov)\nWake County Public Libraries launches New STEAM Labs to support student success Wake County Government (.gov)", "truncated": false}, {"id": "1a09bd310076480a799a4b40", "title": "Baltimore Felon Pleads Guilty to Firearm Charges", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-md/pr/baltimore-felon-pleads-guilty-firearm-charges", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Baltimore Felon Pleads Guilty to Firearm Charges\nPress Release Baltimore Felon Pleads Guilty to Firearm Charges Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Maryland Baltimore, Maryland – A Baltimore man pled guilty in federal court to firearm-possession charges. Arsenio Hall, 37, is charged with possession of a firearm and ammunition by a prohibited person. Hall is prohibited from possessing firearms and ammunition due to a prior felony conviction.  Those convictions include two for second-degree assault, including a domestically related assault, along with drug-related convictions. Kelly O. Hayes, U.S. Attorney for the District of Maryland, announced the guilty plea with Special Agent in Charge Charles Doerrer, Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms, and Explosives (ATF), and Commissioner Richard Worley, Baltimore Police Department (BPD). According to court documents, on December 19, 2024, members of BPD’s SWAT Team arrived at Hall’s residence to assist Baltimore County Police Department detectives with executing an arrest warrant on Hall and a search-and-seizure warrant on his residence. These warrants were related to a November 2024 shooting in Baltimore County. During the search, officers recovered a semi-automatic rifle from a baby crib inside of Hall’s bedroom. Hall faces a maximum of 15 years in prison for possession of a firearm by a prohibited person.  U.S. District Court Judge Ellen L. Hollander set sentencing for Wednesday, Jan. 13, 2027, at 11 a.m. Actual sentences for federal crimes are typically less than the maximum penalties. A federal district judge determines sentencing after considering the U.S. Sentencing Guidelines and other statutory factors. This prosecution is part of Project Safe Neighborhoods (PSN) and Project Safe Home. These programs bring together all levels of law enforcement and the communities they serve to reduce violent crime and gun violence, and to make our neighborhoods safer for everyone. On May 26, 2021, the Department launched a violent crime reduction strategy strengthening PSN based on these core principles: fostering trust and legitimacy in our communities, supporting community-based organizations that help prevent violence from occurring in the first place, setting focused and strategic enforcement priorities, and measuring the results. Project Safe Home is an initiative under the Violence Against Women Act (VAWA) through the Office on Violence Against Women (OVW) to reduce domestic violence and prevent escalation to lethal violence by targeting offenders who use or possess handguns with a history of Domestic Violence. In response, the U.S. Attorney’s Office for the District of Maryland is working closely with its federal, local, and state partners — along with Baltimore City community organizations — to reduce violent crime and enhance public safety.  This partnership and coordination between the U.S. Attorney’s Office a", "truncated": true}, {"id": "16c1ae4410612f7a22199a7c", "title": "Brazilian Man Sentenced to 12 Years in Prison for Managerial Role in Conspiracy to Launder Tens of Millions of Dollars in Illicit Cash Proceeds", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/brazilian-man-sentenced-12-years-prison-managerial-role-conspiracy-launder-tens-millions", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Brazilian Man Sentenced to 12 Years in Prison for Managerial Role in Conspiracy to Launder Tens of Millions of Dollars in Illicit Cash Proceeds\nBrazilian Man Sentenced to 12 Years in Prison for Managerial Role in Conspiracy to Launder Tens of Millions of Dollars in Illicit Cash Proceeds Following Homeland Security Task Force Initiative\nSixth Defendant Sentenced in $30 Million Multi-State Laundering Scheme\nYgor Fokin Saviolli, 35, a Brazilian national, was sentenced today to 12 years in prison for his role as a manager and supervisor in a money laundering organization that laundered more than $30 million in illicit proceeds, including the proceeds of drug trafficking. On September 24, five co-defendants received sentences ranging from 94 months in prison to 12 months and one day in prison.\nGabriel Cezar Menezes, 29, a Brazilian national, was sentenced to 94 months; Omar Aliperti De Mello Correa, 34, a U.S. citizen, was sentenced to 30 months; Leandro De Avila Goncalves, 43, a Brazilian national, was sentenced to 24 months; and Joao Andrade De Mello, 29, and Tadeu Sebastiane Rabelo Alves Barbosa, 30, both Brazilian nationals, were each sentenced to 12 months and one day. The Brazilian national defendants were illegally residing in the United States at the time of their arrests.\nAccording to court documents, Saviolli, Menezes, Correa, Goncalves, De Mello, and Barbosa were part of a sophisticated money laundering organization that operated across the United States and spanned several countries. Members of the conspiracy arranged for U.S.-based couriers to receive bulk cash proceeds from the sale of controlled substances and then to deposit the cash at banks across the country in order to conceal and return those profits to, among others, the sources of drug supply operating outside the United States. Dozens of pickups across the country were coordinated through WhatsApp message chains that included facilitators and couriers. Saviolli provided upfront funds to facilitate the operations of the money laundering organization and oversaw the receipt and laundering of the bulk cash drug proceeds. Menezes also served as a facilitator, providing direction to and oversight of couriers, and personally picked up bulk cash on multiple occasions. Correa, De Mello, Barbosa, and Goncalves all served as couriers for the money laundering organization, conducting bulk cash pickups in cities including Atlanta, Charlotte, Chicago, Cleveland, Minneapolis, Rochester, and Tampa, among others. Over the course of the conspiracy, the organization concealed more than $30 million in cash.\nto conspiracy to commit money laundering were accepted by the district court.\nAssistant Attorney General A. Tysen Duva of the Justice Department’s Criminal Division, U.S. Attorney Jason A. Reding Quiñones for the Southern District of Florida, and Special Agent in Charge Brett Skiles of the FBI Miami Field Office made the announcement.\nThe FBI investigated the case, with assistance from the Drug Enforcement Administration’s Rochester Resident Office and Homeland Security Investigations’ Brasilia Attaché Office and Miami Field Office.\nTrial Attorneys James Hepburn and Jessee Alexander-Hoeppner of the Criminal Division’s Money Laundering, Narcotics and Forfeiture Section (MNF) and Assistant U.S. Attorney Monique Botero for the Southern District of Florida prosecuted the case.\nMNF’s mission is to take the profit out of crime, eliminate drug cartels, and protect the U.S. financial system. MNF pursues criminal prosecutions and criminal and civil asset recovery actions involving: financial facilitators who launder profits for criminals; financial institutions and their officers and employees whose actions threaten the U.S. financial system and financial institutions; international money launderers who support transnational organized crime; and the top command and control of international drug trafficking organizations.\nMNF’s International Unit investigates and prosecutes cross-border money laundering schemes involving transnational criminal organizations, cartels, foreign official corruption and related money laundering affecting the U.S. financial system and prosecutes criminal cases and civil forfeiture matters to recover the proceeds of those crimes.\nThis prosecution is part of the Homeland Security Task Force (HSTF) initiative established by Executive Order 14159, Protecting the American People Against Invasion. The HSTF is a whole-of-government partnership dedicated to eliminating criminal cartels, foreign gangs, transnational criminal organizations, and human smuggling and trafficking rings operating in the United States and abroad. Through historic interagency collaboration, the HSTF directs the full might of U.S. law enforcement towards identifying, investigating, and prosecuting the full spectrum of crimes committed by these organizations, which have long fueled violence and instability within our borders. In performing this work, the HSTF places special emphasis on investigating and prosecuting those engaged in child trafficking or other crimes involving children. The HSTF further utilizes all available tools to prosecute and remove the most violent criminal aliens from the United States.\nCriminal - Money Laundering, Narcotics and Forfeiture Section\nThe Department of Justice Announces Results of Law Enforcement and Private Industry Joint Disruption Week Targeting Online Scam Accounts and Transnational Criminal Networks Operating in West Africa\nTransnational criminal scam networks operating from West Africa suffered major disruptions last week during a coordinated international “Joint Disruption Week,” an effort that produced arrests, seizures, and the largest-ever takedown...\nHomeland Security Task Force Arrests Alvaro Pulido Vargas to Face Charges in Multibillion-Dollar Venezuelan Money Laundering and Bribery Schemes\nA Colombian national charged in two separate federal indictments involving the alleged laundering of more than $2 billion in connection with Venezuelan government bribery schemes will make his initial appearance...\nFormer TD Bank Employee Pleads Guilty to Accepting Bribes, Laundering $4.8 Million to Colombia\nGerardo Aquino, 40, of Hollywood, Florida, pleaded guilty to accepting bribes and laundering more than $4.8 million to Colombia while employed by TD Bank.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "1663eb7e1ff474346c7f1ac9", "title": "Federal Detainees to Be Relocated to Detention Facilities in Mecklenburg County; Partnership Between the U.S. Attorney’s Office, U.S. Marshals Service, and Mecklenburg County Sheriff’s Office Brings Federal Resources to North Carolina", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-wdnc/pr/federal-detainees-be-relocated-detention-facilities-mecklenburg-county-partnership", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Federal Detainees to Be Relocated to Detention Facilities in Mecklenburg County; Partnership Between the U.S. Attorney’s Office, U.S. Marshals Service, and Mecklenburg County Sheriff’s Office Brings Federal Resources to North Carolina\nPress Release Federal Detainees to Be Relocated to Detention Facilities in Mecklenburg County; Partnership Between the U.S. Attorney’s Office, U.S. Marshals Service, and Mecklenburg County Sheriff’s Office Brings Federal Resources to North Carolina Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Western District of North Carolina CHARLOTTE, N.C. – U.S. Attorney Russ Ferguson, U.S. Marshal Terry Burgin, and Mecklenburg County Sheriff Garry McFadden today announced a new agreement between the Mecklenburg County Sheriff’s Office (MCSO) and the U.S. Marshals Service (USMS) to relocate federal detainees from detention facilities outside of North Carolina to facilities in Mecklenburg County. “Housing federal detainees in Mecklenburg County whose cases are pending in this district not only brings millions of federal dollars to North Carolina that otherwise went to other states, it benefits the federal justice system. It means defendants are closer to their attorneys—making it easier to prepare their defense and thus move cases faster—and closer to their families, which makes rehabilitation and reentry to society more likely to be successful. It also means shorter, quicker, and cheaper transportation to court, which makes it easier to schedule hearings and trials,” said U.S. Attorney Russ Ferguson. “I am so thankful to Sheriff Garry McFadden and Chief Deputy U.S. Marshal Chris Edge for making this partnership possible.” “The U.S. Marshals Service is excited to once again house federal detainees with the Mecklenburg County Sheriff’s Office. The MCSO and the USMS have a long-standing partnership. The reopening of Mecklenburg County Detention Center North will allow for the continued due process of federal detainees and ensure the federal judicial system operates efficiently,” said Chief Deputy U.S. Marshal Chris Edge. “When law enforcement and criminal justice agencies work together through transparent collaboration, open communication, and an unwavering commitment to public safety, we reinforce accountability and the integrity of the justice system while demonstrating the unity and professionalism our communities expect and deserve. Today stands as a clear example of that commitment by bringing federal detainees back to the Sheriff’s Office’s North facility. I commend U.S. Attorney Russ Ferguson and Chief Deputy U.S. Marshal Chris Edge for their leadership and partnership in supporting the Sheriff’s Office throughout this process,” said Sheriff Garry McFadden. Because of the new agreement, federal detainees in the custody of the USMS with pending federal cases in the Western District of North Carolina currently housed in detention facilities in other states", "truncated": true}, {"id": "1355f32bf203d8f1fc97cc08", "title": "Floridian sentenced to federal prison, ordered to pay $2.8M in restitution for money laundering scheme in the Eastern District of Texas", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-edtx/pr/floridian-sentenced-federal-prison-ordered-pay-28m-restitution-money-laundering-scheme", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Floridian sentenced to federal prison, ordered to pay $2.8M in restitution for money laundering scheme in the Eastern District of Texas\nPress Release Floridian sentenced to federal prison, ordered to pay $2.8M in restitution for money laundering scheme in the Eastern District of Texas Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Eastern District of Texas PLANO, Texas – A Palm Bay, Florida man has been sentenced to federal prison for a money laundering scheme in the Eastern District of Texas, announced U.S. Attorney Jay R. Combs. Joshua Thomas Jackson, 41, pleaded guilty to money laundering and was sentenced to 51 months in federal prison by U.S. District Judge Sean D. Jordan on October 15, 2026. According to information presented in court, Jackson engaged in a scheme to defraud individuals using false representations and statements.  To facilitate the scheme, Jackson created business entities and marketed opportunities to invest in real estate development, purchases, and rehabilitation schemes. As Jackson carried out his scheme, he falsely represented that funds investors provided would be used to purchase real property or distressed residential properties, that the investors funds were secure and would be repaid within a defined period of time, and that his business activities and the victims’ investments were secure and profitable. During the period of his scheme Jackson received over $3 million from investors and caused losses to investors exceeding $2.8 million. “Investor fraud is a destructive crime and causes significant losses across our nation,” said U.S. Attorney Jay R. Combs. “Together, with our partners at the FBI, we will continue to investigate and prosecute this type of fraud in the Eastern District of Texas, bring justice to these perpetrators, and seek restitution for victims.” This case was investigated by the FBI and prosecuted by Assistant U.S. Attorneys in the Plano office. ### Updated October 16, 2026 Topics Fraud Financial Fraud Components Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) USAO - Texas, Eastern", "truncated": false}, {"id": "133d93bad5bb202ef6c8d5e8", "title": "Scinai Immunotherapeutics Ltd. (SCNI) Raises $4.0M Via Private Placement, Warrant Deals - TradingView", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi3AFBVV95cUxQd3k2QlU3X3lKbEZyUWt0V3V0d0hydGVGM25QY2l1VldBV2tkdXA4dWFQZlJGOVJOOURYVWgxLUgwVkhxWVZwM19nMHFkZVFCSXV0QlRTLV9OcDZjdlVFNkhxNEZDUS1mcWJETTNzc0VLX2d6d1QzQkhCMi1GN29wdkI1RF9HN2FYdG5ua2w2SmRLY2Y0SXRnN2Jnd1huS2J0dVNoeUdISW1CVlJ4ZUtmWU5mZUlidFZsUFEwTVNLMFd", "source": "lane_dilution", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Scinai Immunotherapeutics Ltd. (SCNI) Raises $4.0M Via Private Placement, Warrant Deals - TradingView\nScinai Immunotherapeutics Ltd. (SCNI) Raises $4.0M Via Private Placement, Warrant Deals TradingView", "truncated": false}, {"id": "0e0b3433cd32fb0fbc98f071", "title": "Leader of Lubbock Bandidos Motorcycle Club sentenced to federal prison for meth distribution", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-ndtx/pr/leader-lubbock-bandidos-motorcycle-club-sentenced-federal-prison-meth-distribution", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Leader of Lubbock Bandidos Motorcycle Club sentenced to federal prison for meth distribution\nPress Release Leader of Lubbock Bandidos Motorcycle Club sentenced to federal prison for meth distribution Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Northern District of Texas LUBBOCK, Texas – United States Attorney for the Northern District of Texas Ryan Raybould today announced that a Lubbock man who led a high-purity methamphetamine trafficking operation was sentenced to more than 11 years in federal prison. Steven Glenn Ryals, 72, also known as “Hot Tub,” was convicted of possession with intent to distribute 50 grams or more of methamphetamine. U.S. District Judge James Wesley Hendrix imposed the 135-month sentence today. “Steven Ryals exploited his leadership of the Bandidos’ Lubbock chapter to push high purity methamphetamine into our community,” said U.S. Attorney Ryan Raybould. “Today’s sentence sends a clear message: we will aggressively pursue those who traffic dangerous drugs and dismantle the drug distribution networks that threaten West Texans.” “Today’s sentence reflects the FBI’s continued commitment to protecting our communities from the dangers of high purity narcotics and the criminal networks that distribute them,” said FBI Dallas Special Agent in Charge R. Joseph Rothrock. “This case underscores the impact of strong partnerships among federal, state, and local agencies and our shared resolve to disrupt drug trafficking organizations and hold traffickers accountable.” According to court documents, Ryals was the President of the Bandidos Motorcycle Club’s Lubbock Notorious Chapter and had served with the club for over 50 years. Federal law enforcement began investigating Ryals and other members in early 2025 for distributing methamphetamine in the Lubbock area. On Feb. 27, FBI agents and the Lubbock Anti-Gang Center executed a search warrant at the Bandidos clubhouse, which also served as Ryals’s residence. During that search, agents discovered approximately 3.2 pounds of suspected methamphetamine inside a locked filing‑cabinet drawer, along with Bandidos paraphernalia and tools used to package and distribute narcotics. At sentencing, prosecutors outlined the results of DEA laboratory testing confirming the substance was 1,449.6 grams of methamphetamine hydrochloride at 98% purity, an amount consistent only with high‑level distribution. Additional methamphetamine was found in Ryals’s bedroom closet and in his vehicle during a traffic stop on the same day, all of which he admitted possessing with intent to distribute. Ryals was previously convicted of methamphetamine trafficking and in 1999 received an identical 135‑month sentence for that offense. The FBI, the Lubbock Texas Anti‑Gang (TAG) Center and the Caprock High‑Intensity Drug Trafficking Area (HIDTA) conducted the investigation. TAG Center includes agents from DEA, ATF, Texas DPS, the Lubbock County Sheriff’", "truncated": true}, {"id": "085d25124d17b3e320615dba", "title": "Convicted Drug Dealer Charged with Trafficking Methamphetamine While Armed with Firearm, Extended Magazine, and Drum Magazine", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-hi/pr/convicted-drug-dealer-charged-trafficking-methamphetamine-while-armed-firearm-extended", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Convicted Drug Dealer Charged with Trafficking Methamphetamine While Armed with Firearm, Extended Magazine, and Drum Magazine\nPress Release Convicted Drug Dealer Charged with Trafficking Methamphetamine While Armed with Firearm, Extended Magazine, and Drum Magazine Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               District of Hawaii HONOLULU – United States Attorney Ken Sorenson announced that Shalom Tuimalealiifano, 44, of Honolulu, Hawaii, was indicted on October 15, 2026 with possessing with intent to distribute methamphetamine, possession of a firearm in furtherance of drug trafficking, and unlawful possession of ammunition as a convicted felon. According to court records, Tuimalealiifano possessed methamphetamine with the intent to distribute it to others, as well as a loaded pistol with a 31-round extended magazine, which he used in furtherance of his drug trafficking.  Tuimalealiifano, who has a prior felony conviction for drug trafficking, was also charged with unlawfully possessing ammunition, including 39 rounds in a separate drum magazine. If convicted, Tuimalealiifano faces at least 20 years and up to life in prison, a fine of up to $20,000,000, plus a term of supervised release. The charges in the indictment are merely accusations, and the defendant is presumed innocent unless and until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt in a court of law.  In the case of conviction, any sentence would be imposed by a United States District Judge based on the statutory sentencing factors and the advisory United States Sentencing Guidelines. The FBI is investigating the case. Special Assistant U.S. Attorney Heidi Turner is prosecuting the case. Project Safe Neighborhoods (PSN) is the Department of Justice’s nationwide initiative that brings federal, state, local, and tribal law enforcement, prosecutors, and other public safety partners together to coordinate efforts to combat violent crime and make communities safe.  Coordinated by U.S. Attorneys’ offices in each of the 94 federal districts, PSN is tailored to particular communities to strategically address specific violent crime and public safety challenges.  As a key component of Operation Take Back America, PSN serves a central role in the Department’s commitment to make our country safe.  PSN emphasizes three core principles: rapid federal response to violent crime and criminal offenders; strong, strategic partnerships among law enforcement at all levels; and accountability and deterrence through the prosecution of the most serious, readily provable offenses and other strategies.  These efforts complement and strengthen President Trump’s Homeland Security Task Force, ensuring a comprehensive federal response to the most pressing public safety issues facing communities. Contact Aislinn Affinito Aislinn.Affinito@usdoj.gov Updated October 16, 2026 Topics Homeland Security Task Force Operation Take Back America Project Safe Neighb", "truncated": true}, {"id": "060741eeb16210bc246ca208", "title": "Final Defendant Sentenced in a Conspiracy to Distribute Counterfeit Pills Containing Fentanyl", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/usao-edky/pr/final-defendant-sentenced-conspiracy-distribute-counterfeit-pills-containing-fentanyl", "source": "official_doj", "published_at": "2026-10-16T12:00:00+00:00", "text": "Final Defendant Sentenced in a Conspiracy to Distribute Counterfeit Pills Containing Fentanyl\nPress Release Final Defendant Sentenced in a Conspiracy to Distribute Counterfeit Pills Containing Fentanyl Friday, October 16, 2026 Share Facebook X LinkedIn Email For Immediate Release U.S. Attorney's Office,               Eastern District of Kentucky LEXINGTON, Ky. – A Lexington, Ky., man, Quentin Montez Leavell, 33, was sentenced to 144 months of imprisonment yesterday by U.S. District Judge Chad Meredith for conspiracy to distribute 40 grams or more of fentanyl, distribution of fentanyl, and possession of a firearm in furtherance of a drug trafficking crime. According to his plea agreement, from February 2021 to July 2021, Leavell agreed with others, including codefendants, Dominque Cornell Duff and Lakwon Marquis Givens, to distribute fentanyl contained in counterfeit pills that resembled Percocet, with markings of “M, 30.” Leavell further admitted that he possessed a firearm to protect himself and to deter others from attempting to rob or steal drugs and/or money from him. Codefendants Duff and Givens were sentenced in September 2026, to 155 months and 170 months, respectively, for their roles in the conspiracy. Jason Parman, United States Attorney for the Eastern District of Kentucky, Dennis M. Fetting, Special Agent in Charge, Department of Homeland Security, Homeland Security Investigations (HSI), and Chief Lawrence Weathers, Lexington Police Department, jointly announced the sentences. The investigation was conducted by the Department of Homeland Security, Homeland Security Investigations (HSI) and the Lexington Police Department. Assistant U.S. Attorney Todd Bradbury prosecuted the case on behalf of the United States. This prosecution is part of the Homeland Security Task Force (HSTF) initiative established by Executive Order 14159, Protecting the American People Against Invasion. The HSTF is a whole-of-government partnership dedicated to eliminating criminal cartels, foreign gangs, transnational criminal organizations, and human smuggling and trafficking rings operating in the United States and abroad. Through historic interagency collaboration, the HSTF directs the full might of United States law enforcement towards identifying, investigating, and prosecuting the full spectrum of crimes committed by these organizations, which have long fueled violence and instability within our borders. In performing this work, the HSTF places special emphasis on investigating and prosecuting those engaged in child trafficking or other crimes involving children. The HSTF further utilizes all available tools to prosecute and remove the most violent criminal aliens from the United States. – END – Contact CONTACT: Jenny Parker PHONE: (859) 685-4906 E-MAIL: jenny.parker@usdoj.gov Updated October 16, 2026 Topics Homeland Security Task Force Drug Trafficking Opioids Firearms Offenses Component USAO - Kentucky, Eastern", "truncated": false}, {"id": "89181d7fa4332af4b259bbbe", "title": "Travelers Companies (NYSE:TRV) Announces Earnings Results - MarketBeat", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiugFBVV95cUxQeVNFTW56Q2VzRDNUaU4wbGtVQWJIdmlvY0Uzek9VZGctZk1jaVA0MVRoYS1CQjNUVC1TdXVLc1NSbnE3c1VjWnVoRnZYVkptNmZ4LUpqLUtGNGVRYUxEUElhOXQyT2c4LVR4UVdPRGZHOEp6dWhwTjlMUHIzRlNSMlFDLW1ZSDR6OWdtRnVoaE9MUk1nSzhRcE5Ea3lSNTBFZDV1YkJrU3NrVnkwbHZOOGpTOTRNaGd