e Placement TradingView", "truncated": false}, {"id": "759e176dae412e1a04e1791c", "title": "Aurora Cannabis Doubles Its Production Capacity in Germany - Newsweed", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMifkFVX3lxTE1IQzBZa1lkT3pzbDFtbUMyMGRVN3pVWGc3LVJoUWlESTZ5T3ZFNXVRbTgwRzRQY2NxbEpRZWJkR1BKNWRKc0FlTGFMVmgzN2xBc3VNVThVV3FmdU1ud2tFVjNYbzNIV3k0NHAwVGxmd3dtS1dYcGpHNmRMOGFuQQ?oc=5", "source": "lane_capacity", "published_at": "2026-10-16T11:44:39+00:00", "text": "Aurora Cannabis Doubles Its Production Capacity in Germany - Newsweed\nAurora Cannabis Doubles Its Production Capacity in Germany Newsweed", "truncated": false}, {"id": "6cc2f945a0a151e55f894651", "title": "India's 'cockroach' party founder detained ahead of protest", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmx237n1y9lzo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T11:44:31+00:00", "text": "India's 'cockroach' party founder detained ahead of protest\nAbhijeet Dipke's youth-led Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) has drawn thousands onto the streets in recent months\nZoya Mateen, Azadeh Moshiri & Vikas Pandey\nAbhijeet Dipke, the founder of India's \"cockroach\" movement, and a few others were detained in Delhi as police tried to stop a protest demanding the resignation of the country's election chief.\nHundreds had gathered in Connaught Place, one of the capital's busiest commercial districts, waving Indian flags and shouting slogans despite a heavy security presence.\nPolice sealed off Central Park, the planned site of the protest, and deployed thousands of police and paramilitary personnel to prevent the gathering.\nThe Gen Z-led Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) accuses India's Election Commission of manipulating voter lists to favour Prime Minister Narendra Modi's ruling BJP. Both deny the allegation.\nThe group has held a series of protests in Delhi in recent weeks, with police repeatedly detaining its leaders and supporters. Friday's demonstration unfolded under similarly heavy security presence, though the police response was more restrained than during last weekend's sweeping crackdown.\nHours before the protest, police went to the house where Dipke was staying. In an exchange broadcast live on Instagram, he challenged officers who had come to caution him against holding the march, asking whether waving the Indian flag was against the law.\nHours later, the BBC saw officers surround Dipke as he approached his car and force him into a police vehicle. Some supporters tried to stop it from leaving.\nIt was Dipke's second detention in less than a week. Last Saturday, police had\nhis plane shortly after it landed in Delhi to prevent him from reaching another demonstration. He was released the following day.\nThe movement's two other main leaders, Saurav Das and Ashutosh Ranka, were also detained last weekend and later released. But on Wednesday, they were\nfor five days after returning to Jantar Mantar, a protest site near parliament that police had recently declared off-limits. Friday's march was partly intended to demand the release of Das and Ranka.\nDipke's detention means the movement's three main leaders are once again in custody.\nThe CJP said it had lost contact with him and accused police of taking him to an undisclosed location and denying him access to lawyers.\nBut they insist the arrests and detentions will not stop them. \"They may detain three people, but how will they stop 30 tomorrow? How will they stop 300 leaders?\" CJP spokesperson Mehr Pasricha told the BBC.\nPolice have not publicly confirmed the grounds for Dipke's detention or responded to the CJP's latest allegations.\nAnish Gawande, the opposition politician who had been hosting Dipke, also questioned the police action.\n\"Detaining a lone man walking with the national flag in his hand is a bit of a joke,\" he said.\nHundreds of people gathered in central Delhi to unfurl the national flag as part of the protest\nAt Connaught Place, the police presence dwarfed the protest itself.\nAround 200-300 people initially gathered outside Central Park, which authorities had closed. Unable to enter, they walked around its perimeter, waving Indian flags and shouting slogans. More protesters arrived as the evening progressed.\nOfficers kept emerging from buses parked around the district. Journalists crowded the streets too, and together with police, they far outnumbered the demonstrators.\nFor much of the evening, police largely held back, with the BBC witnessing only a handful of detentions. But as darkness fell, officers began using force against some protesters.\nThe BBC saw some young demonstrators being pushed into a bus where women were already being held. Inside were around 50 female officers, far more than the number of detainees.\nThe BBC also saw two people being injured during the police action. One protester fell to the ground and appeared to have hurt their leg.\n, a prominent leader of the left-wing All India Students' Association (AISA) who has emerged as one of the faces of the recent protests. She had also been detained during last weekend's crackdown.\nStudent activist Neha Bora is among the people detained by the Delhi police on Friday evening\nBarely five months old, the CJP began as an online campaign in May after India's chief justice likened some unemployed young people to cockroaches. Dipke embraced the insult and turned it into a rallying cry.\nBy July, the group had become a formidable force, drawing thousands of young people into protests over leaked exam papers. A march towards parliament in July descended into clashes with police, bringing the movement wider attention and intensifying pressure on the government. The unrest eventually forced the resignation of India's education minister.\nThe CJP has since turned its attention to India's electoral system, demanding the resignation of Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar over a sweeping revision of voter lists known as the Special Intensive Revision (SIR).\nCritics say the exercise risks removing legitimate voters from the rolls, potentially depriving them of the right to vote. The Election Commission says it is targeting ineligible, dead and duplicate voters and denies allegations that it is favouring the ruling BJP.\nto stop CJP supporters from reaching a planned rally, sealing off roads, shutting metro stations and deploying thousands of security personnel across Delhi.\ndozens of protesters being detained across the capital, including students and journalists.\nThe action drew criticism but police defended it as necessary to maintain public order and prevent unauthorised gatherings.\nDipke, Das and Ranka were released the following day after agreeing to maintain peace and good behaviour. But the dispute over where they could protest was only beginning.\nDelhi authorities have said Central Park is closed on Friday and Saturday for \"administrative reasons\"\nPolice removed Jantar Mantar from Delhi's list of approved protest sites, naming three alternative venues.\nBuilt in the 18th Century to study the stars, the observatory stands close to Parliament and has long been one of India's most recognisable stages for public dissent. The CJP accused authorities of trying to push demonstrations away from the seat of power.\nOn Tuesday, Das and Ranka returned to the restricted site with a handful of supporters, unfurling the Indian flag and shouting slogans against Kumar.\nPolice said some protesters had climbed over a locked gate and that the organisers had breached the conditions of their release. The following day, the two men were sent to jail for five days.\nThe arrests did little to deter the movement from announcing another demonstration.\nIn a video message on Thursday, Dipke urged supporters unable to travel to Delhi to raise the Indian flag in their own towns and cities. He also acknowledged the possibility of being arrested again.\n\"I also may be jailed after this, but I am not worried. Tricolour is my pride and I will raise it,\" he said.\nBy Friday morning, police had served him with a notice warning against holding the march without permission.\nAuthorities closed Central Park for two days, citing \"administrative reasons\", and offered to let a small group wave the national flag and sing the anthem at another location.\nThe CJP came up with a different plan. Supporters were told to enter Connaught Place in groups of fewer than five, unfurl the flag, take a photograph and leave.\n\"Waving the Tiranga is not a crime,\" the movement declared in a social media poster, using the Hindi name for India's tricolour. It urged supporters to share their photographs online.\nSporadic protests erupted around the Central Park on Friday evening despite the detentions\nOpposition parties have also been holding protests against Kumar and the Election Commission.\nLast week, Rahul Gandhi and other opposition lawmakers were detained while attempting to march to the commissio", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "f97e062ad0e04da176c6df1c", "title": "Sun Fine Chem clears its IPO listing review, is ready to go public soon - OLED-Info", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMioAFBVV95cUxOYTNOSERJZWdVV1Z6YURBSFdYcmJOYU5FT2wyUmlhMlZMY184MTYtUXM3T25qVlJfU3FpQzVDWUl5cFNfWkxBNk9INkZvOUtHQ2RaQXZGbnlLT09yM1RtR1lBNk5EamQ4eXRhdFF1NXVZQjhza1U4SVV6a3g1LWRRT2treW5qb09uSjJiVXZ0X1c3Q1JkTXNEQzhaMmJmZ1ZF?oc=5", "source": "lane_listing_flow", "published_at": "2026-10-16T11:43:35+00:00", "text": "Sun Fine Chem clears its IPO listing review, is ready to go public soon - OLED-Info\nSun Fine Chem clears its IPO listing review, is ready to go public soon OLED-Info", "truncated": false}, {"id": "b5f9cdc9abd56dba8525e76b", "title": "Canada's retaliatory tariffs hit US products as Trump warns of escalation - ABC7 Bay Area", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiowlBVV95cUxQTlBOZ0RuSXo3QmJ1bVdWeUZNc3N5VXU0TE80bTB1TlRjMUhweXZHOTF0Z1JiSVpFM2xfaXBZaEdmbnUxSk5MT19ITGxmSExCQk03OEhj