6-10-16T11:22:57+00:00", "text": "Iambic prices $150 million IPO to fund cancer pipeline - The Pharma Letter\nIambic prices $150 million IPO to fund cancer pipeline The Pharma Letter", "truncated": false}, {"id": "df13817732684b2b2f259be9", "title": "OPEC’s Smallest Member Rewrites Oil Law to Encourage Exploration - Bloomberg.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMitgFBVV95cUxQZG1INWM0blRUaE5YcHRneDJXZk1qLVNueUplUUFaZi0zZVdLVWxuT25FZzk2MDZWSWt2QzQ5Y01xNWFqTmlTRGZZYUNBbHBHUGVyQTZDSndvRW1SQ1F0TC1lOVlUN3BLamJwMlFwMFJ4VjhOWFZJZHZmM1kyRlRHci1IbVUxUUREZXRwYmpId0MyNGZZYjMwMDRBUXBrdGpta3JzUlpfQ2s0NG5hdW1FWnJJWTladw?oc=5", "source": "publisher_bloomberg", "published_at": "2026-10-16T11:22:30+00:00", "text": "OPEC’s Smallest Member Rewrites Oil Law to Encourage Exploration - Bloomberg.com\nOPEC’s Smallest Member Rewrites Oil Law to Encourage Exploration Bloomberg.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "250bccff086c4eaaa9ad3930", "title": "Connexion Mobility Ltd announces an Equity Buyback for 139,435,481 shares, representing 19.73% of its issued share capital. - www.marketscreener.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi5gFBVV95cUxNQlBfWjdEQVJkbFJkR2VSOGtCNFBCQUY4WC04WDdkSGk5TUYwWFZXVGVyUDg0djk2T2N0MUlPVGxFV2dmbVJzVS1FT3ozQzNxNWgwYTFsMDVVQzdBUlFfRnZaTk9WdXhzaWV5M2VxMFN0Z2dLSGN2VklWZnlmT1hZZEg1Y242X00xRFpzZTYwWHl5RnV4YW5xWXYyMVVPay1LQlF6TU5hclM1YU05YUJvVGJsVHNYSEpfTy1xYkpSam5", "source": "lane_capital_return", "published_at": "2026-10-16T11:22:02+00:00", "text": "Connexion Mobility Ltd announces an Equity Buyback for 139,435,481 shares, representing 19.73% of its issued share capital. - www.marketscreener.com\nConnexion Mobility Ltd announces an Equity Buyback for 139,435,481 shares, representing 19.73% of its issued share capital. www.marketscreener.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "534cf70d2beb06aadf344763", "title": "Second-class price cap stays - PG Buzz", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiYkFVX3lxTFBxLTJMYVpRX3pxaVBaRlFzWi1jSC1tWF9tenRMQ0pZdFZWMzFNd3kwWDVmUGNPUy1xbVhTd09fblN2Szhnd1o4dVBfM2MzdHFNamhUMk00ZmZ6U2hjcTNYUC1n?oc=5", "source": "lane_price_cap", "published_at": "2026-10-16T11:22:01+00:00", "text": "Second-class price cap stays - PG Buzz\nSecond-class price cap stays PG Buzz", "truncated": false}, {"id": "4d0a741db98b48a2171aa369", "title": "India's young workers have a new colleague: AI", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/india-s-young-workers-have-a-new-colleague-ai/a-79678342?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf", "source": "rss_dw_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T11:22:00+00:00", "text": "India's young workers have a new colleague: AI\nThe use of AI is widespread among younger Indians, with a survey finding that 93% of Gen Z respondents and 95% of millennial respondents used AI in their day-to-day work\n, young workers are increasingly turning to\nto speed up routine tasks, whether researching news stories, translating copy, analyzing business data or sorting legal documents.\n, and relying on it too heavily carries risks.\nJournalist Farah Kazmi, 28, who works for a news portal in New Delhi, used AI while reporting on\nand feeding them in gated communities. She asked an AI model to find the Supreme Court's latest guidelines, earlier court rulings and\n\"Since it can take quite a lot of time to search Google for specific articles about court rulings, previous judgments or recent dog-bite cases, I asked AI to find them for me,\" Kazmi told DW.\nIt gave her a starting point for relevant articles and cases, which she then read and cross-checked herself.\nIndia's workers train AI that may replace them\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nKazmi also uses AI to translate English copy into Hindi for the Hindi news team, and Hindi quotes into English. But translations need careful checking, Kazmi said, pointing out that in one case, AI interpreted the name \"Suraj\" as \"sun,\" changing the sentence's meaning.\n\"Before using AI, I mostly relied on Google Translate. It was more literal and did not always understand the context. AI gives a much better and more context-based translation, although it still needs to be checked and corrected,\" she said.\nAI has not fundamentally changed journalism, which still involves talking to people and reporting from the field, Kazmi said.\n\"Ultimately, I see AI as a tool that helps with research and saves time,\" she said, \"but the actual verification and reporting still has to be done by the journalist.\"\nYajatt Misra, 28, business strategy and international markets manager at Kanoria Chemicals and Industries, uses AI to analyze data, summarize large volumes of information, prepare first drafts and structure ideas.\nIt also helps him turn financial and business information from different sources into management updates and presentations.\n\"Things like summarizing information, preparing first drafts, analyzing data and putting together presentations take less time now,\" Misra told DW.\nMisra said the challenge was knowing when to trust the output. AI can miss business context or get details wrong, so its numbers, logic and conclusions need checking.\n\"There is less need to spend time on repetitive tasks and more focus on analysis, problem-solving and providing useful insights to the business,\" Misra said.\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nRakshanda Deka, a 30-year-old lawyer, uses AI to sort large bundles of client documents chronologically or by theme and produce a first list of important dates. Some systems link information to the original documents so lawyers can check the source.\nAI can also help prepare standard applications and petitions using a lawyer's own format and style.\n\"If used sensibly, and without outsourcing the substantive legal thinking, AI is basically like having a sincere fresher or junior colleague,\" Deka told DW.\nBut AI can confidently produce false information — making human checking essential.\n\"AI often gives you false information dressed up very confidently as fact,\" Deka said. \"So it is imperative that you double-check everything.\"\nDeka is concerned that some young lawyers may use AI as a substitute for their own thinking. \"A lot of the youngest lawyers are beginning to use AI as a substitute for themselves,\" she said.\nThe use of AI is widespread among younger Indians, with\nfinding that 93% of Gen Z respondents and 95% of millennial respondents used artificial intelligence in their day-to-day work.\nfound that 82% of respondents aged 15 to 28 used AI tools at least weekly to learn or develop new skills.\nAI is much hyped, frequently feared, and possibly overrated\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nDimple Khubchandani, founder of recruitment firm Synoverge Consultants, told DW that employers increasingly want young professionals who can think critically, solve problems and check AI-generated work rather than simply handle basic research or drafting.\n\"AI is not necessarily eliminating entire job categories, but it is creating leaner teams and raising expectations,\" she said.\nKhubchandani said a marketing agency that once might have hired four junior copywriters could now hire one to use AI for initial drafts, leaving more time to edit, refine the tone and shape strategy.\n\"AI is raising the baseline capability expected from employees on day one,\" she said.\nTata Consultancy Services (TCS), one of India's largest IT services companies, said in its 2025-26 annual report that it had scaled up its \"Human+AI\" operating model and was embedding artificial intelligence across services and software development.\nShrijay Sheth, co-founder of LegalWiz.in, an online legal and compliance service, sees the shift across the economy.\n\"AI's impact on job profiles isn't an anticipation,\" Sheth told DW. \"It is a reality now. From basic research to strategy, operations, marketing, design and development, AI is changing how work gets done.\"\nIn software development, Sheth said, developers are spending less time writing code from scratch and more time monitoring, testing and refining AI-generated output.\n\"Business, product and problem-solving ability, coupled with skills around working with AI platforms, is valued over mere technical ability to work with any particular coding language,\" Sheth said.\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nThe question of what happens to entry-level work is becoming harder to ignore.\nA 2025 report by NITI Aayog, the Indian government's public policy think tank\n, cites data from the Center of Advanced Study in India suggesting that more than 60% of formal-sector jobs could be susceptible to automation by 2030 — particularly in IT and business-process services.\nIt also warns of slower hiring for entry-level software engineers and identifies junior quality-assurance and Level 1 support roles as vulnerable.\nWorld Bank's October 2026 India Development Update\nsays AI's long-term impact on the country's labor market remains uncertain.\nIndia could benefit from its technical workforce, IT sector and digital infrastructure, the report found, but stronger workforce skills and wider access to AI capabilities will be needed if the gains are to be broadly shared.\n\"The productivity enhancement and job loss are both real,\" Sheth said.\nDeka said lawyers would increasingly be distinguished by how rigorously they check AI's work. \"I think the differentiation is increasingly coming from the thoroughness of post-AI verification and confirmation,\" she said.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "f19edb6247275989fd95b9e7", "title": "Connexion Mobility Ltd authorizes a Buyback Plan. - www.marketscreener.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiowFBVV95cUxPVHNXSTVsOXlyclJLNWdRTlJTdFBWaVBnU3ByZzhiSWNoMk5faGVhaFUzdk1WblF1aDBzY0s0UWhTWk1sU1RKMjhC