 every condition the same,”\nsaid HHS Secretary Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.\n“Under President Trump, FDA is rethinking rare disease drug development by putting patients at the center and matching regulatory requirements to the realities of small patient populations. We will remove unnecessary barriers, embrace cutting-edge science, and accelerate promising treatments while upholding FDA’s rigorous standards.”\n“Patients and families living with rare diseases have few or no approved treatment options, and the burden of uncertainty can extend across every part of their lives,”\nsaid Acting FDA Commissioner Kyle Diamantas, J.D.\n“As we learn more about rare diseases and better understand the breadth of suffering they cause, we must ensure that America’s regulatory system remains at the forefront — prepared to respond to emerging science, anticipate the needs of patients and families, and help bring safe and effective therapies to those who need them. The FDA will fight hard for patients by building a more consistent, predictable, and innovative approach to rare disease drug development while maintaining the rigorous standards Americans expect from the FDA.”\nThe FDA has long used regulatory flexibility and developed targeted programs to support innovation in the development of drugs and biologics for rare diseases. These efforts have led to meaningful advances for patients and families. Nevertheless, with only 5% or so of rare conditions having an FDA-approved treatment, the FDA agrees with patients, drug developers, and providers that more must be done to speed the development of safe and effective treatments for rare diseases. In developing the framework, the FDA is committed to the following:\nProviding greater clarity, consistency, and predictability in regulatory decision-making through tailored guidance and transparent communication.\nEmbedding the patient’s voice both early, and at clearly defined milestones, in the drug development and review process.\nFurther leveraging external expertise to support customization of regulatory review.\nBuilding shared resources, robust training, and institutional knowledge on rare disease drug development and review.\nApplying risk-based approaches, particularly to chemistry, manufacturing, and controls review and pre-approval inspections.\nChampioning the use of cutting-edge science, novel methods and innovative statistical approaches in rare disease drug development.\nThe Initiative is led by the FDA’s Rare Disease Innovation Hub, partnering with the Office of the Commissioner, the Center for Biologics Evaluation and Research, and the Center for Drug Evaluation and Research.\n“Patients with rare diseases and their families deserve a regulatory process that addresses the uniqueness of each of their conditions while maintaining the FDA’s rigorous standards,”\nsaid the Hub’s Director, Amy Comstock Rick, J.D.\n“A bold vision to reimagine the framework for rare disease drug development that builds on key learnings and milestones is necessary to meet this moment. This new framework will enhance consistency and predictability for both sponsors and for patients.”\nFurther opportunities to provide input will follow the public meeting, including sector-specific roundtables, Requests for Information, and internal FDA feedback sessions. This feedback will inform the detailed development of a new framework, which might include potential new or revised guidance, revisions to regulations, staff training, and organizational changes.\nThe FDA, an agency within the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, protects the public health by assuring the safety, effectiveness, and security of human and veterinary drugs, vaccines and other biological products for human use, and medical devices. The agency also is responsible for the safety and security of our nation’s food supply, cosmetics, dietary supplements, radiation-emitting electronic products, and for regulating tobacco products.", "truncated": false, "