� �\u0007�T�[\n", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "aae9d72ef63697fac0cfd2d9", "title": "Operation Economic Outcast Targets Iran’s International Financial Networks and Shadow Banking Architecture", "url": "https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0660/", "source": "official_treasury", "published_at": "", "text": "Operation Economic Outcast Targets Iran’s International Financial Networks and Shadow Banking Architecture\nOperation Economic Outcast Targets Iran’s International Financial Networks and Shadow Banking Architecture\nAction Disrupts Iraqi Bank Enabling Iranian Sanctions Evasion and Cuts Off Key Foreign Currency Lifelines\n—Today, as part of Operation Economic Outcast, the U.S. Department of the Treasury disrupted Iran’s efforts to infiltrate the international financial system through an Iraqi bank that also supports Iran’s terrorist proxy militia groups. By dismantling these institutions—including offshore banks and shadow financiers that have moved hundreds of millions of dollars through shell companies and foreign‑exchange specialists across the Middle East and Asia—Treasury is severing critical revenue streams that sustain Iran’s military and proxy forces.\n“The noose is tightening around Iran’s economy, and not even Iran’s so-called allies can save it,” said\n. “The Department of the Treasury will not relent until the Iranian regime’s coffers are empty and every remaining financial lifeline sustaining its war machine has been severed.”\nIn coordination with today’s action, the U.S. Department of State is concurrently designating Cuba-based Banco Internacional de Comercio S.A. (BICSA) pursuant to Section 2(a)(i)(A) of Executive Order (E.O.) 14404 for operating in or having operated in the financial services sector of the Cuban economy. BICSA has provided services to the designated Iran-based\nThese designations, coupled with additional sanctions on Iran’s shadow banking networks, cut off some of the few remaining financial lifelines the regime can access to prop up its failing economy and fund its military. The designations also expose the regime’s deliberate use of complex offshore structures and front companies to circumvent sanctions and stabilize its economy despite mounting pressure. Taken together, today’s measures target the core financial architecture that enables Iran to evade international scrutiny and fund its destabilizing regional activities. Treasury’s action is being taken pursuant to E.O. 13902, which targets certain sectors of the Iranian economy, including Iran’s financial and petroleum sectors.\nOPERATION ECONOMIC OUTCAST IS ISOLATING THE IRANIAN REGIME\non August 24, 2026, dubbed Economic D-Day, Operation Economic Outcast is severing the remaining economic lifelines that sustain the Iranian regime. Treasury has mapped the networks, facilitators, and financial channels that Iran uses to smuggle oil, evade sanctions, and fund terror. Working with partners across the U.S. government, the European Union, the United Kingdom, Gulf partners, and others, Treasury is targeting any source of the regime’s illicit revenue, as well as its sanctions evasion schemes to move funds.\nOperation Economic Outcast significantly expanded sanctions risk for those who continue to choose to do business with Iran. Treasury warned that any entity facilitating money laundering or sanctions evasion on behalf of Iran risks being cut off from the U.S. financial system. It also expanded secondary sanctions exposure for those who continue doing business with the Iranian regime and will accelerate the pace of U.S. enforcement.\nMore information on Operation Economic Outcast is available here\nBecause Iran is already subject to comprehensive U.S. sanctions, it relies on multi-jurisdictional shadow banking networks to generate revenue and transfer funds abroad. Iran uses these shadow banking networks to launder funds, procure weapons, access U.S. dollar correspondent banking relationships, and bankroll its regional terrorist proxy groups. These networks rely on financial institutions such as the recently designated\n, and Operation Economic Outcast will continue to identify these banks and remove their access to the U.S. dollar.\nIRAQI BANK PROVIDING U.S. DOLLAR ACCESS FOR IRAN\n(IDB) provides financial facilitation services and U.S. dollar access for Iran. IDB has abused its access to the now-dissolved U.S. dollar wire auction and maintains ties to Iranian government entities and Iran-aligned militia groups (IAMGs) in Iraq. IDB was initially established by Iranian companies to support the Iranian economy and supply Iran with U.S. dollars. U.S.-designated\n, which was located next to IDB in Iraq, closed due to U.S. sanctions and transferred its capital to IDB. IDB uses Iranian capital to conduct business, then collects any profit made and sends it back to Iran in the form of U.S. dollars each month. The bank’s current leadership received ownership of IDB from a dual-Iranian Iraqi financier working for the U.S.-designated Iranian Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps–Qods Force (IRGC-QF). The financier continues to use his familial relationships to exert influence over the bank.\nIDB is being designated pursuant to E.O. 13902 for operating in the financial sector of the Iranian economy.\nIn addition to establishing correspondent banking relationships with financial institutions in allied countries, the Iranian government has authorized certain banks to operate “\n” in free trade zones. These Iranian banks have been approved by the Central Bank of Iran and the Supreme National Security Council to conduct their operations using foreign currency to aid sanctions evasion schemes. Iran-based\nis an Iranian offshore bank affiliated with Bank Mellat, while\nChabahar International Bank Overseas Company\nis an offshore bank affiliated with Bank Shahr.\nJazireh International Bank Company and Chabahar International Bank Overseas Company are being designated pursuant to E.O. 13902 for operating in the financial sector of the Iranian economy.\nMany of Iran’s key revenue generation streams are corruptly controlled by Iranian regime elites. These revenue streams include the shipping empire of\n, son of Ali Shamkhani, the late advisor to Iran’s Supreme Leader, and the vast overseas property holdings of\n, a key benefactor of Mojtaba Khamenei. One of the elites controlling Iran’s key revenue streams is Iranian national\n(Razavi), son-in-law of Iran’s hardline Paydari Front leader and one of the top oil trustees on whom the Iranian regime relies to sell oil and repatriate the revenue.\nLiving in Dubai as one of Iran’s top oil intermediaries, Razavi has worked hand-in-hand with\n, a Hizballah financier and Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps-Qods Force facilitator designated on April 15, 2026. Prior to the U.S. blockade preventing Iranian oil exports, Razavi was responsible for shipping millions of barrels of oil overseas each month on behalf of the National Iranian Oil Company and its subsidiary, Naftiran Intertrade Company. Razavi has also used his network to move the revenue generated from these oil sales through Iran’s clandestine banking system of shell companies and cash and gold couriers. These networks move funds through Hong Kong to the UAE and Türkiye where they are converted to gold and cash and then smuggled on Mahan Air flights to Iran and its terrorist proxies around the world. Razavi is being designated pursuant to E.O. 13902 for operating in the petroleum sector of the Iranian economy.\nIn addition to his overseas work on behalf of Iran, Razavi manages Iran-based companies\nTejarat Gostar Atlas Parsian Kish Company\n, the latter of which is currently developing the Farzanegan Gas Condensate Refinery in Iran’s Bushehr Province. Tejarat Gostar Atlas Parsian Kish Company and Farzanegan Energy Pars Company are being designated pursuant to E.O. 13902 for being owned or controlled by, or having acted or purported to act for or on behalf of, directly or indirectly, Razavi.\nFarab Sorough Qeshm Company (FSAQ), an affiliate of Iran’s Shahr Bank, relies on a\nto process transactions essential to the operations of Iran’s leading oil sales companies and brokers, including the National Iranian Oil Company, Triliance Petrochemical Company, and the military-controlled Sepehr Energy Jahan Nama Pars Company. FSAQ employee", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "a7f0e5264cc9366485a21fe6", "title": "Postal Service v. Konan", "url": "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/607us2r13_m64n.pdf", "source": "official_scotus", "published_at": "", "text": "Postal Service v. Konan\n>/Encrypt 100 0 R/Filter/FlateDecode/ID[<756A50B37F63CC4287CA2604999CB80D>\n]/Index[99 31]/Info 98 0 R/Length 81/Prev 814760/Root 101 0 R/Size 130/Type/XRef/W[1 3 1]>>stream\nh�bbd`\u0010``b``.\u0006��/��+�T\u0005�\\�@$� �̿\u000f$\u0019�Z�e���$�业\u0005\u0012o�\u0005�ߺ\u0015\u0019��f~\u0006�\u0002�iB�g`��\u0011 �\u0000u{\n�٪}�\u0000���A�\u0011��y\u0013��cZ�)ߗh�甬���'������jw\u0004�,OYM�K&�W�\u000e4�b\u001b�\n��H��B[;6T����0��NQ�l�=�\u0003��[U��z ��\"V[M�\n<@bP\u0001�5��kjcc��i��4n��>A�\u0003\u001a\u0007�\u0005ȣAJ*�\u000f\u0014�*6��z�*�[�\u0010���[����V���!\u0002�t��T��69��qQu���]\u0005��k�5�W\n>>>/EncryptMetadata false/Filter/Standard/Length 128/O(�y�9[f}���\u0017.e�\u000e#+���*�S|:Ö��f�M)/P -1036/R 4/StmF/StdCF/StrF/StdCF/U(�g�� �\u0018h�H��fj�&\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000)/V 4>>\n>]/ON[115 0 R]/Order[]/RBGroups[]>>/OCGs[115 0 R]>>/Outlines 85 0 R/Pages 90 0 R/Type/Catalog>>\nƐp��}�\"��7\u0015Bd��Jg�\u000e�>(pn���% T���\u0003S�r�\\�t��v�S䬑� K{�Pq,V-�ԗs\nz�`��\u000f��oL�v#�����[��|px���*x����hM90}�2�J&�w��ޱI6#J\u0000�J�~W.a]Q\n\u0004\u0013͞J\u0010���,�X��m��K�˞�W���M�\u0017�\u0015�E�W$�=��2��`�T-�9��/|$3õv]`� �4��2�\n\u000eM��Y\u0018�a3Vp�u�ve�\u0010R�\u00076r\u000f\u0004�o��!<$���&\u0017o���K�\n,�4r �U!�v��\u0013��Ck\u0010\\`�+%��\u0004�QT[ɨ&\b���A \u0019t�r\u000f��J�қ\