000f�,ׅX�u\u0003�.�\u001b�����[L�V��(\u0011h�#\u0007�\n��4��\u0013_V�0��U��;�#���\u0000F�(���X�{�CZ�5\u0019�IBA\u0004�)�S������=� 4��̦U�\u00160!��:<\n�3˔\u0019T#d6�\u0002� p�Q�9�*��\u0002I�1�\u0011d�v-7� ���˜�ۡ_�I£\n����8��Q�^0�LPF��&���M\u0010�;��\u0003)Qܫ\u001a����ɀ�yN�lA�=�����ޡ��4ɍ�����c\u0017Q�h��h.\ny���U1�� �x6\u001b۩�ϣ�R�\u001b��\u0018)", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "9f0f6cdea3d7ec63258b550a", "title": "Treasury Targets Cartel del Noreste’s Horse Racing Enterprise and Expansive Criminal Network", "url": "https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0656/", "source": "official_treasury", "published_at": "", "text": "Treasury Targets Cartel del Noreste’s Horse Racing Enterprise and Expansive Criminal Network\nTreasury Targets Cartel del Noreste’s Horse Racing Enterprise and Expansive Criminal Network\nSanctions Disrupt CDN’s Integrated Commercial Operations Supporting Violenceand Cross‑Border Corruption\n— Today, the U.S. Department of the Treasury’s Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC) sanctioned four individuals and 11 entities connected to the U.S.-designated Foreign Terrorist Organization (FTO) Cartel del Noreste (CDN). Those designated include individuals and businesses involved in quarter horse racing, construction, trucking, and the oil and gas sector, highlighting the cartel’s diverse commercial footprint. CDN’s evolution from its predecessor, Los Zetas—responsible for some of the deadliest mass‑casualty attacks in Mexican history—underscores the severity of CDN’s continued threat to the United States and the region.\n“Today’s action exposes Cartel del Noreste’s exploitation of the horse racing sector to launder illicit proceeds and finance cartel operations,” said\n. “This action marks Treasury’s most significant effort to date to disrupt Cartel del Noreste’s financial infrastructure. Treasury, in close coordination with our law enforcement partners, remains committed to protecting the U.S. financial system from misuse by violent cartels.”\nCDN’s stronghold sits directly across the border from Laredo, Texas, the busiest port of entry (POE) on the southern border, where nearly 40 percent of all commercial traffic between the United States and Mexico crosses. This deep entrenchment in cross-border industries, combined with CDN’s ability to infiltrate local governments, steer public contracts, and manipulate regional elections, undermines legitimate authority and destabilizes communities on both sides of the border.\nIn keeping with President Trump’s pledge to eliminate cartels, OFAC has prioritized targeting these and other criminal groups that threaten the welfare of Americans in close coordination with the Homeland Security Task Force (HSTF), taking nearly 40 actions against over 500 individuals and entities since the beginning of 2025. OFAC uses a network-based approach to simultaneously disrupt as many different facets of the cartels as possible, from their leadership and key organizational nodes to facilitators and enablers, such as corrupt officials, complicit family members, and ostensibly legitimate front persons.\nToday’s action reflects a unified, whole-of-government effort to ensure operational coordination and maximize impact against transnational criminal networks. OFAC conducted its investigation in parallel with the HSTF, with support from the Drug Enforcement Administration, the Federal Bureau of Investigation, U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement Homeland Security Investigations, U.S. Customs and Border Protection, including the U.S. Border Patrol, the Internal Revenue Service Criminal Investigation, the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives, as well as assistance from the Federal Bureau of Prisons and the U.S. Marshals Service. OFAC continues to work closely with the HSTF to disrupt the flow of illicit drugs and dismantle the networks, facilitators, and financial infrastructure that enable their production and distribution. Additionally, OFAC coordinated on this action and previous actions against CDN with the Government of Mexico’s financial intelligence unit, the\nThe action was taken pursuant to Executive Order (E.O.) 14059, which targets the international proliferation of illicit drugs and their means of production, and pursuant to E.O. 13224, as amended by E.O. 13886 (collectively, E.O.13224, as amended), which targets terrorists and their supporters. This is OFAC’s fourth and largest action to date targeting CDN networks during the Trump Administration. On\nOFAC designated two high-ranking members and arms traffickers of CDN. On\n, OFAC designated three high-ranking members and one associate of CDN, including famous narco-rapper El Makabelico. Earlier this year, on\nOFAC designated three entities and three associates of CDN, including Casino Centenario, a casino just miles from the U.S. border, and Juan Pablo Penilla Rodriguez, criminal advisor to CDN.\nBased in Mexico directly across from one of the busiest American border crossings, CDN is a violent terrorist cartel founded by the notorious brothers Miguel Angel “Z-40” Trevino Morales and Oscar Omar “Z-42” Trevino Morales. CDN has been involved in the trafficking of narcotics and other illicit drugs, including fentanyl, crystal methamphetamine, heroin, marijuana, and cocaine. CDN, formerly known as Los Zetas, has engaged in horrific violence over the years. This year marks the fifteenth anniversary of the August 25, 2011 deadly Los Zetas arson attack on a casino in Monterrey, Nuevo Leon, Mexico. This arson attack killed 52 people and injured dozens more, with a majority of those killed being women. This firebombing became one of the deadliest cartel attacks against civilians in Mexican history.\nZ-40 and Z-42 were arrested by Mexican authorities in 2013 and 2015, respectively, but maintained control over the cartel utilizing corrupt attorneys, including Juan Pablo Penilla Rodriguez, whom OFAC designated in April 2026. The Trevino brothers were transferred from Mexico in February 2025 and are currently facing drug trafficking, racketeering, firearms, and money laundering charges in the District Court for the District of Columbia, with a maximum sentence of life imprisonment. CDN, then known as Los Zetas, was identified by the United States as a specially designated foreign narcotics trafficker on April 15, 2009, pursuant to the Foreign Narcotics Kingpin Designation Act (Kingpin Act). On\nLos Zetas was named as a transnational criminal organization (TCO) in the annex to E.O. 13581. On\nOFAC designated CDN pursuant to E.O. 14059. On\nthe U.S. Department of State designated CDN as an FTO and as a Specially Designated Global Terrorist (SDGT).\nAMERICAN QUARTER HORSE RACING – A ZETAS TRADITION\nCDN has a long history of money laundering through the American Quarter Horse racing industry. Between 2008 and 2012, Z-40 and Z-42 bought and sold racehorses with drug money and trained and raced them in the United States. Over a 30-month period, Zetas-affiliated front persons and shell companies spent $16 million in New Mexico, Oklahoma, California, and Texas to support the operation and laundered upwards of $22 million for the cartel. In June 2012, the U.S. Government seized 455 horses at an Oklahoma horse farm, racing venues, and training farms.\nCDN’s involvement in horse racing continues to this day.\n) is a horse racetrack, restaurant, and bar located on the outskirts of Nuevo Laredo that is controlled by CDN. As the most prominent racetrack in Nuevo Laredo, La Molienda generates revenue for CDN, charging entrance fees for those racing their horses at the track as well as for spectators.\nCDN leaders and members purchase horses, including many from the United States, to race at La Molienda. La Molienda is controlled by Z-40 but is ultimately overseen by various CDN leaders and associates while Z-40 is incarcerated in the United States.\nBefore a successful bilateral U.S.-Mexico action in April 2026 suspended operations of Casino Centenario, La Molienda and Casino Centenario entered a strategic gambling partnership allowing people to bet on races at La Molienda from the premises of Casino Centenario. In coordination with OFAC’s\ndesignation of Casino Centenario and related entities, on the\nsuspended the activities of these CDN-linked entities.\nOFAC designated La Molienda pursuant to E.O. 14059, and pursuant to E.O. 13224, as amended, for being owned, controlled, or directed by, or having acted or purported to act for or on behalf of, directly or indirectly, CDN.\nINSIDE THE WINNER’S CIRCLE – TRACKSIDE ASSOCIATES OF CDN\n) is a Mexico-based businessman, horse racing enthusiast, and an associate", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "9890b5e3d361983cdeb5137a", "title": "FS Credit Opportunities Corp. v. Saba Capital Master Fund, Ltd.", "url": "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/608us2r47_m64o.pdf", "source": "official_scotus", "published_at": "", "text": "FS Credit Opportunities Corp. v. Saba Capital Master Fund, Ltd.\n>/Encrypt 2186 0 R/Filter/FlateDecode/ID[<8A9BBC34D6417142BCFBAD5CD8C21EF4><5F10E75D61326940BB2F1121947ED71B>]/Index[2185 31]/Info 2184 0 R/Length 89/Prev 340286/Root 2187 0 R/Size 2216/Type/XRef/W[1 3 1]>>stream\n;o�F��4%���r\u0017oY�i\u000fe�\u0011��v�=<<�Y�ɘ��^\u0016�R��b�=\u0005jo\u001b\u001ai$\b\u000e����)���]�)\n���\"bx�˴�sH�\u0018��:#7��Ϯ�RɁ�o�A!X�ψ���A��5��\u0007.�\u0006h�\u001a嬖,�)��#\"�\u000f��y�R|���I��қ�[�\u00077�6\u0011~��o�/�a�\u0017�*�Yp�k#\u0010\\=i\n?~>\u0015t�V����=\u0010��fh\"�\u0006�s�.^����X��a�|�r\u000e\u0019\u000f�G\n>>>/EncryptMetadata false/Filter/Standard/Length 128/O(`���xrd\u0018�\u0012�����Lkt���~\u0014r�\u0005�.�ܟ])/P -1036/R 4/StmF/StdCF/StrF/StdCF/U(��dL $C��Z�c�xs�\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000)/V 4>>\n>/Metadata 121 0 R/Names 2202 0 R/OCProperties<\n>]/ON[2203 0 R]/Order[]/RBGroups[]>>/OCGs[2203 0 R]>>/Pages 2175 0 R/StructTreeRoot 156 0 R/Type/Catalog>>\n>>