�\u001a\u0003�j�\u000fI@P��\u0000���\u0005]\u0019@�����\"\u000e�Hd\u0016���\u0007�}����T�� �IG\u000f&���a\u0002��\u0014�@i\b�7Q���|h���=t_�\u0005&�����F⤍3WT�\u000e� <��3Q�-��\u0003O�x�nrm��?�����U���g\n�jg��fp��܊fA�U�p:�>�Ư�8�\u000f�\u0000�\u0012?��D.��Λ�:\nk4S�(����\u0017p^��������\u0019\u0004Ǆ��`d�޲!�v݀I\bՏiQ\u0001\u0003q��\u000e\nٶ�[�gU��\u0007�\"�����iÃ\u0014.t/�@�&o�I\u0010�0%�̜w$\u00020F\u0006��m\\Kv�\u0000�'\n��e�|vޱ%-���<\u0016\u0011���^\u0014K\u0015��<,\u0006�Ȭ?�$I���S��ii����dx\n�l��56\u0007c��s�y骍]`�sΟ�<ж\u0017��m�\u0017�����{�{#3�y�}�\u0000�����^\u0005+��E��L��G;1\u0012�\u0014{:�t(�\n��N�)����0��l��mC9�JmH�7��k�M�R��ܬ\u000fH�Cˊ§e�J\n�o^�\"��%�_N8��v:\u0000��� ��|t�>c��]�TP�jjw�����И��x\b\u0004� !��)�m4�vR\u0005Y�p�����h|`B�\u0005fcV�dh\u0005Ύ�\u0013br� D\u0017\nJ7�@�?X�\\nF�v�\bs������j�,�@��5�ҖG����\u001bd�Ns>��}�:�T�\u0017�g4�K�i�%��-�1l�a�ئȶ�s��z\u0010C=\u000e�I�563�A\nлd�ǻ,)x\u001b��v^�8t\u000fT�dm��E��s�\u0017��tt��k���nY� ��{'X9\n<��\b��r��\u0006Y�`!��\u000e�'�T#\u0000�BFն�r�I�[�ޯ�\u0010����!�\u0007�7s4m�1�\n��UI:��wχ�U�k\u000e�K�V庘�ztC�bX�[�ŧ���k�d�9�����\nU���#�o�� �>��jnQ���_��\u0005g��FO{\u0004�������/�Q\u0010-�\u001am�C���9\u0017�����\u001b�U�\n�[Mw�N=L�z��`7��D�.\u000f��x��#\u0018N�~��*��ݸb3�\u0003N\u0014����B���#�\nX���&�\u0002����b��Gӳ2M�8� �0�a�۴�}�'�׷�\u000f\u000f4]��+4����u�\u0013��`���֓�m�o�\u001a�L��X޺*�(�V\u0011!\n���`�\u0012=�2Ə�� t�(c�\u0013���<���=����\u0000LH�\u0013o�H�!�\b�n.�", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "885fc90554507006c8825c33", "title": "Statement from U.S. Secretary of the Treasury Scott Bessent for the World Bank Development Committee and IMF International Monetary and Financial Committee", "url": "https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0659/", "source": "official_treasury", "published_at": "", "text": "Statement from U.S. Secretary of the Treasury Scott Bessent for the World Bank Development Committee and IMF International Monetary and Financial Committee\nStatement from U.S. Secretary of the Treasury Scott Bessent for the World Bank Development Committee and IMF International Monetary and Financial Committee\nThis year’s Annual Meetings of the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and World Bank provide an important opportunity to engage on current global economic challenges as well as our work with partners and the international financial institutions to support a pro-growth agenda, macroeconomic and financial stability, and poverty reduction. We thank Thailand for hosting us for the Annual Meetings.\nIn the United States, President Trump’s America First Investment Policy is promoting robust economic growth and prosperity, fiscal prudence, energy abundance, and regulatory modernization. Internationally, we are getting back to basics by forging a broad consensus on the need to promote growth as the key condition on which durable prosperity depends. As G20 President, the United States is focused on promoting strong economic growth, and has prioritized increasing productivity, addressing global imbalances, engaging with the private sector, advancing financial literacy, improving the sovereign debt architecture, and promoting financial sector innovation and regulatory modernization. When focused on their core missions, the IMF and World Bank are well-placed to help advance these important objectives.\nThe IMF is making progress at getting back to basics. Through recent policy reviews, the IMF is upgrading its policy toolkit for surveillance and lending and restoring its focus on its core responsibilities. This includes the external sector issues at the heart of the IMF’s mandate, strengthening surveillance of debt vulnerabilities in low-income countries, and making the financial sector surveillance more risk-based. Moreover, the IMF has rightly focused on strengthening program design to enhance the effectiveness of IMF-supported reform programs to durably address balance of payments challenges and restore external viability.\nOne area where this refocus must go further is in the IMF’s work on global imbalances. This especially includes strengthening its analysis and engagement on the role non-market policies and practices and exchange rates play in driving external imbalances. Policymakers around the world are having difficult discussions about how to sustain market-based systems amid distortionary policies from some economies that skew competition and hurt employment. The IMF was founded to prevent these kinds of distortive policies and negative spillovers, and must exercise its voice on this critical issue or risk being sidelined.\nWith recent major policy reviews complete, the hard work of implementation begins. The United States will hold the IMF accountable for translating these reforms into stronger surveillance, more effective lending, and more robust assessment of debt sustainability. Successfully delivering on the IMF’s core mandate will require commitment from IMF management to be an unflinching truth-teller through surveillance and to hold borrowers accountable in IMF-supported programs. That same commitment is required of the authorities undertaking painful but necessary macroeconomic adjustments and structural reforms.\nThe IMF must continue to prioritize its work and focus its resources on its core mandate. New and emerging issues warrant IMF engagement only where they are clearly macrocritical and within the Fund’s mandate, and adding new priorities should require reducing lower-value work, rather than continually expanding the Fund’s agenda such as financing provided through the Resilience and Sustainability Trust that lacks a clear linkage to balance of payments pressures and veers into development-policy lending.\nThe IMF has sufficient resources to accomplish its mission. The United States remains committed to a strong, quota-based, and adequately resourced IMF, and we continue to work to secure congressional approval for the 16\nGeneral Review of Quotas. Future quota reviews should be based on resource adequacy needs, as reflected in the recently approved Diriyah Guiding Principles on IMF Quota and Governance Reforms. Any future quota realignment should reflect members’ relative position in the world economy and be based on a new quota formula.\nAfter years of mission creep, the World Bank is beginning to refocus on its core mission of reducing poverty and raising economic growth. Over the last year, the Bank has strengthened its development agenda by further implementing its jobs initiative, removing inefficient and distortionary climate finance targets, beginning to phase out lending to certain advanced client countries that have not needed Bank support for years, taking steps to better implement its graduation policy, and devising a strategy for supporting a more diversified minerals value chain.\nThese are necessary steps in returning the Bank to its core mission, but significantly more is needed. The Bank must promote country self-reliance by deploying resources where they are most needed and impactful, graduating more advanced countries from Bank support, delivering better access to reliable, affordable, and abundant energy, and increasing competition and quality in Bank-financed procurement to improve development outcomes. And, in doing these things, the Bank must be more efficient and selective, pursuing operations with the greatest development impact, rather than simply pushing as much money as possible out the door.\nWe expect the Bank to phase out sovereign lending to countries that have long since met the graduation policy criteria so that the Bank can refocus its resources where they are most needed and impactful. Despite being long overdue, the planned phase outs of lending to China and Poland represent an important milestone for both countries. As the Bank continues to implement the graduation policy, we expect to see Graduation Progress Notes that meaningfully assess where graduation-eligible countries fall short of meeting the graduation criteria, and associated Country Partnership Frameworks focusing Bank programming on addressing those shortcomings. As the Bank enters the longest period in its history without a country graduating from lending eligibility, World Bank Management should take concrete steps to ensure that graduations resume.\nSimilarly, the International Development Association (IDA) must facilitate graduation so that it can focus its resources on the poorest countries where its investments in foundational infrastructure, as well as in reforms that promote debt sustainability and transparency, enhanced domestic resource mobilization, and strong public financial management, help countries develop and stand up to coercive and opaque lending terms from some creditors. As we discuss IDA’s resources over the next 15 months, IDA must clearly identify its accomplishments in boosting growth, and reducing poverty. We also support extending the deadline for low-income countries to subscribe to the shares allocated under the 2018 capital increases, helping them preserve and strengthen their representation and voice in the institution.\nContributing to country self-reliance also requires the Bank to focus more on the sectors most critical to development and industrialization, chief among them reliable and affordable energy. Energy abundance sparks economic abundance. We continue to support the World Bank and African Development Bank goal to extend energy access to 300 million more people in Africa. Achieving this goal will require the Bank to take a technology-neutral approach rather than restricting borrower choice, including by removing constraints on its support for natural gas and positively responding to borrower demand for gas projects. Accordingly, the World Bank Group should formally retire its prohibition on financing", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "83e34b9db61bc9cc1f9b6e90", "title": "October 1, 2026 - FDA Approves First Heart Valve Designed to Grow with Children", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-approves-first-heart-valve-designed-grow-children", "source": "official_fda_releases", "published_at": "", "text": "October 1, 2026 - FDA Approves First Heart Valve Designed to Grow with Children\nThe U.S. Food and Drug Administration today approved the Autus Size-Adjustable Valve, a surgically implanted pulmonary heart valve for pediatric patients with congenital pulmonary valve disease. This innovative device may reduce the need for repeat open-heart surgeries in children with heart defects, reflecting the Trump Administration’s commitment to bringing breakthrough medical technologies to market as quickly and safely as possible.\nThe Autus Size-Adjustable Valve is the first heart valve designed to be expanded after surgery to keep pace with a child’s growth, and the first valve approved in the U.S. to use polymeric material rather than animal-derived tissue for its inner flaps (called leaflets).\n“For far too long, children with congenital heart defects who need pulmonary valve replacement have had very limited surgical options, and those options have historically required multiple open-heart surgeries as children grow,”\nsaid Acting FDA Commissioner Kyle Diamantas, J.D.\n“Today’s landmark approval is a historic turning point and reflects the Trump Administration and the FDA’s commitment to help bring innovative, life-changing devices to the patients who need them most.”\nCongenital heart defects are among the most common types of birth defects in the United States, affecting about 1 in every 100 births. Among these conditions are pulmonary valve abnormalities — including pulmonary valve stenosis, in which the valve is narrowed, and pulmonary valve atresia, in which the valve fails to form normally — both of which restrict blood flow from the heart to the lungs. According to the\nCenters for Disease Control and Prevention\n, pulmonary valve atresia and stenosis together affect around 4,200 babies in the United States each year.\nWhile treatment can improve blood flow and help repair these congenital defects, some children may be left with significant pulmonary regurgitation, or leakage of blood back through the pulmonary valve. Over time, this can weaken or enlarge the right ventricle and create a need for pulmonary valve replacement as the child grows.\n“For children born with congenital heart disease, valve replacement timing has always been a difficult decision — wait too long and you risk harm to the child, but intervene too early and they may need multiple open-heart surgeries as they grow,”\nsaid Michelle Tarver, M.D., Ph.D., director of the FDA’s Center for Devices and Radiological Health\n. “The Autus Size-Adjustable Valve gives doctors the option of far fewer trips to the operating room, which may result in better quality of life for these children and their families.”\nThe Autus Size-Adjustable Valve can be initially implanted as small as approximately 13 millimeters in diameter — the right size for a toddler or preschool-aged child — and later widened using a small inflatable tube (balloon catheter) as the child grows, eventually reaching up to 22 millimeters, which is comparable to an adult-sized pulmonary valve.\nIn addition, the Autus Size-Adjustable Valve uses polymeric material for its leaflets — the flaps inside the valve that open and close to control blood flow. Traditional valves use tissue derived from animals, which tends to harden and break down faster in children, often requiring earlier repeat surgery. One-year follow-up data suggest the Autus valve’s synthetic leaflets hold up well over time. This is the first time the FDA has approved a valve that uses polymeric leaflet material for any indication.\nThe FDA’s approval is based on data from a clinical study, which enrolled 62 pediatric patients across 12 U.S. sites. At the six-month follow-up, the first 60 patients demonstrated acceptable hemodynamic performance, meaning blood flow through the right side of the heart was within acceptable limits, and there was no more than mild leakage back through the valve. All patients in the study had a successful procedure, with no deaths, blood clots requiring additional treatment, strokes or other clot-related complications. Three patients experienced a fracture in the valve frame and two patients had reduced movement of one of the valve leaflets (the flaps that open and close), but none of these events resulted in symptoms for the patients. Additionally, valve performance began deteriorating in two patients who started to outgrow it. Both patients had their valves successfully expanded without an invasive surgical procedure, but the experience is limited. Longer-term studies are being conducted to better understand these risks, and to collect more data to show that expansion can maintain a functional valve that may lead to fewer surgical procedures for patients.\nThe Autus Size-Adjustable Valve is the first pediatric approval to come out of the FDA’s\nTotal Product Life Cycle Advisory Program (TAP)\n, a voluntary program for device developers intended to spur more rapid development of high-quality, safe, effective and innovative medical devices for all patients. The FDA previously granted the Autus Size-Adjustable Valve\n, which is meant to expedite the development and review of devices that provide for more effective treatment or diagnosis of life-threatening or irreversibly debilitating diseases or conditions.\nThe Autus Size-Adjustable Valve was approved through the premarket approval pathway, the FDA’s most stringent type of medical device marketing application. The pathway is used for certain Class III devices, which generally pose the greatest risk. Before approving a device, the FDA reviews scientific evidence, including nonclinical and clinical data, to determine whether there is reasonable assurance that the device is safe and effective for its intended use and whether the benefits outweigh the known risks.\nEdwards Lifesciences received approval for the Autus Size-Adjustable Valve, developed by Autus Valve Technologies Inc.\nThe FDA, an agency within the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, protects the public health by assuring the safety, effectiveness, and security of human and veterinary drugs, vaccines and other biological products for human use, and medical devices. The agency also is responsible for the safety and security of our nation’s food supply, cosmetics, dietary supplements, radiation-emitting electronic products, and for regulating tobacco products.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "7e18be0b748ccb0e5da445ca", "title": "Galette v. New Jersey Transit Corp.", "url": "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/607us2r18_d18f.pdf", "source": "official_scotus", "published_at": "", "text": "Galette v. New Jersey Transit Corp.\n>/Encrypt 1525 0 R/Filter/FlateDecode/ID[<5F0469DC08EF7743895CD907D1FA58DE><9894CBECBF44B84898362A8C1640E22E>]/Index[1524 31]/Info 1523 0 R/Length 88/Prev 289596/Root 1526 0 R/Size 1555/Type/XRef/W[1 3 1]>>stream\nh�bbd`\u0010``b``�\u0002\"\u0019_��|\u0010��\u000e\"Y��HΛ ��\u0001�d�j\u0003V�\u000f&�@dK\n\u001b[�*�pQ\u0000\u001bM���\u0011u$����?��\u0005\u0017P\u0012�`?Z\u0017�;}ՠ̯m<�\"��{+_��)�B�y��^6._�\n��6��T�\u0012s_r| ��d��Ӿ��EvghSo]B�D\u0019�6�,ܲ[ty�V�8�\n5��ʎ\u0017�W��u��\u0001�\u0013f��\u0015�\u0001��F����X�S]�ϡ��=_�����e���9\u00156�s \u000fWbt4.�\u00016\u000f4�\u0015�C\u0018�Y8��\u0011\u0006���v�ׄ\u0014��1rr��:~�&�������\n>>>/EncryptMetadata false/Filter/Standard/Length 128/O(�y�9[f}���\u0017.e�\u000e#+���*�S|:Ö��f�M)/P -1036/R 4/StmF/StdCF/StrF/StdCF/U(�A�@��FlQ�Da�iR�\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000)/V 4>>\n>/Metadata 88 0 R/Names 1541 0 R/OCProperties<\n>]/ON[1542 0 R]/Order[]/RBGroups[]>>/OCGs[1542 0 R]>>/Pages 1516 0 R/StructTreeRoot 114 0 R/Type/Catalog>>\n>>>/Rotate 0/StructParents 0/Tabs/S/Type/Page>>\n\u001b&���p)W���x4��\u000e� 