d Drug Administration today announced a request for information (RFI) describing approaches the agency is considering for the design of early-phase clinical trials of ibogaine drug products and seeking public input on those approaches. The RFI seeks comments, data, and information on specific protocol elements relevant to ibogaine drug development, including dose selection and escalation, care setting, safety monitoring, eligibility criteria, stopping rules and safety oversight. This RFI marks the latest FDA action to advance President Trump’s April 2026 executive order on\nAccelerating Medical Treatments for Serious Mental Illness\n, which directed agencies to accelerate research into psychedelic medicine.\n“Patients facing serious conditions that have not responded to existing treatments deserve rigorous scientific investigation of promising new approaches,”\nsaid Michael Davis, M.D., Ph.D., Director of the FDA Center for Drug Evaluation and Research\n. “With ibogaine, there are important scientific questions as well as serious safety concerns. We are seeking high-quality data and input that can help inform clinical research while putting patient safety first.”\nThe FDA is seeking comments, data and information in four primary areas:\n, including appropriate patient populations;\n, including the proposed use of small, sequential dose-ascending groups and a starting dose justified by available data and not exceeding 10 mg/kg;\n, including monitoring for cardiac and neurologic adverse events and other safeguards for clinical trials; and\n, including informed consent and appropriate independent safety oversight.\nThe FDA recognizes the seriousness of the conditions and unmet needs that patients seeking ibogaine treatments face. The information received through this RFI may help the agency better understand the potential uses, benefits and risks of ibogaine drug products and inform approaches to drug development.\nThe FDA has been working across the federal government to support the responsible development of ibogaine drug products through careful evidence-based processes. In July 2026, the FDA issued its finalized guidance for industry,\nPsychedelic Drugs: Considerations for Clinical Investigations\n, which provides general considerations for sponsors developing psychedelic drugs. The FDA has also allowed an\nearly-phase clinical study of noribogaine hydrochloride\n, a derivative of ibogaine, to proceed under an Investigational New Drug application as a potential treatment for alcohol use disorder.\nAs part of the whole-of-government approach to advance psychedelic drug development, HHS, through the\nAdvanced Research Projects Agency for Health (ARPA-H)\n, is funding a program to collect safety and efficacy data through early-phase clinical trials, and the\n(NIDA) is funding research on ibogaine for the potential treatment of opioid use disorder (OUD). Based on available data and the seriousness of the conditions, HHS is prioritizing two initial populations for federally supported research: adults with OUD and adults with post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD).\nThe public will have 45 days following publication in the Federal Register to submit comments, data or information to the docket. Stakeholders are encouraged to submit comments electronically at\n. Written/paper submissions may also be sent to: Dockets Management Staff (HFA-305), Food and Drug Administration, 5630 Fishers Lane, Rm. 1061, Rockville, MD 20852.\nThe comment period closes Friday, November 20, 2026. Late comments will not be considered.\nThe FDA, an agency within the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, protects the public health by assuring the safety, effectiveness, and security of human and veterinary drugs, vaccines and other biological products for human use, and medical devices. The agency also is responsible for the safety and security of our nation’s food supply, cosmetics, dietary supplements, radiation-emitting electronic products, and for regulating tobacco products.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "6129483e4257e705dcf4a4ea", "title": "October 14, 2026 - FDA Approves First Treatment for Certain People With BCG-Unresponsive Bladder Cancer", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-approves-first-treatment-certain-people-bcg-unresponsive-bladder-cancer", "source": "official_fda_releases", "published_at": "", "text": "October 14, 2026 - FDA Approves First Treatment for Certain People With BCG-Unresponsive Bladder Cancer\nThe U.S. Food and Drug Administration today expanded the approved use of Anktiva (nogapendekin alfa inbakicept-pmln), given with Bacillus Calmette-Guérin (BCG), to include adults with high-grade papillary-only bladder cancer that has not spread into the bladder muscle and has not responded to BCG treatment. Anktiva is the first FDA-approved treatment specifically for patients with BCG-unresponsive, high-grade papillary-only disease.\nThe approval marks a new FDA approach to evaluating treatments for BCG-unresponsive non-muscle invasive bladder cancer (NMIBC). The agency based its decision on evidence supporting an earlier approval of Anktiva for adults with another form of NMIBC, carcinoma in situ (CIS), with or without papillary tumors, and scientific similarities between CIS and high-grade papillary-only disease.\n“We listen to patients, clinicians, and researchers, and we act when the science supports a new approach,”\nsaid R. Angelo de Claro, M.D., Director of the FDA’s Oncology Center of Excellence\n. “For Anktiva, our review of the similarities between these cancers allowed us to build on the results in patients with CIS and expand treatment options for people with high-grade papillary tumors alone. This approach may also help guide the development of future treatments.”\nNMIBC is bladder cancer that has not grown into the bladder’s muscle layer and can include CIS and papillary tumors. CIS forms flat patches along the bladder’s lining, while papillary tumors grow from the lining into the bladder as small, finger-like projections. CIS is always high-grade, while papillary tumors can be low-grade or high-grade. High-grade cancers are more likely to grow and spread than low-grade cancers.\nTreatment for high-risk NMIBC often starts with BCG, which activates the immune system inside the bladder to fight cancer. Sometimes, however, the cancer persists or returns. Adding Anktiva gives the immune system another way to fight the cancer, and the combination can help some people whose cancer was not controlled by BCG alone.\nAnktiva’s CIS approval was supported by a trial (\n) that evaluated the treatment’s effectiveness in 77 adults, some of whom also had papillary tumors. Sixty-two percent achieved a complete response, meaning no high-grade cancer was detected during follow-up assessments, and of those, 58% maintained that response for at least 12 months and 40% for at least 24 months.\nFor today’s expanded approval, the FDA considered these results alongside clinical and laboratory evidence showing that CIS and high-grade papillary tumors share important features. They often occur together, can be difficult to distinguish under a microscope and carry a high risk of returning or progressing when BCG treatment no longer works. These similarities supported applying the evidence of Anktiva’s effectiveness in people with CIS to those with high-grade papillary-only disease.\nTo help developers apply this approach, the FDA is working toward finalizing its draft guidance, “\nBacillus Calmette-Guérin-Unresponsive Nonmuscle Invasive Bladder Cancer: Developing Drugs and Biological Products for Treatment\n.” The guidance is intended to include discussion of using evidence from studies in patients with CIS to support approval of treatments for BCG-unresponsive, high-grade papillary-only NMIBC.\nAnktiva is given with BCG directly into the bladder through a catheter. It is given once a week for six weeks, with additional treatment based on how the cancer responds. Treatment may continue for up to 37 months. It should be stopped if the cancer persists after a second six-week course, returns or progresses, or side effects become unacceptable.\nThe most common side effects and laboratory abnormalities included painful or frequent urination, an urgent need to urinate, blood in the urine, urinary tract infections, muscle or joint pain, chills, fever, and increased blood levels of creatinine and potassium. Anktiva’s prescribing information warns that delaying surgery to remove all or part of the bladder can allow the cancer to spread to other parts of the body, which can be fatal.\nThe approval was granted to Altor BioScience, LLC.\nThe FDA, an agency within the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, protects the public health by assuring the safety, effectiveness, and security of human and veterinary drugs, vaccines and other biological products for human use, and medical devices. The agency also is responsible for the safety and security of our nation’s food supply, cosmetics, dietary supplements, radiation-emitting electronic products, and for regulating tobacco products.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "5fa83718a533f4b72f8b15d6", "title": "Pitts v. Mississippi", "url": "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/607us1r01_kjfm.pdf", "source": "official_scotus", "published_at": "", "text": "Pitts v. Mississippi\n>/Encrypt 404 0 R/Filter/FlateDecode/ID[<6CCC2CC3F4762244BEA71712BC779E9D><9837ABBADEDC114DAED939891EA36F67>]/Index[403 31]/Info 402 0 R/Length 87/Prev 223900/Root 405 0 R/Size 434/Type/XRef/W[1 3 1]>>stream\nh�bbd`\u0010``b``�\u0003��/�d*��\u0005���A$�U\u0010Yp H2N�\u0006˲�I \u0010ٚ\n\u0012HP�\u000e99)&:�S�\u0016I&\b��\u0016��ӈ�&=#U\";����֗<9�It���T �\u000f��\n>>>/EncryptMetadata false/Filter/Standard/Length 128/O(/�\u0014�9{�g�Ptq�A-u1�B��Y\u0001ֵ;���L@\u0016)/P -1036/R 4/StmF/StdCF/StrF/StdCF/U(b�]KS�mЀ:�\n>/Metadata 32 0 R/Names 420 0 R/OCProperties<