3b4e3edeaac2591d0d109", "title": "Huhtamaki Extends Maturity Of €450 Million Credit Facility - TradingView", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMixgFBVV95cUxOb3kzTVRRcWJTWjRFcjBtU0pWRm9QTmlDcjdSTElzT1hFVGpORlJhTDlZUnktMXUzeDFvY1Jmby0ybW9wOXp3VGdjVHpSVVBEVl9NMTlveDlCNXZVSDBEX0JaVVEtcGczTnNrOG40WE92OHlfSTF3clFNd0hpeHhCejh4MmNMS2FfNm5MM2x6NWZSdGpfVndVNFNFdE1WUVJseGlJNGp5V2pQdHgzdV9RTHlTTHZiUXJkMUR1ZmdTS1Z", "source": "lane_credit_funding", "published_at": "2026-10-16T09:07:31+00:00", "text": "Huhtamaki Extends Maturity Of €450 Million Credit Facility - TradingView\nHuhtamaki Extends Maturity Of €450 Million Credit Facility TradingView", "truncated": false}, {"id": "ab73513bb6c66f301718cc42", "title": "EU promotes sovereign, trusted AI as it struggles to catch US and China", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/eu-promotes-sovereign-trusted-ai-as-it-struggles-to-catch-us-and-china/a-79667770?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-rdf", "source": "rss_dw_business", "published_at": "2026-10-16T09:07:00+00:00", "text": "EU promotes sovereign, trusted AI as it struggles to catch US and China\nThough Europe is strong in AI research, regulation, supercomputing initiatives and semiconductor equipment, it lags behind in frontier-model development\ndiscipline, there would only be two serious contenders for gold on current trajectories: the\n. The European Union, as has been the case in many areas of cutting-edge\nFormer European Central Bank President Mario Draghi, whose\n2024 report highlighted the EU's desperate need\nto be more innovative to become more competitive, said recently that AI is \"perhaps the most promising\" route the EU has to raise growth today. However, he decried the fact that the European Union controls little of the value chain.\n\"Being cut off from AI, once the economy runs on it, would be more like being cut off from the US financial system,\" he wrote in an opinion piece for the\nDraghi's spirit was on full display in Dublin on Wednesday at an international AI summit the Irish government convened as part of its six-month stint hosting the rotating EU presidency.\nThe summit's motto, \"Harnessing AI to Revolutionize Europe's Competitiveness,\" might as well have been dreamed up by the Italian former prime minister.\nHarnessing AI has become a lot more complicated.\nin June when 1,200 agents hacked an online platform, to an Anthropic-designed agent sending a fake tip to US police over an unsolved murder, have caused alarm.\nMany from within the tech industry, including Microsoft founder Bill Gates and the leading AI frontier labs themselves, have called for a\nin order for safety measures to catch up.\nTrump announces creation of new 'AI Force'\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nYet positivity was largely the name of the game at the summit, with tech and AI industry leaders joining Irish and EU politicians to declare AI's transformative economic and societal potential — while also emphasizing the need for strong regulation and safety standards.\nIrish Prime Minister Micheal Martin made it clear that Ireland was all in on AI. He echoed\n's recent assertion that the technology represented a \"tipping point.\" He also repeatedly highlighted what he sees as the bloc's commitment to safety.\n\"We are determined to capture the dividend by mitigating the risks,\" Martin told the event. \"In Europe, we have charted our path ... balancing innovation and opportunity with a trustworthy, human-centered approach to AI.\"\nMeanwhile, Henna Virkkunen, the European Commission executive vice-president for tech sovereignty, security and democracy, revealed at the event that the EU had begun its first enforcement actions\n, requesting information around security and safety from advanced AI model providers.\nHowever, other topics that concern AI's many critics, from those fearing the massive\nto others warning about possible AI-related job losses, were largely absent from the discussions.\nProtestor Alexandra Day says not enough attention is paid to AI risks\nOutside the venue, a group of protesters led by People Before Profit, a left-wing Irish political party, criticized the summit for not focusing on these issues.\n\"There is a deliberate blurring of the line here with the type of AI that is being promoted,\" Alexandra Day, one of the protest organizers, told DW.\n\"It is really being pushed on workers with very little consent. There's no right for workers to opt out of using AI now that it's coming into the workplace.\"\nA sense of urgency was palpable from the speakers inside the summit. Executives from some of the world's biggest AI and tech firms talked up Europe's potential. Yet the EU evidently has plenty of ground to catch up on in terms of AI development.\nDespite ramping up its data center capacity, it still lags far behind the US and China.\nIt hosts 5% of the world's AI computing capacity\nTrump proclaims US 'golden age' of science\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nThe EU is conspicuously absent from lists of the leading AI frontier labs, with only French company Mistral coming close to rivaling the top labs. Its production of leading-edge chips is also nowhere near that of the top US or Taiwanese producers.\nAs part of its plan to make ground on the US and China, the Irish government, in its role as EU presidency host, presented a policy document at the event.\nThe so-called \"Dublin Declaration\" outlines political commitments to strengthen Europe's AI capabilities, including infrastructure investment and financial backing for European AI companies.\nIn keeping with Draghi's calls for Europe to develop its own AI sovereignty, particularly in supply chains and infrastructure, the declaration suggests that European firms reliant on non-EU cloud and computing services may face additional compliance pressures.\nEddie Wilson, CEO of European airline Ryanair, doubts the EU's capacity to develop AI well\nCan Europe balance innovation and regulation?\nHowever, not everyone is convinced by the EU's capacity to\neither ramp up AI capacity or develop effective regulation.\nEddie Wilson, CEO of European airline Ryanair, said he doubted Brussels could regulate AI well without stifling innovation.\n\"The last thing you want is people regulating this industry who don't know anything about it,\" he told DW. \"That's generally what happens in Brussels.\"\nRyanair recently made a major deal with Google to use its cloud data system and its AI platform Gemini Enterprise. It's precisely the kind of collaboration with US AI supply chains that the EU says it wants companies to move away from.\n\"I worry for Europe because these tech companies need to move at speed,\" said Wilson. He believes the environment in the US is more amenable to innovation.\n\"If they're not going to do it here, they're going to do it elsewhere. They're going to do it in China and they're going to do it in the US.\"", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "8ec3861dcee43ef127407b31", "title": "Gold Advances as Treasury Yield Decline and Dollar Weakness Offset Interest Rate Concerns - Yahoo Finance UK", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMijgFBVV95cUxOdUxzVVRVRFJTQ1BLZEFPaGs4VWhIdFNWdXBNRUpOVlUwdWFHQzExYUpOQWlzbWJIN19kaTRvWEhJeGpXUFdPZHdIVXhFWDVJQzNKSjI1RlpHVXZzbDFIWjhJYktLWTktUzZ6SmRORGhXaFJ6Nno4WnlrWWFpNjY3ZUxKd3JVVkIxNVpjZnJB?oc=5", "source": "topic_government", "published_at": "2026-10-16T09:06:42+00:00", "text": "Gold Advances as Treasury Yield Decline and Dollar Weakness Offset Interest Rate Concerns - Yahoo Finance UK\nGold Advances as Treasury Yield Decline and Dollar Weakness Offset Interest Rate Concerns Yahoo Finance UK", "truncated": false}, {"id": "9b3156ea50446238092d502e", "title": "There's a 35% Chance That the Fed Hikes Rates at Least 4 Times by September 2027, Which Can Decimate the AI-Driven Bull Market - The Motley Fool", "url": "https://news.googl