ccusing US of ‘betraying’ Ukraine The Independent Ukraine Strikes Russian Refinery After Moscow Breaks Trump’s Energy Ceasefire WSJ Exclusive | Vladimir Putin 'giving the middle finger' to Trump by breaking energy cease-fire: GOP congressman New York Post Russia talks up refinery attack as Trump blames Ukraine for expensive fuel Reuters ‘We’re done’: Ukraine resumes strikes on Russian oil refineries as anger at Trump mounts NBC News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "cb2135fb9f057b8c81347552", "title": "Starlink: How Elon Musk's company influences geopolitics", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/starlink-how-elon-musk-s-company-influences-geopolitics/a-75963477?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf", "source": "rss_dw_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T05:51:00+00:00", "text": "Starlink: How Elon Musk's company influences geopolitics\nStarlink satellites have become vital means of communication in war zones\naccess is a major problem in many regions of the world today.\nwas initially developed by the American tech billionaire\nto solve this problem. It was designed as a way of quickly getting the internet into regions all over the world where this was either impossible to do by conventional methods, or so expensive that it wasn't worth attempting.\nStarlink and SpaceX founder, controversial billionaire Elon Musk\nImage: Julia Demaree Nikhinson/AP Photo/dpa/picture alliance\nSince then, Starlink's field of application has widened significantly. It's used as a means of communication in disaster zones, but also by armies or protest movements, for example in regions where the existing internet has been destroyed by enemy fire or shut down by authoritarian governments.\nBut there is a downside: Ukraine's military, for example, is heavily dependent on Musk's satellite system. This has become especially apparent as the service faces external disruption.\nStarlink, which is operated by Musk's SpaceX company, is a satellite-based system consisting of 10,000 small\nlocated around the world. They transfer data packages via laser links and do not require a constant, direct visual link to a ground station.\nUnlike other internet satellites, which orbit at around 36,000 kilometers (22,000 miles) above the Earth, Starlink satellites are only about 550 kilometers up. This means they can transmit internet signals much faster than the others.\nAll users need is a small receiver terminal with an electronically controlled antenna, which automatically steers toward the satellites as they pass overhead. The terminals receive signals from these satellites and transmit them to a nearby router, which supplies the broadband internet connection.\nStarlink receivers smuggled into Iran enabled the opposition to organize despite the internet shutdown\nImage: Middle East Images/picture alliance\nWhere has Starlink already been deployed?\nin the war zone. The Ukrainian armed forces use Starlink to coordinate units, operate drones and control unmanned ground systems. In the civilian sector, the system is used by hospitals, emergency services, energy suppliers and the railway network.\nUkraine offers a particularly vivid example of how heavily its military capabilities now depend on Starlink.\nIn mid-October, Ukrainian army units reported disruptions to data transmission, which are said to have severely affected the control of combat, interceptor- and reconnaissance drones. Elon Musk said that restrictions on the speed and use of Starlink are determined by the US military and ultimately the US president. At this stage, it remains unclear who ordered the restrictions.\nerupted all across the country against the regime in Tehran. The government's\n. Reportedly, thousands of Starlink terminals were smuggled into\nand sold on the black market. This enabled the protest movement to go on coordinating demonstrations against the regime, using Starlink to circumvent state censorship of the internet.\nHow reliable is Starlink as a wartime communications line?\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nThe country's authorities are cracking down on the satellite service and its users. Mohammad Amin Aghamiri, the secretary of the Supreme Council of Cyberspace, described Starlink as a hostile \"tool of war\" and said the regime had means at its disposal to disrupt satellite connections.\nStarlink is officially banned in Iran. Anyone using the service faces a prison sentence of six months to two years. If users are also charged with endangering national security or espionage, the offence can even carry the death penalty.\nIn the Sudanese civil war, Starlink is used primarily by the\n(RSF), a paramilitary group fighting against the internationally-recognized government. Hundreds of devices were smuggled into the country via the United Arab Emirates, and there is now a thriving black market for Starlink terminals in\n. The RSF use them to communicate and coordinate with their militias, while the Sudanese Armed Forces are trying to block both the import and use of the terminals.\nHere too, Starlink has been used in the past to sidestep information blackouts and secure access to the internet. For a long time, the service was only available unofficially, by buying kits from local dealers. Following the US intervention in early January 2026, during which then-head of state,\n, was taken prisoner, Musk offered the people of\na month's free broadband internet service via Starlink.\nStarlink has been used for humanitarian purposes in the devastated\nsince July 2024. Aid organizations and a field hospital use it to facilitate telemedical assistance and logistical coordination. Access for civilians remains limited because of Israeli security concerns.\nStarlink is often the only way to access the internet in conflict zones. This gives Musk and his operating company\ntremendous influence. Previously, for example, Musk refused to activate Starlink in Ukraine to enable an attack\n. US President Donald Trump has also threatened to switch off the satellites in Ukraine, as a way of forcing Kyiv to make concessions in peace negotiations.\nStarlink has provided vital communications for Ukrainian soldiers since the start of the 2022 Russian invasion\nImage: Alexander Polegenko/picture alliance\nThere has also been criticism from an ecological perspective. The Starlink satellites have a lifespan of less than five years. Every day, on average, one or two of them burn up in the Earth's atmosphere. This releases aluminum oxide, which could affect the ozone layer, contributing to global warming. In addition, all those thousands of satellites reflect the sunlight,\n, and this may also have repercussions for the world's animals.\nIn an effort to break Starlink's monopoly, other companies, especially in Europe, are already working on alternatives. At present, the biggest private competitor to SpaceX is a company called Eutelsat. With a fleet of over 600 satellites, its OneWeb service is far smaller, focusing on business customers and maritime use.\nBut Eutelsat is also involved in the development of IRIS2 — an acronym for Infrastructure for Resilience, Interconnectivity and Security by Satellite, an ambitious EU project to establish a highly secure satellite network. The aim is to make Europe\n, but IRIS2 won't be fully operational before 2032.\nElon Musk also has competition inside the US from another billionaire, Jeff Bezos, whose company Amazon is currently working on Amazon Leo, known until recently as Project Kuiper. This is establishing a network of more than 3,200 new low-Earth-orbit satellites. Amazon plans to roll out the service later this year in the US, Canada, France, Britain and Germany.\nChinese competition for Starlink and SpaceX: The launch of a Chinese rocket carrying astronauts to a space station in April 2025\nImage: Andy Wong/AP Photo/dpa/picture alliance\n. China is also refining two satellite communications systems of its own. Guowang, a state project conceived primarily for national and military purposes, will consist of up to 13,000 satellites, while another project, Qianfan, with an estimated 12,000 satellites, is more commercially oriented and aims to provide services to newly industrialized and developing countries. The first mass starts for both of these projects are planned for this year.\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nThis article has been translated from German.\nEditor's note: This piece was originally published on February 14, 2026. It was updated on October 15, 2026, to reflect recent developments.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "6166a94e3c83e245b169ebb0", "title": "First Phosphate Corp. Reports Earnings Results for the Second Quarter and Six Months Ended August 31, 2026 - www.marketscreener.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi5wFBVV95cUxPZFp1bVkyczZVZUxYVFlsQURZU1RPckoxWFVaRllnY3pPMWR4QmFSa2F1MDBhQ0FpZ3VWTmxCbm4tTnBKa3lfV1NsazF5S2VzMWhaSHIySTFLdDIxY0h0cURCUFZ6YW9jNy0zanU3cnlwLUZUTHEyeTRLMDM3S0pqaHRkT1dOV1V3LTdXS3hsZkFpb2xOaFNmRlNIREozR0c3WHY1TUl6WC1sSlJpWVVjX1BScnlhdWxqNmtOd29Wazd", "source": "lane_print_vs_priced", "published_at": "2026-10-16T05:51:00+00:00", "text": "First Phosphate Corp. Reports Earnings Results for the Second Quarter and Six Months Ended August 31, 2026 - www.marketscreener.com\nFirst Phosphate Corp. Reports Earnings Results for the Second Quarter and Six Months Ended August 31, 2026 www.marketscreener.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "9dcf7befd758a9b999694cca", "title": "HIMARAYA Co.,Ltd.'s Equity Buyback announced on October 15, 2026, has closed with 982,700 shares, representing 8.03% for ¥837.26 million. - www.marketscreener.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi4gFBVV95cUxNMC1NZkNpYmpWMXdoM1pKaTRIekFyVFlveEU2bUhtcXlUWlE1a1FCb3cyNlptamw1dkt6ajNTX290NE5YR1U4Ym9JQ05TNnRhdk1zazkzVkZ0MHppMkhLZV94dGtIczB5MFVzY0NxWmtGTnBsTVE4d1NtQ25hUFpWT3NYOEFTSXEydERxLU43VXNlTElRN3QwemJ1Z0JlTHZkdWxw