", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMivgFBVV95cUxPc20yUGdSWHhMS2tVRU1fWU9KWFZfbFlzQmRTQ3pHV3k5bVgxaHRZaFNPRUlKTmY2dlNBOEJyR3JYeDJ6cFZoUzBhOXAzb3ZzbkRJZXVGOS1zVmYyXzhvTDB5ZXgxbjRReW91MThYUGh6VlFPODZLZFAwQnBBMWJ3MF9Hd3JhRGxVcFctUGMwRnRVNmRvMHpSeEF5TWF2ZGtFZFgtZjdxZUJOcTN0U0h5cHd1bWd1NXlqeTlBdkRB?oc", "source": "lane_listing_flow", "published_at": "2026-10-16T05:00:26+00:00", "text": "Shenwan Hongyuan Completes 1.5-Billion-Yuan Bond Repayment, Delisting - www.marketscreener.com\nShenwan Hongyuan Completes 1.5-Billion-Yuan Bond Repayment, Delisting www.marketscreener.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "bb3d54ed3ae76faeb7d9a5c5", "title": "new fda approved weight loss drug 2022 - BusinessWorld Online", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihAFBVV95cUxNYWhLeXJlX3FrTVE1RnhwUzlnTzNncDhheW5wU2V2YThsdlpfRkpkdzNMbHhOLUUtZUJ1Q2l0VjU0UFkzbF9xN2RSeWl2QjJaS3g0dWpHS0sxOUF1dFQ1TTBjQzhuUjkydnllZFRya3h1ZTRIZ2NEZ2N3dGpLNGU0RTBra2w?oc=5", "source": "topic_fda", "published_at": "2026-10-16T05:00:19+00:00", "text": "new fda approved weight loss drug 2022 - BusinessWorld Online\nnew fda approved weight loss drug 2022 BusinessWorld Online", "truncated": false}, {"id": "a06797f3d253aec3882ed218", "title": "Ship attack reports near Hormuz reach record high as oil exports slow", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmde8dpg8g0no?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-16T05:00:17+00:00", "text": "Ship attack reports near Hormuz reach record high as oil exports slow\nThe number of ships that have reported being attacked in and around the Strait of Hormuz has hit a record high, BBC Verify analysis of data from a UK maritime agency shows.\nTwelve vessels were attacked between 5 and 11 October - the largest number of weekly strikes reported to the UK's Maritime Trade Operations (UKMTO) agency since the US-Israeli war with Iran began. The previous week saw 10 ships reporting attacks.\nSix of the vessels were struck last Sunday, according to the UKMTO - a one-day high. So far this week, there have been four reported attacks.\nAs the number of strikes has risen, the recovery in oil shipments through the strait seen last month has slowed, industry data suggests.\nBBC Verify collated incident reports published by the UKMTO on its website and created a timeline based on the first date cited in each report. This is approximate because some incidents use the date of the attack and some the date on which it was reported.\nExperts say Tehran has been attacking ships in an effort to assert greater control over the waterway as the US military has increased the number of tankers it is shepherding through the south of the strait to evade Iranian strikes.\nIran is also carrying out more attacks on vessels further away from the Strait of Hormuz, according to experts.\nThe US military's Central Command, which oversees operations in the Middle East, posted on social media that despite the attacks \"commercial traffic continues to flow through the Strait of Hormuz with co-ordinated protection from US forces and regional partners\".\nTwo crew missing after one of Sunday's attacks\nThe aftermath of one of Sunday's six reported attacks was captured in verified footage showing the Nicaragua-flagged tanker Petro Dolphin on fire, as filmed by the crew on a smaller boat nearby.\nThe vessel was struck by a drone close to the Sohar Anchorage, Oman, more than 250km (150 miles) from the strait, according to the maritime intelligence firm Vanguard.\nEleven Indian seafarers were rescued but two are still missing, the Forward Seamen's Union of India said.\nAttacks have continued this week with the container ship GFS Pride sinking after a strike by an unknown projectile in the strait on Tuesday. Twenty-one crew were safely evacuated.\nIran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) published footage of what appeared to be the same ship sinking. It said the vessel was carrying \"2,500 containers of contraband\" and had taken an \"unauthorised route\".\nMiddle East oil exports have slowed as the number of ship strikes has risen.\nAbout 12.6 million barrels per day went through the strait on 13 October, below the 15 million reported at the end of September, data from trade intelligence firm Kpler shows. This compares with a pre-war baseline of some 17 million barrels per day.\nThe recent increase in attacks comes as US Navy data indicates the number of vessels given assistance to navigate the Strait of Hormuz during the first 10 days of October was up 85% compared with the same period in August.\nMost of the oil being shipped from Gulf Arab states recently has been carried using a \"shuttle\" system where a fleet of tankers load up and sail through the strait before transferring their cargo to larger tankers in the Gulf of Oman.\nThese shuttle ships use a route close to Oman's coastline. Iran says this is \"unauthorised\" and insist vessels take an alternative route closer to its own shore.\nSo-called assisted transits were a key factor in helping drive oil flows from the Middle East back towards pre-war levels in September. This does not include Iranian exports which have completely stalled as a result of the US blockade of Iranian ports and ships.\nMartin Kelly, CEO of Orbital Risk Intelligence, said Iran has \"evolved its strategy\" in response to this loosening of its control over the strait and has been carrying out \"near daily or multiple attacks every single day\".\n\"The aim here is to stop the ships going through and to deter others,\" he added.\nThe IRGC's navy has said strikes would not be \"limited to the Strait of Hormuz\" and later announced a \"new deployment formation\" across the region, according to state media.\nMartin Kelly told BBC Verify that Iran is widening its attacks and targeting ships near the coast of Gulf Arab states and in the Gulf of Oman.\nA tanker was struck by multiple projectiles north of Qatar on Thursday 8 October, more than 500km (310 miles) from the strait, resulting in multiple reported casualties, according to Vanguard.\nAnother tanker was attacked close to the UAE town of Al-Jazirah al-Hamra on Friday 9 October, Vanguard said.\nVerified footage later showed crew members running across the deck and putting on lifejackets as a large cloud of black smoke rose over the ship.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "056788852595e291f70781db", "title": "The IPO drought is driving private equity-owned companies to the debt market to pay investors - CNBC", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMie0FVX3lxTFBvM2hpT0JHd3RwSkh3d1BISWRVVHFYd0FEa1MxV0NzZjBKZlhVR3ktOWQ5VzhTMk40UUQ0d3prWklheEpUOWMwWWVJa25NclVpM1NWMlg2YUFSOU9IN3o2b3I3NXVVTHZZLWpuS0hQXzBPOGpXME1yejVna9IBgAFBVV95cUxPUU9TRWVHc3ZJam9DbHk1N2pfdDE0aHN6Q3Fic0FKTV9ERE5hUTlCV2hBcWdBbWhGZ0RzaUJMZ3pNMGw", "source": "rss_google_markets", "published_at": "2026-10-16T05:00:01+00:00", "text": "The IPO drought is driving private equity-owned companies to the debt market to pay investors - CNBC\nThe IPO drought is driving private equity-owned companies to the debt market to pay investors CNBC", "truncated": false}, {"id": "f6c2dc20e111399886a84ed3", "title": "FDA Approves Genentech’s Enspryng, a First-in-Class IL-6R Inhibitor, as the First and Only At-Home Subcutaneous Treatment Option for Thyroid Eye Disease (TED) - Business Wire", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMitAJBVV95cUxQc3dSSU0xZlo3WUMxSUY2X3AyUk45aUR1NFVJS0I5TjdYTXpVd2lvWlhsVXdLX3lLSFh2Q1E3VXp2RHJhQ3hpT3NjVVVqY2ZuLXhLdWl1UFFtSE5wanpfRlhMS2hGUzJ5b0FNLWtjeloxa3k5QndTNDNuTTRTb2YxUlZKa2FWRmxoQzFxQWZCblJYYXVyMzlSRm5LS3Zwa3cwdU1yT0ROb3hCNTEtVmZVWjRqS0pfanE2RkYxeUNaeE9", "source": "topic_fda", "published_at": "2026-10-16T05:00:00+00:00", "text": "FDA Approves Genentech’s Enspryng, a First-in-Class IL-6R Inhibitor, as the First and Only At-Home Subcutaneous Treatment Option for Thyroid Eye Disease (TED) - Business Wire\nFDA Approves Genentech’s Enspryng, a First-in-Class IL-6R Inhib