, "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMivgFBVV95cUxPcloxNDFRb2FiV001QkJWY2toazdmcGRXR3lFUURyVFVlZmVUb3BXbWN0dlJTVGN2UjdzRFR6Vmt4ZFl5OVBhMTFGd0QtVEp6T2MwUTh6XzRFZHJsZGt5bFFYX1NCdWZGX2dtd2cydlNyaVVoem01enQ4RmFXNGhYU1V3SFdfSzh4MlNDQ0ZPd2pIUG9mcVdKTVNqWDRqME9hS1ZFSEpPeG5hWVROalhZemdGR0RvU05zdmNhdVJR?oc", "source": "lane_credit_funding", "published_at": "2026-10-15T23:25:43+00:00", "text": "Transocean Extends Credit Facility Maturity, Raises Commitments to $750 Million - Moomoo\nTransocean Extends Credit Facility Maturity, Raises Commitments to $750 Million Moomoo", "truncated": false}, {"id": "f355546350ee2ece1a7187d6", "title": "Tear gas, fires and clashes as anti-government unrest escalates in Albania, in photos - AP News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi0gFBVV95cUxPckplS2dfTWhMOXFuUDBzalk1VVl6ellQbUgwT2FBMWw0cktneXZZQTJ6b3VyNkJGS0xYT01xTUx0Tll2UEFIMHZHOFJ3U3B3RzN2VnV3VkN0c3FoVjYtTWo3cEs1VjJ3QWRocVhhVFRja2QyOURtUnQxT2gwcFhJY1Q3RExJNlNwX2hwQS1Rb0hWMWVFRHlKODgzQ183aVBveWsteVRPQU8tNGU3YXUxZzhWM2lGVTFaVlkwcmdMVkt", "source": "rss_ap_topnews:google_fallback", "published_at": "2026-10-15T23:25:39+00:00", "text": "Tear gas, fires and clashes as anti-government unrest escalates in Albania, in photos - AP News\nTear gas, fires and clashes as anti-government unrest escalates in Albania, in photos AP News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "fd23cb2b8e7991af68386121", "title": "Are student protests being manipulated ahead of France's presidential election?", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c8zxlyg965r3o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T23:25:06+00:00", "text": "Are student protests being manipulated ahead of France's presidential election?\nThe French prime minister has accused the radical left of weaponising the high school movement for electoral advantage\nThe French passion for protest is legendary in Europe.\nOver the last few years, we have regularly witnessed farmers angry about costly regulations lobbing manure at local mayoral offices, while masked youths periodically hurl homemade grenades at riot police in disadvantaged city suburbs.\nFor over three weeks now, thousands of students have barricaded entry to high schools and taken their grievances to the streets.\nThis Saturday, hundreds of new demonstrations have been announced across France.\nStudents are expected to be joined by workers' unions and key figures from the so-called \"yellow vest\" movement.\nFrance's Prime Minister, SÃ©bastien Lecornu, suggested this week that student anger was being manipulated as politics heats up ahead of next year's presidential election.\n(yellow vests) demonstrated against cost-of-living hikes for over a year from 2018 - becoming one of the longest-lived protest movements in French history.\nJÃ©rÃ´me Rodrigues (R) played a leading role in the gilets jaunes movement several years ago\nJÃ©rÃ´me Rodrigues, a prominent gilets jaunes activist, has used Instagram to encourage people to join Saturday's demonstrations.\nThe protests should give a voice to those who \"want to be able to live decently, no longer have to count every cent, and no longer have to choose between eating and driving\", he said in a video.\n\"Anger has no political party. An empty fridge has no political party,\" he added.\nSo what is going on in the EU's second largest economy?\nSocial unrest, government borrowing costs at eye-watering highs, a deeply divided French parliament and an apparent revolving door of exasperated French prime ministers - six prime ministers over the last five years - all claiming to want to reduce their country's public debt.\nIs France at breaking-point - and what does this all mean for the eurozone currency the French are such a huge part of?\nWatch: 'If we don't scream, they won't listen to us', student protester tells BBC in Paris\n\"You can't compare France to the Greece debt crisis of 2012 or Argentina in 2001. And the financial markets know it,\" economist Anne-Laure Delatte told me.\nShe is a CNRS Tenured Researcher at the Economics Department of Paris Dauphine University - a well-known face in debates on French media on the parlous state of the country's public debt.\n\"The markets recognised already in 2024 that we would have a budgetary problem after [snap] elections left parliament deeply divided between three [opposing political] blocs but they also realise France is a big country with the ability to raise taxes if necessary.\"\n\"Our situation is more like the UK in 2023 with Liz Truss,\" suggests Delatte, referring to the then UK prime minister's so-called mini budget, which triggered a sharp drop in the pound, a spike in government bond yields and soaring mortgage rates.\n\"The crisis was relatively short-lived because the markets recognised the UK had the financial tools to right itself.\"\n\"Here in France it would be painful, but a 4% increase in VAT would raise the â¬30bn needed to plug government debt. Investors know that,\" she concludes.\nMaybe, in theory. And, as Delatte also points out, the European Central Bank would likely intervene if France's finances nosedived to the point where other European economies would be affected.\nBut with so many French citizens already visibly unhappy with the status quo, imagine the reaction if taxes were to increase, or welfare and education budgets decrease, further.\n(freedom, equality and fraternity), but that is certainly not the sense you get on seething streets and in viral social media posts.\nRadical-left LFI leader Jean-Luc MÃ©lenchon (C) has been accused of exploiting the student protests ahead of next year's elections\nI met 17-year-olds Maxime, Madeleine and Elodie this week, perched on big green dustbins that have been used to block the entrance to their high school as part of the weeks' long protest.\nThey told me things were bad enough here, in the 3rd district of Paris, with holes in classroom walls, swelling class sizes and a dearth of teaching staff, but the teenagers were more than aware, they said, that the situation was far worse in their capital city's disadvantaged suburbs.\n,\" they told me \"but the reality is the poor here are prejudiced against, while the rich benefit.\"\nNone of them will be old enough to vote in the presidential election in France next spring but they questioned whether the changing face of politicians in power made all that much difference anyway.\nThe three young women told me large-scale protests seemed the only way to get heard by French authorities.\n'Everyone hates the police' â what France's school protests reveal about a divided nation\nFrance's school protests: What lies behind the anger\nFrance halts use of stun grenades after boy's hand blown off in student protests\nThey are part of a defiant French tradition.\nCultural icon Victor Hugo, the 19th-Century author of French Revolution classic\n, also an activist for the French Republic, proclaimed: \"\nSi vous n'Ãªtes pas contents, manifestez, criez, hurlez, mais ne subissez pas\n(\"If you are not happy, protest, shout, scream but do not give up and give in.\")\nAmongst crowds of protesting students walking through Paris on Thursday, I came across a number of mums, with placards around their necks, reading: \"Mums in solidarity.\"\nWere they there to protest or to protect their kids, I asked? \"Both!\" was the emphatic response.\nGwen, 37, said she and other mothers were taking it in turns every morning to co-ordinate between students and police at their local school lock-in.\nThis came after mounting accusations of police brutality, denied by France's interior ministry, during protests earlier this month in which hundreds were injured and a 15-year-old boy in the town of Lens lost a hand.\nFrance's interior minister has since suspended the use of stun grenades by police at high school demonstrations.\nGwen told me she did not mind that her son was missing weeks of school as a result of the lock-ins. \"It's a far better education for him to wake up to his civic duty and to fight for what is just in society,\" she said.\nAt Thursday's demo I also came across 17-year-old Lola and her mother, Sandrine, sharing a placard that said revolution was the most beautiful expression of humanity.\nLola (R) was joined in the protest by her mother Sandrine, who had been part of the 1968 student movement\n\"My mum took to the streets in '68,\" Lola said proudly. \"Now it's my turn. No point in waiting for the presidential election in France. We want change now.\"\nStudent protests in Paris in May 1968 are the stuff of legend in France. Demands for better education inspired workers to join the marches which turned into the Â­biggest general strike in French Â­history. It set the standard for revolutionary upheaval for a generation and is credited with creating new opportunities for the French left.\nNow, though, with six months to go before an already fraught presidential election in France, there are accusations that the hard left, under presidential hopeful Jean-Luc MÃ©lenchon, is weaponising student frustration.\n\"Members of [MÃ©lenchon's] France Insoumise (LFI) are instrumentalising the high school movement for their electoral advantage,\" said SÃ©bastien Lecornu on Wednesday. He accused them of encouraging protests, then going through the motions of condemning any violence.\n\"This hypocrisy damages France,\" said Lecornu.\nFrench Prime Minister SÃ©bastien Lecornu has complained that the high school protests are turning into a \"movement of urban violence\"\nBut \"MÃ©lenchon for President!\" is a chant you often hear amongst the students in the streets.\nSupporters applaud his desire for a wealth tax in France, for lowering the voting age to 16 and for b", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "886bcc46304f05d04c5b22fa", "title": "Greenland Energy Company (GLND) Stock Price, News, Quote & History - Yahoo! Finance Canada", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiUkFVX3lxTE5K