ts rise 11.9% amid steel shortages and tariffs ColoradoBiz", "truncated": false}, {"id": "5c85464e40201f7afdf6838a", "title": "Federal Investigation Underway After SPI EDC Cyberattack - Port Isabel-South Padre Press", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiqAFBVV95cUxOMmV5b3RiVW1jejNVTlQwWDNmUnhEUXJLRWl2SV9Wa1dfSVQ1blRYTlNrZWZrd3Rmam1ybHJhWEdsSFV0U25YMXdyTU1FOFpVTXpncFZYbE9iNzV5eERDRTNZZFVxSFpfQ0ZEVDF0M3ZLdzBKb2hOVUxGZlc3MkMybGdoZ09sbUpDUUJNSVcwVXJGTDl5M3g4WXNwbWZyX2wyWFVsVmZPYlg?oc=5", "source": "lane_blast_cyber", "published_at": "2026-10-15T23:20:20+00:00", "text": "Federal Investigation Underway After SPI EDC Cyberattack - Port Isabel-South Padre Press\nFederal Investigation Underway After SPI EDC Cyberattack Port Isabel-South Padre Press", "truncated": false}, {"id": "783ff6f32794cc4ee58acaff", "title": "3 Financial Stocks With Rising Treasury Yield Exposure Including Moodys Stock - Yahoo Finance", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMioAFBVV95cUxPbmNhUFFFM0hvWENWcEpTZG1tdFVfUlhCSGZvZzVod0lzeTA5b1JCY2huODJCVi11MW1wWnpDSFE4YjhuYTd1X3lrRnZ2NUx0UDdjMGJldFZzaXBwZ2lJYmFmUFUzY0hNMWdSWVp6bjlnTVB6emNzWXhyR25CaF9xQnRzLWVtcjRzSkR4aTFhSFk4UzRSamhGcy1aanFNekVm?oc=5", "source": "topic_government", "published_at": "2026-10-15T23:19:34+00:00", "text": "3 Financial Stocks With Rising Treasury Yield Exposure Including Moodys Stock - Yahoo Finance\n3 Financial Stocks With Rising Treasury Yield Exposure Including Moodys Stock Yahoo Finance", "truncated": false}, {"id": "775979a916e77fa2eef4ed19", "title": "Michigan Attorney General Dana Nessel seeks protections for Character.AI users in bankruptcy - WILX", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuAFBVV95cUxOVXNzRmtvdDF0Sm12eGZRSUFfb1QzWVVuZUlRYjVJZU50dG54d0g1Z0Fmdmp0TXNsa3A4Tm9fVlN5d3JGNDJ6WkMtcUk4QjBvaTJPUndicFB1eUJwWjdod0IzT3pQYnUzTEVONVlYWkxNSzhtdFpTT0ZZSkpPTzR5MVowamFUNDFocHNRTWJZMkh1cEF3aW9mNWJiRVlpa1hvYUIyZ3QtN1VVdEY1Z01sd2lhblhiV1V50gHMAUFVX3l", "source": "lane_distress_restruct", "published_at": "2026-10-15T23:19:00+00:00", "text": "Michigan Attorney General Dana Nessel seeks protections for Character.AI users in bankruptcy - WILX\nMichigan Attorney General Dana Nessel seeks protections for Character.AI users in bankruptcy WILX", "truncated": false}, {"id": "2554deac31f8536bae3567f8", "title": "Living with a stink? Technology might help", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cr6240q1w136o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_business", "published_at": "2026-10-15T23:18:21+00:00", "text": "Living with a stink? Technology might help\nThe operators of Newby Island landfill have been ordered to take action over odours\nMilpitas, an affluent city in California's Silicon Valley tech hub, has an odour problem.\nFor decades, residents have had to endure rank smells wafting from industrial sites, among them a 342-acre landfill.\n\"When I first moved here, I could not open my window for over 10 years,\" says Chia-Ling Kong, an engineer for a tech firm who lives around half a mile from the dump.\nWhile things have got better in recent years, Kong says she and her neighbours must still contend with around \"two or three hours of bad smells a day\".\nPerhaps befitting Silicon Valley, the community is putting its hopes in technology.\nThe operators of Newby Island landfill have been ordered by local authorities to use drones and install monitors to systematically track and measure the chemicals responsible for bad odours. They have also been told to automate the collection of landfill gases responsible for smells.\nKong is optimistic a data-driven approach will make a difference. \"Hopefully with this continuous monitoring, it makes it easier to pinpoint problems at the moment it starts.\"\nThe operators of the landfill say they are committed to operating it safely and protecting air quality.\nJust like in Milpitas, where Kong is part of a citizen's campaign against the odours, communities around the world are raising a stink about bad smells making day-to-day life miserable. Sometimes a rubbish dump is to blame, or it might be a sewage treatment works, or a reeking factory.\nWhen the complaints start stacking up, resolving them is not always simple. Many countries have no regulations in place dictating how to manage smells.\nIn the US, for example, the Environmental Protection Agency does not regulate environmental odours as a pollutant. This leaves states and local governments to take charge of the issue.\nUrban sprawl is often part of the problem, bringing communities closer to stinky facilities that may historically have been kept at a distance.\nThis is creating more demand for technologies to monitor and prevent smells. Odour consultant Micheal Bull says people are increasingly concerned about neighbourhood stinks.\n\"You're getting developments being built nearer to odorous processesâ¦ as land has become more valuable, there is also infilling,\" Bull says. He is speaking about his experiences in the UK, but it's a similar story in many parts of the world.\nBad smells from waste sites are among the most difficult to resolve\nThe technologies used to tackle industrial pongs generally fall into three categories: they absorb odours; convert them into something less smelly through a chemical reaction; or burn them away.\nExperts on fixing industrial odours say that the best technologies, when used correctly, can remove 90% or more of the target compounds responsible for smells.\nBut this can be expensive and some operators opt for simpler and cheaper solutions. These include masking odours from landfill or sewage processing by spraying giant quantities of deodorisers.\nOdour experts contacted for this article were dubious about the effectiveness of such industrial scale air fresheners. \"In my view they're not likely to change the odours,\" says Bull, who likens them to fabric conditioner.\nBad smells from waste sites â a common cause of complaints â are among the stinkiest and most difficult to resolve, according to Carlos Diaz, an environmental consultant based in Spain. \"Landfill is difficult to treat because you have a very large surface,\" he says.\nIn some places at least, technology is making a real difference. Residents in Des Moines, Iowa, have long complained of the so-called Eastside Funk, a putrid scent that residents report drifts over parts of the city.\nA study commissioned by city authorities showed that a trio of industrial meat companies were among culprits.\n\"Within 10 miles, there's about half a million people,\" says Dalton Jacobus, who works in the Department of Neighborhood Services. \"If the winds are out of the wrong direction, 100,000 people can be in the middle of that odour plume.\"\nThe authorities are now paying around $240,000 (Â£182,000) a year on technology to track the stink, so they can take action. \"What we wanted to do was build a ring of odour monitors around the facilities that we suspected of emitting odours,\" says Jacobus.\nThe monitors, which measure levels of chemical compounds in the air, work in combination with a weather station to provide real-time data on the concentrations that are coming from each facility based off the wind patterns.\nThis has put an end to finger pointing about who's to blame each time a smell sparks complaints, says Jacobus. \"Now we can say with reasonable certainty that 'this odour event did originate from your facility, what was going on'?\"\nIn Des Moines, authorities have set up \"electronic noses\" to pinpoint bad smells\nMeanwhile, some of the sites that have been blamed for causing unpleasant smells in Des Moines have spent millions of dollars to upgrade their own odour control systems.\nSmithfield Foods, which operates a meat processing in Des Moines, says it has invested $2m to install a regenerative thermal oxidiser that destroys the chemicals associated with bad smells. Even so, the company says it does not believe it is the source of odour complaints.\nBefore installing the monitors â which some local companies help pay for â officials in Des Moines would drive out to the source of a complaint to sniff the air.\nRelying on the human nose to sniff out industrial smells is still commonplace. Claudia Barrios ObregÃ³n from Kunak, the company behind the monitors in Des Moines, says: \"Right now, the way that cities control or measure odours is olfactometry: a human panel measures and gives an opinion of what the odour is like. But that's not that realistic.\"\nThe Spanish company's monitors are an example of so-called electronic noses that make it easier to detect smells.\nWhatever technology is used, it can be hard to keep everyone happy, as smells are so subjective. \"Unfortunately, the general rule is that the nastier we perceive the odour to be, the more sensitive towards it we are,\" says Johan LundstrÃ¶m, a professor in the science of smell at the Karolinska Institute in Stockholm, Sweden.\nThe extreme engineering of aircraft windows\nShould promotion depend on how workers use AI?\nWill self-flying planes transform the skies?", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "9c5dd173551587c4c04123de", "title": "Hughes Satellite Creditors Argue For Right To File Reorg Plan - Law360", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMizgFBVV95cUxONFFJSFJnQUlDajB3eGNSeUp5OTk0bVB0WlFVWGFSVFlMbHVfZWU1WWZpdklBTTNCZEloU1ZvbHBhWEdTYVpyOGJIYXpkU1RtM09fdUUxeTVBOU8zQUFSdldhQ2dBVzlXMjFvci1sUGQwYWo5NlBFQjRYdER0cjc3YlVTV1ZrRnJwQ2htWlp2WWxvQmZDSUdzMlQyZHBpaVJMX251aS1FcDZuRkkwTTFOaDNqRHpkODJFYmJWY1M3Vjd", "source": "lane_distress_restruct", "published_at": "2026-10-15T23:18:15+00:00", "text": "Hughes Satellite Creditors Argue For Right To File Reorg Plan - Law360\nHughes Satellite Creditors Argue For Right To File Reorg Plan Law360", "truncated": false}, {"id": "71efc7950e73657582a976aa", "title": "Insider Selling: Reddit (NYSE:RDDT) CEO Sells $2,744,529.56 in Stock - MarketBeat", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiwAFBVV95cUxNNlhWQkhT