url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiYkFVX3lxTE15TXcyUWRLdE1yelVXZ0VoWUlmN3czbGpKdksyUkNQbjh5eGJ3NHpOZ3RCU0ROQm9fd0czRnJUY1Awb1JxcmdmN3B1ZmdjM2lTZDNIWGwwQWF4VFJLeWluQkJ3?oc=5", "source": "topic_business", "published_at": "2026-10-15T23:00:19+00:00", "text": "EFULR Company Announcements - EFU Life Assurance Ltd (Right Shares) - Investify\nEFULR Company Announcements - EFU Life Assurance Ltd (Right Shares) Investify", "truncated": false}, {"id": "fd8a55fa2c604157e92bd47f", "title": "People in China are spending nearly as much of their income as Americans on healthcare", "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/15/chinese-spending-nearly-as-much-of-income-as-americans-on-healthcare.html", "source": "rss_cnbc_finance", "published_at": "2026-10-15T23:00:01+00:00", "text": "People in China are spending nearly as much of their income as Americans on healthcare\nA new International Monetary Fund book points out China is second only to the U.S. in healthcare costs as a percentage of personal disposable income.\nPer capita disposable income in the U.S. is more than 10 times that of China's in dollar terms.\n\"China's social spending is low compared with international peers, and increasing that spending would lift consumption,\" the IMF said.\nChildren and adults receive vaccinations at the Jiangbei Community Health Service Center on September 15, 2026 in Huizhou, Guangdong province of China.\nBEIJING — High healthcare costs are a big factor driving people in China to save, rather than spend, their money, the International Monetary Fund pointed out in a\ntitled \"Toward a New Economic Growth Model for China.\"\nIn rural China, 8.7% of disposable income is spent on healthcare — just second to the U.S. among 20 major economies, the research found. The U.S. ranks the highest in the world with nearly 9.9% of disposable income spent on healthcare.\nUrban China ranks third, at 5.4%, slightly more than Canada, the data showed.\nThe level of the healthcare burden in China grows starker when considering\nin the U.S. was $68,289 as of the end of last year — more than 10 times more than China's 43,377 yuan in U.S. dollar terms (around $6,400).\nThe gap widens further when looking at China's countryside, which only officially\nUrban China recorded more than twice the disposable income as rural areas, at 56,502 yuan versus 24,456 yuan, according to official data accessed via Wind Information.\nTargeted measures to raise government spending on health and social security in rural areas can significantly reduce the savings rate in rural households, the IMF said, citing prefecture-level analysis.\n\"China's social spending is low compared with international peers, and increasing that spending would\n,\" wrote Yizhi Xu, economist in the north Asia division of the IMF's Asia and Pacific Department, and Fan Zhang, the IMF's deputy resident representative in Beijing.\nThey recorded a savings rate of around 14% for people from rural areas working in China's cities, and about 6% for people with urban \"hukou\" residency rights, which give them more social welfare benefits than migrant workers from rural areas.\n\"Declining house prices in recent years continue to weigh on consumption and contribute to elevated household savings,\" Xu and Zhang wrote in their section of the IMF book.\nChoose CNBC as your preferred source on Google and never miss a moment from the most trusted name in business news.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "e9caf55d2a5564005b8b403f", "title": "High Court rules against Julius Baer’s Singapore trust arm in US$50 million family trust dispute - The Business Times", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiywFBVV95cUxOVXNqQmtqRWpTUDRRZHhPS1JvWWZPcVdXZmFtZ25HSTQ2SElta05GR1IxQXhtaGtiN2R0ZFF6SGlQR0FKMHM4MC1WUFZJQlF3LXAzUFJoa0NleEk2WmE2dDBZSTRoSE5pelFMRzFkVnhWR01kb3IyZjhkRmR1ZTE5bl8xWnR4dEkwSnpsSGozNVI1eDJQZ0FkZzg1VGZIcnJWNThPYkRXWFFNeGFLVDZaYkNrTjRuMnNUNkt5Uk0zSTR", "source": "lane_blast_legal", "published_at": "2