 "text": "Amazon launches new Bee 'Personal AI' devices that listen to conversations - CNBC\nAmazon launches new Bee 'Personal AI' devices that listen to conversations CNBC", "truncated": false}, {"id": "9b02155ed170bee53685247a", "title": "California can’t force ICE officers to wear visible ID, appeals court rules - Sacramento Bee", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiiwFBVV95cUxPbFFDdlJFRFJ1U2NUZWthSXIwaWZ2dzFMMzNTdzk0c0lZSE1WQjhyTy05WGczLVgtQ3VXMVprS1hCSVFKVmllbnlXNXEtcDJPckgyMDRQY1hmb2YyX2FpU0drVXNKSTRYS29HUkFzbF9FMjd3R2kzSkRSZ0RjLWhka05rVjdkMUFKR1ZV0gGLAUFVX3lxTE1lRXZKQjRuZlRWQ3FRMWNMMFF1aDdKZGo5OW5JdnNyOUJZc2Zua2ZWNVB", "source": "lane_blast_legal", "published_at": "2026-10-15T18:00:00+00:00", "text": "California can’t force ICE officers to wear visible ID, appeals court rules - Sacramento Bee\nCalifornia can’t force ICE officers to wear visible ID, appeals court rules Sacramento Bee", "truncated": false}, {"id": "662633f08fd2f28a5f7470ab", "title": "Stock Market Today: Stock Market News And Analysis - Investor's Business Daily", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMid0FVX3lxTE8zdGl1MmxTM2JJa2pLNlFSWVhuZUd1ekRIeVpDcGVqckVJcloxaWlnRHJ3b0hqb291NXk4dlo0SmRQNlhDaGpubDh6ZWNTUHZiYVhjOVJINUxSYXE0NHRiNVE2OW51R2h4N1daaXRIdzVBR1UxT0g4?oc=5", "source": "topic_business", "published_at": "2026-10-15T18:00:00+00:00", "text": "Stock Market Today: Stock Market News And Analysis - Investor's Business Daily\nStock Market Today: Stock Market News And Analysis Investor's Business Daily", "truncated": false}, {"id": "4231526a536598eac4c7f58e", "title": "Lawyer explains denaturalization as Trump administration pursues investigation of Rep. Ilhan Omar - MPR News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMivgFBVV95cUxPQTRDWFF2Zl94QTIzM1dlcDdwMzl4V2Zha1ZvOUhwRUtJa3dqSTRDZmNHQzFGVHVLMnBQNUFFMEkwVi1BQlpNZmxsRDhtQnlkeW5DbFdsUzViOWJ5cUl6aTFTNTNkYzFsa3NHc1gzaEJtN1RxcFNvd1V2N0RBd0pMeVJzai1oT1d6R0tEWmJlVnhxN1JNaGZKc2pNTTNZLURCU3JDa2VqZ2xsMW5NSTQ1elRYQXdwX0NpMVoyajRB?oc", "source": "lane_regulatory_probe", "published_at": "2026-10-15T18:00:00+00:00", "text": "Lawyer explains denaturalization as Trump administration pursues investigation of Rep. Ilhan Omar - MPR News\nLawyer explains denaturalization as Trump administration pursues investigation of Rep. Ilhan Omar MPR News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "30b77afd86fd20a8f0966c09", "title": "Bringing the magic: Google breaks open a bottleneck in quantum computing", "url": "https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/quantum-computing/magic-state-cultivation-quantum-computing/", "source": "company_google_blog", "published_at": "2026-10-15T18:00:00+00:00", "text": "Bringing the magic: Google breaks open a bottleneck in quantum computing\nBringing the magic: Google breaks open a bottleneck in quantum computing\nWe've shown that magic state cultivation works on Google Quantum AI hardware.\nQuantum Software Engineer, Google Quantum AI\nGoogle Quantum AI has demonstratively opened up a key bottleneck in quantum computing: we’ve shown that “magic state cultivation” works in practice on our Willow processor, reducing errors about 40x compared to without it. This breakthrough, published today in\n, is a crucial step towards scaling up quantum computing to practical use.\nSince 2004, long before quantum computing was a practical engineering pursuit, researchers have foreseen a particularly tricky bottleneck.\nTo achieve “universal” quantum computing and execute useful programs, you need to be able to run (and\n) a specific set of quantum operations. You can do this by first creating what are called \"magic states,\" so dubbed because they can help you create the operations you need for going beyond classical computing.\nBut there’s a catch: creating high-quality, error-corrected magic states is incredibly resource-heavy. Some estimates have shown that up to 95% of a quantum computer’s entire capacity might need to be dedicated to making them — leaving little room for actual computing. An extra challenge is that once you use a magic state, you can’t re-use it, making magic states a scarce resource.\nSo for 20 years, researchers have been chipping away at this cost of magic, bringing it lower and lower.\nIn 2024, Google Quantum AI researchers Craig Gidney, Noah Shutty, and Cody Jones harnessed an idea from the literature, refined it, and developed what we now call “magic state cultivation.” Their approach promised an especially large reduction. The idea was that cultivation would act like a filter. You would inject a noisy state into a small region on a quantum chip, and then run a series of highly coordinated measurements to check for errors and remove any flawed attempts. Craig and team showed how that would let you grow, or “cultivate,” an exceptionally pure, low-error quantum state that you could simultaneously adapt into a larger, useful encoding.\nOn paper, it looked great. If we could make this idea actually work on our quantum hardware, it could substantially reduce the size of the quantum computer needed for practical tasks.\nIn practice — and on par with developing many new technologies — it required a whole series of technical innovations and engineering.\nAs just one example, when first testing the cultivation protocol, Google Quantum AI research scientist Emma Rosenfeld was seeing data that seemed to defy the laws of physics. After months of confusion, simulations, discussions with colleagues, and consistency checks, she found that it arose from an incorrect assumption in the team’s go-to measurement technique. Specifically, a particular kind of noise in the lab caused by electrical equipment drift and temperature variation was interfering with the benchmark’s validity. The solution required a pivot in both our theoretical and engineering approaches. The team added extra control to the system to reduce the impact of that noise, and invented a new benchmarking technique to solve the issue more fundamentally, by using cultivation as a tool to measure itself.\nWith this and many more algorithmic innovations and engineering solutions, we established a new state-of-the-art in quantum metrology. On Google Quantum AI's Willow processor, our protocol achieved an extraordinary 40-fold reduction in errors relative to un-cultivated states. And we got T-states with a record logical fidelity of 99.99%.\n, this work is the first demonstration of a practical solution to one of quantum computing's most severe resource bottlenecks. It demonstrates, on real hardware, that we can efficiently create these crucial magic states.\nWe also took the first steps in demonstrating that Craig and others’ theories are compatible with our quantum computing architecture. We learned how to successfully switch between “color codes” (one newer type of quantum error correction, ideal for cultivation) and “surface codes” (another, more established method), grafting states from a distance-3 color code to a distance-5 surface code. Originally developed in the 2024 theory paper, this grafting protocol showed that in practice, purified states can survive and transfer smoothly inside our hardware.\nOf course, we still need to demonstrate magic state cultivation at a larger scale to make it realistic for practical quantum computing, and plenty of technical challenges remain. But this achievement takes a key theoretical idea and turns it into an experimentally demonstrated reality. It brings us one step closer to our goal at\n: building a useful, error-corrected quantum computer to solve otherwise unsolvable problems.\nHow we're helping the world prepare for natural disasters\nOur north star is simple yet ambitious: No one should be surprised by a natural disaster.\nResearch@ 2026: Novel capabilities and intelligence\nApplying technology to understand and treat disease\nAsk a Scientist: How are researchers using AI to help pregnant women access ultrasounds?\nThe latest AI news we announced in September 2026\nOur Project Suncatcher prototype satellite is in orbit.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "424b02bd35eed9a8e5c7886e", "title": "A $1.20 dividend adds stock units for Ryman Hospitality (NYSE: RHP) director H. Eric Bolton Jr. - Stock Titan", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiwwFBVV95cUxQZWlhZl8tcG5mMHpZWVNFeHZjc1RYYjNxRmZlVzZ6clZMLTI1LVU5Y0RZVy1CSWtfQS1DZ0p5a3k4Y1lhX0VJUGlPT2gtYUdCOTJJNGh0VVFDZlU2RTR2OG9wNGNSZEFISGhJQ0lkcWtZbTVmQ2NUQjU3UmxNOUVfakhrY3lrX3BCM2hZWkpiTnNRd0Vpd0NrbFRHWnZKVV9uVjlBS3F3elBubnVZWGZnTk10aEJnS1hFN1lKQ09UQU9", "source": "sec_4:google_fallback", "published_at": "2026-10-15T17:59:53+00:00", "text": "A $1.20 dividend adds stock units for Ryman Hospitality (NYSE: RHP) director H. Eric Bolton Jr. - Stock Titan\nA $1.20 dividend adds stock units for Ryman Hospitality (NYSE: RHP) director H. Eric Bolton Jr. Stock Titan", "truncated": false}, {"id": "968a2661b657d489d6f34ccf", "title": "Northwestern launches national search for next Chief Human Resources Officer - Northwestern Now News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMivwFBVV95cUxNZlFUSWQxcTNYOFpmU1FnN2dfZ2ktYUViYVA2Sng2N2FFVlR1eXh4T2ZqOTgtVW5DOFhtNFh6Z2pjYlY2aVU3TG5sVFg0Y0J3cm1oWkN0WFMzMjlKaGdneXdKZGF2QUJ6Q2VZbW1QVkFodkZST25rc2hITWhaZGtER2dCVHJCbGFQeW9INWxfNE15YUsyN1ZsZTMtWTVNNWhTQmRDdDBHYkd3SjlRY3o0S1lEdThKbTAzaW5uUzg1aw?", "source": "topic_products", "published_at": "2026-10-15T17:59:45+00:00", "text": "Northwestern launches national search for next Chief Human Resources Officer - Northwestern Now News\nNorthwestern launches national search for next Chief Human Resources Officer Northwestern Now News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "ed7b6ced300e84571ebaca5d", "title": "Senate passes workers’ compensation trust fund, federal polytechnics bills - Daily Post Nigeria", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiqAFBVV95cUxOdDFsSnlIZVkwSks5bVpoby02bU14d01KaG9oVC1LUmhreXVvSkVxckJZWjBRZU1Gd3lHb3VOdFlWZnlSZENrcV9RcFlQSEd0VW1YN0RfZHB6SGZlS3JqV2RhZ182ZEU3ZTJKN3owZ0l3Q2M0aS01THB