e order for public safety - knopnews2.com\nGovernor Jim Pillen signs executive order for public safety knopnews2.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "79393db3ef3facde8cc4c308", "title": "Governor Jim Pillen signs executive order for public safety - WOWT", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimgFBVV95cUxQcTJ4TjV1U2U4Y21mYVJRcE5sRG9YUUdVME5zUjZkSGFfMC1ITHQxQ0F5aklnbFFOSFJXazJZeXNVZEVBUzFWWDkyTzU1Z2Zqb0lfZW1FbjVESi1kLWl6c3ZXQVlfdVladGt1eThpNnNOMlFsbTZSU2tYT2ZsR3JYYjcwSGo5S0lITTNhTkVnbGtoS3lmODR0eTR3?oc=5", "source": "lane_tax_fiscal", "published_at": "2026-10-15T17:20:00+00:00", "text": "Governor Jim Pillen signs executive order for public safety - WOWT\nGovernor Jim Pillen signs executive order for public safety WOWT", "truncated": false}, {"id": "78c28305ef04f6e457dc73e5", "title": "An update on independent evaluators - Anthropic", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidEFVX3lxTE8zZVFudWFYcC04RHNOZHI0cGhuSXBUTlk2RWZsUnFkY2MzeWZRY0NVaV9OSTJHbDVkc3BBTHBWd1FKUDVCbjdYLTQtN2tGNUZXdnFYRmt2OUZTTzRoRVZNeWNteUE1Vy1DSXBGTTUxSFJqVmRf?oc=5", "source": "topic_business", "published_at": "2026-10-15T17:20:00+00:00", "text": "An update on independent evaluators - Anthropic\nAn update on independent evaluators Anthropic", "truncated": false}, {"id": "1c5bdd96013dd9ffd1dec687", "title": "Governor Jim Pillen signs executive order for public safety - knopnews2.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMitAFBVV95cUxPaG1TR1MzdXVXd3BtMVczTm5INGpqLVdJU1J0bFI0bWdwdklRVV9selcxZXZnYjdLTmhZdjY5RThPdWJnR01GOWtFR3JXcjh2eVEwM0pqb0FRcTdDYUYyT1V2YlQxZlNXSS1zeTl5NlBGOTE0alRkLWM4aFdxUnBlLXRRelVVc25HYlBFSXEtWVZSRlZ6clhtb2xhZTRxY3J5M0w1YVZkeGtfanBqd3kxX0ZvOWg?oc=5", "source": "lane_tax_fiscal", "published_at": "2026-10-15T17:20:00+00:00", "text": "Governor Jim Pillen signs executive order for public safety - knopnews2.com\nGovernor Jim Pillen signs executive order for public safety knopnews2.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "664049bc3a66e86396f9a73b", "title": "'I paid thousands to a company for jobs that never came'", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c9j3dvezpdz0o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_business", "published_at": "2026-10-15T17:19:49+00:00", "text": "'I paid thousands to a company for jobs that never came'\nApurv Patel said he paid Synetal Solutions Â£3,600 and claimed he was verbally promised a guaranteed sponsored job\nA company has been accused of promising jobs in the healthcare sector which never materialised, leaving people thousands of pounds out of pocket.\nRegistered in 2025, Synetal Solutions Ltd says it offers career-focused training programmes in the healthcare and IT sectors.\nBut the BBC has spoken to a number of people who claim they were promised training and support to secure sponsored healthcare jobs that never became available to apply for.\nLondon Citizens Advice says it has seen an increase in recruitment-related scams since the government\nstopped overseas recruitment for care workers\n. The charity described the firm's practices as \"unethical\".\nSynetal Solutions says it \"takes these concerns seriously and disputes the broad allegation that its services or candidate outcomes are fictitious\".\nApurv Patel, who has been in the UK on a Skilled Worker visa since 2023, said he paid Synetal Solutions Â£3,600 across February and March for healthcare training after he and a friend were verbally promised guaranteed sponsored jobs.\nEight months later he has received no job offers, interviews or meaningful employment support, he said.\n\"I'm frustrated. I paid money, I wasted my time. I did whatever they wanted without any result,\" he said.\n\"It's had a miserable effect on my family and finances.\"\nPatel said he did receive some training but described it as poor quality and ultimately \"worthless\".\nHaving previously worked for two years as a healthcare assistant, he said he became concerned when a trainer was unable to answer routine questions about care work.\n\"She didn't give us any answers and we realised she didn't have any experience of healthcare,\" he said.\nThe company disputes claims that training was ineffective.\nHe also alleged communication with the company deteriorated after he made his second payment, with responses sometimes taking several days.\nPatel has since complained to his bank and sought help from Citizens Advice.\nThe BBC has seen emails from the company advertising a \"three-month recruitment programme\" offering \"training and placement with sponsorship\" for Â£5,500.\nstates agencies may charge for services such as training or CV-writing assistance, but cannot charge fees solely for finding, or attempting to find, employment.\nResponding to questions about its marketing, Synetal Solutions said: \"We acknowledge the 'guarantee' wording in the emails supplied and recognise that contractual terms alone do not resolve concerns about those assurances.\"\nAs part of its programme, Synetal Solutions created accounts for trainees on iHasco, a health and social care training platform.\nThe BBC contacted iHasco, which confirmed the company was a customer. However, it said its terms did not permit customers to resell its training content to individuals who were not employees.\nThe company said it was reviewing Synetal Solutions' account and would take appropriate action if necessary.\nSynetal Solutions said it had acted in accordance with its agreement with iHasco.\nThe Care Quality Commission said there was no single qualification that automatically qualified someone to work as a care worker. Instead, providers are responsible for ensuring employees are appropriately trained and competent for their roles.\nThe BBC also contacted a number of healthcare organisations whose logos appeared on Synetal Solutions' website as examples of employers where graduates had worked.\nThose organisations that responded said they had no links to the company. Some also said they had not authorised the use of their branding, and some said they would not accept certificates provided through the company's training.\nDaniel Blake says the way Synetal Solutions has operated is unethical\nDaniel Blake, director of London Citizens Advice, described the company's approach as \"exploitative\".\n\"The promises that were made are impossible promises,\" he said. \"They are targeting people with vulnerable immigration status.\n\"This company is asking for large sums of money upfront without any reputable standards, without any reputable evidence of success, and yet when it goes wrong they are refusing to take any responsibility.\n\"For us, it seems a very unethical way of conducting business.\"\nHe added that no reputable recruitment agency would guarantee a successful outcome without first assessing a candidate and matching them to a suitable role, and urged anybody asked to pay large sums upfront to seek independent advice.\n\"If it seems too good to be true, it probably is,\" he said.\nThe company's website stated: \"Synetal Solutions provides the right support, guidance, and guaranteed job placement to help you succeed.\"\nBut since the BBC contacted Synetal Solutions mentions of job guarantees were removed from the website.\nIts social media channels also promoted healthcare as \"one of the most reliable paths\" for people seeking visa-sponsored work in the UK.\nThe website previously promised applicants a job - but this has since been removed\nSarah and Rahul, whose names we have changed, said they paid Â£3,000 to Synetal Solutions while seeking sponsored employment before the expiry of their student visas.\nRahul, a former dentist in India, could remain in the UK on a graduate visa for longer, but Sarah's immigration status was close to expiring.\nTo pay the fee, the couple said they borrowed money from a family friend and took out a loan.\nRahul said he was shocked by the quality of the training.\n\"The mentor didn't know anything about healthcare,\" he said.\nThe couple said they were also shown the names of established healthcare providers, including Bupa, as examples of potential employers. Bupa told the BBC it had no connection with the company.\nSynetal Solutions says some of their candidates \"told us they had secured employment with Bupa\" and the reference was \"not intended to suggest a formal partnership with Bupa\".\nIt says it had not verified those outcomes and \"cannot attribute the recruitment decisions to our training\".\nRahul said he secured one or two interviews, but prospective employers rejected the certificates he had received through the programme.\nThe couple said Synetal Solutions had since stopped responding to their messages.\n\"We're desperate,\" Sarah said. \"We don't see any other way out.\"\nAmisha - not her real name - says her certificates were rejected as invalid by one company she had an interview with\nAmisha, whose name has also been changed, said she signed up with Synetal after being told that both a placement and visa sponsorship were guaranteed.\nShe said the training consisted of three sessions lasting around 90 minutes each.\nAfter paying an initial instalment of Â£1,800, she became reluctant to make a second payment because she felt she had received little in return. She said the company warned her that her contract would be cancelled if she failed to pay, prompting her to transfer a further Â£1,800.\n\"I was waiting for interviews for two or three months. They were ignoring me,\" she said.\nAccording to Amisha, the company applied for only a handful of vacancies advertised on job-search website Indeed. She eventually secured an interview for a healthcare assistant role, but said the employer told her the certificates she had received were not recognised.\nA screenshot of a supposed testimonial from Synetal Solutions' website with content that does not appear to make sense\nThe BBC also identified what appeared to be at least a couple of stock photographs or AI-generated images used as testimonials on the company's website.\nThe company said it had not authenticated the two reviews identified, and removed them after we contacted them.\nTeni Shahiean, an immigration lawyer at OTS Solicitors, said the promises being made to individuals facing visa deadlines were \"particularly concerning\"\nTeni Shahiean, an immigration lawyer at OTS Solicitor", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "d7624a5183c15cd693c60b69", "title": "GHK Capital Acquires MSouth-Backed USA Hometown Experts - The Deal Pipeline", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMilAFBVV95cUxQY0N4UlZXWkZrb0dkR0N4VFZDWkQ1WWIxS1BUODR5WGNmRVdwOTgtNmJhTFRMeDI4dTVwWUJMSHZVOHRHYTk2R2JZZmFYemVpanhBS1FLVTVuNkh1YWh5d0xjN3h5WHAxUG5odFMxVGxERGVIc1FYNUctbE5vZlpyOUVfRFlFLVF2QXpONlpiTUgwcEow?oc=5", "source": "lane_corporate_action_mna", "published_at": "2026-10-15T17:19:32+00:00", "text": "GHK Capital Acquires MSouth-Backed USA Hometown Experts - The Deal Pipeline\nGHK Capital Acquires MSouth-Backed USA Hometown Experts The Deal Pipeline", "truncated": false}, {"id": "6b3caed2e4393331987ebcb0", "title": "🔴Live : ଆସିଗଲା ରେଜଲ୍ଟ | Vote Counting | Student Union Election Voting Begins | Utkal University - Kanak News Odisha", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiowJBVV95cUxQYmlKWmhJVENQdnp1aG5QVTJQaFF1SHJHYkpJR3RoREQtY2VFLXZnRE9aMGJ6TWl4dXVrYWE4UVFCYVRIWXpHQjY5a3R4VFJqaTlHYUJGVmhkdkpBaXNvd2IzWE1fVkN0R216RDZqSkFVRGJGX1RoT0VrUHU1T3pMNENFSGMtWDJ5VGF5TVVaU3dHYTlYTG5qT2JJVWdsVVcyeDlvTXFqMHhyU0xZN2c5UnBtLXRrcU5rbFVVR3V5UFJ", "source": "lane_labor_organize", "published_at": "2026-10-15T17:19:29+00:00", "text": "🔴Live : ଆସିଗଲା ରେଜଲ୍ଟ | Vote Counting | Student Union Election Voting Begins | Utkal University - Kanak News Odisha\n🔴Live : ଆସିଗଲା ରେଜଲ୍ଟ | Vote Counting | Student Union Election Voting Begins | Utkal University Kanak News Odisha", "truncated": false}, {"id": "21216fff3d031fbacdb6465a", "title": "Australia Introduces Tax Bill to Enable Cross-Border Exchange of Crypto Transaction Data - bloomingbit", "url": "https