les/CBMie0FVX3lxTFBUU2xHeHdvTF9jQk5hZk91a0dPT1hQMG9QQkFKampCTG5vbWNMU1FYX3VvM3BVdlJibWZRc1JGQnFGU01Jc2xyVU44U2QwSVA3WXFvY3hvYW02QWE5YlVyNzBySnZ3Um80MTQwZGJiUHU5YWZkcnBrd2xVWQ?oc=5", "source": "official_court_search", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:48:56+00:00", "text": "UNITED STATES DISTRICT COURT MIDDLE DISTRICT OF FLORIDA FORT MYERS DIVISION REGINALD DESTINA, Petitioner, v. Case No.: 2:26-cv - United States Courts (.gov)\nUNITED STATES DISTRICT COURT MIDDLE DISTRICT OF FLORIDA FORT MYERS DIVISION REGINALD DESTINA, Petitioner, v. Case No.: 2:26-cv United States Courts (.gov)", "truncated": false}, {"id": "72656a15f87fb3c606318b0c", "title": "Police raid the major pro-opposition Halk TV channel in Turkey", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/police-raid-the-major-pro-opposition-halk-tv-channel-in-turkey/a-79671673?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf", "source": "rss_dw_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:48:00+00:00", "text": "Police raid the major pro-opposition Halk TV channel in Turkey\nThe police raided Halk TV in the middle of a live broadcast\nImage: Emrah Gurel/AP Photo/picture alliance\nwere searched, and a new administrator was appointed by the government to run the media outlet. An arrest warrant was issued for the station's CEO, Cafer Mahiroglu, who currently lives in London. According to prosecutors, 11 people were arrested.\n. Halk TV is one of the country's best-known opposition television stations and regularly broadcasts critical reports about\n. After the raid, journalists were initially prevented from entering the building. Pre-recorded programs were broadcast instead of live reports.\nThe timing is particularly sensitive: Halk TV was raided on the very day that\nwas scheduled to begin his defense against corruption charges in court. The channel has been an important source of information for those following the trial.\nImamoglu, who won the mayoral election in Istanbul in 2019 and remained in office until his\n, is one of Erdogan's most significant rivals, considered a beacon of hope for the opposition. He is still expected to run for president in 2028.\nHe is accused of establishing and running a criminal organization, as well as of various related financial crimes.\nProsecutors are seeking a prison term of 2,352 years related to 142 charges.\nImamoglu denies the allegations, arguing that the case is politically motivated.\n11 people were arrested in conjunction with the raid on Halk TV headquarters\nRaid linked to investigation into Cafer Mahiroglu\nIstanbul prosecutors said that the raid on Halk TV was linked to an investigation into its CEO Mahiroglu, who is accused of bribery, bid rigging and money laundering.\nThey said that arrest warrants had been issued for 17 people, on top of the 11 arrests. Assets belonging to companies allegedly linked to Mahiroglu were also seized and a trustee appointed to run the firms.\nThe opposition responded with sharp criticism of the police raid.\nOzgur Ozel, the leader of the social democratic New Party (Yeni Parti)\n\"The public's right to information is being seized for political reasons,\" Mahiroglu said.\n\"Showing solidarity with Halk TV means supporting the struggle for democracy in\nHe added that \"only dictators\" would launch \"a dawn raid on a television channel, appointing a trustee […]\" and criticized the fact that this had occurred on the day that Imamoglu was due to begin his defense.\nMany people in Turkey protested in support of Ekrem Imamoglu when he was jailed in 2025, like here in Izmir on May 19, 2025\nImage: Berkcan Zengin/Middle East Images/picture alliance\nThe Journalists' Union of Turkey (TGS) also condemned the raid on the platform X, describing it as arbitrary and arguing that the press was free and should not be censored.\nEditor-in-chief Suat Toktas tried in vain to persuade the new state-appointed administrator to continue live broadcasts. In a video released by the TV channel, he said that what was happening was \"illegal.\"\nHalk TV began broadcasting on January 10, 2005 and its live broadcasts gained widespread attention particularly during the 2013 Gezi Park protests. Cafer Mahiroglu took over the station in 2020.\nLegacy of the 2016 coup attempt in Turkey 10 years on\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "d11112a6fc392dd130f0c439", "title": "Albania’s PM dismisses calls to resign as student protests spread - Al Jazeera", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiqgFBVV95cUxPLWdPNUEzQ0haNDFldl9FZURRQ0hsWmFfN3oySGx5V0lvLXZjVUVhZTdXRC1YS3BXT1hJdGpfRHc4aHV1azdaOHFQa3l4YVFtSi1wcmM5RlNWTEdfX3gwRWk4NXFBSGgzenZBcEY0VnJNVUlnYWpod2UtV2xfVzdxLTN0M3NCX3pZSGJxTUtqM2k3TjU5OVdoQ2hRWWhFQ2ZNbFNaUUpBb0dwUdIBrwFBVV95cUxQS3pWVHdhdXJSclN", "source": "rss_google_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:47:39+00:00", "text": "Albania’s PM dismisses calls to resign as student protests spread - Al Jazeera\nAlbania’s PM dismisses calls to resign as student protests spread Al Jazeera Albanian protests turn violent: What we know BBC Albania’s leader defies calls to resign as student protesters rampage in capital NBC News ‘Boiling point’: Why students are protesting against Albania’s Edi Rama Al Jazeera Tear gas, fires and clashes as anti-government unrest escalates in Albania, in photos AP News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "849fdf1c6e8e079003b244f6", "title": "Japan's Takaichi says chores and makeup add hours to her workday, sparking debate on gender roles - AP News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiqwFBVV95cUxOMXBEZnJGQ0hNWGItUFFPaUFaZ0VDQmp4bW1ZbDFCbnh6b251T0xXWk1kNlhjTFlXQm5IVVNvYjFuM3oyRkI4U0pMVEtITW5qaEV4TEtxSTc1bFkxckZCTEVrZ3F6aWxVc1ozNFBmdDJ2VlFhMkducUVDR1VJRlBycWlTSU1DT3JPWFQxcS1sZURmOWlxSnpxRlNQcjU2WjlrSTI5T3FzUHlsdm8?oc=5", "source": "rss_ap_business:google_fallback", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:47:00+00:00", "text": "Japan's Takaichi says chores and makeup add hours to her workday, sparking de