ished_at": "2026-10-15T16:16:00+00:00", "text": "Injunction against Georgia social media parental consent law reversed on appeal - MLex\nInjunction against Georgia social media parental consent law reversed on appeal MLex", "truncated": false}, {"id": "d967e57656b7b02af75bc8ab", "title": "Kimberly-Clark: Preliminary Results Suggest Portfolio Pieces Falling Into Place; Shares Attractive - Morningstar", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi-wFBVV95cUxPQ2I0MzhhTW0tZ0hERkNjYjJDYWxZcnRGY3pPOV9PUlJfaHVLWmIwTEJRUUFVRXhYYldETmpIZ2ZDNEdaUGp6Q3U2VVkyY1pSLVgyRkYyOGRiR2ZJZnI5NFhKTVkxTGpHNmlRNE5TcnNTQ2NtNG1PNGlTcnp6OTNWT0pwS0N5NGRfc2RpT0hLbVFWRE9TOUhnaDBoM0FBTTJLeEZTczJ0cGV0aExvSEdHajZ5Z1kzWkR3aGpWVm9DbG8", "source": "lane_preannounce", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:15:59+00:00", "text": "Kimberly-Clark: Preliminary Results Suggest Portfolio Pieces Falling Into Place; Shares Attractive - Morningstar\nKimberly-Clark: Preliminary Results Suggest Portfolio Pieces Falling Into Place; Shares Attractive Morningstar", "truncated": false}, {"id": "c9e64b0631182f6b92ff84e3", "title": "Wholesale prices jumped 6.3% annually in September, biggest gain since 2022", "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/15/september-producer-price-index-retail-sales-data.html", "source": "rss_cnbc_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:15:44+00:00", "text": "Wholesale prices jumped 6.3% annually in September, biggest gain since 2022\nThe producer price index rose 6.3% year over year, the largest annual increase since 2022.\nEnergy costs drove the index higher, and there are signs of price spillover into other categories.\nThe PPI data comes on the heels of Wednesday's CPI release, which was also hotter than economists anticipated on an annual basis.\nWholesale prices jumped 6.3% annually in September, biggest gain since 2022\ncame in much hotter than economists forecast on Thursday, underscoring the continued inflationary pressure from energy amid the U.S. war with Iran.\nThe measure of underlying inflation pressures climbed 1.3% in September and 6.3% on an annual basis, according to the Bureau of Labor Statistics. That marked the largest monthly and annual gains since March 2022 and December 2022, respectively.\nBoth readings topped expectations of economists polled by Dow Jones, who forecast an advance of 0.6% on the month and 5.5% annually.\nEnergy drove a sizable chunk of those increases as the Middle East conflict curtailed oil supply and sent prices surging. The so-called core PPI, which excludes volatile food and energy prices from the wholesaler inflation index, added 0.7% on the month and 5% over the 12-month period.\nBut there are signs that soaring energy prices are having downstream effects on other categories. The BLS said in Thursday's release that almost half of September's increase in processed goods for immediate demand was connected to the rise in diesel fuel prices.\nIt's the latest report in recent days showing that inflation continues to hit consumers and companies.\nrelease showed a rise of 0.6% from August to September and 3.7% on a 12-month basis. Economists polled by Dow Jones had forecast growth of 0.6% and 3.6%, respectively.\nExcluding food and energy, the core CPI added 0.3% on the month and 2.6% compared with a year ago. Economists anticipated increases of 0.2% and 2.5%, respectively.\nWednesday's Beige Book, a chronicle of economic activity published by the\n, had the highest number of mentions of terms related to inflation on record, according to an analysis from Bespoke Investment Group.\nRetail sales grew 0.8% in September, surpassing the rate of 0.4% anticipated by economists, according to government data released in a separate report Thursday. That marked a slowdown from a month prior, when retail sales rose by more than 1%.\nExcluding spending on autos and gas stations, retail sales increased 0.25% from August to September, a drop from around 1.1% in the prior period. This slowdown offers a sign that high prices related to fuel are causing consumers to tighten the belt elsewhere.\nThis week's data has left a growing minority of fed funds futures traders expecting the central bank to raise rates at its meeting later this month. Traders are now pricing in a nearly 34% likelihood that the Fed hikes borrowing costs on Oct. 28, up from under 18% a week prior, according to CME's FedWatch tool. About 90% expect at least one hike by the end of the year.\n\"With a second day in a row of higher-than-expected inflation numbers, it seems the Fed will have no choice but to raise rates again this year,\" said Chris Zaccarelli, investing chief at Northlight Asset Management.\nThese reports offer some of the final insights into the pace of inflation in the U.S. ahead of the\n, which could impact how much power each political party has in Washington.\nAnalysis: Why a $2 trillion deficit and 5% Treasury yields are becoming America’s biggest economic challenge\nEconomists: Hot PPI shows Fed’s work is not done\nHow high inflation became a ‘fact of life’ in the U.S.\nWholesale prices jumped 6.3% annually in September, biggest gain since 2022\nChoose CNBC as your preferred source on Google and never miss a moment from the most trusted name in business news.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "83b1486e7508d464b3140a5f", "title": "School headscarf ban sparks rare protests in Azerbaijan and row with Turkey", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwly0g2r4k3po?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:15:06+00:00", "text": "School headscarf ban sparks rare protests in Azerbaijan and row with Turkey\nThese schoolgirls were not allowed to enter classes because of their headscarves\n\"You are telling me, I can't come to school?\" says a teenage girl in a navy blue Islamic headscarf, captured on video challenging a staff member at the entrance to her school.\nThe footage has been shared widely on social media, and shows a group of girls in hijabs standing behind her applauding as she continues: \"Why should I have to give up my religion?\"\nLast Monday the education ministry in Azerbaijan began enforcing new uniform rules, turning away female pupils wearing hijabs. Protests began outside state schools in the capital, Baku, and in several southern districts including Masally and Jalilabad.\nProtests are rare in this oil- and gas-producing country of 10.5 million people.\nAzerbaijan is constitutionally secular and the government strongly enforces that.\nPresident Ilham Aliyev has been in power since 2003 and has little tolerance for dissent. Opposition politicians and many independent journalists are in jail.\nAzerbaijan is pulled between two neighbours, Turkey and Iran. Azerbaijanis belong to a Turkic group of nations, speaking a language close to Turkish, and Turkey is its closest ally.\nBut like Iran to the south, Azerbaijan is mainly Shia - over 60% of its people identify as Shia Muslims, while most Turks are Sunni.\nThe interior ministry said it had detained 37 people on Monday who tried to obstruct the rules and \"grossly violate public order\".\nVideos have circulated of protests taking place in and around the capital Baku\nThe rules, approved on 21 September, ban clothing which contradict \"the principle of secularism in education\" in state schools. They do not mention the hijab. Similar guidelines were extended to teachers on 6 October.\nIn one video, pupils outside School No. 69 in Balakhany, outside Baku, hold a banner reading: \"The hijab does not prevent anyone from getting an education.\"\nShahiza Huseynova, a mother who was protesting outside a school in the Mashtaga district of the capital, was detained on Tuesday.\nIn an interview with Meydan TV, an independent Azerbaijani-language channel based in Germany, Huseynova's husband Agha said she had been given a warning: \"Either you take off your child's hijab and send them to school, or you go to prison.\"\nAn example shared by the Azerbaijani government of an acceptable school uniform under the new rules\nFriends have also lost contact with Ilkin Huseynov, an activist who on Tuesday launched an online platform called \"Let Leyli Go to School\". It offers free lessons from volunteer teachers to pupils barred from class.\nAccording to a friend, Huseynov left his home in Baku with unidentified men at about 16:00 on Wednesday, and his phone has been switched off ever since.\nThe authorities have not commented, but friends believe he has been detained. On Thursday, the platform's Instagram page could not be accessed.\nSince the protests began, users have reported problems accessing Instagram and TikTok without a virtual private network (VPN).\nMost of the protests have been in religiously conservative neighbourhoods in Baku's outlying settlements and in southern districts near the 765km (475-mile) border with Iran.\nThe authorities have long been wary of religious influence from Iran, where more ethnic Azerbaijanis live than in Azerbaijan itself.\nOn the night of 13 October, Azerbaijan's State Service for Special Communications and Information Security said a \"foreign hacker group\" had attacked more than 100 state websites.\nA pro-Iranian group calling itself the 313 Team (Cyber Islamic Resistance) claimed responsibility.\nAzerbaijani officials have defended the rules. One MP, Nigar Arpadarai, told the 1news.az website that children did not choose to wear the hijab themselves but were influenced by their families.\n\"A child has no freedom to choose a religion. This is a conscious prerogative of adults,\" she said.\nAzerbaijan pardons Frenchman after EU lifts sanctions on Russian billionaire\nAzerbaijan and Armenia sign peace pledge at White House summit with Trump\nThe row has now spilled over into Turkey, Baku's closest ally, which provided vital support during Azerbaijan's 2020 war with Armenia over Nagorno-Karabakh.\nThe two countries call themselves \"one nation, two states\".\nPresident Recep Tayyip ErdoÄan, a devout Muslim, has spoken of creating a \"pious generation\".\nHis chief adviser, Oktay Saral, has called the Azerbaijani restrictions \"unacceptable\" and urged Baku to \"reverse this mistake\".\n\"A headscarf is not an obstacle; it is a requirement of one's faith!\" Saral wrote on X.\nSeveral MPs from Turkey's governing AK Party joined in.\nSmall demonstrations were held outside Azerbaijan's embassy in Ankara, organised by KÃ¶klÃ¼ DeÄiÅim, an Islamist movement linked to Hizb ut-Tahrir, and outside its consulate in Istanbul, organised by conservative Islamist group ÃzgÃ¼r-Der.\nPresident Aliyev's New Azerbaijan Party accused Turkish officials of an \"openly defamatory\" information campaign and of \"baseless accusations\", warning that Oktay Saral's comments risked harming ties, and cautioned against interference in Azerbaijan's internal affairs.\nEarlier this month, Ilham Aliyev himself said no one should \"stick their noses into our way of life\".\nBehind the dispute there may lie another source of friction. Turkish protesters have linked the headscarf rules to Azerbaijan's close ties with Israel, which sells weapons to Baku and buys Azerbaijani oil.\nTurkey's relations with Israel have collapsed over Gaza, but Azerbaijan has tried to maintain relationships with both countries.\nThat is a difficult balancing act. On Thursday, when Israel's President Isaac Herzog addressed a regional security summit hosted by Azerbaijan in Baku, delegations from Turkey, Iran and other countries walked out of the hall.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "4e4da91e6e6c99a00507c05c", "title": "Angel One’s consolidated PAT up 58.2% y-o-y in September quarter - TradingView", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMivAFBVV95cUxNclNBc0FyV0dNV0VoTWdHX3o2UGNsemlQdVZjdl9NR29NTTdMbVFDbzlaS0dpOVlxcnNjODM4dE9KdE9WS2xDOFRna2s0OXZTVkI0dDN5b3BwN0xRNlF3UmNuU0FrMjBkakN4cURLSG5udkdkQ21VMjlYX19yaGN0SlBtcDlYTnM4dWVMcUpKcEQwdDZ5cUZ2eTlRMWRmd0F3WHY5ODh4eG9UOWpXV25Ec