t Hobart Police Department - WFRV Local 5", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiggFBVV95cUxQdGVNcVBUOVI3OHhYZ2FwcHZSZ1ZrLUhtWE9rVXhQYVdIa1RTREFxSFFIZWthSmNIUTMtLTl0bWNibk9uNDlFa1pJQTlwOFN4cVU1blRKM194eEM3dWQwVHJ2R1ZQVl9WUjZiSExPcFYtYVZHemFVaEVTOG5vTHJZdXl30gGHAUFVX3lxTE5kY0VyMHIxLWg5aG9zMXdZenhoZEp6Rzd3OE1MTlk5Vk5KLTVxQk43ZGQ5XzZiU28tX25", "source": "lane_blast_legal", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:00:24+00:00", "text": "Josh Jacobs files an injunction against Hobart Police Department - WFRV Local 5\nJosh Jacobs files an injunction against Hobart Police Department WFRV Local 5", "truncated": false}, {"id": "fb9b71b9aeee89562a94ea6a", "title": "How we're helping the world prepare for natural disasters", "url": "https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/google-research/crisis-resilience-white-paper/", "source": "company_google_blog", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:00:00+00:00", "text": "How we're helping the world prepare for natural disasters\nHow we're helping the world prepare for natural disasters\nOur north star is simple yet ambitious: No one should be surprised by a natural disaster.\n, when millions practice earthquake drills. Our Android Earthquake Alerts System turns smartphones into a global network that can warn people before shaking arrives, and it's\nWhen a natural disaster strikes, early warnings and trusted information help people prepare, respond, and stay safe. Earthquakes are only one part of our crisis resilience efforts at Google. Our AI models also help people prepare for other natural disasters:\nOur flood forecasting technology provides up to seven days' notice for\nfor more than two billion people in 150 countries and up to 24 hours' notice for\n, a global satellite constellation, is designed to detect and track fires as small as a classroom within 20 minutes once completed.\nIn 2025 alone, Google helped connect people with crisis information more than 10 million times per day, on average.\nis simple yet ambitious: No one should be surprised by a natural disaster.\nLearn more about our progress on crisis resilience and how we are working with partners and communities around the world to better prepare for natural disasters in our new\nBringing the magic: Google breaks open a bottleneck in quantum computing\nResearch@ 2026: Novel capabilities and intelligence\nApplying technology to understand and treat disease\nAsk a Scientist: How are researchers using AI to help pregnant women access ultrasounds?\nThe latest AI news we announced in September 2026\nOur Project Suncatcher prototype satellite is in orbit.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "c08dc5794b018e368be67920", "title": "Android earthquake alerts now available in more than 200 regions", "url": "https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/platforms/android/android-earthquake-alerts-expansion-2026/", "source": "company_google_blog", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:00:00+00:00", "text": "Android earthquake alerts now available in more than 200 regions\nAndroid earthquake alerts now available in more than 200 regions\nWe’ve sent over one billion Android earthquake alerts. Now, we’re expanding alerts to more places and adding new details, helping more people take action and stay safe.\nA few seconds of warning when an earthquake hits can give you crucial time to take cover and protect yourself and your loved ones. To make these early warnings possible at scale, we built the Android Earthquake Alerts System — the world’s largest mobile-based earthquake detection network.\nSince launching in 2021, we’ve sent over one billion alerts to help people stay safe before the shaking even starts. Today, we’re expanding the system from 98 countries and regions to more than 200 — including Panama, Guatemala and many more.\nWe’re also improving our latest “Be Ready” alert to give you more information in seconds. If an earthquake occurs in a region where alerts are active, Android users can expect to see one of three alerts:\nA straightforward alert for estimated light shaking, letting you know an earthquake has been detected nearby.\nA detailed alert for stronger shaking, alerting you to prepare to take action.\nA full-screen alert for moderate to heavy shaking, giving you immediate advice on how to stay safe.\nWe’ve built-out our Be Ready alert with new helpful guidance. In the coming months, you’ll see a map with a radius of where we’ve detected shaking, estimated earthquake magnitude, action you can take, and a button to learn more about the earthquake as information comes in.\nOur physical safety products are deeply important to us. While we hope they never get used, we’re grateful to be able to bring more information to more people when they need it most. Learn more about the\nTo see how we're helping communities around the world better prepare for earthquakes and other natural disasters, read our new\nThese alerts will begin rolling out to users in newly supported regions starting today, and are expected to be fully available over the next week.\nA new app experience is coming to cars with Google built-in\nSwitching password managers is easy and safe on Android\nSeptember Android Drop: Remember where you put things, ease motion sickness, and more\nHere's how to use sign-to-text translation on Pixel 11.\nTap into the power of Gemini in Chrome on Android.\nSharing between Pixel devices is now just a tap away.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "9c2c9f923050a8407dfb58c1", "title": "400 publications are now partnering with us on our News AI pilot program.", "url": "https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/products/news/news-ai-pilot-update-publishers/", "source": "company_google_blog", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:00:00+00:00", "text": "400 publications are now partnering with us on our News AI pilot program.\n400 publications are now partnering with us on our News AI pilot program.\nPeople’s information habits are evolving quickly, and platforms and publishers of all sizes are navigating these changes. To help meet this moment, we’re evolving our partnerships as newsrooms adapt to these shifts in user preferences and new technology. Since\nour News AI pilot program last year with a range of news partners, like The Washington Examiner and The Washington Post, more than 400 publications worldwide are now participating in this program.\nOur newest partners range from global outlets like Axios, CNN, The Financial Times, and Forbes, to local publishers like Post and Courier, Texas Tribune, Oil City News, and Shaw Media in the U.S.\nThrough this licensing program, we pay participating news publications for the delivery and use of enhanced content to develop new AI features. For example, these experiences include in-depth article overviews on Google News publisher pages offering people helpful context before they click and customizable audio briefings for listeners. With clear attribution and direct links, these features build meaningful new ways for readers to connect with the sources they value.\nSee the top style searches from people in big fashion capitals.\nMore ways to connect with the web from Search\nCheck out this year’s buzziest Halloween trends.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "6f446a6a25d74f66ddc8964b", "title": "Healthcare Ransomware Attacks Doubled Since This Time Last Year - Security Magazine", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMisgFBVV95cUxOSWZScWE1Qkhob2gwZTNmLUtkNW1OY3lDS2ctWVJVSVlxdnpSMVh2UVJ5c1V5X2tqWUdiRGhDY1BmMGJJXzNSUVVQdmpMT1p5Wk43WmNGRjRaVDYzNUxBRk1tVXZ3b25IcHZFMTJxV2MwTWNRMUtCQUFsLTRsOVRxbElqRnZlLS00OHBlakFqbUJWTERkQjY1UVEyY3kyR2VndXlCNFdxaWdXZXpBTEFwbVRn?oc=5", "source": "lane_blast_cyber", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:00:00+00:00", "text": "Healthcare Ransomware Attacks Doubled Since This Time Last Year - Security Magazine\nHealthcare Ransomware Attacks Doubled Since This Time Last Year Security Magazine", "truncated": false}, {"id": "3a7fcda725cd0753fe16698d", "title": "Philex resolves labor strike - Philstar.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiiAFBVV95cUxPWWFxVVBSemc5clg1RllPNGZlOG9FV2F2T3BXbzhYUEVIM2pYekpTR1JhR0NUTUg2NjRUVTZONWtHY19TbFZHLWV4YjdLOTM2dzg4TmRaSzh5U1NHdFRwMllIZmRiVWJiWUxTd1RBVE04VHptc24wRlYyYTdWZW1rV0NLU1RLaDJj0gGPAUFVX3lxTFBWdTBucUpIWjdIRkpUMmN3NEdDS2FCV3BLa0RyTjlOYUIxM1NIQ2lQNGFDbkM", "source": "lane_labor_stop", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:00:00+00:00", "text": "Philex resolves labor strike - Philstar.com\nPhilex resolves labor strike Philstar.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "1689b62220a46f251f1c77b7", "title": "Cloud-native infrastructure for scalable AI workloads - Microsoft Azure", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiqgFBVV95cUxORGFsUkNNdHhEVkI2TjRFN0xldm9zcUMwWjdJT29IT1h1U2x3SUprdC1YWktsTkNMTnZPS05CX3FUNk9MSHVETWFBaWRUS3ZEc1EwcWdxMGlHY0pLR3hnckluRlNJUnQ3ZHliSlVUX0NRM3hhMFFDS3JTSVRuS2lYQldra1VVanU2LVFzVjBJRHh1eGFQQjlyZlVJUXJxT2tEZUNuODJTaU5hZw?oc=5", "source": "topic_technology", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:00:00+00:00", "text": "Cloud-native infrastructure for scalable AI workloads - Microsoft Azure\nCloud-native infrastructure for scalable AI workloads Microsoft Azure", "truncated": false}, {"id": "09cafcfe0698167f2399633a", "title": "More ways to connect with the web from Search", "url": "https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/products/search/updates-ai-mode-overviews-explore-web/", "source": "company_google_blog", "published_at": "2026-10-15T16:00:00+00:00", "text": "More ways to connect with the web from Search\nMore ways to connect with the web from Search\nWe’re updating AI Mode and AI Overviews with clearer inline links, context about sources, timely articles and more to help you explore the best of the web.\nGoogle is updating its AI Search features to help you explore the web more easily. You will now see more inline links, dedicated sections for official websites, and visual carousels for news. These changes also include clickable creator profiles to help you better understand the sources behind your search results.\nSummaries were generated by Google AI. Generative AI is experimental.\n\"More ways to connect with the web from Search\" makes finding information easier and faster.\nNew inline links let you explore topics directly while reading AI-generated responses.\nA dedicated section now highlights official websites to help you find exactly what's needed.\nUpdated carousels and visual links make discovering news and related articles much more engaging.\nClickable creator profiles now provide quick context on the people behind your favorite videos.\nSummaries were generated by Google AI. Generative AI is experimental.\nGoogle is updating its AI search to make finding information easier and more visual. You’ll see more links directly in AI answers so you can quickly click through to the original websites. They’re also adding better ways to find news and learn more about the creators behind the content. These changes help you dig deeper into topics you care about without any extra hassle.\nSummaries were generated by Google AI. Generative AI is experimental.\nWhether you’re coming to Search to quickly find information, explore new topics, or discover new voices, we want to make it as easy as possible for you to dive deeper and engage with the content and sources you value. We’re seeing people take advantage of tools that give them more choice: the number of people who now use our Preferred Sources feature has grown by more than 7x since the start of the year. Today, we’re announcing updates across AI Search to make exploring the web simpler, more visual, and more engaging.\nInline links, which are links embedded in words or phrases in an AI response, help you dive deeper into a topic the moment you’re reading about it. We’ve made continued and substantial increases to the presence of inline links in our AI experiences. We're also launching updates to show inline links higher up in responses more frequently, helping to ensure key sources are easily clickable right when you need them.\nSometimes, there are only one or two official places on the web to complete a specific task or get the exact info you need. We'll be testing a new display in Search AI experiences to prominently surface these links in a dedicated section at the top of a response, with context about why each link is useful. With these links and additional context, you can be more confident you’ve found what you’re looking for. This expands on what we already do today to show links front and center when you’re looking to go to a particular site.\nyou see for timely articles in AI Search features to additional locations and languages globally. These carousels also highlight your Preferred Sources, making it easier to find coverage from the websites you value. This feature makes timely articles more visible on a wider range of queries.\n4. Dive deeper with prominent links to related articles\nOnce you’ve gotten the lay of the land on a topic, you might want to dig deeper on interesting angles – and we want to make it easy to find links that spark your curiosity. So we’re making the links that we show at the end of some AI responses more engaging and prominent, with image thumbnails and a more clickable design.\nThis week, we started testing clickable creator and publisher names on video results that link directly to their\nin AI Mode. These profiles give you a quick way to learn about creators and their broader work, giving you even more context. Eligible creators and publishers can learn more about Search profiles and\nThese updates help you dive further into the information you’re looking for, and we'll keep refining these experiences based on what works best.\nGet the latest news from Google in your inbox\nSign up for our newsletters with product updates, event information, special offers, and more.\nCheck your inbox to confirm your subscription.\nYour information will be used in accordance with\nSee the top style searches from people in big fashion capitals.\n400 publications are now partnering with us on our News AI pilot program.\nCheck out this year’s buzziest Halloween trends.\n6 ways to trick-or-treat yourself to the ultimate Halloween with Search\nWheels up: Trends and tips for 2026 holiday travel", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "e70f8344e94b826d08fdfdb9", "title": "Risk advisory: ransomware planning still starts too late - SC Media", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimAFBVV95cUxNNWdBeS13UGEwUWtOX0dQWmpaR1NXRzZNSFhJbzhWUGpDcHVxbVpvaWhiWW5tLVNXekxLYmUtLTBLYVpLb0trRU5lVUlWb0xWZmtLZEkteHhXZktUY1lhVkh0Vmhva0pwTVFJZEFfc1daSzE2cUIzWW4zdU9uZ0sxTHRTOFVCUklNbG9RS25wVXNqUkRnMUlMOQ?oc=5", "source": "lane_blast_cyber", "published_at": "2026-10-15T15:59:28+00:00", "text": "Risk advisory: ransomware planning still starts too late - SC Media\nRisk advisory: ransomware planning still starts too late SC Media", "truncated": false}, {"id": "93fba249b034063b6b5bf6fb", "title": "DeWine signs executive order allowing Ohio colleges to extend eligibility for 2022 high school graduates - WV News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihgJBVV95cUxQeWpVOU1PS2dQTkxzQVNnZFdnRDNvOV9iNjA1b0VETDZBcUNNOUszUnc1d3BNMVNUQzN4SHBCSlg5a1V1azBFRXpGWXN0Y2xoNWxoVEdkN0VGUDkwRVBjUlJrRUNVTHpWeno3RzU1X3pDZHBDUFFzNEx1eVh0b0NOMFk3blhkSUNIQnFISWJJZFo2djRnSVZ5eER3NUpEOU5MY0RLSXZxN053YXlNWTI2YXFKWDFHVFlfMlhHM1pJNFh", "source": "lane_tax_fiscal", "published_at": "2026-10-15T15:59:00+00:00", "text": "DeWine signs executive order allowing Ohio colleges to extend eligibility for 2022 high school graduates - WV News\nDeWine signs executive order allowing Ohio colleges to extend eligibility for 2022 high school graduates WV News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "f6aaf935a1c5b46405f83d0f", "title": "BNC Service Interruption Update - Bankruptcy Court for the Northern District of Texas (.gov)", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidEFVX3lxTE1RTUdNT2U1bG9RUmhYTm1qUTB1emRSUGdESkFTT1B