eter model that delivers the fastest task completion and highest accuracy for specialized always-on agents.\nJoining it are two new edge models: Nemotron 3.5 Bolt 74B-A9B 24GB, optimized to balance intelligence and speed per GB for local agentic workloads, and Nemotron 3.5 Bolt E4B 4GB, optimized for intelligence per GB for fast conversational agents.\nSuper VL and Bolt 74B-A9B are available now, with Bolt E4B coming Oct. 23.\ntogether cover the full range of agent deployments, from large enterprise servers down to edge devices and local hardware.\nAny organization that wants to run agents on NVIDIA infrastructure now has an open, optimized model for their tier, without having to choose between capability and deployability.\nSuper VL and Bolt join the Nemotron family alongside\nLightning, released in August, handles high-throughput workloads; Ultra handles the hardest reasoning tasks; and Bolt brings intelligence to the edge.\nNemotron 3.5 Super VL: Frontier Accuracy at One-Fifth the Size\nSuper VL, built in collaboration with the\n, delivers leading accuracy on multimodal agentic tasks — instruction following, tool use, multistep business workflows, and visual understanding — comparable with NVIDIA’s largest reasoning model at roughly one-fifth the parameter count.\nNemotron 3.5 Super VL delivers frontier-level intelligence in its class for high-volume agentic workflows.\nAs the fastest open agentic model in its class — 5x faster than the next best — it helps enterprise agents complete high-volume tasks faster across workflows that span days or weeks.\nNemotron 3.5 Super VL, on Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index leadboard, achieves up to 5x faster throughput compared to other models in its class.\nUnlike closed frontier models, organizations can post-train Super VL on their own data, deploy it on their own infrastructure and oversee what they build.\nNemotron 3.5 Bolt: Agentic Intelligence at the Edge\nBolt 74B-A9B 24GB is designed to run everyday agentic tasks locally on NVIDIA RTX/DGX Spark, using a native 2-bit quantization to fit in 24GB of memory and run alongside other applications.\nRelative to other models with a similar memory footprint, it provides a good balance of intelligence and speed: smart enough to get most jobs done, yet much faster than the smartest models.\nWith Bolt 74B-A9B 24GB, users can delegate their projects to popular local agent harnesses — from researching the web to writing to debugging code — all without sending those workloads to the cloud. It is the #1 open model in its class on\nBolt E4B 4GB is built for physical and conversational agents on GeForce RTX, Jetson, and RTX/DGX Spark. It combines natural conversation with multi-turn agentic capabilities to power fast local voice assistants, interactive characters and embedded interfaces.\nDozens of organizations across industries are post-training Super VL on their own data, tools, policies and workflows to push accuracy higher on the specialized tasks that define their agent deployments.\nSuper VL is being post-trained and customized by organizations across industries.\npost-trained the model for Skydance’s vendor credit finance agent and for EY’s M&A due diligence agent.\ncustomized the model for Hebbia for credit agreement analysis.\npost-trained the model for cybersecurity attack resilience.\npost-trained the model on APEX-SWE coding benchmark, for coding agents that plan, write and debug code.\nenables developers to fine-tune the model through its\nframework for specialized multimodal applications.\nAdditional organizations customizing Nemotron 3.5 Super VL include\n, sovereign AI-powered cybersecurity research and vulnerability analysis;\nfor medical claims analysis and revenue cycle management.\npost-trained the model with dermatology data from Nolla, which just became the first company approved for AI to write initial patient prescriptions.\nSoftware platform companies integrating Nemotron 3.5 Super VL include:\nfor tasks like agentic search across apps such as Jira and Confluence.\n, which is customizing the model for agentic blue teaming in enterprise security.\n, powering engineering design and simulation.\n, for search and agentic tasks in Perplexity Search and Computer.\n, which is customizing Nemotron to power AI agents that digitize and automate complex government workflows in secure customer environments through Missionforce Operations.\nsoftware for semiconductor design verification interoperates with the model.\nstrengths are targeted at what agents encounter in practice: optical character recognition, tables, charts and structured information in visual documents.\nIt processes a web page, extracts text, reads graphs and charts, and returns insights from what it finds. The same applies to PDFs and other documents agents commonly work with.\nNemotron 3.5 Super VL understands images, webpages and PDFs and acts on that understanding. Combining text and visual understanding in one model means agents don’t need to route tasks between separate systems — context carries through as work moves from document to data to decision.\nOpen for Maximum Control, Security, and Privacy\nOpenness and customizability reinforce each other. The model ships with open weights, open datasets and training recipes.\nControl runs in two directions. Organizations can run the model wherever their security and data-residency requirements dictate. They also choose to adopt a model they can adapt, rather than one subject to a provider’s restrictions.\nIn insurance, for example, a customized agent can scan claims for fraud markers on repeat. In financial services, an agent can review credit scores and flag activity against an organization’s own lending criteria. The model stays on premises. The data stays private.\nCustomization is supported through NVIDIA NeMo, Python-based pipelines and open datasets.\nNemotron 3.5 Super VL was trained on about 26 trillion tokens across\nand continued pretraining, then post-trained with roughly 3.8 million samples and reinforcement learning across dozens of environments.\nNVIDIA is also releasing open datasets on Hugging Face spanning\n, finance, instruction following, long context, fairness and vision-language understanding, along with the reinforcement learning environments in NeMo Gym.\npackages the model as a production-ready microservice with performance-optimized, validated configurations and enterprise-grade support, refreshed every two weeks with security patches and performance improvements.\nAnd with the model running on NVIDIA Dynamo, organizations can serve up to 8x more users with 85% lower latency.\nNemotron 3.5 Super VL is available now on Hugging Face, Open Router,\nNemotron 3.5 Super VL runs across NVIDIA’s full hardware stack: on\n, RTX PRO and RTX systems for local intelligence at the desktop, on\nat the edge, and across data center and cloud environments.\nis available now on Hugging Face, with support for\n, explore customization resources with NVIDIA NeMo, or read the\nself-paced video tutorials and livestreams\nRTX Spark Ignites Global Celebration as New Windows PCs Arrive\nBe There for the Beginning: Celebrate the Launch of NVIDIA RTX Spark\nOpen From Data Center to Desktop: NVIDIA Expands Nemotron Family With Highest Efficiency Super VL and Bolt Models\nThe Nordics and NVIDIA Show What’s Possible When a Region Commits to Advanced Computing", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "bb8348ecb9e3af14b3300213", "title": "Ondo Court Dismisses UBA Appeal, Upholds N9million Damages Over Wrongful Restriction Of Customer’s Account - Sahara Reporters", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMivwFBVV95cUxObkR0SktaWFFLb1c5VmZVS0JsX0VjY2xOeEQ5S0FEa2psa2lEbU1KSVBXbTBpYk9aaWNveExPaFJZbXRqTzlZTTlaQk1zcHVkMFFMcW92dmlzQl9vQ0lCYnhnMTJoOTljdjdXLTNVN2dXLXp3eEpJeHZscFA1NUc4WTJYSWxaTWtZR05TcW5Wb2RhMWRYQnRMT01BbUY2RUpoenlCNXM1UHQxQ25IVjU3T1AydTdxVEhZamVubDVQdw?", "source": "lane_blast_legal", "published_at": "2026-10-15T15:00:15+00:00", "text": "Ondo Court Dismisses UBA Appeal, Upholds N9million Damages Over Wrongful Restriction Of Customer’s Account - Sahara Reporters\nOndo Court Dismisses UBA Appeal, Upholds N9million Damages Over Wrongful Restriction Of Customer’s Account Sahara Reporters", "truncated": false}, {"id": "3eeb7207e207a87c06eab679", "title": "Samuel L. Jackson’s ‘Tulsa King’ Spin-Off Officially Sets Release Date With First Trailer - Collider", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiZEFVX3lxTE1DTmxsNmhNYmRzQldxNGVFMFFINnR4dE9BR0dFRXFpWVppU2RkaDVxdFF4SXUxOGNkc2ZVWGFXVEowQXhwMXl0SW5VOTRfamI3dkcxRzF0cm9FOHBSUkV6bkxrOVY?oc=5", "source": "lane_corporate_action_spinoff", "published_at": "2026-10-15T15:00:13+00:00", "text": "Samuel L. Jackson’s ‘Tulsa King’ Spin-Off Officially Sets Release Date With First Trailer - Collider\nSamuel L. Jackson’s ‘Tulsa King’ Spin-Off Officially Sets Release Date With First Trailer Collider", "truncated": false}, {"id": "d9e40c1e28588cff59b3bc1f", "title": "Was Neanderthal life violent? This wound suggests lethal fighting was more common", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6jrwn3y3xe9o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T15:00:05+00:00", "text": "Was Neanderthal life violent? This wound suggests lethal fighting was more common\nA wooden spear caused the hole in this Neanderthal shoulder blade, scientists say\nA Neanderthal who died 75,000 years ago may have been struck through the chest by a sharpened wooden spear, archaeologists say.\nThey say a 15mm hole found in a shoulder blade in a cave in Iraqi Kurdistan could be the earliest evidence of lethal violence involving such a weapon.\nFrom the angle of the wound and other evidence, the researchers believe the spear entered the individual's chest and passed through the body - probably damaging a lung and major blood vessels - and exited through the left shoulder blade to leave a near-perfect hole.\nThe research is the latest in emerging evidence that suggests Neanderthal societies experienced more violence than previously thought.\nNeanderthals were a separate species of humans that went extinct around 40,000 years ago. They lived across a large expanse of Europe and western and central Asia, and sometimes overlapped with our species, Homo sapiens.\nThe wounded Neanderthal's remains were found in a cave in Iraqi Kurdistan. As well as the left shoulder blade, the individual's right hand, three ribs and some teeth were also found.\nA hole in the shoulder blade may be the earliest evidence of \"lethal interpersonal violence\" from a wooden spear, according to Dr Emma Pomeroy, study lead author from Cambridge's Department of Archaeology.\n\"The fatal strike may have been an intended heart shot,\" she told BBC News.\nScientists have used Neanderthal remains to imagine what they could have looked like\nArchaeologists call the remains Shanidar A, named after the cave in which they were found. Remains of at least 10 Neanderthals were discovered in excavations in the cave in the 1950s.\nThe wound is thought to be the first evidence for use of sharpened wooden spears in the prehistoric Middle East, according to Pomeroy.\n\"It is not the kind of break that you would get naturally, because it is so circular,\" she said. \"Experiments with reconstructed wooden spears produce very similar round holes in bone.\"\nBut the interpersonal violence is by no means certain, she says. It may be a case of friendly fire, a wound caused as a result of a hunting accident.\nBut Prof Chris Stringer of the Natural History Museum in London, who was not involved in the research, told BBC News that an attack by a rival was the more plausible explanation.\n\"The researchers leave it open whether this resulted from a hunting accident or interpersonal violence, but in my view the latter seems more likely, given the positioning and extent of the wound,\" he said.\nThis discovery has echoes of an earlier find in the same cave, in 1957. Those remains involved another Neanderthal who lived more recently, around 50, 000 years ago. Their ribs had evidence of a partly healed wound, which archaeologists believe was probably caused by a stone-tipped spear.\nWe can't tell for sure whether these Neanderthals were hurt by a companion during a hunt or murdered by a rival. But they join a small but growing body of evidence which point to the possibility that human-on-human violence was more common and goes back further than the current scientific consensus would suggest.\n\"I unfortunately see the growing body of evidence as an indication that interpersonal violence goes deep into human prehistory,\" Stringer told BBC News.\nA key piece of that evidence is a study published in 2022 of very early Neanderthal skulls from northern Spain at an archaeological site called \"the Pit of Bones\". It suggests that deadly violence was taking place about 430,000 years ago.\nResearchers found 57 healed head injuries among 20 individuals. Nine individuals had potentially fatal skull fractures. Six had similar puncture wounds at the back-left of the head. Because the wounds repeatedly occurred in almost the same place, the researchers concluded that deliberate blows were more likely than accidental damage. One skull had two wounds that had probably been inflicted with the same object.\nThe Pit of Bones findings do not show that everyone in this population was habitually violent, but they provide strong evidence that repeated killing occurred among these people hundreds of thousands of years ago.\nFor decades, many archaeologists linked the beginnings of organised warfare to farming, when people began living in permanent settlements and competing over land, livestock and food stores.\nBut evidence from earlier hunter-gatherers and still older human relatives has since shown that individual killings pre-date farming by a very long way.\nThe live scientific debate is over how common such violence was, and when sporadic attacks became organised conflict and wars between groups.\nThe research has been published in the journal Scientific Reports.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "57b76aa81b22738df258e1ba", "title": "Hole in Neanderthal shoulder bone linked to ancient wooden spear - BBC", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiW0FVX3lxTE9hekFjRDFFVG1oVUFJQnBMeWl4RFBWaUlrakJ1WEZiV3RfNlR5WDhTQWdvSUI2MXRReWZ1QXVoZFYxbS1ZQl9ZMzQ0b1R1NF9xX3QzeDdKQVVaN1U?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T15:00:05+00:00", "text": "Hole in Neanderthal shoulder bone linked to ancient wooden spear - BBC\nHole in Neanderthal shoulder bone linked to ancient wooden spear BBC A murder mystery reveals realities of Neanderthal life and death CNN Bones from Iraqi cave hint at violence and compassion in Neanderthals Reuters 75,000 years ago, this Neanderthal was stabbed with a spear Popular Science News - Possible Fatal Wound Identified in Neanderthal Bone Archaeology Magazine", "truncated": false}, {"id": "f11b45ea662a0e8e3e73a666", "title": "The rumor mill at work: When gossip protects coworkers and when it backfires - AP News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiqwFBVV95cUxPdkpzdHViYkVnRWszMkNSX0ZGdmtGaERGLUYzeTY5dmZRVVkzSmRZZXBNYjlxck1zdVc4MmlfdV96VHFTYjJQeko1Vms1X2R1WXo4dlhFYmRVSE5oSnAwZWpieHNIdTFoVHZfM0ExUExFTGl6YnhmOC0yTk1rd2ZnckVBZG5WM1BvY1F3TnRPclVaOHhoNHNqcC1oeloyWEliaXI2UnJObWs3MHc?oc=5", "source": "rss_ap_business:google_fallback", "published_at": "2026-10-15T15:00:00+00:00", "text": "The rumor mill at work: When gossip protects coworkers and when it backfires - AP News\nThe rumor mill at work: When gossip protects coworkers and when it backfires AP News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "eb87a961690530ae6e851651", "title": "NVIDIA's Nemotron 3.5 Super VL with post-training beats Claude Opus 5, GPT-6.1 Sol on specialized task benchmarks - VentureBeat", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi6wFBVV95cUxOLWRFblUzdUF1ejJTVzQwQ2s0WHhj