"text": "U.S. Treasury dismisses IMF debt warning and 'messy' forecasting\nTreasury counselor David Zervos told CNBC the IMF should examine its own forecasting record before criticizing U.S. fiscal policy.\nIMF Managing Director Kristalina Georgieva said economic growth alone would not reduce U.S. debt fast enough and called for fiscal measures.\nThe exchange comes as U.S. debt exceeds $40 trillion and 10-year Treasury yields remain above 5%, intensifying scrutiny of the fiscal outlook.\nDavid Zervos, Chief Market Strategist of Jefferies LLC, speaks during an interview with CNBC on the floor at the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) in New York City, U.S., September 3, 2025.\nThe International Monetary Fund should check its own forecasting record before criticizing U.S. fiscal policy, David Zervos,\nnewly appointed counselor to the Treasury Secretary\n\"When we see pieces on the IMF, it's good to remember how messy their forecasts have been in the past,\" Zervos said in emailed comments to CNBC.\nThe former Wall Street economist was responding to the IMF's Managing Director Kristalina Georgieva, who cautioned during the IMF-World Bank annual meetings in Bangkok on Wednesday that strong U.S. economic growth would\nnot be sufficient to put mounting federal debt\nGeorgieva was herself responding to U.S. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent, who told CNBC in August that the U.S. could\nAnalysis: Why a $2 trillion deficit and 5% Treasury yields are becoming America's biggest economic challenge\nZervos, formerly chief market strategist at Jefferies, said Thursday: \"In 2008, [the IMF's World Economic Outlook] had\nand we had the first global contraction in post-world history a year later. We saw them positive on Southeast Asia in 1997 before the collapse.\"\n\"We saw them very negative on Russia in 2022, and Russia actually barely saw a blip down in growth\n. We saw them very positive on Argentina after their\n. And the history books go on and on, whether it's Greece, whether it was the\n\"So take it all with a grain of salt,\" he added.\nTicking debt time bomb: Global debt hits record $365 trillion\nhave raised the risk of an unsustainable debt spiral. However, they are divided on whether the U.S. could be pushed into a\nThe IMF's Georgieva has issued similar warnings to other big economies over the need for fiscal consolidation, telling CNBC on Oct. 7 that her\nmessage to France was: \"Get your house in order.\"\nOther economic institutions are also sounding the alarm over the rising cost of servicing global debt, which\ntopped $365 trillion in the first half of the year\n. The Institute of International Finance argued in a September report that a \"vicious cycle between elections and short-term quick fixes\" to debt challenges was reducing the ability to spend money on public services.\nChoose CNBC as your preferred source on Google and never miss a moment from the most trusted name in business news.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "c2c0c8ca06720c2e5406e4ef", "title": "Accenture Bets on AI, Targets Mid-Single-Digit Growth for the Next Two Years: Read-Across for Indian IT - Kalkine India", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi3wFBVV95cUxQcUFTemtfM3NLVFdfbDZVMUY1OFVSRzJxNUs1LURVSlltX3JzdnlWNDB6M3hVU1J5dWEwNlJHYTBXTGN2Q3FJZzRJZ3ZUbmFZLUlRbzE1T2JINExUSWZZdEx3SGJFWWZMNlQ3ZUF6SW0tdXdrblFuNVFUY05KZTREckw5ZXMxaGpySWRMQ28tVDZYRUFOTTJtZ2E1SHNERHB3aTFKQmlSdzI0SHNXbW95SWJlRUtkMF9XYzVoMk9HY0w", "source": "lane_peer_spill", "published_at": "2026-10-15T13:42:00+00:00", "text": "Accenture Bets on AI, Targets Mid-Single-Digit Growth for the Next Two Years: Read-Across for Indian IT - Kalkine India\nAccenture Bets on AI, Targets Mid-Single-Digit Growth for the Next Two Years: Read-Across for Indian IT Kalkine India", "truncated": false}, {"id": "79d6a206cada17c38d0ef9c7", "title": "Compare Today’s Refinance Rates - Forbes", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidkFVX3lxTE9ERXVoRmdrMnFCZHB0cDEwZlUwRDZCTmdQYUVURFplYm5