Confirms His Return in Samuel L. Jackson’s ‘Tulsa King’ Spin-Off [Exclusive] - IMDb", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiZ0FVX3lxTFBOdlFkdzZEZUd6bmU5bnJkb2toVjZPdjByVnRTX2tBa25JQktuUkd3dzJpNUxQOTFCQmVQbTdIb3Z5RUZhVjZSWXlCVDZCUk5maE1xM0F3ZEY5N0tWZ3UzeGpXMzNXQlE?oc=5", "source": "lane_corporate_action_spinoff", "published_at": "2026-10-15T11:35:42+00:00", "text": "Sylvester Stallone Officially Confirms His Return in Samuel L. Jackson’s ‘Tulsa King’ Spin-Off [Exclusive] - IMDb\nSylvester Stallone Officially Confirms His Return in Samuel L. Jackson’s ‘Tulsa King’ Spin-Off [Exclusive] IMDb", "truncated": false}, {"id": "0b7274ba05c98359d99b394f", "title": "AI giant Anthropic woos potential investors ahead of stock market debut", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqzjx448vxkgo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_business", "published_at": "2026-10-15T11:34:55+00:00", "text": "AI giant Anthropic woos potential investors ahead of stock market debut\nArtificial intelligence (AI) giant Anthropic has been drumming up support for its expected stock market listing as it seeks to reassure investors of its profit-making potential.\nAnthropic revenue surged by 45% to $16.8bn (Â£12.7bn) in the three months to the end of September, the BBC understands.\nIn common with other AI firms, it is seeking to position itself as being able to generate huge profits despite spending billions on tech.\nIt also emphasised safety at its event after warnings by current and former researchers of serious and even existential risks to humanity posed by AI.\nAnthropic's revenue has risen in successive quarters this year, from $4.7bn in the first three months to $11.6bn the following period.\nThe company also told investors during the event at its headquarters in San Francisco that it had made a third quarter profit on an adjusted basis, an accounting method which ignores non-recurring costs.\nInvestors reportedly left the Anthropic investor event optimistic about the company's rapid revenue growth and path to profitability.\nAlongside concerns about profit generation, there have also been fears about the safety of AI models.\nAnthropic has been vocal about AI's risks, with its chief executive Dario Amodei\ncalling on the pace of development to slow down\nIn September one of its researchers warned of a greater than 10% chance that AI could \"kill all humans\"\n, and a former researcher said people working on the technology were \"genuinely frightened\"\nabout the speed of its advancements and what it could mean for humanity\nThe firm itself said in September it had identified and disrupted attempts to use its AI model for \"malicious activity\" which\ncould support the development of biological weapons\nHowever, US President Donald Trump has said researchers have been \"\n\" and that in a race between the US and China to develop the technology, \"whoever wins AI, wins\".\nRival tech firm OpenAI has also warned about safety, with its chief executive Sam Altman saying in September that his company will not go public this year as it addresses concerns.\nThe ChatGPT-developer is also eyeing a bumper stock market listing, which is expected to value the firm at more than $1tn.\nHow backlash against data centres could shape the US midterm elections\n'I applied for 200 jobs' - How 'spray and pray' AI job applications can backfire\nAnthropic's revenue figures, along with those of rival OpenAI, are an important gauge of the AI industry's growth and ability to finance the infrastructure needed to develop the technology.\nStocks in some US technology companies dropped on Wednesday over concerns around investments in AI.\nShares in chip makers - including AMD, Nvidia and Intel - fell sharply.\nIn Asia, shares in Japanese tech investment firm Softbank were down by more than 9% on Thursday. South Korean semiconductor giants also dropped, with Samsung falling\nShares have been falling this week after remarks by Hock Tan, the chief executive of US tech manufacturer Broadcom, over AI spending.\nTan said at a conference in Singapore on Monday that many investors have \"no clue\" and have set \"extreme expectations only to find out that it's almost like they are in a casino\".\nThe falls deepened after US inflation data released on Wednesday showed price rises accelerated by more than expected in September.\nInvestors are concerned that higher inflation will mean the US Federal Reserve is more likely to put up the cost of borrowing.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "4e99aac43bfb601917850355", "title": "From Import Ban to Smuggling Complicity: How Pakistani Medicines Still Reach Afghanistan’s Markets - 8AM Media", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMif0FVX3lxTFByZWZEVWdBLUdNOVA5SGxzcXpSd1JMd2ZndWplekZLM2pSQVFXSFRsY2ZSMlJpSlpkTTJTbWpzZFFPZWtjc2F5M0VLNFZoUkpSc0JmTThMbi01V25EbC1oZzNxM010Q016clVTQlNKLW9VZ3ZMTHVDVkdKNEQ4Wjg?oc=5", "source": "lane_access_control", "published_at": "2026-10-15T11:33:59+00:00", "text": "From Import Ban to Smuggling Complicity: How Pakistani Medicines Still Reach Afghanistan’s Markets - 8AM Media\nFrom Import Ban to Smuggling Complicity: How Pakistani Medicines Still Reach Afghanistan’s Markets 8AM Media", "truncated": false}, {"id": "dbc9b6f15aa91aa48dfcd94d", "title": "Fuel, input costs squeeze cement companies' margins despite healthy volume growth - Moneycontrol.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi6wFBVV95cUxPcm5ZQVdoRzVLYmFla2dBb09kT09oUmxoTU1PelBkeW1tNDRORUlNMzZTWjVVR1F0Z1h3N0R1ZVc1Ylp6YzhUSldneGozTEVfaTNnbm42MTZUVk5WLUZKMHBFdGwxcXR3V3Z2dEI5bmNmVExFSkJGNC01WUVzQnlpdHlkcXZ4aHdDckZlcUt3ejJzUURXT3dKNDM0UDZ4TDc4M3NIZ19IZWdkX1BxTTNpbG5menB0TjJkcGI5MHhJTEZ", "source": "lane_input_cost", "published_at": "2026-10-15T11:32:00+00:00", "text": "Fuel, input costs squeeze cement companies' margins despite healthy volume growth - Moneycontrol.com\nFuel, input costs squeeze cement companies' margins despite healthy volume growth Moneycontrol.com",