T11:29:47+00:00", "text": "360 One Wam Dividend Alert: Company announces 750% interim dividend; check record date - Zee Business\n360 One Wam Dividend Alert: Company announces 750% interim dividend; check record date Zee Business", "truncated": false}, {"id": "9e744b9a110a0a0a780c3fa1", "title": "Ag Economy Barometer | Agricultural sentiment declines as input costs weigh on producers - https://en.edairynews.com/", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMilwFBVV95cUxQb3VXMXRRbDhaR3FPQUp0TkkwMTZxTXA5bENXU2l4Zks4dWJCRDdqdGpzSm8xSWIwbWhiNnoxdDhjVWhJdDJreDE5RE9ybWF6Z2FLbzJGdWZPS01KV0VHT2tWOTJxUVpDYXhVOGY1LVhYTUU4bmEzUVhKNnhLd25POW0zbzN3QTMwT0pweTZqUVcyaHJWRjZB?oc=5", "source": "lane_input_cost", "published_at": "2026-10-15T11:29:03+00:00", "text": "Ag Economy Barometer | Agricultural sentiment declines as input costs weigh on producers - https://en.edairynews.com/\nAg Economy Barometer | Agricultural sentiment declines as input costs weigh on producers https://en.edairynews.com/", "truncated": false}, {"id": "851070ff997f31552695dd50", "title": "South America: a growing market for motorbikes – KTM opens plant in Manaus, Brazil - SPEEDWEEK.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMifEFVX3lxTE9SWFdWNEQ3aE9ja0VKbmJEWkRSNGJnMUlfOEF5cHhyVVd6QnA3ekh1WnBRdEdyYkR2VU1QWElRaUxmMkcwQUtFYXVnUWZ2UW9rbW5sVXpBckVEakNaemp2a1drZ2JpdUVfYU4yMHFCbVZTQTQtakE5NmJDNlA?oc=5", "source": "lane_capacity", "published_at": "2026-10-15T11:28:26+00:00", "text": "South America: a growing market for motorbikes – KTM opens plant in Manaus, Brazil - SPEEDWEEK.com\nSouth America: a growing market for motorbikes – KTM opens plant in Manaus, Brazil SPEEDWEEK.com", "truncated": false}, {"id": "0820a407d796fc03a5581491", "title": "New 'Rocky' Spin-Off Clip Spotlights Star's Jaw-Dropping Transformation Into Sylvester Stallone - MovieWeb", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMifkFVX3lxTFA3cTlYTlhrUzZXS2VKbTkwTEJCY3NkRE9LRHdrRXBsTmtzcXhmZFl1ZDBvUFFmUVAyeWo3RXFFVXJQNTQwSDE2QlNoNG4xMThUN2Z0ZDJRdWJ4YTROWTJhNmU2NkEzWW5tY1Q0ampCaGZGZjM4WkVKOGRwZkdldw?oc=5", "source": "lane_corporate_action_spinoff", "published_at": "2026-10-15T11:26:03+00:00", "text": "New 'Rocky' Spin-Off Clip Spotlights Star's Jaw-Dropping Transformation Into Sylvester Stallone - MovieWeb\nNew 'Rocky' Spin-Off Clip Spotlights Star's Jaw-Dropping Transformation Into Sylvester Stallone MovieWeb", "truncated": false}, {"id": "6dbdbd1096f471b83fd6dc53", "title": "Why is Starlink internet so important on the battlefield in Ukraine?", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqkgk4zg54reo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T11:25:19+00:00", "text": "Why is Starlink internet so important on the battlefield in Ukraine?\nThe Starlink terminals, which connect to thousands of SpaceX satellites in obit, are often camouflaged on the battlefield\nStarlink satellites operated by Elon Musk's SpaceX provide high-speed internet - something modern war relies heavily on.\nFor Ukraine, it is vital for communications on the battlefield and effective drone warfare.\nUsing Starlink, Ukrainian drones can stay connected to operators over longer distances than conventional radio networks and are less vulnerable to jamming.\nBut on Tuesday night, Starlink suddenly and unexpectedly\nrestricted the speed at which drones could fly while still maintaining internet connection\n. This disrupted some battlefield \"missions\", a top Ukrainian official said, and hampered Kyiv's ability to protect itself from incoming Russian attacks.\nThe restriction at 100mph (160km/h) meant that any drone travelling faster than that lost connection and was effectively useless.\n\"Starlink gives Ukraine the ability to see what's coming, to protect people,\" Kurt Volker, former US ambassador to Nato, told the BBC's Radio 4 Today programme. \"If you cut that off, then more people are going to get killed - and that is a very cynical and callous decision to slow that down the way that has been done.\"\nConnectivity had been restored by Wednesday evening.\nThe move has once again shown how Starlink can impact the conflict, which has been raging since Russia launched its full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022.\nOperators use the Starlink network to stay connected to weapons like drones, so they can see targets in real time\nStarlink is an internet network that uses satellites in low Earth orbit to provide connectivity, particularly in remote areas and battlefields.\nIt currently relies on more than 10,000 active satellites, making Starlink the world's largest satellite constellation.\nIt is owned by Elon Musk's SpaceX, and is the company's biggest revenue driver.\nWhile Starlink is for civilian use, Starshield is its military-grade sister service, designed for the Pentagon.\nStarshield relies on the Starlink network, but has greater security like enhanced encryption so that sensitive information can be transmitted without interception.\nThe US government has allowed Ukraine to use Starlink and Starshield in its defence against Russia.\nMusk cut Moscow's access to the service in February\n, giving Kyiv an advantage in the conflict.\nPut simply, Starlink uses its thousands of satellites to ping high-speed internet back to almost every corner of the globe, using terminals on the ground that users can easily buy and set up themselves. As Musk said, they have two instructions: \"point at sky, plug in\".\nThis makes them ideal for use on the battlefield.\n\"It's a connectionâ¦ that you can basically use pretty fast, you don't need any special equipment, you don't need to prepare, you just go to the field and usually it works,\" Neo, a soldier with one of Ukraine's drone reconnaissance units, told the Today programme.\n\"It simplifies our work because we can translate or stream our reconnaissance operations,\" he added.\nThe network has become \"the backbone of military communications in Ukraine\", George Barros from the Institute for the Study of War (ISW) said, and is used across Kyiv's naval, land, and air forces.\nIn Ukraine, both Starlink (the civilian service) and Starshield (the military service) are used, depending on what communications are needed.\nIt is crucial for the effective use of weapons such as drones, which connect to the network so that operators can see their targets in real time, effectively minimising the time between \"sensor-to shooter\", Barros told the BBC.\nBecause Russia is not authorised to use Starlink, when Ukraine sends long-range missiles deep into Russia to hit oil refineries, the network does not work.\nWhen it comes to Ukraine's self-defence, it relies on the Starlink network to protect its own skies against incoming Russian attacks, especially jet-powered Shahed drones, Barros added.\nShaheds fly at speeds in excess of 198mph (319 km/h), meaning the Starlink restriction made Ukrainian efforts to chase them down all but impossible.\nFor Neo, this means that any deliberate restrictions on the Starlink network are \"a betrayal\".\nWith no ready replacement for the Starlink network, any speed restrictions or loss of connectivity have major implications for Ukraine defences, and the recent limits have shown how dependent Kyiv is on the network.\nStarlink terminals, like this one in Ukraine's Donetsk region, are quick and easy to set up, making them ideal for use on the battlefield\nWhile Starlink can provide access nearly everywhere in the world, that does not mean it can be accessed everywhere, and countries must be licenced to use it.\nRussia is one place where it is not authorised, although Kyiv has in the past said that Moscow was still getting hold of the terminals and using them in occupied territory in Ukraine.\n\"If SpaceX obtains knowledge that Starlink terminal is being used by a sanctioned or unauthorized party, we investigate the claim and take actions to deactivate the terminal if confirmed,\" Starlink said in 2024.\nBut with tens of thousands of terminals in Ukraine alone, it could be a near impossible task to determine which terminals are authorised and which ones are not.\nDuring a visit to London on Wednesday, Zelensky had said he hoped it was just a mistake, however, in a later interview with the Financial Times, he said: \"It was not a technical problem, it was the will.\"\nMusk said that Starshield is managed by the US government and suggested the Pentagon could be responsible.\n\"Starshield systems are owned and controlled by the US government,\"\n. \"Any limits on speed or usage are decided by the US military and ultimately POTUS [the president of the United States].\"\nHe added: \"Starlink is a commercial service owned and controlled by SpaceX. Usage of Starlink is limited to civilian or defensive activities and cannot be used for offensive military action, as doing so would be inappropriate for a commercial company.\"\nThe BBC has contacted the White House and Pentagon for comment.\nA Ukrainian soldier attaches a Starlink transmitter to a 'Vampire' attack drone in the Donetsk region, eastern Ukraine, in June\nThe restrictions on Tuesday night came as Russia launched deadly attacks against Ukraine that left 600,000 without power and hit communications and energy facilities used by Ukraine's military. Residents said they received no warning of incoming drones before they hit.\n\"It is clear that this action cannot be a coincidence and is related to the current political situation,\" Zelensky's adviser Serhiy Beskrestnov wrote on Telegram on Wednesday morning.\nRelations between the US and Ukraine have worsened recently, especially since the US announced a deal with Russia to import its diesel into the American market.\nZelensky called it a gift to Russian President Vladimir Putin, and in response Donald Trump said he thinks \"it's time for Ukraine to get a new president\".\nOn Sunday, Trump announced a ceasefire on energy infrastructure, but three days later,\n, accusing Russia of ignoring it (the Kremlin said it had never agreed to a ceasefire), and the US of withdrawing crucial support.\nZelensky has accused the US of threatening to withhold intelligence if Ukraine continues striking Russian oil facilities. Trump has denied this, but last week told reporters that Zelensky had \"better damn well stop\" the strikes.\nWhile in London, Zelensky agreed that he felt betrayed by the US.\nTrump's deal with Russia is his administration's latest effort to lower US fuel prices ahead of next month's midterm elections, after months spent grappling with the political consequences of the Iran war. That conflict, which started in February, has seen prices skyrocket, souring his standing with the American public.\nBurnham treads carefully with Zelensky visit, balancing Trump and Ukraine\nRu", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "81665439498193efdfc61f12", "title": "Auriginal Mining Insiders Lose Out As Stock Sinks To CA$0.05 - Simply Wall Street", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi2wFBVV95cUxQMnpLZURGdGRGUng4TzdqcFREQ05fLTFEZDFBQ01JVWxTb1dxNzlBXzJWWEFjRXhfV2JFeFFmb1pIMXJhektaRHJKb01PZW5VNGtjUUhyRmliVDY5VFZTVUNiMTVnN3NfdXVQUHdqcHRIRjE2cHJkbjdoS3ZadUZ3YTFpa3dDcUhMSldVX0lhNF83QWhIR3VzUFc2Xzc0N3FrNmREdFpDSzF3WXQzd3J2YVg3Qmo5bzVqUzRUWWJqRjZ", "source": "lane_insider_flow", "published_at": "2026-10-15T11:24:04+00:00", "text": "Auriginal Mining Insiders Lose Out As Stock Sinks To CA$0.05 - Simply Wall Street\nAuriginal Mining Insiders Lose Out As Stock Sinks To CA$0.05 Simply Wall Street", "truncated": false}, {"id": "dd84b2b4500773fcaddf1a2e", "title": "Reynolds Puts Nearly 183,000 U.S. Contracted Outlets Under Age-Verification Rules, Covering 94% of Volume — Can It Shift an Unauthorized Vape Market? - 2Firsts", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMigAJBVV95cUxNWmN5R1JmY0c0cWRGWFRiaEJISU9ucWFtbTVQYTZwaXpRSElqal9qRWh1WW9GV0c2Ukk0QVFXaFN6U05KVzQ2T05xOWNCeUhfclI3eGY3OFZ3cXVhU19RcE5SWTdzcmFMaTB4X1hoYTlYTFBtc29wNTh1Ry1WUjBXMzZfMmFTbHhYYTlDR29HTXhlblV3WmIxMTZUaH