0amRRRExvbVpTM1cyRUNfQjlxd09hNDNQM01WQWtwUkJHOHdjX3dRRThsUVRuZGcxRXF3SWlBYWotWEhHdHMycHA?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T10:05:55+00:00", "text": "EU leaders to urge Zelenskyy to rein in criticism of Trump - Financial Times\nEU leaders to urge Zelenskyy to rein in criticism of Trump Financial Times Zelensky says Ukraine has right to defend itself after Trump calls latest strikes a 'disgrace' BBC EU rallies in support of Ukraine amid Trump standoff Al Jazeera Zelensky praises ‘real leaders’ in Europe as his relationship with Trump deteriorates CNN Zelensky praises ‘real leaders’ in Europe with Trump relationship strained The Hill", "truncated": false}, {"id": "21cfcfde28364c476ff2e797", "title": "Survey: 47% of Mississippi high earners say their income tax bill should be higher - Darkhorse Press", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMixgFBVV95cUxQcy1PbmdjMjJVNWRXVHgzU0w1d3c5Vng2RmRNZUFXaUNIMHkxNWJlS2FkRU1ZM1hzMEV4VVdxTlZQY2QyQnVLUGoxVmdBUEktc1VSRF9nTExkaW14cklQaDZtNERfc0E1SFNsdlprbXlab2ZaMmlmS2U0OU5JNFNHWk1lQ01BbVFVbU4yblA5VG5aN1VNVDl2TjRFbzlSVnl5bVBOc19kRGJsX0JEZjVWTk1ESEZNNTktYWhwSmc0Tkp", "source": "lane_tax_fiscal", "published_at": "2026-10-15T10:05:52+00:00", "text": "Survey: 47% of Mississippi high earners say their income tax bill should be higher - Darkhorse Press\nSurvey: 47% of Mississippi high earners say their income tax bill should be higher Darkhorse Press", "truncated": false}, {"id": "6c74bc37416ac683bef63988", "title": "Two dead in Istanbul after heaviest rainfall in 84 years, governor says", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cq398x7j1lrwo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T10:05:29+00:00", "text": "Two dead in Istanbul after heaviest rainfall in 84 years, governor says\nFlooding in the Kucukcekmece district of Istanbul\nTwo people have died after severe flooding caused by what the governor of Istanbul said was the heaviest rainfall in 84 years, BBC Turkish has reported.\nA14-year-old boy died after he was electrocuted when he stepped in a puddle, while a 34-year-old man attempting to cross the road slipped and fell, hitting his head on the pavement.\nNearly 600 people have been rescued after becoming stranded, the city's deputy mayor said, with cars submerged in water in some districts.\nResidents have been told by Istanbul Governor Davut GÃ¼l to avoid leaving their homes unless absolutely necessary.\nThe city's governorship is investigating the death of the teenaged boy who was electrocuted.\nSome areas of Istanbul have seen rainfall equivalent to one seventh of the annual average, according to the interior minister. Schools were ordered to close on Thursday and Friday, as the rain is expected to continue.\nNuri Aslan, deputy mayor of the city, said 595 people had been evacuated to safe areas after becoming trapped, while more than 8,000 workers were responding to the flooding.\n\"I urge all our citizens to remain vigilant against the expected heavy rain and storm, particularly during nighttime hours,\" he wrote on X.\nHe told people to use public transport instead of private vehicles, with the city's metro hours expanded to 02:00 local time.\nThe flooding also impacted air travel, with 46 planes unable to land at airports in Istanbul, BBC Turkish said.\nA car park in a shopping centre was flooded in the Sariyer district, with multiple vehicles submerged. Sections of roads in other areas collapsed, according to local media, and a river has burst its banks.\nMore than 16 million people live in the broader Istanbul metropolitan area. The interior ministry has also warned of sudden flooding in several provinces.\nIn response, employees who are disabled, pregnant or veterans will be granted administrative leave on Thursday, unless they work in health, safety, and rainwater drainage fields, Turkish media reported. Women working in the public sector who have children under the age of eight will also be granted leave.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "34cb638e2c856701137b145d", "title": "Predator Oil & Gas shares fall 42% as company announces £2.5 million placing - Share Talk", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiogFBVV95cUxNcTBYTk5jRWx4dFBBX0JwNkZ3UFdmdEI5T05KbG5MVmJPb0dVZ0Z3NmRDVW9KRy03b25ROXVsT0pacG1VSmdqbWxOOFdGZHQ5T0l0TWY1aEJqdUdkWGNTcTJYZlhSdFd1UGw5Uzhrd2lncktYdTI4dTVSUjI2NkFzYjBxd2pVWG5FQkc5ZTRJWXB0OUtXc2Jac3NPWVZrcVBkT2c?oc=5", "source": "lane_statement_public", "published_at": "2026-10-15T10:05:18+00:00", "text": "Predator Oil & Gas shares fall 42% as company announces £2.5 million placing - Share Talk\nPredator Oil & Gas shares fall 42% as company announces £2.5 million placing Share Talk", "truncated": false}, {"id": "8f1a51f1c78587093dd0c59e", "title": "Brazil's Congress approves shorter working week in win for Lula - BBC", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiW0FVX3lxTFBEOFF6VlNoZVZOYkNMUFduM1l4S2ZSTGl5bW5id0tYa2Rkcmk1UEhLdlRDZjVMZGNhWnRVSHp3WTk5cVlLSUpqdXhmU2pBRHhfZjIwSFprXzhlYU0?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T10:05:17+00:00", "text": "Brazil's Congress approves shorter working week in win for Lula - BBC\nBrazil's Congress approves shorter working week in win for Lula BBC Brazil abolishes six-day workweek in win for Lula Financial Times As Lula Passes Shortened Workweek, Polls Show Race Tied Up –- Bolsonaro Attacks Unions in Court Payday Report Lula pledges free weekend transit nationwide as Brazil runoff nears Yahoo Brazil cuts work week to 40 hours in win for Lula ahead of runoff The Guardian", "truncated": false}, {"id": "63493345091f6632ee39c047", "title": "Brazil's Congress approves shorter working week in win for Lula", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmwy43pp08elo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T10:05:17+00:00", "text": "Brazil's Congress approves shorter working week in win for Lula\nThe measure had broad support and trade unions held rallies in its favour in recent weeks\nBrazil's Congress has approved a constitutional amendment that will cut the working week from 44 hours to 40 and give employees two days off per week without cutting their salary.\nThe change represents the biggest overhaul to workers' rights since Brazil's return to democracy in 1988.\nSenators passed the amendment, which left-wing President Luiz InÃ¡cio Lula da Silva had championed, less than two weeks before a second round in the Latin American nation's presidential election.\nLula's supporters are hoping that its passage, which is estimated to benefit more than 30 million workers, will boost his chances of closing in on his right-wing rival, FlÃ¡vio Bolsonaro, in the 25 October run-off.\nThe son of former President Jair Bolsonaro won the first round of the presidential election with 47% of the vote, ahead of Lula on 45.2%. Both fell short of the 50% of valid votes needed to stave off a decisive second round.\nOpinion polls suggest that the move to cut the working week is very popular, with one nationwide survey finding that almost 70% of Brazilians backed it.\nThe measure had already passed in the Chamber of Deputies, Brazil's lower house, in May, before senators overwhelmingly backed the measure on Wednesday by 75 votes to one.\nFlÃ¡vio Bolsonaro, who is currently a senator, dropped his previous opposition to the measure and also cast a \"yes\" vote.\nAnnouncing his change of heart on Tuesday, he argued that he had been swayed by the measure's popularity, commenting: \"Most Brazilian workers âwant to â see the end of the six-day workweek, so I'm going to vote in favour of ending it.\"\nThe younger Bolsonaro had earlier denounced the proposal as an \"opportunistic\" attempt by his presidential rival to appeal to working-class voters.\nBefore his about-turn, he had said that it should be down to employees and employers to agree on a work schedule, rather than restrictions being imposed by a constitutional amendment.\nLula described Bolsonaro's policy reversal as \"electoral opportunism\" and celebrated the amendment's approval, saying it would make a real difference to workers.\n\"It means more time to live, rest, be with family, watch your children grow up, look after yourself and enjoy life,\" he wrote on social media.\nParent groups were among those to back the change\nCritics of the measure have warned that it will raise production costs, which will eventually be passed on to the consumer, resulting in higher prices.\nBut its supporters argue that having two days off a week will allow workers to be better rested and therefore more productive in the hours that they do work.\nThe change is due to be phased in, with the working week first being shortened to 42 hours after a two-month grace period and then again to 40 hours at a later stage.\nThe changes will take more than a year to entirely take effect but many of its backers are already celebrating.\nLuciana de Almeida, 36, who works six days a week as a cashier in a supermarket, said it would \"make a lot of difference\" to her life.\nThe single mother, who lives in Rio de Janeiro, said she has been missing out on time with her three children as the only day off she had was usually spent doing domestic chores.\n\"My body, and my mind in particular, are very tired,\" she told BBC Brasil.\n\"I need time with my daughters, have some leisure time, or even just some time resting at home.\"\nRight-wing FlÃ¡vio Bolsonaro wins first round of Brazil election\nWatch: How Brazil's dramatic election unfolded\nBrazil's Lula bans online gambling ahead of presidential election", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "64ec07e66554e6c543ac2ee2", "title": "‘Sodcit’ – after battlefield setbacks, Russia returns to destroying Ukraine’s morale - The Conversation", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiswFBVV95cUxOSkQyN0RPYnFPbGE5aG5LX3puS0YyOGtXaTVYTW9SVG5DcjBELVJvYUpCd1lBanJZbWFlMGp6bXZ0d0w2QjhqbUlQRzMyZ3N