e}, {"id": "c7c249385b0a6df840b845d8", "title": "Unprecedented Cyberattack Used Malware Ads To Spy On Journalists, Report Says - Forbes", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimgFBVV95cUxORVp0cURvcmNJa3FWalFmT3lhUkE3NUpxRHMwQmxfaTFNMlRoMVhBenJoR2VaX3VLSWZTSUpkWnpSdm9YMG55bDYwc1dlVUIyUVVSdDAwb3NPN2N5b0dJdjlfWjJQWUF3TW5ZVUFtaURzYmhNZXc5SF96R0F4UlJMcVNUbWt3V2c4UGg3RjJWcE1PZE1EU1dlNzln?oc=5", "source": "lane_blast_cyber", "published_at": "2026-10-15T10:00:19+00:00", "text": "Unprecedented Cyberattack Used Malware Ads To Spy On Journalists, Report Says - Forbes\nUnprecedented Cyberattack Used Malware Ads To Spy On Journalists, Report Says Forbes", "truncated": false}, {"id": "fb231790bb312d5b8f0e8281", "title": "NVIDIA Expands Advanced Technology Access for Europe’s Scientific Community Through Open Learning Resources, Collaborations and Compute", "url": "https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/ec-scientific-computing-pledge/", "source": "company_nvidia_blog", "published_at": "2026-10-15T10:00:12+00:00", "text": "NVIDIA Expands Advanced Technology Access for Europe’s Scientific Community Through Open Learning Resources, Collaborations and Compute\nMuch of today’s scientific progress begins with computation.\nBut turning accelerated computing’s potential into scientific progress — modeling climate systems, designing molecules, simulating materials and more — depends on more than breakthrough technology. Especially for smaller labs, early-career scientists or science-based startups, the hardest part of research can be everything that comes before the science: assembling tools, finding compute and building the expertise to use both.\nAt the AI in Science Summit in Dublin today, the\nlaunched the AI in Science Enablers Initiative, a shared framework connected to RAISE — the Resource for AI Science in Europe — that brings private-sector support closer to Europe’s research community.\nAccess to accelerated tools, models and platforms\nCompute, cloud resources, data and scientific resources\nCollaborative projects and pilots along with support for researchers and talent\nSupport for startups and ecosystem development\n“Europe’s ambition in AI in science can only become reality if we bring together the strengths of our research community, industry and other partners,” Ekaterina Zaharieva, European Commissioner for Startups, Research and Innovation. “Through the AI in Science Enablers campaign, we are turning this ambition into concrete commitments that give researchers access to the tools, expertise and resources they need to innovate.”\nNVIDIA’s pledge begins with open source technology.\nNVIDIA has released more than 250 open models, 250 open datasets and over 1,400 open source software projects spanning large language models, healthcare, robotics, climate science, physical AI, autonomous vehicles and scientific research. The models and datasets are available on Hugging Face and GitHub.\nBuilding on this open ecosystem, researchers can build agentic reasoning capabilities with\n— a family of architectures, open weights, open source models, training data and recipes — and scientific software including\nto accelerate research across biology, chemistry, molecular design, weather and climate, energy, engineering, physics and material sciences.\nBuilding advanced intelligence capability across Europe requires knowledge that travels as freely as compute.\nThe NVIDIA Deep Learning Institute has launched a new\nwith hands-on course materials covering accelerated computing and deep learning — freely downloadable and openly licensed, with no enrollment, fees or deadline.\nModular by design, the courses can be adapted for university curricula, supercomputing center bootcamps or developer community events, and serve as a direct bridge to NVIDIA’s broader\nNVIDIA is collaborating with cloud infrastructure partners and European national AI factories — including in\n— to open new routes for startups and small- and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) to access sovereign European compute and services.\nprogram for startups provides access to compute resources and technical support, lowering the barrier to entry for startups seeking high-performance infrastructure and technological education.\nin France is the first AI factory to have collaborated with the Inception program for four months, demonstrating strong results.\nAI Factory ran a pilot initiative and is now rolling out its first full accelerator program, with growing interest from other European national AI factories in replicating the model in 2027. Beyond startup support, this program is designed to contribute to a broader strategic objective: building self-sustaining AI ventures in Spain and Europe.\n, one of Europe’s 19 EuroHPC-funded AI factories, announced a collaboration with NVIDIA to open its infrastructure and expertise to Inception startups and SMEs enterprises across sectors including life and material sciences, autonomous systems and gaming.\nFinally, EuroHPC supercomputing centers such as\nprovide academia and startups with free access to NVIDIA GPU infrastructure and services for open research. NVIDIA complements this access with office hours, bootcamps and hackathons that help researchers fine-tune models on their own datasets.\nAs an example, NVIDIA is co-organizing an European AI hackathon this month alongside\nHigh-Performance Computing Center Stuttgart\nTo top it all off, NVIDIA is powering Europe’s most ambitious expansion of AI infrastructure to date, with\ncurrently in development across the continent — the largest single-year buildout of supercomputing capacity Europe has ever seen.\nwith next-generation infrastructure, accelerating discovery across domains from climate science and drug development to materials research and beyond.\nRally Up: ‘Gears of War: E-Day’ Launches on GeForce NOW\nFall Into 25 New Games on GeForce NOW This October\nContain the Chaos: ‘CONTROL Resonant’ Launches on GeForce NOW\nCute Critters Come to the Cloud: ‘An