NnakJENTNObndsYWp2bXN0X2U4b0JwUzNKQWdIUGo2ZC1oWlgtalRYb3dwMDVOc3RQclFGX0c2TEx0a0dVbm5WOC1MN2JwQ3JTTFJGR2JuaDB6bDBLX2xyQUlzSG5KQmwzVTBDTjZwNXpn0gGTAUFVX3lxTE1yWmdIS3RZVUR5UTZnem9RTGRJdUhxYWtoNS15UXJNOGgxSjdzMm9", "source": "lane_blast_cyber", "published_at": "2026-10-15T09:55:19+00:00", "text": "CenterPoint Energy Data Breach Impacts 1 Million Texans - SecurityWeek\nCenterPoint Energy Data Breach Impacts 1 Million Texans SecurityWeek", "truncated": false}, {"id": "b1258c5564b67efd506f616a", "title": "In charts: How one of the world's biggest economies got trapped by debt", "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/13/france-2027-budget-debt.html", "source": "rss_cnbc_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-15T09:54:54+00:00", "text": "In charts: How one of the world's biggest economies got trapped by debt\nFrance's National Assembly begins debate on the revenue side of the 2027 budget on Oct. 13 as lawmakers confront a difficult fiscal outlook.\nFrance posted a 5.1% budget deficit and debt of 115.6% of GDP in 2025, leaving the country under pressure to consolidate its public finances.\nStudent protests over education resources are adding pressure as lawmakers weigh spending choices ahead of the 2027 presidential election.\nBank of France governor: Political uncertainty driving bond yields higher\nFrance's parliament begins debate on the 2027 budget Tuesday as high deficits, rising debt and student protests sharpen the political cost of fiscal consolidation.\nThe euro zone's second-biggest economy is barreling toward a debt crisis, and risks dragging the continent down with it, unless it sustainably brings down its national deficit,\nEven as thousands take to the streets to protest years of underfunding in public services, particularly education, France has run one of Europe's highest deficit-to-GDP ratios for nearly a decade under President Emmanuel Macron.\nGovernment spending remains well above its peers in the politically sensitive realm of social protections, particularly pensions and healthcare, while tepid economic growth has failed to compensate.\nFrench student protests heat up as budget battle looms\nWith presidential elections looming next year and parliamentarians proving reluctant to compromise, France's economic and political uncertainty is\nin nearly a quarter of a century as jittery bond markets have responded to the country's failure to impose fiscal consolidation.\nThe widening gap in French bond yields over their German equivalents has raised questions over whether the European Central Bank would have to take the\nexpensive and politically controversial step of intervening\nto stabilize the market. While this could halt a panic that fragments the currency union, the legacy of the Sovereign Debt Crisis — which hammered countries such as Greece, Ireland, Portugal, Spain and Italy — means it would require drastic action from France.\n\"The ECB will not step in just to 'bail out' France. If there was a European crisis they will intervene in some form but they will not save a country that cannot save itself,\" Antonio Fatas, professor of Economics at INSEAD Business School, said by email.\nNobel winner Krugman says France may have become 'too big to save'\nStudent riots engulf France as far-right presidential frontrunner Le Pen vows fiscal turnaround\nFrance to release diesel reserves as student protests resume\n'Get your house in order': IMF chief's stark warning for France over surging bond yields\nFrance’s fresh budget battle threatens to topple another government\nFrance is becoming the 'poster child' of sovereign debt problems as government borrowing costs hit near 2008 highs\nFrench contagion fears rise, as Le Pen outlines economic vision\nChoose CNBC as your preferred source on Google and never miss a moment from the most trusted name in business news.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "1783833d670823d6e8773003", "title": "Update Regarding BNC Service Disruption and Accessing List of Creditors in CM/ECF