nd China Open Their Biggest Border Bridge - The New York Times\nNorth Korea and China Open Their Biggest Border Bridge The New York Times North Korea gets closer to China and Russia with new bridges Financial Times North Korea-China bridge finally opens but will it help Pyongyang evade sanctions? CNN North Korea says opening ceremony was held for new bridge to China Reuters A new bridge linking China and North Korea will open more than a decade late AP News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "7bbabdd652173574962bd836", "title": "Leonidas triples production capacity with a new 100-million-euro factory - RetailDetail EU", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMisAFBVV95cUxNUS15UGY2b0U2MlNqTnppRGF6bmdWZEZQNlhLV0pacFZZbUdTM3JLYVlUZUxCdko3b09PdEpXUGx2c0xVRjh5d09MRWw5TjRsc1VmOXJVQTNXVWVSeDdTbTFMRndaMEo4NGNrZWg4bktQSjNNek5IaVJoQVhhdU5mSDNCeXExY3J2YjdPLWdJRHpUTnh0SFdkSUJtQVhQaGxqeWV4Yld0bTBvMW5LVXVKRw?oc=5", "source": "lane_capacity", "published_at": "2026-10-15T08:48:53+00:00", "text": "Leonidas triples production capacity with a new 100-million-euro factory - RetailDetail EU\nLeonidas triples production capacity with a new 100-million-euro factory RetailDetail EU", "truncated": false}, {"id": "359d314e5c4df7e3b7e321ce", "title": "Delta Airlines: What does profit warning mean for airline sector? - Yahoo Finance UK", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiiwFBVV95cUxOR092MDNDSEFiNkNrUm44SF9VdnROZXp1TUI4cVF4dmVXcHlDVVJ4VmpRem1CWUlsYmZ4eW1zT2paMENjZ2UzTi1KS1B4R0MtY3ZjVEIyMkk2bDN6S1ZWNU50WUN6VXRNTktyWVlDTHJQcktJbzExaXlHaURudWVtVEVoMkc3ZHNYQnUw?oc=5", "source": "lane_preannounce", "published_at": "2026-10-15T08:48:39+00:00", "text": "Delta Airlines: What does profit warning mean for airline sector? - Yahoo Finance UK\nDelta Airlines: What does profit warning mean for airline sector? Yahoo Finance UK", "truncated": false}, {"id": "55a0fc82dcfb93df758baca0", "title": "Bank of England's Breeden says cost-cutting by firms helping central bank - Reuters", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMitAFBVV95cUxQTlRqOEp1TktZM3ZxOUZ4ZU9jUHJCX1JkRjJFS2Z6T0NwT00zSk8weGswQ3ZJWmVIdGxveko1ZUg0SU9kQ1Z6RXlMYUltaDZMSlRiRE9YeFlBbDVxRXlxOThrYTFsejg3ZEJjWkNLM3ZqaXpuMmZqTlo2aW4wUEdjS1pibTNEb0RQc09SNzNNQTRfUUVsUWFYMTNZRHN4T29EZFExX0RRUVgzcUhLeTl1T2xWWTA?oc=5", "source": "topic_central_banks", "published_at": "2026-10-15T08:47:37+00:00", "text": "Bank of England's Breeden says cost-cutting by firms helping central bank - Reuters\nBank of England's Breeden says cost-cutting by firms helping central bank Reuters", "truncated": false}, {"id": "f01b60a337339dd7a3cb95c8", "title": "France investigates cyberattack that hacked retailers' refrigeration systems - NPR", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMivAFBVV95cUxOWEZRbTF3LXYtOHROTzJ6em9xc1MzVjFoWGNCc2I1MWVBelFiMlg5ZGtOQ2dZZm9uUENLVzZJUHllREs4Tk8xSlVFNTJrc19jVXdTVmNNOFRna1ZBb1d0eE5mdlFuczNBSXBpamRscFc3dG82MGEtbjMxbng0Y0xrQ0t0SVNISXljbkwtRjdVVl9OVDNDSmJ6eEJ4LXFBbVpMcmNMMmQtSWpaVEpiR25fM1ozNmY2NFA4eDdUbw?oc=5", "source": "lane_blast_cyber", "published_at": "2026-10-15T08:46:19+00:00", "text": "France investigates cyberattack that hacked retailers' refrigeration systems - NPR\nFrance investigates cyberattack that hacked retailers' refrigeration systems NPR", "truncated": false}, {"id": "c7a4226a46e16cba51f3d957", "title": "Why Chinese tech brands are outpacing Western rivals from EVs to robot vacuums", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/why-chinese-tech-brands-are-outpacing-western-rivals-from-evs-to-robot-vacuums/a-79646187?maca=en-rss-en-bus-2091-rdf", "source": "rss_dw_business", "published_at": "2026-10-15T08:45:00+00:00", "text": "Why Chinese tech brands are outpacing Western rivals from EVs to robot vacuums\nAs technologies become more embedded in daily life, policymakers are weighing the benefits against concerns over data security and foreign influence\nDo you remember the days when GoPro was synonymous with action cameras?\nAt its peak in 2015, the US company reigned supreme with around 6.6 million units sold that year and a market share of over 70%. Today, the pioneer is fighting for survival.\nGoPro's biggest rivals, DJI and Insta360, entered the action camera market in 2019 and 2021, respectively. Today, they account for more than 90% of global smart handheld camera sales.\nGoPro was saved from bankruptcy in September after failed product bets and competition from smartphones contributed to its decline\nImage: Christian Ohde/imageBROKER/picture alliance\nAlexander Hannemann, who regularly uses action cameras to film his motorcycle rides, says he switched from GoPro to Insta360 around two years ago.\n\"The quality is much better than a GoPro, even when I'm speeding up and going into corners at like 80 kilometers per hour,\" he told DW.\nGoPro's case is not an exception. Since around 2010, many Chinese brands have not only caught up but surpassed once-dominant Western rivals across the consumer technology industry, from smart TVs to robot vacuums.\nGone are the days when \"Made in China\" stood for cheap, mass-produced products.\n\"China is still making affordable products today,\" says Chris Pereira, a Canadian entrepreneur and author with over 20 years of experience in China. \"But the products are also high quality.\"\nThis combination has led to well over half of new consumer tech products globally coming from Chinese suppliers, Pereira adds.\nBYD, Xpeng and other Chinese car manufactures\nsold almost two-thirds of all electric cars\nlast year, surpassing incumbents like Tesla. In robot vacuums, Chinese brands dominate the global market, leaving former pioneer US company iRobot struggling to compete.\nSo why do Chinese companies increasingly outperform their Western rivals?\nPereira, whose consultancy iMpact helps Chinese companies gain traction overseas, attributes China's success to fierce domestic competition in cities like Shenzhen, Hangzhou and Shanghai; highly integrated supply chains and a can-do business culture that allows companies to innovate; and respond to problems quickly.\nOther key reasons for China's competitive edge include unparalleled\nin manufacturing, responsiveness from regulators as well as relatively cheap labor, which together leads to significantly lower production costs and much faster innovation cycles than in the world's most advanced economies.\nJoey Cheng, founder of Prolo Ring, which makes rings that let consumers control remotes and other devices with only their thumbs\nJoey Cheng, who moved to the US for his PhD studies over 20 years ago, is familiar with both the American consumer and the Chinese producer sides.\nWhile his startup is based in the US, almost all the manufacturing is done in Shenzhen, where a large share of the world's hardware and consumer electronics are made.\n\"The supply chain in Shenzhen is basically inevitable for cost cutting and quick product development,\" Cheng says. \"Especially as a startup you must get the product out quickly, or you'll get blown out of the water.\"\nAlthough consumers are flocking to Chinese companies, some reservations persist.\nfound that more than 90% of Europeans do not trust Chinese technology companies with their data, while about 80% distrust US firms. Germans expressed the highest level of mistrust.\nNadine, a German travel blogger who uses DJI and Insta360 products, says she wouldn't use any Asian products at home.\n\"My home robot vacuum is from a German company for precisely this reason: So the data remains compliant with German regulations. After all, manufacturers can connect directly to the camera,\" she said, referring to camera and audio hacking incidents involving robot vacuums.\nXreal, a Chinese smart glasses maker, recently entered the European market through retailers like the UK's Currys in an attempt to grow consumer trust\nImage: Andy Wong/AP Photo/picture alliance\nStill, Pereira says that Chinese companies expanding abroad have to pay a \"trust premium.\"\n\"This trust deficit means that they need to be extra competitive in terms of product and price in order for the market to choose them,\" he says, adding that the gap can be narrowed by a presence in overseas markets like\n, expected to open in Hungary later this year.\nChinese companies say they comply with EU\nrules. However, domestic security laws in the US and China can require firms to hand over data to authorities, and German officials note limited oversight of whether data is transferred from European servers abroad.\nIn addition to privacy concerns, Chinese consumer tech firms have come under scrutiny over espionage and other risks, most recently regarding connected devices like EVs.\n, the US and several other Western countries have hit Chinese imports and companies with tariffs, blacklists and strict requirements. In late July, the Trump administration\nof humanoid robots manufactured outside the US.\nA representative of Chinese robot maker Primebot told DW that all Chinese consumer tech companies are feeling the effects.\n\"We might seek cooperation with a local US company. Another potential solution is building a factory there,\" he said.\nChris Pereira foresees that the tariffs will accelerate two trends among Chinese companies: Moving more manufacturing overseas and an increasing focus on challenging top-tier Western brands with premium goods.\n\"Chinese officials and companies recognize that manufacturing needs to be localized to a certain degree. One reason is to assuage the challenges of exporting, another is to create trust locally,\" Pereira sums up.\n\"The other trend is that once duties wipe out the price advantage at the bottom, the margin left is at the top, so Chinese brands are increasingly competing directly with established Western names, where trust matters most.\"\nChina's robot revolution leaves Hong Kong playing catch-up\nTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\nAs China's dominance keeps growing, Western companies are scrambling to find ways to stay competitive.\nKaz Merchant, who works for a UK-based company that imports and exports IT hardware, says smaller companies, in particular, must carve out a geographic niche.\n\"They need to have their own unique selling point for a certain product, which China might not have for that region. Or they need to partner with a Chinese company,\" he says.\nTo Pereira, a key issue Europe needs to solve is regulators' responsiveness.\n\"Chinese regulators tell you very clearly and quickly what you can do. That speed helps companies iterate and succeed overseas,\" Pereira says.\n\"If you're waiting weeks for a response, you're going to lose business.\"", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "6c07f7f6dc79c7c266382753", "title": "Supreme Court upholds Bombay HC ruling quashing YES Bank's Rs 8,415 crore AT1 bond write-off - The Economic Times", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiiAJBVV95cUxORHo1UWRwdWpsM2tadkM1OXJmUUhBUlFqUVlxYTIxMy1zU0lnaDF3OXRuckktc0ZhSjBMMWkydi1fd2x0NklLZ3RVQUZYaFdyMGhlWnUwRTZZcmpnbnYwVlpoSFBhNXdjenlqNGJyUXdaaE1KNVFrWkw0MDBqLTdLVTRvR29IZXFBMUdFNUJHdkRIWGFyd1lyMFgtc2NXbTdlQ25BV0pkbXByWDNTWG82Z3FXS1lfdG5lMWttSUJJaFZ", "source": "lane_blast_legal", "published_at": "2026-10-15T08:44:54+00:00", "text": "Supreme Court upholds Bombay HC ruling quashing YES Bank's Rs 8,415 crore AT1 bond write-off - The Economic Times\nSupreme Court upholds Bombay HC ruling quashing YES Bank's Rs 8,415 crore AT1 bond write-off The Economic Times", "truncated": false}, {"id": "a29f715fce1ce49d3c738adb", "title": "UK economy grew 0.1% in August, beating expectations", "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/15/uk-gdp-august-2026-economy.html", "source": "rss_cnbc_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-15T08:44:17+00:00", "text": "UK economy grew 0.1% in August, beating expectations\nOn an annual basis, GDP grew by 2.1% in August, beating economists' expectations of 1.7%.\nThe U.K. is currently grappling with a cost-of-living crisis sparked by sticky inflation and surging energy costs in the wake of the Russia-Ukraine war and the Iran war.\nBoE's Breeden: Surprised how well UK economy has absorbed the energy price shock\nThe U.K. economy grew by a better-than-expected 0.1% in the month to August, with production growth offsetting weak output in services and construction.\nEconomists polled by Reuters had been expecting GDP to shrink by 0.1% between July and August. But the reading still marked a slowdown in momentum for British gross domestic product, which grew by 0.4% month-on-month in June and July.\nProduction grew by 0.8%, the U.K.'s Office for National Statistics said in a release on Thursday, while services showed no growth and construction fell by 0.2% in August.\nOn an annual basis, GDP grew by 2.1% in August, beating economists' expectations of 1.7%. The ONS said it marked the highest year-on-year GDP reading since August 2022.\njumped 0.4% against the U.S. dollar following the release, while yields on U.K. government bonds — known as gilts — fell across the curve. The yield on the benchmark\nwas last seen trading 9 basis points lower at 5.3364%.\nThe U.K., a net energy importer, is currently grappling with a cost-of-living crisis sparked by sticky inflation and surging energy costs in the wake of the Russia-Ukraine war and the Iran war.\nThe government will unveil its closely watched Autumn Budget later this month. It's tasked with sticking to the previous government's so-called fiscal rules aimed at reigning in public debt while delivering on promises to soften the blow of surging consumer costs.\nAnalysts said the slowdown in growth added to pressure facing U.K. finance minister John Healey as he prepares to deliver his first budget on Oct. 28.\nJoe Nellis, head of economic research at accountancy firm MHA, said in a note that the slowing economy will weigh on Healey's \"fiscal headroom\" – the amount of leeway the government has to either increase spending or cut taxes.\n\"While continued economic growth should provide some support for tax revenues, the economy remains fragile. Rising debt interest costs have severely constrained fiscal headroom, with long-term government borrowing costs remaining exceptionally high,\" he said.\nHe added that any decision to raise taxes to balance the books \"carries considerable risks,\" threatening to weaken consumer spending, undermine confidence and discourage corporate investment. But at the same time, Nellis said, significant unfunded spending commitments could unsettle financial markets and push government borrowing costs higher.\nScott Gardner, investment strategist at J.P. Morgan Personal Investing, said in a note following the ONS release that while it was positive to see the economy continuing to grow despite global challenges, the outlook for the rest of the year \"remains uncertain with a tricky Autumn Budget ahead.\"\n\"It's unclear right now what fiscal headroom will be left given the servicing of UK debt has increased over the last year. Many will be waiting nervously to see what the Chancellor announces,\" he said.\nSanjay Raja, chief U.K. economist at Deutsche Bank, struck a more optimistic tone.\n\"While we aren't seeing any dramatic course correction (so far), there are signs that the engine of the U.K. economy is slowing from its torrid pace in the first half of the year,\" he said, noting that consumer-facing services, construction and the IT sectors all slipped from the previous month.\nBut while he said there would \"undoubtedly… be some hiccups to come in the run up to year end\" as energy prices continue to bite into household incomes and higher interest rates drag on spending and investment, Raja argued that \"the U.K.'s resilience story continues.\"\n\"It's hard to overstate how strong the U.K. performance has been all year,\" Raja said. \"The U.K. economy remains the fastest growing G7 economy. What's more impressive? If you believe the HMRC jobs data, U.K. GDP growth hit 1.8% in the first eight months of the year, with 95k FEWER jobs. Who said productivity growth was dead in the U.K.?\"\nChoose CNBC as your preferred source on Google and never miss a moment from the most trusted name in business news.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "deb2505f683dde32a9e6111c", "title": "Cardoso: Economic Buffers, Fiscal Coordination Paved Way For Rate Cut - LEADERSHIP Newspapers", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMilAFBVV95cUxPeVlZNHBDNDRxem9EcEtseVJHa01qU3ozQUo3MmRDNVFJc1RKUjBFYlhnc3N5czF3LWRPVFdDRGJQcWhzQXRja3lUX1hyRWdHMWdjZk9CNnQtTFVWQnpzQjRKLS1XR1FKRzVKdGRiN091QndZWmJaMENreG5GRzdQck5lYVB2QlRzdWU4SUYxa2wxdk9G?oc=5", "source": "lane_factor_impulse", "published_at": "2026-10-15T08:42:35+00:00", "text": "Cardoso: Economic Buffers, Fiscal Coordination Paved Way For Rate Cut - LEADERSHIP Newspapers\nCardoso: Economic Buffers, Fiscal Coordination Paved Way For Rate Cut LEADERSHIP Newspapers", "truncated": false}, {"id": "92b78e72a4b80618bfbb03c4", "title": "CBN’s 350bps Rate Cut Took Market By Surprise, Cardoso, IMF - LEADERSHIP Newspapers", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihgFBVV95cUxNMnM4U1QwNV9nMU5vUHN1WHNKVW91NWZYQVhsYmkyUU5XMkhnNXhVVDg2SDRWVHBfaWE2OGdmLWdtSVN2d1lrQXFteGs4ald4ZmVxekpnR010NjB0a04wc21sVXgtUHdjMFRaTC1HRG12SFBITE90X0xmREZKSnV5bEJqd3NEZw?oc=5", "source": "lane_factor_impulse", "published_at": "2026-10-15T08:41:48+00:00", "text": "CBN’s 350bps Rate Cut Took Market By Surprise, Cardoso, IMF - LEADERSHIP Newspapers\nCBN’s 350bps Rate Cut Took Market By Surprise, Cardoso, IMF LEADERSHIP Newspapers", "truncated": false}, {"id": "4ed1aa2b2d85e0cfc8b8f4cc", "title": "White House defends new press secretary over her legal credentials", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckqxne2evqreo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T08:41:04+00:00", "text": "White House defends new press secretary over her legal credentials\nThe White House is defending its new press secretary, Katie Zacharia, as she faces scrutiny over her legal credentials.\nPresident Donald Trump described Zacharia as an attorney when announcing her appointment last week. She has referred to herself as a lawyer.\nBut when she was asked on Tuesday by a reporter in which state she had passed the bar exam required to be an attorney in the US, she said standing alongside the president: \"When I moved to China.\"\nShe does not appear to be registered to the bar in any state where she has lived. White House communications director Steven Cheung said Zacharia is \"uniquely qualified and whip smart to help inform the American people\".\nResponding to questions about her credentials, Zacharia said in a statement this week: \"I practised international transaction law, with all the appropriate credentials.\"\nOn Wednesday, a watchdog group, Campaign for Accountability, filed a complaint with the California State Bar Association.\nIt is seeking an investigation into whether Zacharia \"repeatedly held herself out to the public as entitled to practise law without a licence to do so, conduct California law treats as a crime\".\nThe 42-year-old political strategist graduated from law school at Pepperdine University in California in 2011, the university confirmed.\nA law degree is not a licence to practise law in the US.\nThe new White House press secretary told the New York Times on Wednesday that when she first began appearing on cable news, she had asked Pepperdine how she should identify herself.\nShe said she was advised by the university president that she could call herself a lawyer, but not an attorney.\nThe new press secretary also told the paper that when she answered China to a question about her bar exam, it was a misunderstanding. She had mistakenly thought, above the din of Marine One, the reporter had asked: \"Where were you after law school?\"\nZacharia took the bar exam in California after graduating from law school but did not pass, according to the Wall Street Journal. California's bar exam is widely regarded as one of the hardest in the US to pass.\nA person with direct knowledge of the matter told the outlet that Zacharia took the exam around the same time that she was planning a wedding and preparing to move to China with her husband-to-be.\nWatch: How did Katie Zacharia do at her first - very brief - White House briefing?\nShe moved to Beijing because of her husband's job at an American private equity firm. Zacharia has said she worked on \"international transaction law\" there.\nHer LinkedIn profile states that she worked as an associate at Zhong Lun Law Firm in Beijing from 2011-13.\nIn China, only Chinese citizens are eligible to obtain a licence to practise law. Foreign employees may work at law firms in other roles.\nIn February last year, Zacharia said on conservative radio programme The Clay Travis and Buck Sexton Show that she had worked as outside counsel for the International Finance Corporation (IFC) and the World Bank during her time in China.\nThe BBC has contacted the World Bank and IFC for comment, as well as Zhong Lun Law Firm.\nIt is understood the World Bank Group sometimes retains outside law firms, and that those firms decide which of their lawyers are assigned to which parts of any work.\nZacharia has also served as general counsel for Fix California, a conservative non-profit started by Trump ally Ric Grenell, according to her LinkedIn profile.\nGet our flagship newsletter with all the headlines you need to start the day.\nCommentator Katie Zacharia picked as new White House press secretary", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "ca70bd9f2628ee0827fb0c81", "title": "BREAKING | Yes Bank AT-1 Bond Write-Off: Supreme Court Refuses To Interfere With Bombay High Court Order... - LiveLawBiz", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi8wFBVV95cUxPdC1ueTZnQkRQd2hHT2wtT0FpNS1ON19tTVFmOU1kd1VBQWd6b3owY1JtN3FITERTNmFTVWY3UUVHeWVKQy1yZVc5NEpJSnM2M1pFRnNKcXRLOWhmeW05a1RJUkdDaThvTWQxTUV1VldM