3V0Ykg3?oc=5", "source": "rss_ap_topnews:google_fallback", "published_at": "2026-10-15T06:40:20+00:00", "text": "New China-North Korea bridge opens after more than a decade of delay - AP News\nNew China-North Korea bridge opens after more than a decade of delay AP News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "881d2205c0613c2e54243981", "title": "Trillion-dollar IPOs and the new Magnificent 10 - Daily Maverick", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMioAFBVV95cUxOZE04RnlZT3RQeHFQRElNcXVhN1pDcXVwUzlILXQ5RnNYZXZweVRQdXEyZHU5UmI4TXNCT0RmNTNBSFZ5aTFfOGNubWdrUkQySGV2bjZYdmd3SE0xLTZoYURzZW9pbXRNeW5nWm9KZE05MmlzeFREUUJ3Z3YtU1R3aWZoMU8zNFdHRGMtTjR4NF80LUZ4NTJwbU5PNWhPc3N0?oc=5", "source": "lane_flow_index", "published_at": "2026-10-15T06:39:51+00:00", "text": "Trillion-dollar IPOs and the new Magnificent 10 - Daily Maverick\nTrillion-dollar IPOs and the new Magnificent 10 Daily Maverick", "truncated": false}, {"id": "c1a49938e764ee2884d949c0", "title": "User-82e25cd8(@Square-Creator-82e25cd8848c5)'s insights - Binance", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiaEFVX3lxTE4tQVBycTZ3S3V2cWdKZ1E2UElBVlFKeFAzTkFlc0R1bV9namg5ajlvWktLZDN1Y3lxZVR2NVZQeTdxdk5DNE1aSEdWZHF1TE1qRjdDWGtLcWNLQ1FmaWZYdmhxZ3VjYnl2?oc=5", "source": "lane_regime_state", "published_at": "2026-10-15T06:39:01+00:00", "text": "User-82e25cd8(@Square-Creator-82e25cd8848c5)'s insights - Binance\nUser-82e25cd8(@Square-Creator-82e25cd8848c5)'s insights Binance", "truncated": false}, {"id": "ae150682667bf40a536c32e8", "title": "$261M: U.S. Spot Bitcoin ETFs Face Major Outflows - Coinfomania", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMie0FVX3lxTE9UYk91LU5wa1dsc0RtN0NMbGpCUUdxSHVzSVZGanozN2dIcTBxUGxWZjhQY2k2VURyeDNPMWkxSWhFbFhhSC1qdkFROUdjYWNFa1NvWUU0WnhMZldPV1Q4LUNVSV9HQlFBQXpEQkV0YXdrR3hFdFp5anRHaw?oc=5", "source": "lane_flow_mechanical", "published_at": "2026-10-15T06:38:04+00:00", "text": "$261M: U.S. Spot Bitcoin ETFs Face Major Outflows - Coinfomania\n$261M: U.S. Spot Bitcoin ETFs Face Major Outflows Coinfomania", "truncated": false}, {"id": "ac02d1f764fb7f216b723a0e", "title": "IMF sees milder global slowdown as AI boom offsets war hit — warns resilience is nearing limits", "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/12/imf-world-bank-annual-meetings-october-bangkok-asia-.html", "source": "rss_cnbc_economy", "published_at": "2026-10-15T06:35:31+00:00", "text": "IMF sees milder global slowdown as AI boom offsets war hit — warns resilience is nearing limits\nGrowth is seen slowing to 3.1% this year from 3.5%, but better than the IMF's 3% July estimate, aided by AI investment and fiscal support.\nInflation is expected to rise to 4.7% this year, driven by higher energy costs and an AI investment boom, and then ease to 4.1% in 2027.\nThe IMF now expects the Strait of Hormuz to reopen in March 2027, with conditions returning to a pre-war state by October 2027.\nIMF cut its growth forecast for China to 4.5% this year and 4.0% next year; upgraded India to 7.0% for both 2026 and 2027.\nThe IMF downgraded sharply its forecast for Gulf countries, including Saudi Arabia, under the shadow of a regional war.\nCORRECTION / IMF Chief Economist Silvana Tenreyro (C) speaks next to IMF Research Department Deputy Director Petya Koeva Brooks (R) and IMF Communications Officer Jose Luis De Haro (L) during the World Economic Outlook press briefing at the Annual Meetings of the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Bank Group (WBG) at the Queen Sirikit National Convention Center (QSNCC) in Bangkok on October 13, 2026.\nBANGKOK — The International Monetary Fund has raised its forecast for global growth this year as the AI-driven investment boom helps offset some of the impact from surging energy costs, while warning that economic resilience could be nearing its breaking point.\nThe IMF expects the global economy to grow at 3.1% in 2026, up from\n, but slower than the 3.5% rate last year. For 2027, it kept the forecast unchanged at 3.4%.\nThe UN agency expects inflation at 4.1% in 2027,\n– when it estimated the Strait of Hormuz would reopen by mid-July, with conditions broadly returning to pre-war levels by March 2027. For the current year, it maintained its 4.7% outlook.\n\"The global economy has been resilient, but that resilience is reaching its limits,\" Silvana Tenreyro, economic counsellor and director at the IMF research department, told reporters Tuesday, urging policymakers to restore fiscal space and supply-chain security.\nThe IMF now estimates that the Strait of Hormuz will reopen in March next year and return to pre-war state by October. Gulf crude exports averaged 88% of pre-conflict levels in September as traffic picked up, the international body said.\nThe report was released at the IMF and World Bank annual meetings in Bangkok this week, where finance ministers and central bank governors from the fund's 191 members are meeting to discuss geopolitical tensions, mounting public debt and artificial intelligence.\nAI-related investment gave the global economy a \"demand impulse\" that helped activity in the first half of the year hold up better than expected, despite the Middle East war and trade tensions, the IMF said. Financial markets also stayed largely calm, which the fund said may partly reflect \"AI-related exuberance.\"\nWTO says AI investment boom is driving trade growth\nFor now, \"use of buffers and adaptability have helped economies weather the negative shocks,\" the IMF said, citing measures such as diversifying fuel sources, tapping strategic reserves that have contained the energy-market turmoil unleashed by the Middle East war.\nBut that cushion is \"rapidly narrowing\" in the near term, it said. Global oil reserves are being drawn down by about 2.7 million barrels a day, nearing levels that could trigger sharp price moves, and energy constraints could impair the AI-led momentum. The growing unease over governments' fiscal health also adds to the headwinds.\nTenreyro said monetary policy should remain \"sufficiently restrictive\" to return inflation to target in a timely way, and that central bank independence is critical as governments face mounting financing pressure.\nOverall growth is also becoming increasingly uneven. Thirty-two economies now account for 95% of global growth, down from 54 in April.\nCountries that have limited exposure to the war-led supply shock and are catering to the AI-led demand are holding up or strengthening. Taiwan is forecast to grow 10.7% this year and South Korea 3.3%, up from 1.1% in 2025 on semiconductor demand. The U.S. is projected to expand 2.3% in both 2026 and 2027, supported by private consumption, fiscal policy and tech investment.\nSUWON, SOUTH KOREA: An employee checks a server room in the Samsung Networks' Telco Data Center at the Samsung Electronics HQ, Samsung Digital City, on June 13, 2023 in Suwon, South Korea. (Photo by Chung Sung-Jun/Getty Images)\nChung Sung-jun | Getty Images News | Getty Images\nEnergy importers outside that tech supply chain face higher import bills, weaker real incomes and less fiscal room. The euro zone is estimated to eke out just 1.1% growth this year amid higher energy costs and weak consumer confidence. The Middle East and Central Asia is projected to contract 0.3% this year, with Bahrain, Iraq, Kuwait and Qatar facing double-digit declines before double-digit rebounds in 2027.\nChina is forecast to grow 4.5% this year and 4.0% in 2027, with higher oil prices and structural headwinds weighing on activity. Second-quarter growth of 4.3% was weaker than expected as sluggish consumption and property strains outweighed robust exports.\nIndia remains the fastest-growing major economy, with the IMF forecasting growth of 7.0% in both 2026 and 2027, upgrades of 0.6 percentage point and 0.3 percentage point from July, respectively, driven by robust domestic demand, services activity and AI-related investment.\n\"The global economy is not simply slowing or accelerating; rather, growth is being reallocated across countries according to their exposure to the war-induced supply shock and AI-related demand strength,\" the IMF said.\nWhile AI has underpinned resilience, the investment boom is also adding to inflationary pressures and financial fragility, the IMF said.\n\"This is a large, front-loaded demand shock\" that sees massive investment pouring into sectors such as semiconductors, data centers, and power generation, before any broad productivity gains have materialized, it said in the report.\nA potential reassessment of AI-related profitability and productivity prospects could see excessive investment pull back and force a sharp correction in equities. The IMF warned that in a scenario where AI productivity gains fail to materialize, U.S. equities could fall 20% and global growth could end up 1.1% below its baseline estimates by 2028, with Asia 1.7% lower.\nAdvanced-economy benchmark yields have climbed to multidecade highs as resurgent inflation, the expected path of policy rates and fiscal concerns push up borrowing costs, the IMF said. Technology firms borrowing to finance AI investment are adding to the pressure.\nPublic debt in advanced economies is projected to reach 115% of GDP by 2031, from 108% in 2025. In emerging and developing economies, it is forecast to climb to 84% from 74%. Primary deficits in the largest economies remain substantially larger than before the pandemic, and governments face higher real interest rates and shorter debt maturities as defense spending pressures mount.\nRebuilding fiscal space is \"increasingly urgent,\" it said, urging governments to keep support for households facing higher energy and food costs \"temporary, targeted, and nondistortionary.\"\nIts models put the probability that global inflation exceeds 5% in 2027 at about 35%, up from 30% in April. Risks remain \"tilted to the downside,\" the fund said.\nCorrection: This story has been updated to reflect in the key points that the IMF cut China's growth forecast.\nChoose CNBC as your preferred source on Google and never miss a moment from the most trusted name in business news.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "f9f275bd5e94295153d41458", "title": "Ether (ETH) price drops nearly 6% to $2,359 ami... - Pluang", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidkFVX3lxTE1uNUd2a3R0SVZ1cTJXT1dEX0pjTFo2LXZVemJpOXVqSlc4UmdrQnNaTGE5ZDFRbFdUNVV5YWJlako1UVQtTGFjcll3TWd2bFIxc2pFTjhUMks0SlR6amNWTkhKU253TUdKZjFnN0kzVk15OHNyWWc?oc=5", "source": "lane_regime_state", "published_at": "2026-10-15T06:35:04+00:00", "text": "Ether (ETH) price drops nearly 6% to $2,359 ami... - Pluang\nEther (ETH) price drops nearly 6% to $2,359 ami... Pluang", "truncated": false}, {"id": "b2f92cab9db4594d5d77d9a8", "title": "Are Rolls-Royce shares too expensive to justify its £2.3bn buyback? - The Twelfth Magpie", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiqwFBVV95cUxOYmdYWnp4akN1c2xsUTZrdm8zUmowVEl1YXQtRENld3lJZ2xveXNSaUpJZElQUF9JOXA4dTdxWkQyRE5oQ0x2N3hYeHRLWk1raHBZckFKTU9XSHU5ZElPeWhMaVQzUEVSME82TUNGLXBqOWRDdENvdXhMMjFxNDZJTm9IcjJFWkdPZExncFc2VmZTb0tEcVVGTk1LNGhodzJTYm1rMFZxQTN4R2c?oc=5", "source": "lane_capital_return", "published_at": "2026-10-15T06:35:00+00:00", "text": "Are Rolls-Royce shares too expensive to justify its £2.3bn buyback? - The Twelfth Magpie\nAre Rolls-Royce shares too expensive to justify its £2.3bn buyback? The Twelfth Magpie", "truncated": false}, {"id": "7368bbd62c6b781387e83942", "title": "Are Rolls-Royce shares too expensive to justify its £2.3bn buyback? - Yahoo Finance UK", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiiAFBVV95cUxNamJOME1aaFRvNVVLNlJJOWk4aWViRXhYQkIzSXVTbzhRV2hZVllEeFBZSk9UcEtrbXJNVksxTGpKN3JteVhMektUTjh0bEcxZjJXemNBbC1xeWhGLWdCVVNrOWJuODVReVVOSWRseVhMRGpoWG00eWZfdFVMZ2lNMkhMY1RYUTAz?oc=5", "source": "lane_capital_return", "published_at": "2026-10-15T06:35:00+00:00", "text": "Are Rolls-Royce shares too expensive to justify its £2.3bn buyback? - Yahoo Finance UK\nAre Rolls-Royce shares too expensive to ju