d": false}, {"id": "66faf15d71f1406dfdf44328", "title": "Seoul seeks W8.1b subsidy to cover Han River bus operating losses - 헤럴드경제", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiV0FVX3lxTE92WG85cVlJSFhSV0l5cU1tZmRMSkpFdEJoWW9sNnZhTVNTS016Z3hnWVJnWmRybURqM0NVU3lGUVFTbm5Bc0pwVWFld1hpUVMwaDk5UFE5Zw?oc=5", "source": "lane_subsidy", "published_at": "2026-10-15T05:02:57+00:00", "text": "Seoul seeks W8.1b subsidy to cover Han River bus operating losses - 헤럴드경제\nSeoul seeks W8.1b subsidy to cover Han River bus operating losses 헤럴드경제", "truncated": false}, {"id": "5051c4b95e96b39fd68cf863", "title": "Why Hamas Cheered Navi Pillay’s Nobel Peace Prize - The Dispatch", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMieEFVX3lxTE11MkhOT3VvQVlnWGYwVXN4dDFTUUE4ZlRJYWhvRkZyQlZvZXhtZnFBa0tIbGhVeDRBM09LYWtSc0lIX1FhbDFuRHlsY1JTbXU0NEVQODdTMUljOXR4S1VPS0sxVHYzN1V4YTUweHpGaWQtZTJSN1Zlbg?oc=5", "source": "rss_google_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T05:02:36+00:00", "text": "Why Hamas Cheered Navi Pillay’s Nobel Peace Prize - The Dispatch\nWhy Hamas Cheered Navi Pillay’s Nobel Peace Prize The Dispatch Navi Pillay, International Law and the Finding of Genocide in Gaza World Politics Review Analysing Africa newsletter: A Nobel and ignoble day for the rule of law The Economist My relative defined ‘genocide.’ Honoring him means scrutinizing Navi Pillay’s Israel findings. Jewish Telegraphic Agency Pillay decries US sanctions on ICC, where she is ‘confident’ Netanyahu will one day stand trial The Times of Israel", "truncated": false}, {"id": "826b277b001bf77cef5662fa", "title": "Pidilite Raises Fevicol Prices By Rs 5 Per Kg In October Amid 40-50% Input Cost Surge - NDTV Profit", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi0AFBVV95cUxQNDNtUW8tYjUzNXBmZXU4dHR6WmdIX2VpRXh3ank4R2hhRF9DZWprV19QWVg1MkFnbFVESnpmcWgwaW40a01nQU1ibmJoWEp4WldQSEloQl9kNWdGT3pxbllKaXYtRTUwMlAxR2xWd3JOU3pxTHNFTVBFWUpyZ015MFpSeTR6dmJFeW50bkI5MmhkRENSdHdOZXU5ZGlJWWdrMUV3NDhqTXJxQVBqSXRKVTFrY3hoNFprWGYtVVNEVjR", "source": "lane_input_cost", "published_at": "2026-10-15T05:01:21+00:00", "text": "Pidilite Raises Fevicol Prices By Rs 5 Per Kg In October Amid 40-50% Input Cost Surge - NDTV Profit\nPidilite Raises Fevicol Prices By Rs 5 Per Kg In October Amid 40-50% Input Cost Surge NDTV Profit", "truncated": false}, {"id": "6ae63a8a2e6821947bed92e9", "title": "Bessent calls for World Bank reforms, including upstream oil and gas financing - Reuters", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuAFBVV95cUxOdmVBc2lZeXZJNlZpZ3VMVkdXTENJanEzampXOWtLcmRpVkFoOFFKTktkTWI2akVZTG9wV1UyY1ZreW02TzBDTG5LWUxiOUl4bnl0TW5JRG52X21aT3A1TkdyRV9ObEVBZWl3TmxKODB5aUVQLXFCa0NBY1VCQ2pZUEtDZUpiaDYxUjhoanZPQ0FCam8xcmJuVS13b3lQLXBPb2dSb044ZTlvYnF6X293STlMOXJBa21k?oc=5", "source": "topic_government", "published_at": "2026-10-15T05:01:00+00:00", "text": "Bessent calls for World Bank reforms, including upstream oil and gas financing - Reuters\nBessent calls for World Bank reforms, including upstream oil and gas financing Reuters", "truncated": false}, {"id": "921ac389ebb4e517b7be9b52", "title": "Hundreds of IDF investigations into killings still have no public outcome, BBC Verify finds", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c574e228vlygo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_world", "published_at": "2026-10-15T05:00:23+00:00", "text": "Hundreds of IDF investigations into killings still have no public outcome, BBC Verify finds\nBBC Verify has identified 254 incidents in which the Israeli military has been accused of killing or injuring people in Gaza, Lebanon, the occupied West Bank and Israel that it said it was investigating, but have no public outcome.\nUsing purpose-built AI tools, BBC Verify combed through more than 60,000 news reports and examined hundreds of incidents in which the Israel Defense Forces (IDF) faced allegations of harm since the start of the Gaza war. Following extensive manual checking of each incident, we found 300 cases that the military said it was looking into, only 28 of which had outcomes published on the IDF website. For a further 18 incidents it was unclear if the investigation has been concluded.\nBBC Verify approached the IDF about each case multiple times but only received information on two incidents, which the IDF said are still under review. \"The IDF takes every allegation or report raising concerns about conduct that may be inconsistent with the law or IDF directives with the utmost seriousness,\" it said in a statement.\nThe International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC), which works in warzones to uphold the Geneva Conventions, publishes guidelines that state investigations into potential violations of international humanitarian law, whether criminal or otherwise, should be as transparent as possible.\nOf the 254 incidents identified by BBC Verify, 158 occurred in Gaza, 48 in the West Bank and 32 in Lebanon. A further 16 took place in Israel or in IDF detention. All happened between 7 October 2023 and 19 August 2026, and at least one person was reportedly killed in more than 200 of them.\nSome of the incidents without public outcomes date back to late 2023. One of the deadliest cases involved a strike on a school building in Gaza in August 2024 when at least 93 people were reportedly killed, including six women and 11 children.\nFor three years, the Israeli military has often responded to accusations of harm by stating an incident is being investigated in some form, for example that it is being \"looked into\", \"reviewed\", \"checked\" or \"examined\". Often, that is the last that is heard about a case.\nA number of incidents have been escalated for criminal investigation by the IDF. Two that came under intense public scrutiny involved the killings of five-year-old Hind Rajab, and of 15 Palestinian Red Crescent emergency workers who were shot more than 100 times and allegedly targeted at close range.\nThe results of IDF military investigations are not routinely made public, including those leading to criminal investigations of its soldiers.\nA number that have been published relate to hostages in Gaza who were allegedly killed as a result of Israeli operations. In some cases, the military has only announced it is investigating an incident after being approached by BBC Verify or other media organisations.\nIn August, the IDF published a rare report detailing internal probes into five incidents in Gaza, including the killings of Hind Rajab and the 15 Palestinian emergency workers, which it revealed had been escalated to criminal investigations.\nIn the three other cases, which involved the killings of seven aid workers from the World Central Kitchen (WCK) in an air strike and the killing of four MÃ©decins Sans FrontiÃ¨res (MSF) aid workers in two separate Gaza attacks, the IDF said it did not find a basis for or reasonable suspicion to begin a criminal investigation.\nThe report also stated Israel's General Staff Fact-Finding and Assessment Mechanism (FFAM), which handles\nIDF investigations into \"exceptional operational incidents\"\n, had completed the review of approximately 150 cases since the start of the war.\nThese cases have now been transferred to the IDF's Military Prosecution for examination, according to the report. The IDF did not outline what the 150 incidents were, and did not clarify whether they were separate to the cases identified by BBC Verify when asked.\nDespite multiple requests, the IDF has not provided a transparent breakdown of the status of the 300 investigations identified by BBC Verify.\nIn a statement, the IDF said it \"publishes information regarding the outcomes of investigations and examinations when possible, subject to legal and operational considerations, as well as restrictions relating to privacy and information security\".\nIt added that certain incidents are examined through command-level operational reviews, which are \"intended to establish the sequence of events, identify failures and draw lessons for the purpose of improving operational conduct and taking any necessary command measures\".\nMore than 900 killed in Gaza this year despite ceasefire, Hamas-run health ministry says\nSatellite images reveal scale of Israeli demolitions as Lebanese villages destroyed\nIsraeli 'bunker buster' bombs used in Gaza hospital strike, experts say\nThe IDF also said that during the war it has taken disciplinary action in dozens of cases against soldiers who failed to meet the standards expected of them and acted contrary to IDF directives, including being removed from combat positions and from reserve service.\nFreedom of Information data obtained by Yesh Din, an Israeli human rights group, showed that between October 2023 and August 2024 at least 1,456 Gaza-related incidents were referred to the FFAM. Twelve of these reviews were completed and referred to the Military Advocate General (MAG) Corps, two of which resulted in criminal investigations.\nDan Owen, a researcher at Yesh Din which has been monitoring military investigations since 2006, told BBC Verify that he believes the IDF will often try to \"prolong\" investigations. \"They try to stretch out these processes to the point where people forget and people don't care about them,\" he said.\nJanina Dill, a professor of international law at the University of Oxford, said that according to the Geneva Conventions a party to armed conflict has a responsibility to \"investigate and potentially prosecute its own\".\n\"That's the absolute clear bottom line,\" she added. \"There are also guidelines for investigation, endorsed by the ICRC, so they have some authority. And they emphasise promptness, thoroughness, impartiality.\"\nEran Shamir-Borer, director of the Center for Security and Democracy at the Israeli Democracy Institute (IDI) and a former top lawyer for the IDF, said there are \"genuine challenges\" investigating allegations in conflicts Israel is involved in, given the complex combat environments.\n\"[In] a territory which is hostile, not always under your control, collecting evidence could be very challenging. Lots of forces on the ground, many of them executing fire,\" he said.\nShamir-Borer added: \"The idea of investigating soldiers in the context of a war is never a popular thing to do,\" which he said is also seen in other law abiding countries.\nHe added that Israel would only benefit from being more transparent about its investigations, and he would encourage the IDF to be \"more forthcoming with information that they are sharing with the public on the progress they are making in investigating various allegations\".\nSince the Hamas-led attacks in Israel on 7 October 2023, in which about 1,200 people were killed and 251 others were taken hostage, the IDF has carried out extensive military operations in Gaza which it said aimed to bring back the hostages and dismantle Hamas.\nMore than 74,000 Palestinians have been killed in Israeli attacks since the start of the war, according to the Hamas-run health ministry.\nalleged misconduct carried out by Israeli soldiers\nin the only Catholic church in the territory in December 2023, during an incident that the IDF later said was under review.\nA mother, Nahida Anton, and her daughter, Samar Anton, were killed by sniper fire inside the grounds of the Holy Family church in Gaza, according to the Latin Patriarchate of Jerusalem.\nNahida and Samar Anton were killed by Israeli tr", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "c46dcdda349de0a51015711c", "title": "The Nordics and NVIDIA Show What’s Possible When a Region Commits to Advanced Computing", "url": "https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/nordics-technology-ecosystem/", "source": "company_nvidia_blog", "published_at": "2026-10-15T05:00:12+00:00", "text": "The Nordics and NVIDIA Show What’s Possible When a Region Commits to Advanced Computing\nThe Nordic countries have become the European leader in unicorn startup density, producing the most emerging companies valued at $1 billion or more per capita on the continent.\nNow, a new chapter for the region is unfolding as countries race to build one of Europe’s most consequential technology ecosystems.\nThe many computing advancements already underway in the Nordics, combined with the opportunity that lies ahead, signal a region moving from ambition to application.\nOver the past year, NVIDIA has invested in the technology ecosystem in the Nordics, including Sweden’s leading startups\n, a Stockholm-based startup, provides a software creation platform that enables anyone to build and run software using plain language. In collaboration with NVIDIA, Lovable is optimizing inference for open models and training its models using NVIDIA Dynamo software and Nemotron tools.\nprovides an agentic operating system for legal work, supporting lawyers in research, review and drafting across complex matters.\nThe foundation for establishing the Nordics as a technology powerhouse is already being built and the region holds a structural advantage: abundant renewable energy, rigorous data governance and a research tradition that bridges academia and industry.\nThe Nordics are one of Europe’s fastest growing hubs for infrastructure. AI clouds are expanding with a projected 1 gigawatts of capacity to be deployed across the region by the end of 2027. Among these cloud providers are\nEricsson, AstraZeneca, Saab, SEB and Wallenberg Investments,\nand 1,152 GPUs. The consortium will build services using\nopen models, which allow researchers to customize and deploy their models in a secure environment.\nSome of the Nordics’ leading technology companies are now building proprietary intelligence on NVIDIA infrastructure.\n, which personalizes music, podcasts and audiobooks for hundreds of millions of listeners, uses NVIDIA TensorRT-LLM inference software to run AI-powered podcast recommendations faster and cheaper at scale. The deployment is a case study in inference efficiency: delivering the same recommendations to a global audience at a lower cost per query.\nlibraries to bridge the gap between robot simulations and real-world performance. By combining NVIDIA’s advanced simulation capabilities with ABB Robotics’ virtual controller, running the same firmware as its physical robots, the solution enables engineers to train, test and validate robots virtually, while using real-world data to continuously improve the digital model. According to ABB Robotics, the solution delivers up to 99% simulation accuracy and reduces commissioning time by up to 80%.\nis launching a beta of Moontalk AIRI, a real-time enterprise agentic assistant that retrieves information, captures insights and documents decisions during meetings and calls. Built on NVIDIA Nemotron open models and distributed infrastructure, it uses company data and low-latency networking to deliver fast, context-aware responses.\nSweden’s startup ecosystem raised $2.8 billion in the first half of 2026 and is projected to reach $5 billion for the year. This next generation is building fast — and on open models.\nis reinventing how companies protect their inventions with intellectual property. The Stockholm-based startup is post-training NVIDIA Nemotron open models to scale patent analysis from a single patent to entire portfolios — work that was previously out of reach. Nemotron gives\nthe language model accuracy for continuous portfolio monitoring at scale, across the thousands of patents that large industrial and technology companies hold.\nbuilt on NVIDIA Nemotron-3-Embed, delivers more than 20% improvement in cross-lingual retrieval accuracy across English, Finnish and Swedish.\n, a platform that analyzes customer conversations in real time, is using NVIDIA Nemotron 3.5 Lightning to speed up Revenue Mining solution on live sales calls. By routing knowledge retrieval to Nemotron 3.5 Lightning, Agaton can cut response latency to deliver fast responses that inform customers’ buying decisions.\n, a Swedish speech lab, fine-tuned the NVIDIA Parakeet v3 model to create Pianissimo — a Swedish transcription model that reduces errors by 75% while being 64x faster than the most accurate alternative. Built on over 50,000 hours of Swedish speech using the open source\nframework, the model is freely available on Hugging Face.\n, founded in Gothenburg and a member of the\nprogram for cutting-edge startups, and Space Cargo Unlimited unveiled an onboard prototype that monitors protein crystallization, flags anomalies and recommends actions on a single NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX module, without continuous ground connectivity.\n— one of Europe’s 19 EuroHPC-funded AI factories — announced a collaboration with NVIDIA to open its infrastructure and expertise to Inception startups and small- and medium-sized enterprises across industries, including life and material sciences, autonomous systems and gaming.\nAutonomous Machines on Nordic Roads and Factory Floors\nIn Sweden, advanced computing is also powering vehicles on the road and machines in factories.\nis combining its industry-leading vehicle safety systems and engineering expertise with\n, Halos OS and Alpamayo Stack to enable future Volvo cars to safely navigate complex real-world environments from door to door.\nis building its next-generation Einride Driver on\n— the world’s leading forklift manufacturer — is bringing autonomous machines to factory floors. In a collaboration with NVIDIA and\nis adopting a simulation-first approach to autonomous material handling, cutting autonomous solution deployment time by up to 50% across its global engineering, manufacturing and innovation teams.\nand NVIDIA are opening the Stockholm AI Lab, a physical hub for AI-native founders working on global challenges, set to open in 2027.\nFinally, Swedish companies are advancing quantum research using NVIDIA’s quantum computing platform.\nused NVIDIA accelerated computing to train a model that reduces the execution time of quantum processors by 33% without sacrificing performance. Validated on a\nquantum processor unit, this model optimizes how the operations needed by quantum algorithms are turned into detailed hardware instructions, accounting for detailed updates on how the hardware is calibrated.\nTune in to NVIDIA founder and CEO Jensen Huang’s\nGermany Wants to Lead the World in Industrial AI: How NVIDIA Will Help\nNVIDIA Advances Agentic Security for Media, From European Public Service Broadcasting to Global Content Production\nRTX Spark Ignites Global Celebration as New Windows PCs Arrive\nOpen From Data Center to Desktop: NVIDIA Expands Nemotron Family With Highest Efficiency Super VL and Bolt Models", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "6f7706721bc7c958ad7f0e21", "title": "Guwahati: NSUI Sweeps Karmashree Hiteswar Saikia College Students’ Union Election - guwahati Plus News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMipwFBVV95cUxOTE85SWsxdTd6TWxpaEp1cy1MZkRDTTk1SW52eUY1bklnMl9BbEoybXJObVlxblVPd21JYk9VckdYZXNzUFplUXVLcmRBQ055M0E3cURiSU1WMkhpZUFPajl4QmtxRmc4TDRFS3lUVEZIREo3dVplLWp3NEt0bkU1ZVFxcm0xQ1QxdXFtNzJJZHlFeEk0SjdPdFlpZlNBUUJKZ1FNVVk4RQ?oc=5", "source": "lane_labor_organize", "published_at": "2026-10-15T05:00:10+00:00", "text": "Guwahati: NSUI Sweeps Karmashree Hiteswar Saikia College Students’ Union Election - guwahati Plus News\nGuwahati: NSUI Sweeps Karmashree Hiteswar Saikia College Students’ Union Election guwahati Plus News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "6aeffabf7af8f121538cd027", "title": "Guwahati: NSUI Sweeps Karmashree Hiteswar Saikia College Students’ Union Election - guwahati Plus News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuAFBVV95cUxQNFp5cGpvWHlLNUNZX1NaQ25RcWN6TTA0SnBPRWl6X0hxUDg5QjlaYXZGUFlkQ2E1Um9vTFUwQ2V5cVRBWWk0NlJxQkZRWTltWW5FTV9sLXpkSzg5M2FxaHpZV1loQUZMMEN1TFNMR3dqNk11TnRwTTRKNEE4dmN2MHlCT3duZWFHR2t6Zms5eEhTR0FEbUxrUjNqM3UzOXVrekxMcEwwNi1iQWpFaHNmaHMxVklBbjdH?oc=5", "source": "lane_labor_organize", "published_at": "2026-10-15T05:00:10+00:00", "text": "Guwahati: NSUI Sweeps Karmashree Hiteswar Saikia College 