, "published_at": "2026-10-15T02:57:18+00:00", "text": "Speech by Vice Chair for Supervision Bowman on supervisory modernization after Silicon Valley Bank - Federal Reserve (.gov)\nSpeech by Vice Chair for Supervision Bowman on supervisory modernization after Silicon Valley Bank Federal Reserve (.gov)", "truncated": false}, {"id": "1294507171f23353261b547d", "title": "[SMM Analysis] Construction Steel Supply-Demand Fundamentals Maintain a Weak Balance in the Period - Shanghai Metals Market", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi2AFBVV95cUxPVlA1OXdQQXBkZzNkNzZzQ1h0VTBSNnI0UUlyc2prMENlVmw5UGVFc1laS3h3QkNpdTR2ZVVOSVZUZEdOUERkc3dFaHpXTWh4SkFBZ3F2NjJyNGtFTnBDdVN0UFU1VUlLY1pmOWttdVowVmp6dloxWDhRV0FuT0Vzckt4SUVuOGswSkVEZ2cxd1daeXJZN21uS2EwNE1xUkxidEtPQURhakhLNWhzaUNBZGFaZVpZQW5LaTFmVGJYQ2p", "source": "lane_channel_stock", "published_at": "2026-10-15T02:57:00+00:00", "text": "[SMM Analysis] Construction Steel Supply-Demand Fundamentals Maintain a Weak Balance in the Period - Shanghai Metals Market\n[SMM Analysis] Construction Steel Supply-Demand Fundamentals Maintain a Weak Balance in the Period Shanghai Metals Market", "truncated": false}, {"id": "6dbda82e3d43d333a46cb306", "title": "ByteDance reportedly in talks to buy MediaTek AI chips for data centers - Crypto Briefing", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMif0FVX3lxTE1TUWZjUVl2YWpub3dSc2w3d3I1emRZZ1UtSnZGV3RISXVBYkU3M2ZHQUZCUVRWMzhrdzZSaERnaWpmQ1k4Qzc1T2dYNEQtSVBvaTZTOGdXM0dwZ0VUbUZnc01pWXg3SXAxeGRheUczMzFKeFYyaktJQ0c2M2ZFMFk?oc=5", "source": "lane_rumor", "published_at": "2026-10-15T02:55:12+00:00", "text": "ByteDance reportedly in talks to buy MediaTek AI chips for data centers - Crypto Briefing\nByteDance reportedly in talks to buy MediaTek AI chips for data centers Crypto Briefing", "truncated": false}, {"id": "9c2e70ef035e5c9105ceefde", "title": "Bowman, Supervisory Modernization After Silicon Valley Bank", "url": "https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/bowman20261014a.htm", "source": "official_fed_speeches", "published_at": "2026-10-15T02:55:00+00:00", "text": "Bowman, Supervisory Modernization After Silicon Valley Bank\nSupervisory Modernization After Silicon Valley Bank\nVice Chair for Supervision Michelle W. Bowman\nAt the Bangkok Economic Festival, Reinventing Bretton Woods Committee, Bangkok, Thailand\nThank you for the invitation to join you today. It is good to be with you to discuss ensuring that the global financial system is positioned to adapt to the changing economic environment.\nThe pace of change has been brisk. Our financial system is evolving rapidly. Therefore, it is important that we periodically review our regulatory and supervisory frameworks to consider how to best keep pace with that evolution.\nToday, I will discuss three related aspects of supervisory modernization. First, I will describe some lessons learned from a newly released independent assessment of the failure of Silicon Valley Bank (SVB) about the importance of timely supervisory action. Second, I will discuss how the Federal Reserve is enhancing our supervisory effectiveness so that decisionmaking authority is clear and examiners are more accountable for taking timely action. And, third, I will describe how modernization can improve the work of supervision around the world, including under the direction of standard-setting bodies like the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision (BCBS) and the Financial Stability Board (FSB).\nIndependent Review of Silicon Valley Bank\nThe failure of SVB in 2023 is a case study with critical lessons for bank supervisors at home and abroad. While prior reports on SVB provided important information, they were produced on an expedited basis with a limited scope and failed to comprehensively address important data related to the failure. As a result, these reports were incomplete and perpetuated a number of unreliable conclusions. That is why, earlier this year, I commissioned an independent review of the bank failure and the Federal Reserve's related supervision. The review closes prior gaps and corrects important misimpressions regarding SVB's failure. Its preliminary findings substantiate difficult but important critiques regarding Federal Reserve System supervision failures to act in a timely manner and to communicate actionable guidance to SVB.\nA central finding is that supervisors had information about SVB's major vulnerabilities many months before its failure but did not make appropriate use of it. Frontline examiners, risk and surveillance specialists, and Reserve Bank and Board staff knew about SVB's exposure to interest rate and liquidity risk at least as early as nine full months before the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation took the bank into receivership. The review concludes that long-standing uncertainty about decisionmaking authority and the lack of clear accountability impeded timely decisionmaking.\nThe preliminary review also corrects earlier mischaracterization that social media was to blame for the run on SVB, and that the Fed's failure to act was caused by statutory tailoring or regulatory changes.\nInstead, the report notes long-standing problems in supervisory accountability and decisionmaking as the most significant contributors to the failure to issue timely critical enforcement actions related to core risks.\nWhile supervisory decisions must be grounded in evidence, legal authority, and judgment, the impact of supervisory decisions is only effective if delivered in time. When supervisors note vulnerabilities as they emerge, banks have more options and flexibility to course correct. At the point when weakness emerges, a bank can often gradually reduce exposure, strengthen its liquidity position, improve contingency planning, or raise capital under normal market conditions.\nBut where supervisors perpetually delay communication or actionâto continually wait for additional evidence, more concurrence, or another committee meetingâsupervision bogs down and loses effectiveness. Timely and efficient action is key to successful supervisory outcomes.\nThe review notes that supervisory attention at SVB often centered on process, procedure, governance, and controls rather than on mitigating the underlying interest rate and liquidity exposures. SVB had 54 matters requiring attention or immediate attention between 2019 and the time of its failure, yet none of those supervisory findings required the bank to address its core financial risks. In other words, a supervisory organization can be extremely busy and still fail to focus sufficiently on the risks that matter most. It can issue many findings, require firms to spend significant time and resources on remediation of less important process-related issues, and devote enormous resources to governance and process while failing to compel action on the vulnerabilities most capable of threatening the institution's existence.\nSupervisory Prioritization and Accountability\nA supervisory culture of timely action must also be paired with clear roles and responsibilities so that supervisors are empowered to act. The independent review describes uncertainty within the Federal Reserve System about decision rights and lack of accountability across the supervisory system. In our structure, different groups identified risks, many others were expected to concur, and yet no individual clearly owned the obligation to reach a decision and propel action.\nA modern supervisory organization needs clear answers to the basic questions of: who owns the decision to move forward? Who has authority to act? Who is accountable for ensuring that a material risk is addressed? And when different parts of the organization disagree, who is responsible to resolve that disagreement and keep pressing forward?\nModern supervisory organizations increasingly rely on surveillance, specialist teams, and data analysis that allow us to identify patterns across institutions. Those capabilities are enormously valuable. But identifying a risk matters only if there is a clear path from information to judgment and from judgment to action.\nThat is why we at the Federal Reserve System are considering ways to enhance our supervisory decisionmaking framework to ensure\nmore direct allocation of responsibility, authority, and accountability between the Board and Reserve Bank staff;\nclear decision rights for examiners and supervisory leadership; and\ndirect escalation channels from examination teams to senior supervision leadership.\nIn the fall of 2025, the Board published a Statement of Supervisory Operating Principles clearly stating our expectations about how supervisors should conduct supervision. We have refined those principles, most recently to incorporate lessons learned from the independent review of the 2023 banking stress. Publishing these principles further improves supervisory transparency and accountability. They refocus supervisory efforts on what matters most: early identification of material risks, exercising sound supervisory judgment, and a broad range of supervisory tools to enable timely and proportionate action to address material risks before they threaten the safety and soundness of an institution or the stability of the financial system.\nInternational Implications and Proportionality\nThe benefits of our supervisory modernization efforts are not limited to the Federal Reserve. They have implications far beyond the United States. Our reforms and the lessons learned from the independent review are equally applicable to supervisors around the world. As chair of the FSB's Standing Committee on Supervisory and Regulatory Cooperation, I have engaged many member jurisdictions to advance the FSB's regulatory and supervisory modernization agenda. The FSB's 2026 work program reflects that countries around the world are also evaluating whether regulatory and supervisory frameworks remain well suited for future financial systems.\nThere are important common themes in that work