dkJhdEhxTnNlOWY5T2xQY0VjWS1VaW5sLUdfZDE5dEc", "source": "lane_insider_flow", "published_at": "2026-10-14T23:03:26+00:00", "text": "Selling HK.AI Capital Shares at a Lower Price Than Current Market Value May Have Been a Costly Mistake for Insiders - Simply Wall Street\nSelling HK.AI Capital Shares at a Lower Price Than Current Market Value May Have Been a Costly Mistake for Insiders Simply Wall Street", "truncated": false}, {"id": "1ce0082074cabbe94e9dfe57", "title": "Will Board Appointment Change Rathbones Group's (LSE:RAT) Integration Story - Simply Wall Street", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi6wFBVV95cUxQMWxWYmdIRmdkaUNtaVM1b29VckxhbF82aVhObUozeThMT0syRXlteTlhQ256eWFnQU85N0pTamJyUHVzQzBEajV4WVJ5c29hclpMSENQR2sxQ0xWc196QTNIRWZ5c29lOEtZV29qb05TdGhUQ01HcTNtUHlNclpHWDMyRUNWUXE2RFhDdmd6VFp2enFhQkU4M1JkXzhqOUhjcHJleF9CeUNrSzN2amlKcjJzcE95S2hkU1pUdXZiSnN", "source": "lane_key_person", "published_at": "2026-10-14T23:03:26+00:00", "text": "Will Board Appointment Change Rathbones Group's (LSE:RAT) Integration Story - Simply Wall Street\nWill Board Appointment Change Rathbones Group's (LSE:RAT) Integration Story Simply Wall Street", "truncated": false}, {"id": "5ec6c57b107a3bc21b4415c3", "title": "The Bipartisan American Affordability and Jobs Act of 2026 and the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission’s Backstop Siting Authority - Congress.gov", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiV0FVX3lxTE14b0hwanQxNTRNSnc2ZkQzS3lqcnA0R3NlSWZmVTVteERKQXhXcmZzRWwzcWJOZTRib1g1WWU1LVVDUi1UNzNSb3F1cy1NRENnRERTd3BEMA?oc=5", "source": "official_congress_search", "published_at": "2026-10-14T23:03:10+00:00", "text": "The Bipartisan American Affordability and Jobs Act of 2026 and the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission’s Backstop Siting Authority - Congress.gov\nThe Bipartisan American Affordability and Jobs Act of 2026 and the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission’s Backstop Siting Authority Congress.gov", "truncated": false}, {"id": "9b3951232f611f831cdaa05b", "title": "Federal appeals court upholds Missouri’s sex offender registry law - Hannibal.net", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMinwFBVV95cUxNTU1NTnNFTElSNXl6SDdKSm5WQUxMZHlMVGtnV1B4UDJ6MWZ3TUtfWC1fSFVNZ0o0Z0tQenk0Y1JSNzZaMWFlSmhST3pXdWhjb3NVaDB6Si11dm9OVWpNTW5leGFjMmIyVk1icnlXbkVzeWFyODVBUC00c0xOdi1LMWczY1pqcjd5Z014aEluMXdXMmhkR3B4RnRpV29kMVk?oc=5", "source": "lane_blast_legal", "published_at": "2026-10-14T23:03:01+00:00", "text": "Federal appeals court upholds Missouri’s sex offender registry law - Hannibal.net\nFederal appeals court upholds Missouri’s sex offender registry law Hannibal.net", "truncated": false}, {"id": "0dea10406e1d83cc6768ba21", "title": "How backlash against data centres could shape the US midterm elections", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c0rezqpnekro?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_business", "published_at": "2026-10-14T23:02:57+00:00", "text": "How backlash against data centres could shape the US midterm elections\nWatch: The BBC's Shaimaa Khalil is in Arizona to hear from residents about the expansion of data centres.\nA year ago, a longtime US senator was not thinking much about data centres. ââ\nNow, according to one of his advisers, he is having regular meetings with staff to track their impact and working on new legislation aimed at curbing their rapid expansion across the country.\n\"This happens sometimes, something starts slow and then it goes fast,\" the senior policy adviser to the Democratic senator told the BBC. The person spoke on condition of anonymity as they are not permitted to speak to the press.\nThe 180-degree turn in this senator's attention was driven almost entirely by a growing wave of public backlash that has seen politicians across the country scramble to keep up. Public sentiment has turned against the expansion of data centres, the large warehouses that store processing power needed for internet activity.\nPacked with rows and rows of computer chips, sometimes seven-feet high, data centres have turned into more than infrastructure for the internet. They are now the physical embodiment of the recent and explosive growth of artificial intelligence (AI) technology, and the varied fears people associate with it.\n\"I don't want to be the guinea pig for this,\" Indiana resident Angelita Soriano told the BBC.\nA backlash over data centres is another threat to the AI juggernaut\nA new Amazon data centre is being built directly across the street from her home. When complete, the 26-building structure will take up more than a square mile in her neighbourhood and maintain 782 diesel-powered generators.\nSome data centres are even larger. In Ohio, a joint effort between OpenAI, Nvidia, SB Energy and the US Department of Energy is set to build a data centre complex that will stretch for roughly six square miles, the same size as most townships in the US.\nThere are about 3,000 data centres currently operating in the US, with that number potentially doubling in the next few years, according to\nData centres are built through very local rules and permits, meaning federal lawmakers like US senators have little say in the process.\nYet, opposition has become so strong so quickly in the US that suddenly, politicians at all levels are now all but required to take a stand.\n\"This isn't just an issue on the left, there is huge pushback among conservatives to data centres,\" Chloe Autio, an AI policy adviser, said. \"Whoever articulates a clear position and sticks to it, in either party, really stands to benefit.\"\nHer analysis was echoed by the adviser to the US senator working on data centre legislation.\n\"There is a clear policy need here that we're looking to fill,\" he said. \"This is a major backlash across the country - constituents are seeing the impacts of data centres and they do not like them.\"\nThat has put many on Capitol Hill - even Republicans - in direct conflict with President Donald Trump, who has championed the unrestrained growth of AI technology.\nTrump has dubbed the backlash to data centres a \"sick conspiracy\" and said people and towns working to keep the buildings out are \"backwards and poor\".\nHis ire has been used as a boon to Democratic politicians looking to draw midterm votes from unhappy or undecided constituents.\nThe BBC spoke to residents of Indiana, Arizona, Michigan, Pennsylvania and Oregon, all of whom have been organising locally against the development of data centres. They expressed concerns about many aspects of the buildings, including their size, construction and operational noise, their impact on air, water and energy resources, as well as the speed at which they seem to be approved by local authorities.\nDuring the first few months of this year, community groups organising against data centres more than doubled to 833, cropping up in almost every US state, according to research from Data Center Watch. The firm collects data for AI industry stakeholders and is part of an AI startup 10a Labs.\nStephanie Maldonado, the political director of LUCHA, a community organising group based in Arizona, referred to the pushback against data centres as \"a David and Goliath story\" that began several years ago.\n\"People have been ready to put a stop to it,\" Maldonado added. \"Now, people are angry.\"\nshows that AI regulation and data centres are among some of the top issues for voters this election cycle, behind the ongoing Iran war and various aspects of the economy.\nProponents of building data centres often argue the projects will create more, and better paying, local jobs.\nfrom the Brookings Institution, a non-partisan policy research group, found that a new data centre creates between 100 and 200 local jobs, which is \"fewer than industry advocates claim\". The projects also have essentially no impact on local wages.\nLUCHA, a community organising group based in Arizona, has made stopping data centre development one of their key issues\nArizona Governor Katie Hobbs was one such proponent - until public pressure pushed her to the other side of the issue. In July, she enacted a three-year pause on any new tax break given to a company for a data centre project.\nArizona is far from the only state to see politicians react to data centre backlash.\nAbdul El Sayed, a Democrat running for Senate in Michigan, made new rules and regulations for data centres a clear part of his successful primary campaign for Senate.\nIn the race for governor of Georgia, a Democrat who is calling for a pause on data centre development is in a dead heat with a Republican who disagrees.\nRepublican Senator John Husted of Ohio, who is fighting to keep his seat against Democrat Sherrod Brown, has also had to shift his position.\nhave found that the majority of Ohio voters oppose data centres. He has softened his support, saying that their arrival should be up to local citizens and elected officials.\nMeanwhile, Brown has stumped on his opposition to new data centres. He leads Husted in recent polls.\nHusted's trouble in Ohio led the National Republican Senatorial Committee, the major fundraising arm for conservatives in the Senate, to write in a\nthat Republican politicians needed to entirely reframe how they spoke to voters on data centres.\n\"This has become a sleeper issue for the entire election cycle,\" the NRSC added.\nTexas is perhaps the most clear cut example of how backlash has turned into an undeniable issue for even the most pro-business politician.\nThe state currently has almost 300 operational data centres, with another 170 planned for development, according to Pew data.\nAt the end of last year, Republican Governor Greg Abbott had boasted of Texas as \"the epicentre of AI development\" because of the number of data centres it housed.\nWithin months he was backtracking, citing concerns of \"safety and quality of life\".\nIt could play a part in the state's upcoming Senate race too.\nDemocrat James Talarico's campaign is pushing for an end to \"sweetheart tax deals\" for Big Tech. He's now seen as a frontrunner for the historically Republican seat, where he is facing off against Republican Attorney General Ken Paxton.\nPaxton said in a late September interview that there was still \"plenty of open space in Texas that we can put data centres\".\nDemocrat James Talarico has made data centres a part of his campaign for Senate\nMany of the actions or proposals that have come from politicians looking to appease or address these voters are temporary. Most of the moratoriums, for instance, will end in one to three years.\nIt is possible that the political winds will have shifted by the time such moratoriums expire, leaving Big Tech and AI firms to build as they wish.\nFor now, people's feelings toward them are strong.\nBalaji Tammabattula, the chief executive of Barupon, is developing a roughly 700-acre data centre and technology campus in Liberty, Texas. Despite the buildings being designed to use rain capture for water and supply its ", "truncated": true, "full_text": true}, {"id": "afc44ce2a1c7c7fc2f4434c2", "title": "TSMC 'prudent' going into 2027 after posting sixth straight quarter of record profit - Reuters", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMixgFBVV95cUxOWE9HY3JEZl96LXVuQmdXM1I4VVBoUUI4d09uNk9qYUdDSXpBTE8zYnJsNUwxUEljLXJ2aTYxYW5rcnFza0duNVNVRnFCWVAzd19MSXVzUTc2cnhnZkxfaVNXS3I2b25JNlJHZnlpMmpiTkQ1NXF5cWNVTG5sRTk3TE9fZExqRExHcjRUa3EybXEzZ1JRNjJPYlZUTWVaMk5hdWJta0g0UWtHZ251dE5BLTk5bk9nYm5vTDh5alo5c05", "source": "topic_business", "published_at": "2026-10-14T23:02:00+00:00", "text": "TSMC 'prudent' going into 2027 after posting sixth straight quarter of record profit - Reuters\nTSMC 'prudent' going into 2027 after posting sixth straight quarter of record profit Reuters", "truncated": false}, {"id": "6cf513a2aadcf3b85d612ee8", "title": "As Christmas rush looms, how do China-made toys find their way to the US?", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5djvyde0y1jo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_business", "published_at": "2026-10-14T23:01:28+00:00", "text": "As Christmas rush looms, how do China-made toys find their way to the US?\nChina is the world's largest toymaker, producing the majority of toys on sale in the US\nHost â Follow the Money: Business Daily\nOn the underside of most children's toys on sale in the US you'll find the same three words â \"Made in China\".\nNearly eight in 10 toys (78.3%) imported into the country in 2024 came from China, according to\nLast year, toys imported from China were worth $9.8bn (Â£7.4bn),\nWith Christmas on the horizon, the toy industry in the US and elsewhere is about to have its busiest time of the year by far.\nAs retailers prepare their stocks for the festive rush, be they online giants or \"mom-and-pop\" physical stores, the BBC World Service's Follow the Money series traces how a toy gets from a factory in China to the US. And where the money goes.\nGuangdong province, which neighbours Hong Kong in southern China, is the heart of the country's toy manufacturing industry.\nIn the city of Dongguan workers on the floor of the Mihox factory pop plastic toys out of steel moulds, and pile them into crates ready for painting.\nThe company says it employs more than 800 people and makes around 55 million toys a year for the American market, which represents half of its business.\nFollow the money: The journey of a $30 toy from China to America\nEvery toy starts with a design from a customer, such as an American toy company. The factory then makes a prototype, and builds moulds from which plastic parts and other components are formed.\nGoing from the first idea to finished toy takes one to two years, says Melanie Wang from the Mihox sales team. Much of that is the customer going back to the drawing board. \"They modified the design again, again, and again to improve it.\"\nOnce the design is signed off, things move more quickly. A mould takes about a month to make, and the production run lasts about three months.\nThe Mihox factory in Dongguan employs more than 800 people\nSo, how much of the US retail price does the factory get? \"About 20%,\" says Wang.\nOn a $30 toy, that's $6, which has to cover the price of the plastic and other materials, production costs including wages, and what profit the factory makes.\nChinese factories typically make a profit of between 3% and 10%, according to Jay Foreman, chief executive of US toy firm Basic Fun. So for a $30 toy that's between 60 and 18 cents of the $6 the factory charges to make it.\nSamson Ko is president of the Toys Manufacturers' Association of Hong Kong. Many of his members moved their factories across the border into mainland China in the 1980s, attracted by cheaper land and labour.\nHe says the way toys are made has barely changed since then. \"Automation now takes place mostly in the moulding and the packaging process. But for soft toys, or even some of the electronics nowadays, it's still very labour intensive.\"\nAnd Chinese labour isn't cheap anymore, he adds, after recent rises.\nMelanie Wang explains that Mihox gets 20% of a toy's retail price\nAverage pay in Vietnam is about half of that in China, said one\nYet fewer than 10% of Ko's members have moved any production outside China, and in those cases mostly only because customers asked them to do so.\nThe Chinese factories remain busy, he says, because a single toy can undergo more than one manufacturing process, from moulding the parts, to adding electronics, sewing materials, and final assembly. This often requires it travelling to different factories.\nKo adds that in Guangdong these separate facilities are located close to each other, and used to \"working in harmony\". He says this makes the whole manufacturing process run more smoothly than in other countries.\nFrom the factory, the toy goes by truck to a port, such as Shenzhen, the biggest in Guangdong.\nIvy Tse is co-founder of FreightAmigo, a Hong Kong logistics company that runs an online platform for both Hong Kong and mainland China exporters. These businesses can book a cargo container, sea passage to an overseas destination, arrange insurance cover, and secure finance.\nTse says it takes about 15 days for a cargo ship to get from Shenzhen to the US west coast, and 25 to 30 days to the east coast. This year's Christmas toys left China in August and September.\nFreight, from factory to the US, usually adds about 10% to the manufacturing cost of a toy, adds Tse. So if we stick to a toy that retails for $30 in the US, and therefore costs $6 to make in China, the cost of getting it to the states is 60 cents.\nOnce the toy reaches the US there is currently a tariff on Chinese toys that has to be paid to the US Customs and Border Protection (CBP).\nFollowing talks between President Trump and Chinese leader Xi Jinping last month, the two governments have recommended toys for lower tariffs. But no new rate or start date has been set, and the US must first hold a public consultation. For now the basic rate is 12.5%, and some toys pay more.\nThis is charged on the manufacturing price of the toy, not on how much it retails for. So for a $30 toy, that basic rate is 12.5% of $6, which is 75 cents.\nThis tariff is paid for by the American company that has imported the toys. One such firm is Learning Resources, a family business based near Chicago that sells educational toys. Half of these it imports from China.\n\"We pay 100% of the tariffs, let there be no doubt about that,\" says the company's chief executive Rick Woldenberg.\nJay Foreman says that most of the money spent on a toy in the US doesn't leave the country\nThe cost of making the $30 toy, getting it to the US, and paying the import tariff, comes to $7.35. So that leaves $22.65, or 75.5% of the total.\nThat money never leaves the US, explains Foreman from Basic Fun. His firm, which is based in Florida, makes Tonka trucks and Care Bears cuddly toys.\n\"It's kept by my company, the [US] transportation company, the retailer,\" he says.\nIf we allow $2.65 for the US delivery firm, the toy company and the retailer will typically share the remaining revenues 50-50. So that would give them $10 each.\nWhile profit margins are commercially sensitive and therefore not generally revealed, industry studies show that the toy firm and retailer would usually make a profit of up to 25% or $2.50.\nGetting a toy onto the shelves in the US requires both a lot of work and a long journey.\nHow oil-rich Alberta's economy might fare if it broke away from Canada\nThe wealthy Cuban Americans ready and waiting for Havana to fall\nHow the oil capital of the US welcomed a solar power boom", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "21c8f65b5ef4b79f64c9363c", "title": "How much is the minimum wage?", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cy7e8mpdxpro?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "source": "rss_bbc_business", "published_at": "2026-10-14T23:01:11+00:00", "text": "How much is the minimum wage?\nThe official minimum rates of pay rose for 2.7 million workers on 1 April.\nThe rate for workers aged 21 and over is called the National Living Wage, while those aged 18 to 20 are paid the National Minimum Wage.\nWhat is the National Living Wage, and how much is it worth?\nFrom 1 April 2026, workers over 21 on the National Living Wage are paid Â£12.71 an hour, 50p more than in 2025.\nFor someone working full time (37.5 hours a week), that amounts to Â£24,784.50 a year - an increase of Â£900.\nWhat is the National Minimum Wage and how much is it worth?\nThe rate for 18 to 20-year-olds has gone up to Â£10.85 an hour.\nIt amounts to an increase of Â£1,500 a year for a full-time worker.\nThe government said its goal is to eventually scrap this separate rate for 18 to 20-year-olds, and have one rate for all adults.\nThe minimum wage for 16 and 17-year-olds has also risen to Â£8 an hour.\nWhat is the apprentice rate and how much is it worth?\ndepending on their age and what stage of apprenticeship they are in.\nApprentices aged 16 to 18 are entitled to the National Minimum Wage rate for that age group which is currently Â£8.\nThose aged 19, or in the first year of their apprenticeship, are paid the same amount.\nBut those over 19 - or who have already completed their first year - are entitled to the appropriate National Minimum Wage or National Living Wage rate for their age.\nWhat is an apprenticeship and how much is the pay?\nWho doesn't qualify for the National Minimum Wage and National Living Wage?\nSome workers do not qualify for either the National Minimum Wage or the National Living Wage.\nThis includes the self-employed, company directors, volunteers, members of the armed forces and prisoners.\nPeople with disabilities or in long-term unemployment who take part in government work programmes are paid fixed amounts at different stages of the scheme.\nThese are less than the equivalent National Minimum or Living rate.\nDo employers have to pay the National Minimum Wage and National Living Wage?\nYes. It is a criminal offence for employers not to pay the correct National Minimum and Living Wages to eligible employees.\nThe rates apply even if workers are not paid by the hour.\nIf you think you are being paid the wrong amount, you can\nWhat happens if employers don't pay the right wage?\nAny employer not paying the correct amount can be penalised by HMRC.\nthe government said that 389 employers had been fined around Â£12.6m for failing to pay staff properly\nThey must also repay the missing Â£7.3m to the around 60,000 workers who were underpaid.\n, Norwich City Football Club, Hays Travel and Costa Coffee.\nWhat is the Real Living Wage and how much is it?\nis an unofficial hourly rate of pay which is overseen by the Living Wage Foundation charity, who say it is set at a rate to meet the cost of living.\nSome firms choose to pay the Real Living Wage, but it is not mandatory.\nAccording to the charity almost 500,000 employees working for nearly 17,000 firms receive the voluntary rate of pay.\nIn October 2026, the foundation said the UK rate would increase by 4.8% to Â£14.10 an hour, while the London Living Wage for workers in the capital would go up by 4.4% to Â£15.45.\nThe Living Wage Foundation says the rate is worth over Â£2,700 more per year than the legal minimum wage in the UK, and over Â£5,300 more in London.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "da2891027cfb918a05d179b8", "title": "United States Court of Appeals - United States Courts (.gov)", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMi7gFBVV95cUxPX0xLemNGYmt4T215cHk0aWhRNDl3N2hTSURBQUxIZDB6YzRQQXBXRzJ0UmpsTjB0a0RXUVd6Wk90b3E5T0VlX3A0OWdTMi1maC1IMUpfMlo1TmIwWDctaGoyZ0h1TXU2eTBFRWZVNlpiVkZqMEx2UUFmWFd3TlJ6WmxmMl8zRDFJTFRUdHVmR0t5WXk0d1MwY21hRHdTODkyUlVrWlYwQzVTQlY3MDU1Rk5jNVV2RG9wLWlYMUxtYjh", "source": "official_court_search", "published_at": "2026-10-14T23:00:48+00:00", "text": "United States Court of Appeals - United States Courts (.gov)\nUnited States Court of Appeals United States Courts (.gov)", "truncated": false}, {"id": "925069aac867b8ef874b9e0b", "title": "'Doctor Who' Showrunner Officially Confirms Cancelled Plans for Sci-Fi Spin-Off - Collider", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMipwFBVV95cUxQOUlTMHNFRHl1My1Uc0ppZ08zVUhrMjBlRXMyZGFidmp0akRzTy12QnhDN1hGdS0zUWFfaElYVGNEX3VJcTVMWFQxeFIzaVlFcGMwcUJfSFZUSGVKSGZqdGRDOHlwOGFlQ25mMmMxQTlYS1JjQWxWSmdkVExqOXhoaXR6ZnhSMnFkbUt6SG9aTDRxeFlkWTJMcnlLdjlXaDBWSmxMQTZwYw?oc=5", "source": "lane_corporate_action_spinoff", "published_at": "2026-10-14T23:00:12+00:00", "text": "'Doctor Who' Showrunner Officially Confirms Cancelled Plans for Sci-Fi Spin-Off - Collider\n'Doctor Who' Showrunner Officially Confirms Cancelled Plans for Sci-Fi Spin-Off Collider", "truncated": false}, {"id": "9ae5e81389d34a99f28f9ff7", "title": "Oil Market Tightens as Tanker Attacks and Inventory Draws Mount - Crude Oil Prices Today | OilPrice.com", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiqgFBVV9