://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/14/morgan-stanley-ms-q3-2026-earnings.html", "source": "rss_cnbc_finance", "published_at": "2026-10-14T20:22:09+00:00", "text": "Morgan Stanley tops estimates as equities trading revenue beats expectations by $909 million\nMorgan Stanley topped expectations on the top and bottom lines for the third quarter.\nResults were powered by equities trading, which exceeded revenue expectations by $909 million.\nStill, the results were good, not great, as some business metrics showed slower improvement in the quarter, according to Wells Fargo analyst Mike Mayo.\nMorgan Stanley CEO Ted Pick: 'I'm cautiously optimistic about the fourth quarter'\nposted third-quarter results that beat expectations on blockbuster results in its equities trading franchise.\n$20.34 billion vs. $19.95 billion LSEG estimate\nThe company said profit climbed 6.5% to $4.91 billion, or $3.03 a share. Revenue rose 12% to $20.34 billion.\nThe firm's equities trading division, long a marquee business for Morgan Stanley, posted a 41% increase in revenue to $5.8 billion, exceeding the StreetAccount estimate by about $909 million.\nMorgan Stanley noted widespread strength across its equities products and regions, but cited Asia as a source of particular strength in the quarter. The gains in that business helped offset disappointments elsewhere.\nFixed income trading revenue fell 7% to $2.01 billion, driven by declines in credit and commodities, which is roughly $260 million below expectations.\nInvestment banking revenue edged 2% higher to $2.16 billion, about $110 million below expectations. The bank cited an increase in advisory revenue on more completed mergers, but declines in equity underwriting on lower initial public offerings and a drop in fixed income underwriting.\nStill, the results were \"good, but not great,\" as some business metrics showed slower improvement in the quarter, Wells Fargo analyst Mike Mayo said Wednesday in a note.\nMorgan Stanley shares declined about 3% Wednesday.\nThe economy is subject to two key forces right now: the boost from the artificial intelligence build-out and the drag from geopolitical strife, Morgan Stanley CEO Ted Pick told CNBC's Leslie Picker on Wednesday.\nStarting in early September, concerns with overseas conflict, inflation and surging yields slowed activity across Wall Street, Pick noted.\n\"The interaction of the AI phenomenon and geopolitics has been the dominant reality the whole year,\" Pick said. \"It's really two very different stories, and I'd like to think that we are going to have the first narrative, the positive narrative, if we can get some control over the geopolitics.\"\nPick is \"cautiously optimistic\" about markets in the fourth quarter, he added.\nChoose CNBC as your preferred source on Google and never miss a moment from the most trusted name in business news.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "d9e502b74bb0f81730f31a3e", "title": "Press Release: Ansari Marks Signing of Bipartisan Billion Dollar Boondoggle Act Into Law - Quiver Quantitative", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiwAFBVV95cUxOUW94cXpHSzNQcFpaaUVELXl6YlljaURjb2VVRW5SUmZNMnJ1MUxtVFFVSVpDQUlFV0ZGNnhTbnBVcjRVTDBUUlZhVEJDVzRhSHdmdkxwdFRRdHB6OWFEV0s0bF9RanRWNlBsdUU4Y3ZnVmM4bDdjdnQzaE9vc1lHd1ZkLVVweEZrREFFN00yRWxFWXJQWDNkSXU2R1BZM0xMNnluOEVJYndSSFJHRGNWSGt4d0Vlb1NkWXJBcXZUcmY", "source": "lane_tax_fiscal", "published_at": "2026-10-14T20:21:00+00:00", "text": "Press Release: Ansari Marks Signing of Bipartisan Billion Dollar Boondoggle Act Into Law - Quiver Quantitative\nPress Release: Ansari Marks Signing of Bipartisan Billion Dollar Boondoggle Act Into Law Quiver Quantitative", "truncated": false}, {"id": "194a2638e3fbf6130c149b46", "title": "AI now linked to 84% of ransomware attacks, report finds - CBS News", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMigwFBVV95cUxNZFMwTlpuVFB3TTdSZ2d5Y281SHdwYXZpNnNDUjFscUswSGdHRW1fLW42OVVlRkZJYWFEdEV1cWZ4bWZFR3VGV3NLa0M2VjhDM1lMZkROSjQwNExEbU5aeXFTbDdBVGVINEJYNF8yRHk5dVhOdVY2U2ZUNEt2bjJIeDdTaw?oc=5", "source": "lane_blast_cyber", "published_at": "2026-10-14T20:21:00+00:00", "text": "AI now linked to 84% of ransomware attacks, report finds - CBS News\nAI now linked to 84% of ransomware attacks, report finds CBS News", "truncated": false}, {"id": "0648410ae1c741c3de675138", "title": "Bank of America shares drop after third-quarter revenue miss and regulatory penalty", "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/14/bank-of-america-bac-q3-2026-earnings.html", "source": "rss_cnbc_finance", "published_at": "2026-10-14T20:20:46+00:00", "text": "Bank of America shares drop after third-quarter revenue miss and regulatory penalty\nBank of America's third-quarter profit fell 17% to $6.93 billion, or 91 cents a share.\nRevenue was below expectations by about $570 million, largely on misses from Wall Street trading.\nMisses in fixed income and equities trading revenue totaled roughly $750 million.\nThe trading disappointment comes after CEO Brian Moynihan told analysts last month that trading revenue would be roughly flat; instead the combined bond and stock trading results fell by 5%.\nBank of America CEO on $1.2 billion fine: We're on the right course to remediate everything\non Wednesday posted a sharp profit decline after being hit with a\nby U.S. regulators, while revenue also disappointed after trading operations generated about $750 million less than expected.\nShares of the bank fell almost 5% on Wednesday.\n91 cents, may not compare with $1.10 expected by LSEG\n$30.12 billion vs. $30.69 billion expected by LSEG\nThe bank's third-quarter profit fell 17% to $6.93 billion, or 91 cents a share. Excluding the penalty, which sapped income by 17 cents, EPS would've been $1.08 a share.\nCompanywide revenue rose 3.2% to $30.12 billion in the quarter, on an FTE basis, but that was below expectations by about $570 million, largely on misses from Wall Street trading operations.\nFixed income trading revenue dropped 17% to $2.55 billion on weakness in mortgages, municipals and credit activity. That is roughly $450 million below the StreetAccount estimate.\nEquities trading revenue climbed 10% to $2.51 billion, but that was also below analysts' expectations, by nearly $300 million.\nCEO Brian Moynihan told analysts last month\nthat trading revenue would be roughly flat; instead the combined bond and stock trading results fell by 5%.\nInvestment banking revenue dropped 11% to $1.8 billion, roughly in line with expectations and Moynihan's September guidance.\nNet interest income, a key profitability driver watched closely by Bank of America investors, rose 7.9% to $16.6 billion, slightly edging out the $16.53 billion StreetAccount estimate.\nIn a research note published Wednesday, Wells Fargo analyst Mike Mayo called the firm's capital markets results \"easily worst in class\" for the quarter.\nThe Treasury episode, in which U.S. regulators\nthe company failed to report billions of dollars in suspicious transactions, sparked questions as to whether Moynihan has a handle on controls at the second-biggest U.S. bank by assets.\nOn the company's earnings call Wednesday, several analysts remarked on the sharp decline in Bank of America shares and pressed Moynihan about earlier guidance that compliance costs would be contained.\n\"Clearly you've had a huge charge this morning,\" JPMorgan analyst Vivek Juneja said. \"It's not that you can't absorb a billion. … But obviously it's a reputational and confidence issue.\"\nOther analysts questioned management on the pace of future share repurchases and asked about the bank's underperformance in trading. Management blamed the firm's bond trading results on the sharp rise in interest rates during the period.\n\"It just happens when rates go up 85 basis points in a quarter, that tends not to be good for\" credit and mortgage trading businesses, CFO Alastair Borthwick said.\nLater Wednesday in an interview with CNBC's\n, Moynihan didn't commit to a timeline on the resolution of regulatory issues.\n\"We got to do the work, and then the regulators look at it,\" Moynihan said. \"So I don't want to speculate about the precise timeline.\"\nThe CEO added that he was \"confident that we are on the right course to remediate everything.\"\nMoynihan took the helm in 2010 and spent the early years of his tenure making amends for mortgage-related abuses in the aftermath of the 2008 financial crisis.\nThe Treasury fine, announced on Tuesday, added 11th-hour intrigue to what was an otherwise fairly routine bank earnings week. On Tuesday,\nwith JPMorgan missing EPS and revenue estimates for the first time in years.\nWatch CNBC’s full interview with Bank of America CEO Brian Moynihan\nCorrection: Bank of America reported third-quarter revenue on an FTE basis of $30.12 billion compared with LSEG estimates of $30.69 billion. An earlier version misstated the comparison.\nChoose CNBC as your preferred source on Google and never miss a moment from the most trusted name in business news.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "67e0561e6c24b364b747d95c", "title": "Revenue rose 8.6%, yet Better Home & Finance (BETR) missed guidance in preliminary Q3 results. - Stock Titan", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuwFBVV95cUxNYzNHdk91NjRFcFNwaDFPVEVLYm5naFNNaTJ6MUR0c2pta1h5TkFhWWVTT2xmaHhOQ3I0M1N3RU9tQWl6Zmlja1FJUWlmTUV0cTRvQnZSNVhWci1DTkVYRUp5Z2pTaDF5NFk0NTJweWdpOG1qTFo5Mm94WWVVdEM4UVNtU1BUT3BkN2VpU2Utdm13Q2NUdXZqZlk0dzVMdVNVRnl0OEdvbTR4Z19UYmxaUlUzbnU2OHVJSzQ0?oc=5", "source": "sec_8-K:google_fallback", "published_at": "2026-10-14T20:20:36+00:00", "text": "Revenue rose 8.6%, yet Better Home & Finance (BETR) missed guidance in preliminary Q3 results. - Stock Titan\nRevenue rose 8.6%, yet Better Home & Finance (BETR) missed guidance in preliminary Q3 results. Stock Titan", "truncated": false}, {"id": "5e270a8a2a32344537f0a5dd", "title": "Genesis HealthCare Wins Court Approval for Nearly $1B Sale of 175 Nursing Homes to New Generatio