X1lfX09lTWhCTFFZNmg5Z3d6MmRsZ25mcDBXXzJlaWlDbEtVdDMwZmZDVXU1TURXc1BYZlY0T3FWY1Z4UXVuVm90dGpxQ0FYdjdSdTZweFF4LV8xdThqcE83YXlNNHU?oc=5", "source": "lane_cat_weather", "published_at": "2026-10-14T20:07:46+00:00", "text": "Sonoma County homes and businesses brace for power shutoffs - Northern California Public Media\nSonoma County homes and businesses brace for power shutoffs Northern California Public Media", "truncated": false}, {"id": "0257aee9abcb01f1cba66968", "title": "Bank of America hired 1,000 contractors to patch software flaws exposed by Anthropic's Mythos", "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/14/anthropic-mythos-bank-of-america-hiring-cost.html", "source": "rss_cnbc_finance", "published_at": "2026-10-14T20:06:28+00:00", "text": "Bank of America hired 1,000 contractors to patch software flaws exposed by Anthropic's Mythos\nBank of America added 1,000 third-party consultants to support 3,000 internal staff in patching proprietary and vendor software flaws exposed by Anthropic's Mythos.\nSoftware patching, combined with anti-money laundering compliance, added hundreds of millions in third-quarter costs, which CEO Brian Moynihan expects will decline over time.\nWhile defensive patching creates short-term expenses, BofA’s 130 internal AI projects are projected to drive $700 million in savings next year.\nBank of America CEO on $1.2 billion fine: We're on the right course to remediate everything\nhas hired an army of external contractors to help it patch software vulnerabilities exposed by advanced artificial intelligence models including\n, Moynihan said that earlier this year the company rushed to bolster a wide spectrum of its internal and vendor code using thousands of employees and outside personnel.\n\"That risk is real risk, and that's why when the frontier-type models came out. … A lot of us sat down and said, 'How do we figure out how to get through this quickly?\" Moynihan told CNBC's\n\"We put on 1,000 contractors to help us do the patching work in the second quarter, and those people remain here today, helping us, on top of the 3,000 people we have in that area,\" he said.\nThe bank spent a \"couple hundred million plus\" on the software remediation and\nrelated to anti-money laundering compliance in the third quarter, Moynihan told analysts earlier Wednesday.\nThe comments are some of the first by a major U.S. corporation to specify the manpower and dollars spent to protect software systems from Mythos, which was\nto a small group of banks, technology firms and government agencies in April.\nThey also highlight the dual-edged nature of AI: While it can help companies save money by boosting productivity, the ability of models like Mythos to uncover and exploit decades-old software flaws has also forced firms into a costly defensive scramble.\n\"We've got to stay ahead of them, and we've got to be mindful that the people doing the software development are the people trying to sell us the services,\" Moynihan said.\nStill, Moynihan — speaking as his company's shares\n— sought to reassure investors that the spending increase was temporary.\n\"Once you patch, you don't have to patch as much. … The pipeline moves through,\" Moynihan said, adding that the bank expects remediation costs to decline over time.\nIn other places, AI is helping to reduce expenses. Bank of America has about 130 internal AI projects, and savings from those efforts are expected to rise from $300 million this year to $700 million in 2027, Moynihan told analysts.\nBank of America's head count fell by about 3,400 employees in the past year as the firm cut roughly 5% of managers, he said. It's unclear how much of that workforce reduction is attributable to AI.\nChoose CNBC as your preferred source on Google and never miss a moment from the most trusted name in business news.", "truncated": false, "full_text": true}, {"id": "751a7f022c39e5ecc8e1f29e", "title": "Coinbase Announces Date of Third Quarter 2026 Financial Results - StreetInsider", "url":